\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US companies accused of \u8216\'20\'18AI washing\u8217\'20\'19 in citing artificial intelligence for job losses\b0\par\par\uc2 Eric Berger \par \uc2 1,186 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 February 2026\par 16:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While AI is having an impact on the workplace, experts suggest tariffs, overhiring during the pandemic and simply maximising profits may be bigger factors\par \par \uc2 Over the last year, US corporate leaders have often explained layoffs by saying the positions were no longer needed because artificial intelligence had made their companies more efficient, replacing humans with computers.\par \par \uc2 But some economists and technology analysts have expressed skepticism about such justifications and instead think that such workforce cuts are driven by factors like the impact of tariffs, overhiring during the Covid-19 pandemic and perhaps simple maximising of profits.\par \par \uc2 In short, the CEOs are allegedly engaged in \u8220\'20\'1CAI-washing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can say, \u8216\'20\'18We are integrating the newest technology into our business processes, so we are very much a technological frontrunner, and we have to let go of these people,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Fabian Stephany, a departmental research lecturer at the \uc2 Oxford Internet Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 2025, AI was cited as a reason for more than 54,000 layoffs, according to a December report from the consulting firm {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Challenger-Report-December-2025.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  alone laid off {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/28/amazon-global-job-cuts-email-error-workers-sent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16,000 workers}}}\uc2  after making 14,000 reductions in October.\par \par \uc2 Beth Galetti\uc2 , senior vice-president of people experience and technology at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , explained in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-workforce-reduction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an October memo}}}\uc2  that they were trimming staff because \u8220\'20\'1CAI is the most transformative technology we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen since the internet, and it\u8217\'20\'19s enabling companies to innovate much faster than ever before.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re convinced that we need to be organized more leanly,\u8221\'20\'1D Galetti added.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Enrique Lores\uc2 , also said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/7HP.DE/earnings/7HP.DE-Q4-2025-earnings_call-371600.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZ29vZ2xlLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAAKAWCE9wpJkX3nvSgsZaWz-CBicrXUcoNknxhbdprF0DqrhQtlhL8j36d1eNzg4S-Kwx4Nf1ihYMrEeYAQJzTT7hVanUlNYFFwoZ7irjeio12HAGavgMlfuZzoLR0GDq5FAZpEwfoHcLIq0J4QqQZDv2I_g76qSfF5FzmU31L8eI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November earnings call}}}\uc2  that the company would use AI to \u8220\'20\'1Cimprove customer satisfaction and boost productivity\u8221\'20\'1D, which means the company could cut 6,000 people in the \u8220\'20\'1Cnext years\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In April, \uc2 Luis von Ahn\uc2 , CEO of the language-learning app company \uc2 Duolingo\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/duolingo_below-is-an-all-hands-email-from-our-activity-7322560534824865792-l9vh/?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop&rcm=ACoAAAaYK5sBg9iWr5g0F7T3PBY3JYYXFK1Tj9c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced that}}}\uc2  the venture would \u8220\'20\'1Cgradually stop using contractors to do work that AI can handle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But the reason for such layoffs is often actually financial, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forrester.com/press-newsroom/forrester-impact-ai-jobs-forecast/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a January report}}}\uc2  from the market research firm \uc2 Forrester\uc2 . The company projects that only 6% of US jobs will be automated by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Companies could use AI to replace people working in call centers and technical writing, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t yet have apps that can replace most occupations and probably won\u8217\'20\'19t soon, said JP Gownder, a Forrester vice-president and principal analyst.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of companies are making a big mistake because their CEO, who isn\u8217\'20\'19t very deep into the weeds of AI, is saying, \u8216\'20\'18Well, let\u8217\'20\'19s go ahead and lay off 20 to 30% of our employees and we will backfill them with AI,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Gownder said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you do not have a mature, deployed-AI application ready to do the job \u8230\'20\'26 it could take you 18 to 24 months to replace that person with AI \u8211\'20\'13 if it even works.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But there are benefits to attributing layoffs to AI even if that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 For example, the Challenger report stated that tariffs were cited as the reasons for fewer than 8,000 layoffs, a fraction of the number attributed to AI.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost economists would tell you that that was implausible,\u8221\'20\'1D said Martha Gimbel, executive director and co-founder of the Budget Lab at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CChatGPT was only released three years ago \u8230\'20\'26 It is not the case that a new technology develops and the workforce adjusts immediately. That is just not how it works.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/apr/29/amazon-trump-tariff-costs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 news report}}}\uc2  stated that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  planned to display how much \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariffs increased product pricing, the White House described it as a \u8220\'20\'1Chostile and political act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson then said, \u8220\'20\'1CThis was never approved and is not going to happen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have seen a real hesitance among some parts of corporate America to say anything negative about the economic impacts of the Trump administration because they feel that there will be consequences,\u8221\'20\'1D Gimbel said. \u8220\'20\'1CBy saying that the layoffs are due to new efficiencies created by AI, you avoid that potential pushback.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 CEOs could also be blaming layoffs on AI advancements when they actually just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/economy/jobs/u-s-companies-are-still-slashing-jobs-to-reverse-pandemic-hiring-boom-abf1b94e?gaa_at=eafs&gaa_n=AWEtsqcHhyIpAVVDhFBzS4yFD0N7arJSiRMkuhQpK--uczoyDXXr2XNJP19_LMMwYA%3D%3D&gaa_ts=6984b6e2&gaa_sig=X0vYp4Tq1JimJxF1VgzW1Gd95K_6zOONm5ey2wBLYhRjicZUFnieWtsD1NhI2ap0OiYL_ClOjaXeoT1fF5HRKg%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overhired during}}}\uc2  the pandemic, Gownder said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was driven by low interest rates. That was driven by talent wars. That was driven by some dynamics that are not in place any more,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Still, there are instances where CEOs linked layoffs to AI where that is more likely to be the legitimate reason, the economists said.\par \par \uc2 For example, \uc2 Marc Benioff\uc2 , CEO of the cloud-based software company \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , said during an interview on the podcast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://podcasts.apple.com/cy/podcast/ep-149-marc-benioff-ceo-salesforce-predicts-half-of/id1606770839?i=1000724017332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Logan Bartlett Show}}}\uc2  that he reduced his customer staff from 9,000 to 5,000 because he now uses AI agents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI need less heads,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Stephany said that was plausible.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe work that has been described \u8211\'20\'13 particularly online and customer support \u8211\'20\'13 is, in terms of tasks and required skills, relatively close to what current AI systems can perform,\u8221\'20\'1D Stephany said.\par \par \uc2 But that does not mean the public should just accept Benioff\u8217\'20\'19s claim, the AI researchers said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think CEO statements are possibly the worst way to figure out how technological change is affecting the labor market,\u8221\'20\'1D Gimbel said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is not to say that CEOs are lying \u8230\'20\'26 It\u8217\'20\'19s to say that there\u8217\'20\'19s incentive effects in what gets covered.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not long after Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s vice-president linked the October layoffs to AI, the CEO, Andy Jassy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/oct/30/aws-revenue-outage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backpedaled}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said they were \u8220\'20\'1Cnot really financially driven, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not even really AI-driven, not right now. It really is culture.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And months after the Duolingo CEO stated that the company would be \u8220\'20\'1CAI first\u8221\'20\'1D and only add to its headcount \u8220\'20\'1Cif a team cannot automate more of their work\u8221\'20\'1D, he told the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/17/business/duolingo-luis-von-ahn.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  that the company had never laid off full-time employees and did not plan to.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrom the beginning, we\u8217\'20\'19ve had contractors that we use for temporary tasks, and our contractor force has gone up and down depending on needs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 An employee laid off by Amazon in October described herself as a \u8220\'20\'1Cheavy user of AI\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere were certain tools that I built specifically for my team\u8217\'20\'19s use, as well as some of our customer teams to use,\u8221\'20\'1D said the former principal program manager, whose last day at Amazon was in January and asked not to be identified to protect her privacy because she has not yet received severance pay.\par \par \uc2 She does not think that AI is why she was terminated but instead \u8220\'20\'1Cmaybe aided the ability to have a more junior person do some of the work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 After an employee told her, \u8220\'20\'1CBring me up to speed on the stuff you were working on. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to assign this to one of these new people,\u8221\'20\'1D it became clear \u8220\'20\'1Cthat this work wasn\u8217\'20\'19t going to stop but that they were going to get someone who was paid far less to do that work\u8221\'20\'1D, she said.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CI was laid off to save the cost of human labor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260208em28003h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
