\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Workers accuse new company managing Australia\u8217\'20\'19s immigration detention centres of running them \u8216\'20\'18like a prison\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Ariel Bogle and Christopher Knaus \par \uc2 1,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2026\par 14:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: Critical incidents and workplace safety concerns have increased in the 10 months since MTC took over management of the centres, staff claimGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s new onshore immigration detention operator has been accused by workers and their union of unsafe and \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliberate\u8221\'20\'1D levels of understaffing, as the number of critical incidents increase in the centres.\par \par \uc2 This sharp reduction in staffing levels has prompted a rare public intervention from the Australian government, which said it was aware of \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerns\u8221\'20\'1D about its primary immigration detention contractor and signalled it has raised the issue with the company.\par \par \uc2 The US private prison company \uc2 Management & Training Corporation\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/jan/29/australia-immigration-detention-company-involved-trump-ice-migrant-crackdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 played a key role}}}\uc2  in the US immigration crackdown \u8211\'20\'13 has operated Australia\u8217\'20\'19s onshore detention network since March 2025.\par \par \uc2 Its local subsidiary, Secure Journeys, took over from embattled provider \uc2 Serco\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/dec/11/secure-journeys-onshore-detention-operator-us-prisons-albanese-government-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 winning a contract}}}\uc2  worth $2.3b in late 2024. MTC is also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/sep/29/us-private-prisons-operator-paid-790m-to-hold-100-people-on-nauru-in-quiet-expansion-of-contract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 being paid}}}\uc2  $790m to hold 100 people on Nauru.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Australian Border Force\uc2  spokesperson said the company is required to meet strict contractual obligations in providing detention services. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Department \u8230\'20\'26 continues to work with Secure Journeys to ensure each facility is safely and appropriately staffed,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 The United Workers Union (UWU), which represents workers in the centres, alleged understaffing is driven by a business model that \u8220\'20\'1Cprioritises lower headcounts and flexibility over safety\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMorale was at an all-time low with \uc2 Serco\uc2 , and now it\u8217\'20\'19s lower than that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Godfrey Moase, executive director of allied industries at the UWU.\par \par \uc2 The issue is driven in part by onerous roster patterns and payroll issues, the union and staff claim, which is driving workers to exhaustion and out of roles.\par \par \uc2 The UWU estimates there were about 1,000 workers across the network as of late 2025, including contractors and subcontractors \u8211\'20\'13 down from approximately 1,900 nationally in 2023. It claims that understaffing issues are most acute in the largest and highest-risk centres.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed&CMP=emailbutton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Sign up: AU Breaking News email }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are running it like a prison,\u8221\'20\'1D said John*, a detention service officer at Brisbane Immigration Detention Centre. He estimates that a compound which used to run with four workers may now be run with two or sometimes only one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COvernight, there are \u8230\'20\'26 compounds [with] only one staff member], which is absolutely unsafe,\u8221\'20\'1D he alleged.\par \par \uc2 SafeWork NSW said it has made multiple visits to the Villawood centre since March 2025, and been notified of three workplace incidents and received five requests for service regarding worker health and safety. It did not provide any further detail about the incidents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSafeWork NSW continues to monitor the progress of the business in taking the required actions,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 Simon*, a detention service officer at Villawood under both Secure Journeys and \uc2 Serco\uc2 , said there has been a significant reduction in staff numbers on site. He\u8217\'20\'19s also questioned whether some new officers, who are sent to a five-day bridging course, are being properly trained.\par \par \uc2 The death of a detainee at Villawood in January has contributed to employees\u8217\'20\'19 low morale, Simon said. Police {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.police.nsw.gov.au/news/news_article?sq_content_src=%2BdXJsPWh0dHBzJTNBJTJGJTJGZWJpenByZC5wb2xpY2UubnN3Lmdvdi5hdSUyRm1lZGlhJTJGMTIyOTA4Lmh0bWwmYWxsPTE%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged}}}\uc2  a 29-year-old man who was also a detainee at the centre with murder. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter that incident, people are a bit apprehensive,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not the only incident. In December, a detainee {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afp.gov.au/news-centre/media-release/detainee-charged-allegedly-assaulting-officers-immigration-detention#:~:text=The%20AFP%20has%20charged%20a,%2C%20and%2013%20December%2C%202025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was charged}}}\uc2  after allegedly assaulting multiple security officers at Yongah Hill Immigration Detention Centre in Western Australia, including pressing a cigarette into the neck of one and punching another in the face. At Melbourne Immigration Detention Centre in August, another male detainee allegedly assaulted two male officers and was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afp.gov.au/news-centre/media-release/melbourne-immigration-detention-centre-detainee-charged-over-alleged"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 later charged}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.homeaffairs.gov.au/research-and-stats/files/immigration-detention-community-statistics-30-nov-2025.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 of the population}}}\uc2  in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s onshore immigration detention network are now the subject of character-based visa cancellations. As at 30 June 2025 there was a rate of 59 critical incidents per 1,000 detainees, according to the Home Affairs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.homeaffairs.gov.au/reports-and-pubs/Annualreports/home-affairs-annual-report-2024-25.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2024\u8211\'20\'1325 annual report}}}\uc2 , compared with 27.84 critical incidents per 1,000 detainees in the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Sexual assault critical incidents increased from 19 incidents in 2023\u8211\'20\'1324 to 30 incidents during 2024\u8211\'20\'1325.\par \par \uc2 Moase said those numbers reflect the industrial and safety issues \u8220\'20\'1Cthat members are raising to us on a near constant basis\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Those detained in the centres have also noticed the low staffing numbers.\par \par \uc2 Michael*, who is detained at Yongah Hill, said there is now only one officer for each compound where there used to be two to three, which he feels is unsafe for everyone. The number of activity officers, who might guide events like coffee clubs for detainees, has also declined.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had twice as many activities under \uc2 Serco\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CPretty useless activities, but at least it\u8217\'20\'19s something that breaks up your days, breaks up the monotony.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several sources in immigration told Guardian Australia that staff from Parklea, a private prison run by MTC for Corrective Services NSW, have been seconded to some of its immigration detention centres.\par \par \uc2 Michael said he had also noticed a high rate of staff turnover and workers brought over from MTC\u8217\'20\'19s private corrections facility. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople that have not been fully trained up that this is not a prison,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Staff now often work across multiple roles, according to several sources inside the centres. Incident Response Team (IRT) members, for example, who are meant to respond to critical incidents in the centres like fights, may be used to take detainees to appointments. The union claims response times to incidents are significantly delayed as a result. The company is now also using labour hire drivers for detainee transport.\par \par \uc2 Greens senator David Shoebridge accused global prison operators like MTC of aiming to maximise profits, causing harm to people in immigration detention and workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPutting vulnerable people into mandatory detention always creates a risk of harm, when you add privatisation into the mix it guarantees it,\u8221\'20\'1D he claimed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMTC won\u8217\'20\'19t safely staff these centres and this means the contracts should be terminated and the centres should be closed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 John said there have been \u8220\'20\'1Cserious safety and security breaches across the network\u8221\'20\'1D since MTC took over. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at what happened in the last 10 months, we had multiple escapes. We had repeated assault on staff because we are outnumbered,\u8221\'20\'1D John said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt not only compromises the safety of staff, it compromises the safety of detainees that we look after every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 MTC and Secure Journeys declined to answer questions.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260206em26003pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
