\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US job openings dropped to a five-year low in December 2025, report shows\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2026\par 17:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Data from November 2025 was also revised lower amid a softening in labor market conditions at the end of the year\par \par \uc2 US job openings dropped to the lowest level in more than five years in December and data for the prior month was revised lower amid a softening in labor market conditions at the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 Job openings, a measure of labor demand, decreased by 386,000 to 6.542m by the last day of December, the lowest level since September 2020, the labor department\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  said in its Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey, or Jolts report, on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Data for November was revised down to show 6.928m job openings instead of the previously reported 7.146m. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast 7.20m unfilled jobs. Hiring increased by 172,000 positions to a still-low 5.293m in December.\par \par \uc2 The data came as other numbers showed a larger-than-expected increase in Americans filing new applications for unemployment benefits last week, but the underlying trend remained consistent with a stable labor market.\par \par \uc2 The biggest rise in weekly jobless claims in nearly two months reported by the labor department on Thursday probably reflected distortions from snowstorms across much of the country as well as normalization after volatility linked to difficulties adjusting the data for seasonal fluctuations around the holiday season and at the turn of the year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no sign of the kind of layoffs we expect to see in a weakening labor market during the early days of a recession,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Carl Weinberg\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe level of claims is just very low. Claims are well within the recent range over the last two years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Initial claims for state unemployment benefits jumped 22,000, the largest increase since early December, to a seasonally adjusted 231,000 for the week ended 31 January, the labor department said on Thursday. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast 212,000 claims for the latest week.\par \par \uc2 Heavy snow and freezing temperatures blanketed large portions of the country towards the end of January, which could have left some people unemployed temporarily. Claims shot up in Pennsylvania, New York, New Jersey, Illinois, Missouri, Ohio and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Claims are also probably rising as the volatility in late 2025 and early this year washes out of the data.\par \par \uc2 Through the distortions, the labor market remains in what economists call a \u8220\'20\'1Clow hire, low fire\u8221\'20\'1D mode.\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s job report, which would give the most accurate snapshot of the jobs market, has been delayed by the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s latest shutdown.Reuters contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260205em25004pk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
