\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Mass layoffs fuel fears of \u8216\'20\'18death spiral\u8217\'20\'19 at \uc2 Washington Post\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeremy Barr in Washington \par \uc2 1,087 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2026\par 15:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, storied newspaper axed nearly one-third of company after earlier unpopular moves by owner \uc2 Jeff Bezos\par \par \uc2 Under Marty Baron, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/washington-post"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2  won 11 Pulitzer prizes and expanded its newsroom to house more than 1,000 journalists. The storied newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s future is now in question, according to its former executive editor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe aspirations of this news organization are diminished,\u8221\'20\'1D Baron told the Guardian in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19ll translate into fewer subscribers. And I hope it\u8217\'20\'19s not a death spiral, but I worry that it might be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Matt Murray, who now leads the Post as editor in chief, promised staffers on Wednesday that the news organization had a plan to survive and thrive into the future \u8211\'20\'13 as it executed one of the largest layoffs in American newspaper history. Nearly one-third of the entire company \u8211\'20\'13 which stood at 2,500 employees in late 2023, before a round of buyouts \u8211\'20\'13 was axed.\par \par \uc2 The cuts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/feb/04/washington-post-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decimated large swaths of the newspaper}}}\uc2 , shuttering its sports department and shredding its teams covering local news, style and the world, not to mention its audio and video departments, which had already been battered by previous cuts. Commercial teams were also cut.\par \par \uc2 All told, the Post is now a significantly smaller news organization, and one that many journalists who care deeply about the institution worry will be significantly less ambitious, just as the media industry confronts both financial headwinds and animosity from Donald Trump and his officials.\par \par \uc2 Trump regularly threatens news networks and encourages his regulatory arms to crack down on outlets he dislikes. During an incredibly newsworthy period in American history, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/oct/29/cbs-news-layoffs-paramount"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 news networks like CBS News}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 now owned by the Trump-friendly Ellison family \u8211\'20\'13 and even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/business/2026/02/the-ajc-to-cut-staff-as-it-continues-digital-transformation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newspapers like the Atlanta Journal-Constitution}}}\uc2  have endured rounds of layoffs in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Baron, who led the Post for eight years until 2021, wrote favorably about the tech billionaire \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stewardship of the paper in his 2023 memoir. But now, Baron said, \u8220\'20\'1Che\u8217\'20\'19s not the owner he was\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He attributes this change to the re-election of Trump in November 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 and a desire for Bezos to stay on his good side to protect the other companies he controls: \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , the retail giant, and the spaceflight startup \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bezos, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/feb/03/washington-post-layoffs-jeff-bezos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who remained silent in recent weeks}}}\uc2  as Post employees pleaded with him to avoid cuts, has not responded to the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the most important thing that\u8217\'20\'19s changed is that Donald Trump is back in the White House, and he clearly would seek vengeance against his political enemies,\u8221\'20\'1D Baron said on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CI understand why Bezos might fear the consequences of that. I understand that he would fear that Trump would deny contracts to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and deny contracts to \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think that the Post is important, too, and it\u8217\'20\'19s even more important than those other enterprises to American democracy. I firmly believe that Bezos is prioritizing his other business efforts over the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Much of the blame \u8211\'20\'13 from employees past and present \u8211\'20\'13 has centered on the Post\u8217\'20\'19s publisher \uc2 Will Lewis\uc2 , who was hired by Bezos in late 2023 to help reverse the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s fortunes. Lewis did not appear during a \uc2 Zoom\uc2  conversation with employees on Wednesday morning, which struck some employees as odd.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think he was awol today, and I think he\u8217\'20\'19s been awol in the past,\u8221\'20\'1D Baron said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s kind of the invisible publisher. When you announce something as traumatic as what they announced today, shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t the publisher be on the call?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Donald E Graham\uc2 , who sold the Post to Bezos in 2013, on Wednesday broke his general policy of not commenting on the current ownership. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a bad day,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/share/p/1EKgiJ8BNA/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a widely shared post on Facebook}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI am sad that so many excellent reporters and editors \u8288\'20\'60\u8211\'20\'13\u8288\'20\'60 and old friends \u8288\'20\'60\u8211\'20\'13\u8288\'20\'60 are losing their jobs. My first concern is for them; I will do anything I can to help.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During a virtual meeting with employees on Wednesday morning, Murray said the Post\u8217\'20\'19s largest reporting group would be its politics and government team, whose work \u8220\'20\'1Cwill remain central to our engagement and subscriber growth\u8221\'20\'1D. The Post will also cover national news, science, technology, climate and business, though with diminished staffs.\par \par \uc2 But, as a smaller news organization focused primarily on covering politics, campaigns and the operation of the federal government and Trump administration, the Post faces stiff competition from outlets like Politico and \uc2 Axios\uc2  that have been laser-focused on those areas, not to mention smaller upstarts like Punchbowl News, which covers Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have no clue what their strategy was or is,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Jim VandeHei\uc2 , who left the Post in 2006 to co-found Politico, and later co-founded \uc2 Axios\uc2 , where he remains CEO. \u8220\'20\'1CWe do: be useful, indispensable [and] illuminating to people who care deeply about government, AI, business and the information ecosystem. And then match the business expertise with our editorial excellence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the fall of 2024, the Post lost hundreds of thousands of subscribers after Bezos {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/oct/25/washington-post-will-not-endorse-presidential-candidate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abruptly shelved its planned endorsement of Kamala Harris}}}\uc2  for president. He also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/feb/26/jeff-bezos-washington-post-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reoriented the paper\u8217\'20\'19s opinion pages}}}\uc2  to a more narrow focus on supporting \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal liberties and free markets\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There are concerns that Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s cuts will create a new groundswell of cancellations that could further damage the Post\u8217\'20\'19s financial health, not to mention the millions of dollars that could be paid out in severance.\par \par \uc2 Robert Allbritton, the former owner of Politico, said he was saddened by Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s layoffs. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are some wonderful journalists who have produced quality work,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey will land at other publications, but the disruption is painful and not their fault.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, \u8220\'20\'1Cthese layoffs signal that Jeff wants to move the Post back to sustainability\u8221\'20\'1D, Allbritton added. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m hopeful that once the Post regains its financial footing that Jeff will continue as a responsible owner of one of the most important brands in media.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some Post veterans feel differently, and have called for Bezos to sell the newspaper. The union representing most Post employees said on Wednesday: \u8220\'20\'1CIf Jeff Bezos is no longer willing to invest in the mission that has defined this paper for generations, and serve the millions who depend on Post journalism, then the Post deserves a steward that will.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Baron highlighted a big question surrounding any potential sale: \u8220\'20\'1CTo whom?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260205em250020a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
