\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s an absolute bloodbath\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  lays off hundreds of workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeremy Barr in Washington \par \uc2 1,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2026\par 17:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former Post executive editor blasts owner \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18sickening efforts to curry favor\u8217\'20\'19 with Trump\par \par \uc2 TheWashington Post laid off hundreds of employees on Wednesday, which its former executive editor said \u8220\'20\'1Cranks among the darkest days\u8221\'20\'1D in the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s history. Approximately one-third of employees were affected.\par \par \uc2 Staffers at the Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/jan/26/washington-post-february-job-cuts-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been on edge for weeks}}}\uc2  about the rumored cuts, which the publication would not confirm or deny. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s an absolute bloodbath,\u8221\'20\'1D said one employee, not authorized to speak publicly.\par \par \uc2 During a morning meeting announcing the changes, the editor in chief, Matt Murray, told employees that the Post was undergoing a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrategic reset\u8221\'20\'1D to better position the publication for the future, according to several employees who were on the call.\par \par \uc2 Murray acknowledged that the Post had struggled to reach \u8220\'20\'1Ccustomers\u8221\'20\'1D and talked about the need to compete in a crowded media marketplace. \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is taking a number of actions across the company to secure our future,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, according to an audio recording of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Murray told employees that the Post was ending the current iteration of its popular sports desk, though some employees would remain on a new team. The Post is also restructuring its local coverage, reducing its international reporting operation, cutting its books desk and suspending its flagship daily news podcast Post Reports.\par \par \uc2 Murray said that while the Post\u8217\'20\'19s international coverage team will be scaled back, approximately 12 bureaus will remain \u8220\'20\'1Cwith a focus on national security issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe all recognize the actions we are taking today will be painful \u8211\'20\'13 most of all, of course, for those of you who are directly affected, but for everybody,\u8221\'20\'1D Murray told staffers on the call. \u8220\'20\'1CI know that the reset is going to feel like a shock to the system and raise some questions for everybody.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Martin Baron, the Post\u8217\'20\'19s executive editor until 2021, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis ranks among the darkest days in the history of one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s greatest news organizations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Seeking to lay out the business case for the layoffs, Murray said the move was \u8220\'20\'1Cabout positioning ourselves to become more essential to people\u8217\'20\'19s lives in what is becoming a more crowded and competitive and complicated media landscape\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that these last couple of weeks have been an unusually tense and distracting time for all of us, and I know it will be a difficult day,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CI appreciate the excellence of your work during this time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Murray said the Post\u8217\'20\'19s largest team would be focused on covering politics and government, and the paper would also prioritize coverage of nationals news and features topics such as science, health, medicine, technology, climate and business.\par \par \uc2 Post employees who have been laid off will continue to be on staff through 10 April, though they will not be required to work. They will receive six months of continued health insurance coverage.\par \par \uc2 The affected employees include Caroline O\u8217\'20\'19Donovan, who primarily covers \uc2 Amazon\uc2  , the company founded by the Post owner, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  . Other staffers, including the sports journalist Neil Greenberg, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/ngreenberg/status/2019048595320881275"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have also announced}}}\uc2  that they were affected.\par \par \uc2 One editor who was laid off on Wednesday laid much of the blame at the feet of the Post\u8217\'20\'19s publisher, \uc2 Will Lewis\uc2  , who did not speak on the company\u8217\'20\'19s morning conference call.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Will Lewis\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s legacy (already pretty bleak to begin with) will be having enabled Bezos to tank an American institution,\u8221\'20\'1D the staffer said, requesting anonymity to speak candidly. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t even brave enough to face his staffers more than once in his tenure at the Post. Embarrassing to say the least.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After years of growth under owner Bezos, the Post has been shedding staff over the last few years. About 240 staffers left via buyouts offered at the end of 2023, and another chunk of staffers took buyouts last year, which were offered to any employee with more than 10 years of experience.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs, particularly of journalists in the newsroom, have been less common. In fall 2024, the Post laid off 54 employees from the division responsible for its proprietary publishing software, and in January 2025, the Post laid off about 4% of staffers who worked in advertising, marketing and print operations.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, Post employees had been urging Bezos to stop \u8211\'20\'13 or at least soften \u8211\'20\'13 the planned cuts, signing letters and sending personalized messages on social media that conveyed the importance of the journalism the Post produces.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/feb/03/washington-post-layoffs-jeff-bezos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 But Bezos has remained silent}}}\uc2 , and did not respond to a series of letters sent by staffers representing the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s foreign, local and White House reporting teams.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, though, he was there in person to warmly greet \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  , the US defense secretary, on a tour of another one of the companies he owns, his \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  spaceflight startup in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Baron, the former executive editor, warned: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Washington Post \u8217\'20\'19s ambitions will be sharply diminished, its talented and brave staff will be further depleted, and the public will be denied the ground-level, fact-based reporting in our communities and around the world that is needed more than ever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Baron called out Bezos for an ill-timed decision to pre-empt the Post\u8217\'20\'19s planned endorsement of Kamala Harris for president in fall 2024, as well as a decision to narrowly focus its opinion page to prioritize writing \u8220\'20\'1Cin support and defense of two pillars: personal liberties and free markets\u8221\'20\'1D, decisions that led to the resignation of a top editor and quickly cost the Post hundreds of thousands of subscribers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBezos\u8217\'20\'19s sickening efforts to curry favor with President Trump have left an especially ugly stain of their own,\u8221\'20\'1D Baron said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a case study in near-instant, self-inflicted brand destruction.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union representing most Post employees said the cuts made on Wednesday were not \u8220\'20\'1Cinevitable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA newsroom cannot be hollowed out without consequences for its credibility, its reach and its future,\u8221\'20\'1D the union said. \u8220\'20\'1CContinuing to eliminate workers only stands to weaken the newspaper, drive away readers and undercut The Post\u8217\'20\'19s mission: to hold power to account without fear or favor and provide critical information for communities across the region, country and world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union suggested that Bezos might not be the right owner for the Post. \u8220\'20\'1CIf Jeff Bezos is no longer willing to invest in the mission that has defined this paper for generations and serve the millions who depend on Post journalism, then the Post deserves a steward who that will.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union has organized a protest of the cuts to be held in front of the Post\u8217\'20\'19s Washington DC headquarters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260204em24003s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
