\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California faces teacher strikes across state for better pay and work conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano \par \uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2026\par 20:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Action could begin next week in some of state\u8217\'20\'19s largest districts including San Diego, San Francisco and LA\par \par \uc2 California is facing the prospect of massive teacher strikes across the state as conflicts over working conditions, pay and special education staffing reach a boiling point.\par \par \uc2 The strikes, which could begin as soon as next week, have been approved by thousands of educators \u8211\'20\'13 affecting schools in some of the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest districts including San Francisco, San Diego and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/us/sf-teachers-overwhelmingly-authorize-their-first-strike-in-a-half-century/ar-AA1VoWDt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco,}}}\uc2  97.6% of teachers voted in favor of a strike, setting the stage for the first such action in the city in nearly 50 years. Teachers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aol.com/articles/lausd-teachers-union-members-authorize-130000360.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles}}}\uc2 , where the LA unified school district is considering layoffs to address its budget woes, authorized their union to move ahead with a strike. Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/education/2026/01/san-diego-teachers-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Diego}}}\uc2  educators are preparing for their first walkout in 30 years, while five unions around {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sacbee.com/news/local/education/article314193206.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sacramento}}}\uc2  have said they are ready to strike if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The development comes just one year after the \uc2 California Teachers Association\uc2 , which represents 310,000 of the state\u8217\'20\'19s educators, kicked off the We Can\u8217\'20\'19t Wait campaign uniting 32 districts across the state for demanding better wages, smaller class sizes and more student resources.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable that in the \u8216\'20\'18Golden State,\u8217\'20\'19 with its vast wealth and resources, our communities struggle with fully staffing our neighborhood schools,\u8221\'20\'1D David Goldberg, the \uc2 California Teachers Association\uc2  president, said in a statement announcing the effort last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is why educators are coordinating to take a stand statewide. Together with parents, students and our communities, we demand that districts prioritize resources for our students and ensure California leads the country in providing a quality public education for every student.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year\u8217\'20\'19s announcement cited a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cta.org/document/the-state-of-ca-public-schools"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  with troubling stats about education in the state, including that 84% of teachers surveyed said they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to live near their workplaces, 81% said their salaries weren\u8217\'20\'19t keeping up with the cost of living and four out of 10 educators were considering leaving the profession.\par \par \uc2 Every school district on the verge of striking in Sacramento, San Francisco, Los Angeles and San Diego is a part of the We Can\u8217\'20\'19t Wait collective bargaining campaign.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco educators could start striking as soon as next week after more than 5,200 members of the United Educators of San Francisco voted to authorize union leadership to call for a strike. The union and the school district have been bargaining for nearly a year and both parties await the results of a fact-finding report this week before UESF can move ahead with a walkout, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/us/sf-teachers-overwhelmingly-authorize-their-first-strike-in-a-half-century/ar-AA1VoWDt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco Chronicle}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat comes next is ultimately up to the district and their actions, but San Francisco educators are making it clear that we are more than willing to do everything we can for our community,\u8221\'20\'1D the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/photo/?fbid=1483802937080390&set=a.525227492937944"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UESF}}}\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Educators in San Diego held a rally last week in what the union dubbed as \u8220\'20\'1Cour last attempt to get the district to stop understaffing special education\u8221\'20\'1D ahead of the strike set to begin on 26 February. The San Diego Education Association has said the district has for years violated the special education staffing requirements laid out in their contract, and about 90% of members voted to strike.\par \par \uc2 The San Diego unified school district has announced schools will be closed the day of the strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will have an opportunity to put the eyes of the whole city on our fight to stop the special education staffing crisis in our district,\u8221\'20\'1D the San Diego Education Association said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260202em22005h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
