\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US jobs report delayed again amid government shutdown\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 331 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2026\par 19:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 January 2026 report to be rescheduled after \uc2 BLS\uc2  has already been faced with major delays from last year\u8217\'20\'19s shutdown\par \par \uc2 The US\u8217\'20\'19s closely watched jobs report will once again be delayed, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ) announced on Monday, amid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The January 2026 jobs report, originally scheduled to be released on Friday, will be rescheduled when federal funding resumes. Data collection for the report has been completed, but the shutdown has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/federal-shutdown-delays-fridays-jobs-report-5397e815?mod=hp_lead_pos4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forced}}}\uc2  a delay to releasing the report, which will provide crucial jobs data on the US labor market following the weakest year for job growth since 2020, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2026/01/09/nx-s1-5670392/jobs-employment-labor-market-economy-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 addition}}}\uc2  of only 584,000 jobs in 2025 compared with 2 million in 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Employment Situation release for January 2026 will not be released as scheduled on Friday, February 6, 2026. The release will be rescheduled upon the resumption of government funding,\u8221\'20\'1D Emily Liddel, associate commissioner of the \uc2 BLS\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  has already been faced with significant delays and setbacks resulting from the longest federal government shutdown in US history, 43 days in October and November.\par \par \uc2 Federal funding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/31/us-government-shutdown-dhs-funding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lapsed on Sunday}}}\uc2  following a standoff in Congress over restrictions on \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  following the killings of two 37-year-old US citizens by federal agents last month. Democratic senators are refusing to vote for a bill authorizing continued spending by the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  (DHS), demanding the bill be rewritten to include new restrictions and guardrails on ICE agents.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Senate passed five separate measures to fund government agencies through September and a two-week funding bill for DHS, which must be voted on in the House.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats have so far not guaranteed the votes to pass the funding measure.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  House speaker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/mike-johnson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mike Johnson}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/01/government-shutdown-democrats-dhs-funding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  that House Republicans had enough votes on their own to reopen the government by Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260202em22005bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
