\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  tells workers it will cut 16,000 jobs worldwide in second big wave of layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Partridge \par \uc2 581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2026\par 18:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 31\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers informed after message erroneously said affected employees in US, Canada and Costa Rica had already been told\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  has told workers it is cutting 16,000 jobs around the world to streamline its operations, hours after sending out a message to staff about the layoffs apparently in error.\par \par \uc2 It is the second big wave of job cuts at the US online retail company, and comes just three months after the company said it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/oct/27/amazon-corporate-jobs-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slashing 14,000 roles}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employs about 1.5 million workers worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Most job cuts are expected to be in the US, although some roles in the UK may also be affected.\par \par \uc2 The company said the \u8220\'20\'1Cadditional organisational changes\u8221\'20\'1D were designed to \u8220\'20\'1Cstrengthen our organisation by reducing layers, increasing ownership, and removing bureaucracy\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-layoffs-corporate-jan-2026"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blogpost to staff}}}\uc2  from \uc2 Beth Galetti\uc2 , the senior vice-president of people experience and technology.\par \par \uc2 Galetti said some teams had not been affected by the job cuts announced in October, but this was not the beginning of regular waves of layoffs. \u8220\'20\'1CSome of you might ask if this is the beginning of a new rhythm \u8211\'20\'13 where we announce broad reductions every few months. That\u8217\'20\'19s not our plan,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote.\par \par \uc2 US-based employees who are affected will be offered 90 days to find a new role internally, with the timing for applying for new roles varying in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Galetti said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  would continue to hire new staff and invest in \u8220\'20\'1Cstrategic areas and functions that are critical to our future\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The official announcement came just hours after workers at \uc2 Amazon Web Services\uc2  (\uc2 AWS\uc2 ) received an online meeting invitation from a top executive on Tuesday for the following day \u8211\'20\'13 subsequently cancelled \u8211\'20\'13 that also contained a draft email revealing the cuts.\par \par \uc2 The message erroneously said that affected employees in the US, Canada and Costa Rica had already been told they had lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 It was signed by Colleen Aubrey, a senior vice-president of applied AI solutions at \uc2 AWS\uc2 , while the layoffs were referred to in the email as \u8220\'20\'1CProject Dawn\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CChanges like this are hard on everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D Aubrey wrote in the email, which was seen by multiple news outlets including Reuters and Bloomberg. \u8220\'20\'1CThese decisions are difficult and are made thoughtfully as we position our organisation and \uc2 AWS\uc2  for future success.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The email referred to a separate message from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s human resources boss, which did not appear to have been sent.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has been trying to reverse a pandemic hiring spree in an effort to cut costs and slim down its vast operation.\par \par \uc2 Its chief executive, \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , has previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/18/amazon-boss-tells-staff-ai-means-their-jobs-are-at-risk-in-coming-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned white-collar workers}}}\uc2  at the company that their jobs could be taken by AI in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 The news of fresh \uc2 Amazon\uc2  layoffs came as the US delivery company \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  (UPS) said it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/jan/27/ups-job-cuts-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut up to 30,000 jobs this year}}}\uc2 , adding to last year\u8217\'20\'19s staff reductions, as it focuses on higher-margin shipments.\par \par \uc2 UPS has been working to cut millions of low-value deliveries it carries out for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which is its largest customer but increasingly also a delivery rival. UPS has called its business with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinarily dilutive\u8221\'20\'1D to margins.\par \par \uc2 It is understood that a few hundred of the job cuts are expected to be in UK corporate offices in London and Manchester, with warehouses unaffected.Additional reporting by Sarah Butler\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260128em1s002gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
FF
