\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New York nurses continue to strike in frigid weather as Mamdani shows support\b0\par\par\uc2 Adam Gabbatt \par \uc2 877 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2026\par 16:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New York mayor was joined by Senator Bernie Sanders at a Tuesday rally with nurses as strike entered second week\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York City}}}\uc2  may be experiencing some of its coldest weather of the winter, with sub-zero temperatures biting fingers and nipping cheeks, but that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t prevented thousands of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/nursing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nurses}}}\uc2  from taking to the picket line for what is the largest nurses strike in the city\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 Almost 15,000 nurses who work for three separate hospital systems have been on strike since 12 January, holding out for increased staffing, better safety in hospitals, and improved healthcare benefits. The \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2  (\uc2 NYSNA\uc2 ) has pointed to the giant pay packages that hospital CEOs have received, at a time when nurses say there are too few of them to adequately care for patients.\par \par \uc2 The strike has pulled support from Zohran Mamdani, the New York City mayor, and Bernie Sanders, the democratic socialist Vermont senator, both of whom joined striking nurses outside a Mount Sinai hospital on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we see a strike, people forget that that is not where workers want to be. The strike is an act of last resort. What workers want is to be back at work. This is about safe working conditions. This is about a fair contract. This is about dignity,\u8221\'20\'1D Mamdani {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=57ZoAmurMe0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat this is in fact about is recognizing the worth of each and every nurse in this city.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Picket lines have been established outside Mount Sinai, Montefiore and NewYork-Presbyterian hospitals, representatives of whom were scheduled to return to negotiations with the \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  on Thursday at the urging of Mamdani and Kathy Hochul, the New York governor. The nurses have endured frigid temperatures dipping towards 19F (-7C) as Mamdani and Sanders spoke on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people of this country are sick and tired of the greed of the healthcare industry,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re tired of the drug companies ripping us off, the insurance companies ripping us off and hospital executives getting huge salaries. Don\u8217\'20\'19t tell me you can\u8217\'20\'19t provide a good nurse-staff [patient] ratio when you\u8217\'20\'19re paying your CEO at NewYork-Presbyterian $26m a year, the CEO at Montefiore $16m a year. Mount Sinai $5m a year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cecilia Barreto, who has been a nurse at \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  for two years, was watching from under a large coat as the pair spoke.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a pretty awe-inspiring moment,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBernie Sanders is a big union champion. So to have learned about his life\u8217\'20\'19s work in school, and then to see him standing 15ft away from me, was an amazing experience. And to also see somebody like Mayor Mamdani come and support us since day one of the strike has been really, really inspiring, because it just lets us know that we have people in government who are working to help us get a fair contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hospitals have spent millions of dollars on hiring thousands of temporary nurses to cover the strike, something Barreto, a 28-year-old from Queens, New York, said was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery disappointing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s incredibly disheartening to have the hospitals be stalling on this because at the end of the day, striking is a last resort, and we want to be at the bedside. We want to be taking care of the patients,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  has said the union is making \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme economic demands\u8221\'20\'1D, while a spokesperson for Montefiore said the union was making \u8220\'20\'1Creckless demands\u8221\'20\'1D. The hospitals said union-registered nurses make an average of $165,000 a year at Montefiore, $162,000 at Mount Sinai, and $163,000 at NewYork-Presbyterian\u8217\'20\'19s Columbia University Irving medical center. Montefiore claimed the union\u8217\'20\'19s asks would raise the average to $220,000 in three years; Mount Sinai said the average there would reach $275,000.\par \par \uc2 Nurses say they have unmanageable workloads, and are asking for better security in the hospitals.\par \par \uc2 They also want better security measures in the workplace. The \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  has pointed to an incident in November when a man allegedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/upper-east-side-nypd-shooting/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened}}}\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cshoot up\u8221\'20\'1D a Mount Sinai hospital before being killed by police. In another incident in early January, a man allegedly threatened staff with a sharp object before barricading himself {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/nyc-police-shooting-hospital-brooklyn-dc316db04f860e772d2c974d02f94c28"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a Brooklyn hospital room}}}\uc2 . He was shot and killed by police.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m one of countless nurses who are struggling, exhausted and increasingly afraid to come to work \u8211\'20\'13 the main reason is the safety in our hospitals. It\u8217\'20\'19s not an exaggeration, it\u8217\'20\'19s a daily reality. We\u8217\'20\'19re on strike because of the lack of safety. We have staffing shortages, and we have increased unprecedented levels of workplace violence,\u8221\'20\'1D said Aretha Morgan, a nurse at NewYork-Presbyterian who has been practising for 34 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hospitals are attempting to tarnish our reputation as a respected profession, they are trying to portray us as being unreasonable and demanding, when in reality, we are just fighting for safe staffing, safe environments and the basic resources required to protect our patients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Morgan added: \u8220\'20\'1CThey need to come to the table in good faith, and let\u8217\'20\'19s talk about it, and let us get this done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260123em1n00361\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
