\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New York City expects biggest nurses strike as nearly 15,000 set to walk off job\b0\par\par\uc2 Marina Dunbar and agencies \par \uc2 862 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2026\par 16:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Strike, amid an intense flu season, is expected to disrupt activity at institutions such as Mount Sinai and Montefiore\par \par \uc2 Thousands of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/nursing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nurses}}}\uc2  are set to walk off the job at several of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York City}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s largest hospitals on Monday, staging a strike amid an intense flu season.\par \par \uc2 The action comes three years after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/12/nurses-strike-victory-new-york-city-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous strike}}}\uc2  that compelled some of the same hospitals to move patients elsewhere and reroute ambulances.\par \par \uc2 Hospital operations are expected to be disrupted at a number of major private institutions, including Mount Sinai in Manhattan, Montefiore medical center in the Bronx, and NewYork-Presbyterian/\uc2 Columbia University\uc2  Irving medical center.\par \par \uc2 Close to 15,000 nurses are participating, making it the biggest nurses strike the city has ever seen. Most union members voted last month to authorize the walkout.\par \par \uc2 Anticipating the possibility of a strike, New York\u8217\'20\'19s governor, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kathy-hochul"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kathy Hochul}}}\uc2 , declared a state of emergency on Friday and urged hospital administrators and union leaders to reach a last-minute agreement. She warned that a strike \u8220\'20\'1Ccould jeopardize the lives of thousands of New Yorkers and patients\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m strongly encouraging everyone to stay at the table, both sides, management and the nurses, until this is resolved,\u8221\'20\'1D Hochul said.\par \par \uc2 As in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/09/nurse-strike-mount-sinai-montefiore-new-york-city"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2023 labor dispute}}}\uc2 , the current conflict centers on a complex mix of grievances, rebuttals and hospital-specific concerns. Staffing remains a key source of contention, with nurses arguing that well-funded hospitals are unwilling to commit to standards that ensure safe and manageable workloads.\par \par \uc2 In this round of negotiations, the union is also pressing for limits on hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 use of artificial intelligence, along with enhanced workplace safety protections. In November, a gunman entered \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 , and earlier this week a man armed with a sharp object barricaded himself inside a Brooklyn hospital room; police ultimately killed both individuals.\par \par \uc2 The private, non-profit hospitals involved say they have improved staffing since 2023. Some hospital representatives argue that the union\u8217\'20\'19s full slate of demands would be prohibitively costly.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, dozens of nurses gathered in Manhattan to protest, saying their focus is on patient care and accusing hospital systems, whose top executives earn millions annually, of prioritizing profit and refusing to compromise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy hospital tries to cut corners on staffing every day, and then they try to fight historic gains we made three years ago,\u8221\'20\'1D Sophie Boland, a pediatric intensive care nurse in the NewYork-Presbyterian hospital system, told the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 Hospital officials have described the union\u8217\'20\'19s strike threat as \u8220\'20\'1Creckless\u8221\'20\'1D. In a statement released on Thursday, they said they would \u8220\'20\'1Cdo whatever is necessary to minimize disruptions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Hagans\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2  union, has also stressed that patients should not delay care during a potential strike.\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  has hired more than 1,000 temporary nurses and held preparatory drills for a strike that could affect its 1,100-bed main hospital and two affiliates \u8211\'20\'13 Mount Sinai Morningside and Mount Sinai West \u8211\'20\'13 with about 500 beds each.\par \par \uc2 NewYork-Presbyterian said it also had arranged for temporary nurses but, in the event of a strike, some patients might be moved to new rooms or advised to transfer to another facility. Montefiore posted a message assuring patients that appointments would be kept.\par \par \uc2 The same union mounted a three-day strike at the Mount Sinai flagship facility and Montefiore in 2023, when nurses expressed their sacrifices during the challenging height of the Covid-19 pandemic and the national nurse staffing crisis that followed.\par \par \uc2 The walkout prompted those hospitals to postpone non-emergency surgeries, tell many ambulances to go elsewhere and transfer some intensive-care infants and other patients. Temporary nurses and even administrators with clinical backgrounds were tapped to fill in, but some patients noticed longer waits and understaffed wards.\par \par \uc2 The strike ended with an agreement on raises totaling 19% over three years and staffing improvements, including the possibility of extra pay if nurses had to work short-handed. The previous nurses\u8217\'20\'19 contract expired on 31 December.\par \par \uc2 Now, hospitals are retreating from those guarantees and falling short on other promises, the union says.\par \par \uc2 Montefiore, for example, agreed to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake all reasonable efforts\u8221\'20\'1D to stop keeping some emergency room patients in hallways while they wait for space to open up in other wards. Yet three years later, nurses still scramble to treat \u8220\'20\'1Challway patients\u8221\'20\'1D, Michelle Gonzalez, a Montefiore intensive care nurse, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/nyc-nurses-negotiations-strike-threat-hospitals-f6b8598107039b1d6ceade0155e1ac36"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the Associated Press on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Montefiore has suggested it has made some progress: the hospital told elected officials in a letter in October that there has been a 35% reduction in the time it takes from emergency admission to a clinical unit bed.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the hospitals say they have greatly reduced nursing job vacancy rates in the last three years, and Mount Sinai and NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia Irving University medical center say they also have added hundreds of nursing positions.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, several smaller hospitals \u8211\'20\'13 including multiple Northwell Health facilities on Long Island \u8211\'20\'13 averted potential walkouts by striking deals or making what the union viewed as adequate progress.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260112em1c003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
