\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US hiring held firm in December capping weakest year of growth since pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2026\par 18:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 42\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 White House is meanwhile facing questions about an apparent embargo breach over Trump\u8217\'20\'19s social media post\par \par \uc2 Hiring held firm in the US last month, official data showed, amid uncertainty over the strength and direction of the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 50,000 jobs to the US labor force last month, capping the weakest year of growth since the pandemic, according to data released from the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The closely watched reading was slightly shy of the approximately 73,000 jobs economists expected to be added in the US economy in December.\par \par \uc2 Previous readings for October and November were also revised lower, with the \uc2 BLS\uc2  now estimating that the US added 76,000 fewer jobs during those two months. In October, during the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/12/house-bill-government-shutdown-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longest US government shutdown in history}}}\uc2 , the US economy shed 173,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/16/jobs-report-october-november"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose to a four-year high of 4.6%}}}\uc2  in November, fell back to 4.4% in December.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  was re-elected after promising to rebuilding the US economy, which he claimed had been destroyed under predecessor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He also pledged to bring down prices rapidly for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 The US president claims the economy has since taken off on his watch \u8211\'20\'13 and growth {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/23/us-economy-gdp-third-quarter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surged in the third quarter}}}\uc2  of last year. But the labor market, which economists watch closely to determine the strength of the broader economy, has slowed dramatically.\par \par \uc2 US employers added 584,000 over the course of 2025, the first year of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second term, according to official data. In 2024, the last year of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, they added 2 million.\par \par \uc2 The White House is meanwhile facing questions after Trump posted a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/115862513677602476"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chart}}}\uc2  on his Truth Social platform on Thursday evening \u8211\'20\'13 ahead of the following morning\u8217\'20\'19s official data release \u8211\'20\'13 which appeared to use data from Friday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report to show growth in private sector jobs.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg News was first to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-09/trump-posted-unpublished-jobs-data-early-in-social-media-post"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  the apparent embargo breach.\par \par \uc2 While the president is typically briefed on the jobs data the day before its release, publicly commenting on economic data before it is officially published is an unusual breach in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48161"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protocol}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, the White House confirmed that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was an inadvertent public disclosure of aggregate data that was partially derived from pre-released information\u8221\'20\'1D and that it is \u8220\'20\'1Caccordingly reviewing protocols regarding economic data releases\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren, a Democratic senator from Massachusetts, said: \u8220\'20\'1CJob growth in 2025 was the weakest in over a decade, outside of the pandemic. Instead of lowering costs like he promised, the second year of the Trump presidency is kicking off with a weaker job market and higher prices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Economists describe the labor market as being in a \u8220\'20\'1Cno hire, no fire\u8221\'20\'1D phase, in which job growth continues but remains subdued. Data from the outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/2025-year-end-challenger-report-highest-q4-layoffs-since-2008-lowest-ytd-hiring-since-2010/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 showed}}}\uc2  that layoffs in December were nearly half the level of those recorded in November.\par \par \uc2 After the government shutdown halted economic data collection in October and the beginning of November, December was the first month that the statistics bureau could fully collect jobs data.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials are expected to weigh this data at their next policy meeting at the end of January, when they will decide whether to lower interest rates, which sit at a range of 3.5% to 3.75%, or keep them on hold.\par \par \uc2 Officials have signaled that a pause in cuts is likely. Minutes from the board\u8217\'20\'19s December meeting revealed stark division when members made their third consecutive cut to rates last month. \u8220\'20\'1CSome participants suggested that, under their economic outlooks, it would likely be appropriate to keep [rates] unchanged for some time,\u8221\'20\'1D the notes said.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , the chief US economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said on Friday: \u8220\'20\'1CDecember\u8217\'20\'19s labor market report is weak enough to keep the possibility of a March easing of Fed policy firmly in the mix. The sluggish headline payroll print was underpinned by a mere 37,000 increase in private payrolls.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a press conference last month, the Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , said officials would proceed with caution as they remain hopeful that the labor market will stabilize in the upcoming year and inflation will start to cool. Prices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/18/november-us-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose}}}\uc2  2.7% in November, a cooling-off after rising 3% in September.\par \par \uc2 But the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s vigilance over the economy continues to clash with the outlook of Trump and his economic advisers, who have demanded lower interest rates. Cutting rates could stimulate economic growth, including bolstering the labor market, but at the risk of making prices rise faster.\par \par \uc2 In a speech on Thursday, the treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/08/treasury-secretary-bessent-says-more-fed-rate-cuts-are-only-ingredient-missing-for-stronger-economy.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 urged}}}\uc2  the Fed to continue cutting rates this year. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the only ingredient missing for even stronger economic growth, which is why the Fed should not delay,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260109em19003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
