\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Doge cuts cost US taxpayers $10bn to cover workers\u8217\'20\'19 paid leave, analysis finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Perkins \par \uc2 858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2026\par 17:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Analysis estimates Trump administration \u8216\'20\'18wasted\u8217\'20\'19 billions to compensate over 154,000 federal employees on leave\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwasted\u8221\'20\'1D $10bn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peer.org/letter-to-opm-acting-director-charles-ezell-federal-employee-administrative-leave/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on paid leave}}}\uc2 , or paying workers to stay home, as part of the \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8217\'20\'19s\u8221\'20\'1D assault on the federal workforce, a new analysis by \uc2 Public Employees for Environmental Responsibility\uc2  (Peer) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/12_30_25-letter-to-GAO-and-attachments.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimates}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/12_30_25-letter-to-GAO-and-attachments.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter sent to}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  (GAO), Peer estimates that more than 154,000 employees were put on paid leave in 2025, making up nearly 7% of the federal civilian workforce. That costs taxpayers approximately $10bn in compensation for workers who were staying home and not working.\par \par \uc2 Peer alleges the Trump administration violated the law with extended paid leaves, but enforcement is complicated. It would require navigating a complex administrative or legal process, or a US attorney with the Donald Trump-controlled Department of Justice to pursue criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSpending over $10bn of taxpayer money to prevent people from working is a hell of a way to run a railroad,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peer senior counsel Peter Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIronically, this outrageous waste of human capital \u8211\'20\'13 when key federal agencies such as the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  were horribly understaffed \u8211\'20\'13 was done in the name of government efficiency.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among others on paid leave are \uc2 US Environmental Protection Agency{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2025/02/06/nx-s1-5289331/epa-environmental-justice-office"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 environmental justice employees}}}\uc2 , who are stuck in legal limbo.\par \par \uc2 There are few legal remedies in part because of the labyrinthine regulatory process that involves multiple interchanging parts across various laws and oversight bodies, and because the Trump administration has taken legally questionable steps to hamstring the response and protect its paid leave operation. That has culminated in an \u8220\'20\'1Caccountability black hole\u8221\'20\'1D, said Madeline Materna, a \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  doctoral student in comparative science who studies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govexec.com/workforce/2025/05/agencies-are-violating-law-administrative-leave-and-taxpayers-are-paying-price/405486/?oref=ge-author-river"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the issue}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Until 2016, federal agencies sometimes used administrative leave as a way to punish employees with whom they had a problem, like whistleblowers. Some were put on involuntary leave for years without recourse.\par \par \uc2 Peer lobbied for laws to prevent this strategy, and Congress in 2016 passed the Administrative Leave Act (ALA), which limits imposition of paid leave to no more than 10 workdays in any calendar year, with very limited exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The Trump paid leave strategy seems to be a clear violation of the ALA, but the first Trump administration did not implement the ALA\u8217\'20\'19s specific regulatory rules that agencies have to follow. Opposition from agencies\u8217\'20\'19 management impeded implementation during \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration, which also did not develop the rules until {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peer.org/feds-regulate-administrative-investigative-leave/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Peer sued}}}\uc2  in 2024, said Jeff Ruch, senior counsel at Peer.\par \par \uc2 The regulatory rules did not go into effect until September 2025, which complicated ALA enforcement, even if the extended paid leaves seemed to clearly violate the law.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration carved out separate classifications of paid leave, and claimed the ALA 10-day rule only applies to what it calls \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestigative\u8221\'20\'1D paid leave, Materna said. But the provision does not apply to the various other types of paid leave the administration has created.\par \par \uc2 The classifications \u8220\'20\'1Ctotally gut the law\u8221\'20\'1D, Materna said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey did not lawfully implement Congress\u8217\'20\'19s statue, they did something else.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Anti-Deficiency Act prohibits the government from spending money on employees who should not be on leave, because Congress did not appropriate the funds for purposes related to the paid leave program.\par \par \uc2 The first step to accountability lies in Peer\u8217\'20\'19s complaint with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/legal/appropriations-law/resources"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Government Accountability Office}}}\uc2 . The GAO must opine that the paid leave is a violation of the law, and agency heads who violate the rules can be criminally prosecuted, removed from office, and fined.\par \par \uc2 That, however, would require a \u8220\'20\'1CUS attorney armed with a profile in courage because they would be prosecuting their own administration\u8221\'20\'1D, Ruch said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a nonstarter, but it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Materna said criminal prosecution is also complicated because agency managers must knowingly violate the law, and they could argue that they have been following the rules that were implemented.\par \par \uc2 Peer has filed an administrative complaint before the \uc2 US Office of Special Counsel\uc2 . If that office finds wrongdoing, the cases could be taken before a little-known office called the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 . It has authority to take action, but Ruch said it hears cases involving termination, demotion or suspension without pay for two weeks. It may not have jurisdiction over paid leave cases.\par \par \uc2 Notably, the Trump administration fired all Democrats on the board, which has left it without a quorum.\par \par \uc2 A federal lawsuit over the administration\u8217\'20\'19s legally questionable implementation of the ALA rules is possible, but only if a plaintiff can show that they were harmed by the rules\u8217\'20\'19 implementation, Materna said. A hypothetical plaintiff would have to get a favorable ruling from the Merit Systems Protection Board to have standing for a lawsuit. But the barriers to the Merit Systems Protection Board make that virtually impossible.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s clever dancing around the law, and things that make it challenging to take the issue to court or any regulatory body, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why [the paid leave program] has been allowed to proceed,\u8221\'20\'1D Materna said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260109em19003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
