\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy added fewer jobs than forecast in December, but January interest rate cut very unlikely \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 3,885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2026\par 16:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists say fall in US unemployment rate last month means \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  likely to leave borrowing costs unchanged at this month\u8217\'20\'19s meetingUS hiring held firm in December capping weakest year of growth since the pandemic\par \par \uc2 4.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up!\par \par \uc2 Hiring held firm in the US last month, official data showed, amid uncertainty over the strength and direction of the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 50,000 jobs to the US labor force last month, capping the weakest year of growth since the pandemic, according to data released from the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The closely watched reading was slightly shy of the approximately 73,000 jobs economists expected to be added in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  in December.\par \par \uc2 Previous readings for October and November were also revised lower, with the \uc2 BLS\uc2  now estimating that the US added 76,000 fewer jobs during those two months. In October, during the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/12/house-bill-government-shutdown-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longest US government shutdown in history}}}\uc2 , the US economy shed 173,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/16/jobs-report-october-november"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose to a four-year high of 4.6%}}}\uc2  in November, fell back to 4.4% in December.\par \par \uc2 Economists predicted that a cut to US interest rates this month was very unlikely, given the drop in the unemployment rate.\par \par \uc2 In other news\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  and \uc2 Glencore\uc2  have restarted talks over a merger that would create the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest mining company.\par \par \uc2 The talks come almost a year after previous discussions between the two mining companies collapsed. If a deal is agreed, it would create a global mining business with an enterprise value of more than $260bn (\u163\'00\'A3193bn).\par \par \uc2 Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s has blamed \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant headwinds\u8221\'20\'1D from weak consumer confidence, heavy online competition and widespread discounting for a fall in sales at its \uc2 Argos\uc2  chain over the all-important {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/christmas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christmas}}}\uc2  quarter.\par \par \uc2 The Charity Commission\uc2  has opened a statutory inquiry into City & Guilds\u8217\'20\'19 sale of its qualification awards business to a private company last year.\par \par \uc2 The announcement has been made after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/22/bosses-at-city-and-guilds-handed-million-pound-bonuses-after-training-firm-is-privatised"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian revealed last month how City & Guilds bosses were handed million-pound bonuses}}}\uc2  after the charity privatised its business arm.\par \par \uc2 4.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The London stock market has ended the week at a new closing high.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 share index has closed at 10,124 points, up 0.8%, slightly higher than its previous closing high set on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mining company \uc2 Glencore\uc2  (+9.6%) was the top riser, following the news that it could be taken over by RioTinto (-3%).\par \par \uc2 4.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Oil at one-month high as Iran protests spread\par \par \uc2 Back in the financial markets, oil has hit its highest level in a month.\par \par \uc2 US crude is up almost 3% at $59.43 a barrel, the highest since 8 December.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the international benchmark, is up 2.5% at $63.47 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Both measures also jumped yesterday, as protests spread in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Charalampos\par \par \uc2 Pissouros, senior market analyst at Trading Point, says:\par \par \uc2 4.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  is expected to issue rulings next Wednesday, Reuters reports, so we might get a ruling on the legality of the Trump trade war then\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 3.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The rollout of artificial intelligence services could also be slowing demand in the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Richard Vernazza, chief international economist at \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 3.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 It appears that President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posted some details from today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report last night on social media!\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/115862513677602476"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A post on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s truth Social site}}}\uc2  around 14 hours ago declared that \u8220\'20\'1CFederal government jobs are down 277,000\u8221\'20\'1D since January, a fact that appears in today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report which was released just over 2 hours ago.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  explains:\par \par \uc2 The jobs report is meant to be kept closely under wraps, given its reputation for moving the markets.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a screengrab of the post:\par \par \uc2 3.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Our US Politics Liveblog is covering the Supreme Court non-decision on tariffs:\par \par \uc2 3.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court will not rule on Trump tariffs today\par \par \uc2 Alas, we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to get a ruling over the legality of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trade wars today.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will not issue a ruling on Friday in a major case which could test the legality of the sweeping global tariffs announced by President DonaldTrump last year.\par \par \uc2 Today had been identified as the first day that the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  might give its ruling on whether the international Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) permits the imposition of widespread tariffs\par \par \uc2 US \u8216\'20\'18prediction\u8217\'20\'19 markets suggest there\u8217\'20\'19s roughly only a 25% chance that \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  will back Trump, when the decision comes, and a 75% probability that they will rule against the White House.\par \par \uc2 3.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US consumer confidence rises\par \par \uc2 Consumer confidence has risen across America this month, despite lingering concerns about inflation and the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Index of Consumer Sentiment has risen to 54.0 this month, up from 52.9 in December. That\u8217\'20\'19s its highest reading since last September.\par \par \uc2 Surveys of Consumers director Joanne Hsu explains:\par \par \uc2 More than 90% of interviews for this release were collected prior to the capture of president Maduro in Venezuela, so we can\u8217\'20\'19t yet see what impact that has had\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 2.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Daniel Casali, chief investment strategy at wealth managers Evelyn Partners, points out that the Trump White House\u8217\'20\'19s immigration crackdown has slowed the jobs market this year:\par \par \uc2 2.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The latest market pricing confirms that a cut to US interest rates this month is very unlikely.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CME Group\u8217\'20\'19s Fedwatch tool}}}\uc2  shows there\u8217\'20\'19s a 95% chance that the US Federal Reserve leaves interest rates on hold at its meeting at the end of January, and just 5% chance of a quarter-point cut.\par \par \uc2 2.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened a little higher, as investors digest today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report.\par \par \uc2 The DowJonesindustrialaverage has risen by 170 points, or 0.35%, at the start of trading to 49,436 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 share index is up almost 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 2.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Chris Beauchamp, chief market analyst at IG, also predicts that US interest rates will be left on hold later this month, due to the drop in the jobless rate:\par \par \uc2 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Strategist: January rate cut very unlikely following drop in jobless rate\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s drop in the US unemployment rate means there\u8217\'20\'19s almost no chance of a cut to interest rates this month, argues Seema Shah, chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management, said:\par \par \uc2 2.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Richard Carter, head of fixed interest research at Quilter Cheviot, says there\u8217\'20\'19s little sign of an economic bounceback following the end of the US government shutdown:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Where were jobs created in December?\par \par \uc2 More than half of the 50,000 jobs created in the US last month were at food services and drinking places, where payrolls rose by 27,000.\par \par \uc2 Health care continued to add workers in December; payrolls rose by 21,000, including 16,000 in hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Employment in social assistance rose by 17,000.\par \par \uc2 But it was a tougher jobs market in retail, where 25,000 jobs were cut.\par \par \uc2 Federal government employment rose by 2,000, suggesting that the Doge jobs cull may have abated \u8211\'20\'13 after 277,000 jobs were lost through 2025\par \par \uc2 1.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate dips to 4.4%\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s jobless rate has fallen, to 4.4% in December, down from 4.5% in November (which I think has been revised down from 4.6%)\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s employment data shows that the unemployment total dipped from 7.781m in November to 7.503m in December.\par \par \uc2 The number of people employed rose, month-on-month, but the labor force participation rate dipped from 62.5% to 62.4%, implying more people were neither in work nor looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report also shows that federal government employment has fallen by over 9% since the start of the year, when Elon Musk\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doge.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Doge}}}\uc2 ) began cutting jobs.\par \par \uc2 The BLS says:\par \par \uc2 New analysis released today shows that the Trump administration \u8220\'20\'1Cwasted\u8221\'20\'1D $10bn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peer.org/letter-to-opm-acting-director-charles-ezell-federal-employee-administrative-leave/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on paid leave}}}\uc2 , or paying workers to stay home, as part of Doge\u8217\'20\'19s assault on the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US job creation slowed in Trump's first year\par \par \uc2 We can also see today that much fewer jobs were created in Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first year than the final year of Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration.\par \par \uc2 The Establishment Survey Data released by the Bureau of Labor Statistics today shows that payroll employment rose by 584,000 in 2025, which is an average monthly gain of 49,000.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s rather less than the increase of 2.0 million in 2024 (an average monthly gain of 168,000).\par \par \uc2 1.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US lost 173,000 jobs in October\par \par \uc2 Oof! It now appears that many more jobs were lost in October than first estimated.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows that nonfarm payroll employment in October fell by 173,000, much worse than the estimate of 105,000 job losses.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s partly because many federal employees took deferred buyout offers from the Trump administration and were paid at the end of September, meaning they dropped out of the non-farm payroll in October.\par \par \uc2 So, with 8,000 fewer jobs created in November than previously thought, that means employment in October and November combined is 76,000 lower than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 1.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US added fewer jobs than forecast in December\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added fewer jobs than expected last month.\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll rose by 50,000 in December, missing forecasts of a 60,000 rise.\par \par \uc2 Employment continued to trend up in food services and drinking places, health care, and social assistance, the Bureau of Labor Statistics reports, while retail trade lost jobs.\par \par \uc2 That shows a hiring slowdown, compared with the previous month; the BLS now estimates that 56,000 jobs were created in November, 8,000 fewer than its first estimate of 64,000.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Charity Commission investigating City & Guilds over concerns about asset sales\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, the Charity Commission has opened a statutory inquiry into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://click.agilitypr.delivery/ls/click?upn=u001.2EFiBZ2PU6zxTCJPfKiB1Tb36o0FsUbY2cntEgVUVjzp4OajJ1tfOV9GKJHJtI-2BX1jWGpzfh2ox-2FnmFMZE8hE7FwtFzBCELM5f3hzExKzC0oKCWB-2BxXRtzrUgiwqtrwCSqa0xoHABWT5qfajXm7UEC-2BlbbSfgMFCq4gOHgXqKlVImlRm4zYY3c-2B9vFIRQM0Z-2BvFE5kvIXsj-2BSVGmLkQj9mD6JkQoR3GBjKxx5mOy4hBwgP-2B2QXGlM1IiumTh-2Bt3Ks62afMootKKNipG3QUDYsQ-3D-3D3DPW_z7txIHmiMxMYlag5G1si6v4QJdqEnfoOjCtzGhatR5W0FZxlA0i5bH6DGXNezNf-2FLrMG3u7SDcG7PlDeC-2B-2FhgXa3AXByeihEjOR-2B5BaFUnonaWi6yGes07aUkEsCjzRSKoBdIk-2FjpCcMY20DBE1Ie-2BNrQqOuWuKzzQUN4gugzuutQ-2Fw2PvMkBApZx4f7PculFF6rEj2tB19Z48l68JiOvna77ZvgNppiyBvcNfJTLiYSFzhWOSfBR5Ogc-2FZWrPdL3sv8lb5HvQJQ9RSJOPGVDbWjnrEUB-2FW8LE4bo5ZymoeGvq1hNyKqx-2FTSuyGKWazPyDiRhmPS-2FsIj4M1VH-2FfY8YFeXDGgd-2FvA3e95q4SxK2TNTjMIPOz57YJlxaT5WWKzvAafK9Tiov6bKgLCvlVzxdZ-2Btnjr-2BgkL7L35PFmMtBUoFhESZu-2B76Z67yqTIB9L0H2lhgJrxb9CZBX-2ByOCVUTtNTAeky-2B7zFaH-2BoUxU9cqQ-3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 City and Guilds of London Institute}}}\uc2 , following reports in the Guardian about the sale of its training and awards business.\par \par \uc2 The Commission says the inquiry will examine information provided ahead of the sale, \u8220\'20\'1Cfollowing concerns raised in public reporting relating to the sale and bonuses awarded to its executives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last month, my colleague Simon Goodley reported that two City & Guilds executives have each been awarded million-pound bonuses and sizeable salary increases following the sale of its training and awards operation, City & Guilds (C&G), {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.peoplecert.org/news-and-announcements/peoplecert-announces-the-acquisition-of-City-and-Guilds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to PeopleCert}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Commission will also look into the trustees\u8217\'20\'19 decision-making regarding the sale and entering into a \u8216\'20\'18coexistence agreement\u8217\'20\'19 with the new company.\par \par \uc2 1.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Markets brace for US jobs report\par \par \uc2 The financial markets are limbering up for the final, and most important, major economic news of the week \u8211\'20\'13 the US jobs report for December, due in half an hour\u8217\'20\'19s time.\par \par \uc2 Economists predict that December\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report will show that the US economy created around 60,000 new jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 That would be a slight slowdown on the 64,000 added in November, but rather better than October\u8217\'20\'19s shockingly bad report, which showed 105,000 jobs were lost!\par \par \uc2 The non-farm payroll could shift market expectations around the US\u8217\'20\'19s growth prospects, and the likelihood of interest rate cuts this year.\par \par \uc2 It should also give us a better insight into the health of the economy, after last autumn\u8217\'20\'19s shutdown disrupted the usual flow of economic data.\par \par \uc2 Julien Lafargue, chief market strategist at Barclays, says:\par \par \uc2 12.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the UK property sector, home sales in November last year was 8% higher than the same month in 2024, according to HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC) figures.\par \par \uc2 Around 100,350 homes changed hands in November 2025, which is also 1% more than in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 An estimated 732,310 home sales have taken place in the financial year to date, running from April to November 2025, compared with 746,220 a year earlier, according to the HMRC figures.\par \par \uc2 12.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 EU backs Mercosur trade deal despite farmer protests\par \par \uc2 European nations have backed the biggest ever free trade agreement with a group of South American countries, ending 25 years of negotiations but risking further tensions with farmers around the bloc.\par \par \uc2 France, Poland, Austria, Ireland and Hungary opposed the deal in the face of protests from the agricultural sector, but Italy dropped its opposition allowing the landmark deal to be adopted under the majority voting system.\par \par \uc2 The deal with Brazil, Argentina, Paraquay and Uruquary, must still get the approval of the European Parliament but bar any major U-turns by member states, it could be signed off as early as next week by European Commission president Ursula von der Leyen.\par \par \uc2 The European Commission which concluded negotiations a year ago had hoped to get the deal over the line at the European Council summit but it was pulled from the agenda at the last minute after opposition by France and Italy, two of the bloc\u8217\'20\'19s biggest agricultural producers.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday night French president EmmanuelMacron announced it would vote against the deal after farmers rolled into Paris on tractors in the latest protests against the pact which they fear will flood the bloc with cheap and substandard meat.\par \par \uc2 A senior European Commission official described Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s decision to also vote against the deal as \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointing\u8221\'20\'1D claiming the country\u8217\'20\'19s concerns had been address.\par \par \uc2 Belgian abstained from the vote, reflecting differences in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the deal say it will help the EU diversify and compensate for the new trade barriers erected by Donald Trump and lower tariffs on the equivalent of just 1.5% of beef produced in the EU.\par \par \uc2 It will see import duties phased out on 91% of EU goods and allow freer access to critical raw materials like lithium needed for batteries in the European car industry.\par \par \uc2 The German federation of industries has welcomed the Mercosur deal saying it is an important day for the Germany and European economies showing that the \u8220\'20\'1CEU can be a relevant geostrategic actor\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 We have some rather mixed economic data from Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy this morning.\par \par \uc2 German exports unexpectedly fell by 2.5% in November, with shipments to the US, and other European Union countries, both declining.\par \par \uc2 But factory output has risen unexpectedly in November, by 0.8%, while factory orders surged by 5.6% in the month.\par \par \uc2 Holger Schmieding, chief economist at Berenberg, says this shows the German government\u8217\'20\'19s stimulus package is starting to work, lifting domestic demand, explaining:\par \par \uc2 10.53am GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2026/jan/09/rio-tinto-glencore-merger-talks-mining-sainsbury-us-jobs-report-business-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6960afbe8f08d5605e6e887d#block-6960afbe8f08d5605e6e887d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 I mentioned earlier}}}\uc2  that coal could be a stumbling block in the Rio-Glencore merger talks, but apparently it\u8217\'20\'19s not!\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg are reporting that RioTinto is open to retaining Glencore\u8217\'20\'19s coal business if merger talks between the two companies are successful.\par \par \uc2 That would be a return to coal for Rio, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2018/mar/28/rio-tinto-sells-its-last-australian-coalmine-for-225bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sold its last Australian coal mine in 2018}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-09/rio-tinto-said-to-be-open-to-owning-coal-if-it-buys-glencore?srnd=homepage-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg say}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 9.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Falling food inflation could encourage central banks to keep cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 UK government bonds have rallied this week, partly due to predictions that weaker inflation could lead to more Bank of England interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 According to Bloomberg data, benchmark 10-year UK gilts are on track for the biggest weekly gain since October; as bond prices rise, the yield (or rate of return) on the debt falls.\par \par \uc2 9.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK food inflation to continue to fall in 2026, says Sainsbury's boss\par \par \uc2 The boss of supermarket chain Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s has predicted that food inflation will continue to fall in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Following this morning\u8217\'20\'19s Christmas trading update, Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s CEO Simon Roberts told reporters:\par \par \uc2 Roberts said that commodity prices were likely to be more stable in 2026 \u8211\'20\'13 speaking shortly before the UN reported that global food prices had dropped again in December ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2026/jan/09/rio-tinto-glencore-merger-talks-mining-sainsbury-us-jobs-report-business-live-news-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6960c48d8f08e0dc61f234f2#block-6960c48d8f08e0dc61f234f2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see previous post}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 9.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 World food prices fall again in Decemeber\par \par \uc2 Global food prices have fallen again, offering some hope to families around the world struggling with the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations\u8217\'20\'19 monthly Food Price Index shows that average price fell by 0.8% month-on-month in December, pulled down by dairy products, meat and vegetable oils which offset increases in cereals and sugar.\par \par \uc2 Following falls in recent months, the index is now 2.3% below its level one year ago, and 22.4% below the peak reached in March 2022, after Russia invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The report shows that dairy prices fell by 4.4% in December, with butter prices fall sharply, driven by seasonally higher cream availability in Europe, while ehole milk powder (WMP) prices also declined, reflecting peak seasonal milk output in Oceania.\par \par \uc2 Meat prices fell 1.3% in the month, but were still 3.4% above their level a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The UN says prices declined across all meat categories, with those of bovine and poultry meats falling the most, adding:\par \par \uc2 Vegetable oil prices fell 0.2% in December to a six-month low, due to lower world prices of soy, rapeseed and sunflower oils.\par \par \uc2 Global soyoil prices declined on ample export supplies from the Americas, while larger rapeseed outputs in Australia and Canada exerted downward pressure on rapeseed markets, the UN says, while there was \u8220\'20\'1Csluggish\u8221\'20\'1D demand for sunflower oil.\par \par \uc2 But, cereal prices rose by 1.7%, amid renewed concerns over Black Sea exports as the Russia-Ukraine war continued.\par \par \uc2 Sugar was pricier too, with prices up 2.4%, due to a sharp drop in sugar production in Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s key southern growing regions.\par \par \uc2 8.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 The copper price is rising again today, as both base metals and precious metals remain in demand.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange has risen by 1.20% to $12,873 a ton this morning, which puts it on track for a gain of more than 3%.\par \par \uc2 Copper is a key factor in the Rio Tinto-Glencore talks. In December, Gary Nagle, Glencore\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, said the company\u8217\'20\'19s aim was to become \u8220\'20\'1Cthe biggest copper producer in the world\u8221\'20\'1D. It is currently the world\u8217\'20\'19s sixth-largest copper producer and the largest listed coal producer.\par \par \uc2 Copper, widely used in industry, has been rising in price amid concerns about tightening supplies, and speculation that Donald Trump could impose a tariff on imports of the metal.\par \par \uc2 8.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 rises as 'the mother of all mining deals' returns\par \par \uc2 News of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2026/jan/09/rio-tinto-glencore-merger-talks-mining-sainsbury-us-jobs-report-business-live-news-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6960a80e8f085fa3d4b40d08#block-6960a80e8f085fa3d4b40d08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the potential mega-mining deal involving Rio Tinto and Glencore}}}\uc2  is sending ripples though the London stock market.\par \par \uc2 UPDATED: Shares in Glencore, which would probably be acquired if a deal goes though, jumped by more than 6% in early trading, and are now up 8%.\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto, the likely buyer if a deal is reached, are down 2.6%\par \par \uc2 Other mining stocks are also up this morning; copper producer Antofagasta has gained 1.5% while AngloAmerican ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/09/anglo-american-merge-teck-copper-london-listing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 involved in its own takeover, of Canada\u8217\'20\'19s }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/09/anglo-american-merge-teck-copper-london-listing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Teck}}}\uc2 ) has gained 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 So overall, the FTSE100 share index is up 0.25% or 24 points at 10,069 points.\par \par \uc2 Derren Nathan, head of equity research at Hargreaves Lansdown, says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 On Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s results, retail analyst NickBubb says:\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s continue to expect to deliver an underlying operating profit of more than \u163\'00\'A31bn, after upgrading its guidance in November.\par \par \uc2 There is one upgrade to guidance, though; the company now predicts free cash flow of more than \u163\'00\'A3550m (previous guidance: more than \u163\'00\'A3500 million), which it says reflects \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong working capital performance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Sainsbury's shares fall after Christmas trading report\par \par \uc2 UK supermarket chain J Sainsbury appears to have disappointed the City with its Christmas trading performance.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s have dropped by almost 4% at the start of trading in London, after the company reported weaker-than-expected sales growth over the crucial festive period.\par \par \uc2 Like-for-like sales excluding fuel across the company, which includes Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s supermarkets and the Argos catalogue chain, rose 3.4% in the last quarter (to 3 January), below analyst forecasts according to Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 Simon Roberts, chief executive of J Sainsbury, says the company won grocery market share for the sixth consecutive Christmas period, adding:\par \par \uc2 Grocery sales at Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s supermarkets rose by 5.4% in the quarter, but general merchandise and clothes sales fell by 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 Argos also struggled, with its sales falling by 1% in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 Roberts insists Argos is making progress, telling the City:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Coal could be a potential stumbling block in these latest Rio Tinto/Glencore talks.\par \par \uc2 Rio\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2018/mar/28/rio-tinto-sells-its-last-australian-coalmine-for-225bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 divested itself of the dirty, polluting fossil fuel in the last decade}}}\uc2 ; in 2018 it became the only major global mining company to have no coal assets.\par \par \uc2 Glencore, though, is{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.glencore.com/what-we-do/energy/coal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest producers and exporters of seaborne traded thermal and steelmaking coal}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 7.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 Rio and Glencore also held talks in 2024 about a possible tie-up, but were unable to reach agreement on issues such as valuation.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Rio Tinto has appointed a new chief executive, Simon Trott, who has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.investments.halifax.co.uk/research-centre/news-centre/article/?id=21326702&type=bsm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 focusing on cost-cutting and asset sales}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 John Ayoub, a portfolio manager at Rio shareholder WilsonAssetManagement, says (via Bloomberg):\par \par \uc2 7.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Rio and Glencore in talks over mega-merger\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Talks are underway to create the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest mining company.\par \par \uc2 Rio\par \par \uc2 Tinto is in talks to buy rival Glencore, a move which could create a company with a combined value of over $200bn (\u163\'00\'A3152bn).\par \par \uc2 The two companies confirmed in statements overnight that they have been discussing a potential combination of some or all of their businesses, which could include an all-share takeover.\par \par \uc2 Both companies hold significant copper mines, which are profiting from prices hitting record levels; a combination could create a powerful rivalt to the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest miner, BHP Group.\par \par \uc2 Glencore told shareholders:\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto confirmed that the two companies have been \u8220\'20\'1Cengaging in preliminary discussions about a possible combination of some or all of their businesses\u8221\'20\'1D, and that any merger transaction would involve it buying Glencore.\par \par \uc2 The two companies have previous; back in 2014{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2014/oct/07/rio-tinto-shares-rise-glencore-approach-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 , }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2014/oct/07/rio-tinto-shares-rise-glencore-approach-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rio}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2014/oct/07/rio-tinto-shares-rise-glencore-approach-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  rejected a merger approach from smaller rival }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2014/oct/07/rio-tinto-shares-rise-glencore-approach-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glencore}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2014/oct/07/rio-tinto-shares-rise-glencore-approach-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 .}}}\par \par \uc2 The mining sector is no stranger to talkover talks, and indeed failed approaches! BHP has been rebuffed in its attempts to take over rival miner AngloAmerican, which is itself taking over Teck Resources.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am GMT: German trade and export data\par \par \uc2 * 9am GMT: UN food price inflation report\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm GMT: US non farm payrolls\par \par \uc2 * 3pm GMT: University of Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s US consumer confidence report\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260109em19001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
