\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Thousands of New York City nurses set to strike amid contract disputes\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 797 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2026\par 15:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 16,000 nurses to join union-led strike on Monday to demand large hospitals across NYC \u8216\'20\'18put patients over profit\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Nearly 16,000 nurses in New York City are set to strike on Monday amid a battle over safe staffing, healthcare benefits, pay and workplace safety during contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The action, due to take place across five large hospitals, is being organized by the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2  (NYSNA), which is demanding the hospitals put patients over profit.\par \par \uc2 The hospitals insist they continue to bargain in good faith, and have accused the union of trying to disrupt patient care.\par \par \uc2 CEO compensation at three hospitals \u8211\'20\'13 Montefiore, Mount Sinai, and New York Presbyterian \u8211\'20\'13 is nearly 12,000 times more than that of the average nurse, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Hagans\uc2 , president of \uc2 NYSNA\uc2 , who has worked at Maimonides medical center in Brooklyn for over 30 years, told the Guardian that the hospitals are pushing to scale back safe staffing ratios, refusing to guarantee healthcare benefit coverage for nurses, and declining to address workplace safety issues.\par \par \uc2 Three of the major hospitals involved in contract negotiations have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysna.org/press/nysna-nurses-deliver-10-day-strike-notice-twelve-private-sector-hospitals-new-york-city"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1.6bn}}}\uc2  cash or cash equivalent on hand, according to the union, which said they have already started hiring travel nurses to replace those striking in preparation for a strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we are asking them is to take those millions and invest it into the community so we could continue to work to care for our patients, invest it into staffing, invest those millions into our medical coverage so we could come to work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hagans. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not taking this lightly. We\u8217\'20\'19re not asking to strike. Management is the one who\u8217\'20\'19s telling us that we need to strike. They are the ones putting us on the picket line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cited safety issues that stem from a drastic increase of assaults on healthcare workers since the Covid-19 pandemic, and an active shooter incident at Mount Sinai last November, where three nurses were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2025/12/04/mount-sinai-shooting-nurses-nysna/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly disciplined}}}\uc2  for speaking out afterwards.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe large, wealthy hospitals are much better off financially than they were three years ago, but yet they\u8217\'20\'19re asking for takeaways,\u8221\'20\'1D claimed Hagans. \u8220\'20\'1CThey told us to choose between our wages or keeping our healthcare and safe staffing, because we cannot have it all. The safety of a patient should never be a bargaining chip. Nurses will not cut corners on patients and their safety. Nurses are just demanding that New York City private hospitals put patients over profit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nurses {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysna.org/press/nysna-nurses-deliver-10-day-strike-notice-twelve-private-sector-hospitals-new-york-city"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represented}}}\uc2  by \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  issued 10-day strike notices on 2 January at a string of New York hospitals. On Tuesday, seven of the smaller hospitals moved forward in contract negotiations, with the union rescinding their strike notice.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://longisland.news12.com/hospital-contingency-plan-remains-unclear-if-1000-northwell-nurses-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 About 1,000 nurses }}}\uc2 at three Northwell hospitals in Long Island have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysna.org/news/northwell-nurses-deliver-10-day-strike-notices-three-long-island-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  strike notices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur nurses have been at the bargaining table since September 2025 with little to no movement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Simone Way, a member of the \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  executive committee, and a nurse at Mount Sinai Morningside hospital. \u8220\'20\'1CEven though we started this process in September, there have been committees in the hospital having these same discussions on staffing since January 2025, so it\u8217\'20\'19s no surprise to our management the things nurses feel we need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Way also criticized hospital management\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to blame the strike on nurses, claiming they are putting patients at risk and asking for too much. \u8220\'20\'1CThey do have the money, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t appear to be willing to invest that money in their nurses or into the ability for us to provide the best care humanly possible to our patients,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  denied retaliation claims against nurses following November\u8217\'20\'19s active-shooter incident, and denied attempting to take away healthcare benefits for nurses. A spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are continuing to bargain in good faith in the hopes of reaching an agreement that is fair, reasonable, and responsible, but unfortunately just a week before a potential strike \uc2 NYSNA\uc2  has refused to move off of its extreme economic proposals that demand hospitals like ours spend billions of dollars on increased nursing compensation and additional hires.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They claimed \u8220\'20\'1Cmost of the union\u8217\'20\'19s claims about bargaining are untrue\u8221\'20\'1D, and confirmed that the hospital was taking steps to prepare in case of a strike.\par \par \uc2 New York Presbyterian blamed the union for not budging on wages. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 NYSNA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threatened strike is intended to disrupt patient care across the city,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have taken the necessary steps to ensure that our patients will continue to receive safe, exceptional care. We will always meet our fundamental obligation to the communities we serve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Montefiore did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260109em190033a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
