\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 The 2025 US economy \u8211\'20\'13 in charts: rising prices, hiring slowdown, rollercoaster growth\b0\par\par\uc2 Callum Jones and Aliya Uteuova \par \uc2 757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2025\par 12:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Looking at the government\u8217\'20\'19s own report cards, how did the US fare under the first year of Trumponomics 2.0?\par \par \uc2 The US economy is thriving, according to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 : jobs are surging, prices are falling, wages are soaring. The government\u8217\'20\'19s own official statistics paint a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/20/trump-us-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more complicated picture}}}\uc2  of 2025.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump Economic Golden Age is FULL steam ahead,\u8221\'20\'1D the president claimed on social media, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/23/us-economy-gdp-third-quarter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 growth data}}}\uc2  for the third quarter of the year \u8211\'20\'13 covering July, August and September \u8211\'20\'13 was unexpectedly strong.\par \par \uc2 But other key indicators have been far less robust in 2025. If Trump is right, and an unprecedented economic boom is about to take hold, the foundations appear fragile.\par \par \uc2 Inflation\par \par \uc2 The cost of living \u8211\'20\'13 and its sharp rise, for many Americans, in the aftermath of the Covid-19 pandemic \u8211\'20\'13 was central to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s fight for re-election in 2024. While inflation peaked at its highest level in a generation in 2022, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/18/november-us-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prices remained high}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, Trump repeatedly promised to tackle this problem and bring down not only inflation \u8211\'20\'13 the rate at which prices rise \u8211\'20\'13 but prices themselves.\par \par \uc2 But inflation held firm in 2025, as the White House pressed ahead with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s flagship economic policy. Despite his rhetoric on reducing prices, many economists feared the president\u8217\'20\'19s sweeping tariffs on imports from overseas risked driving them higher.\par \par \uc2 Interest rates\par \par \uc2 Price growth fell sharply after policymakers at the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  launched an aggressive campaign to cool the US economy, rapidly increasing interest rates in an attempt to reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 By the turn of the year, the Fed had already started to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/sep/18/inflation-fed-rate-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bring rates down}}}\uc2  from their two-decade high. But policymakers, led by the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , wanted to tread carefully \u8211\'20\'13 and were anxious that cutting rates too aggressively would imperil the progress they had made on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Trump, no careful treader, returned to office demanding a rapid return to low rates to spur economic growth and berating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/18/trump-calls-fed-chair-a-numbskull-who-makes-it-difficult-for-people-to-buy-a-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cnumbskull\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  Powell (whom he appointed). His public demands and attacks were coolly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jul/24/trump-jerome-powell-federal-reserve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brushed off}}}\uc2  by Powell. But by the end of the year, Trump had tried to bolster the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s rate-setting board with officials who were more in line with his thinking, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/10/fed-interest-rates-us-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dividing opinion}}}\uc2  on the Fed. Next year, he gets to replace Powell.\par \par \uc2 Employment\par \par \uc2 A post-pandemic surge in jobs growth was already losing steam by last year. Trump tapped into Americans\u8217\'20\'19 frustrations with the economy, promising a \u8220\'20\'1Cmanufacturing renaissance\u8221\'20\'1D that would set the stage for another jobs surge.\par \par \uc2 The renaissance has yet to arrive, however, and over the summer, growth in the labor force appeared to stall altogether. Rather than add jobs, the US economy actually shed them in June and August. And in October, amid the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/12/house-bill-government-shutdown-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longest government shutdown in history}}}\uc2 , it lost an estimated 105,000.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate \u8211\'20\'13 the percentage of the US labor force that is out of work \u8211\'20\'13 has meanwhile been rising throughout the year, and in November scaled its highest level since September 2021.\par \par \uc2 The president tried to push back, claiming \u8220\'20\'1Cthe only reason\u8221\'20\'1D unemployment had climbed was because the federal workforce had declined \u8211\'20\'13 although the federal workforce has fallen 271,000 since January, while the number of unemployed Americans has risen by about a million.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI wish the Fake News would report the 4.5% correctly,\u8221\'20\'1D he added on social media earlier this month, after the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  put November\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate at 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 Growth\par \par \uc2 By the main measure of economic growth \u8211\'20\'13 gross domestic product, or GDP \u8211\'20\'13 the US economy has endured a rollercoaster year. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/30/economy-gdp-q1-trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shrank in the first quarter}}}\uc2 , amid an influx of imports ahead of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s expected tariff onslaught, before bouncing back in the second and gathering steam in the third.\par \par \uc2 After growth of 2.8% in 2024, the US economy is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.imf.org/external/datamapper/NGDP_RPCH@WEO/USA?zoom=USA&highlight=USA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected}}}\uc2  to expand by 2% this year, and 2.1% next year. But many economists did not anticipate such a robust GDP reading for the third quarter, which came ahead of the federal shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The last time growth was this strong, during \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency, rising prices and patchy labor growth left many struggling to reconcile the headline figures with the day-to-day reality of their lives.\par \par \uc2 Trump is promising a historic economic boom. Like Biden, he may well find the real task is not just to deliver it \u8211\'20\'13 but to ensure Americans feel it, too.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251228elcs0020b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
