\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised a new \u8216\'20\'18golden age\u8217\'20\'19 for the US economy. Where is it?\b0\par\par\uc2 Callum Jones, Andrew Witherspoon and Aliya Uteuova in New York \par \uc2 1,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2025\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Most Americans have yet to see this boom \u8211\'20\'13 but they\u8217\'20\'19ve certainly heard a lot about it from the president\par \par \uc2 Moments into his second term, opening his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/remarks/2025/01/the-inaugural-address/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inaugural address}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was unequivocal. \u8220\'20\'1CThe golden age of America begins right now,\u8221\'20\'1D he declared.\par \par \uc2 At a White House reception last weekend, a little over 10 months later, the US president appeared to acknowledge just how far his timeline had shifted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to have \u8230\'20\'26 I say it\u8217\'20\'19s the golden age of America,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump told his audience. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an age that\u8217\'20\'19s coming up, the likes of which \u8230\'20\'26 this country has never seen. And I just look so forward to the results. You\u8217\'20\'19re going to see results in six months to a year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The US economy has come under pressure in 2025. A golden age did not promptly take hold, as Trump initially claimed, upon his return to office in January.\par \par \uc2 On two areas the president pledged to rapidly improve \u8211\'20\'13 jobs and prices \u8211\'20\'13 the results have so far been lackluster.\par \par \uc2 The labor market started the year steady. It had slowed, but still managed to expand by an average of 168,000 jobs each month, in 2024.\par \par \uc2 During his joint address to Congress in March, however, Trump claimed to have inherited an \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomic catastrophe\u8221\'20\'1D from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration, and vowed to create jobs \u8220\'20\'1Clike we have never seen before\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A new \u8216\'20\'18golden age\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Jobs growth has, in fact, stalled in 2025 \u8211\'20\'13 with an average of 55,000 jobs added each month, according to official data for the first 11 months of the year, down 67% on 2024.\par \par \uc2 The headline unemployment rate, which fluctuated between 3.9% and 4.2% in the last year of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, has climbed under Trump to 4.6% \u8211\'20\'13 its highest level in more than four years \u8211\'20\'13 in November.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail last year, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/24/us/politics/trump-economic-policy.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promised}}}\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew American industrialism\u8221\'20\'1D on his watch, through which the US would become a \u8220\'20\'1Cmanufacturing powerhouse\u8221\'20\'1D once again, with a resurgence in factory jobs.\par \par \uc2 In reality, manufacturing employment has not increased this year. The US economy has so far added factory jobs in just two of 10 months after Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return to office.\par \par \uc2 Realizing this distant dream of an industrial renaissance, according to the White House, will require ramping up the president\u8217\'20\'19s flagship economic policy: tariffs.\par \par \uc2 And Trump has moved to do just that, hiking the overall average effective US tariff rate from 2.4% to 16.8%, the highest level since 1935, according to analysis by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://budgetlab.yale.edu/research/state-us-tariffs-november-17-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Budget Lab}}}\uc2  at Yale.\par \par \uc2 The rollout of higher tariffs has been erratic and marred by uncertain deadlines, delays and reversals. The process has been \u8220\'20\'1Cmore painful than I expected\u8221\'20\'1D, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s chief of staff Susie Wiles {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/news/story/trump-susie-wiles-interview-exclusive-part-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conceded}}}\uc2  to Vanity Fair.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Nobody can believe what\u8217\'20\'19s going on\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 For all the pain this agenda has caused inside the White House, economists have repeatedly warned of a potentially stark impact on millions of Americans. Tariffs on goods from overseas, often passed along by the businesses that pay them, can lead to higher prices on store shelves.\par \par \uc2 Officials and policymakers inside the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  have spent months considering whether the ultimate effect of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs will be a one-time jolt to prices, or something more lasting.\par \par \uc2 Inflation surged to its highest level in a generation in 2022 as the Covid-19 pandemic unleashed a series of supply and demand shocks on the world economy. By last year, however, the US consumer price index (CPI) was falling back down towards typical levels \u8211\'20\'13 and continued to do so during the early months of 2025.\par \par \uc2 By Trump\u8217\'20\'19s telling, though, his administration inherited an \u8220\'20\'1Cinflation nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D from Biden, which he vowed to turn around. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/18/trump-end-year-primetime-nation-address-opinion-polls-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 primetime TV address}}}\uc2  on Wednesday, he declared: \u8220\'20\'1CI am bringing those high prices down and bringing them down very fast.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But CPI has held firm since the spring. In November, it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/dec/18/november-us-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose by an annual rate of 2.7%}}}\uc2 , according to data released by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The president has repeatedly denied responsibility for inflation this year, and dismissed concerns about prices as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccon job\u8221\'20\'1D. Asked by Politico this month how he would grade himself on the economy, he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/12/09/trump-interview-7-takeaways-economy-health-care-europe-00681724"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 replied}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CA-plus-plus-plus-plus-plus\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 During his TV address this week, Trump insisted he was \u8220\'20\'1Csolving\u8221\'20\'1D grocery prices, claiming that turkey and egg prices were down sharply. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything else is falling rapidly,\u8221\'20\'1D he claimed. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19s not done yet, but boy, are we making progress. Nobody can believe what\u8217\'20\'19s going on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Hard to see how 2026 will be better\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 There have been signs, however, that the administration is moving to reduce pressure in an economy rated so highly by the president.\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/14/trump-tariffs-food"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moved to lower tariffs}}}\uc2  on some imports, including beef, tomatoes, coffee and bananas, last month. The administration has also unveiled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/08/trump-farmers-aid-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $12bn in economic assistance}}}\uc2  for farmers, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/09/trump-tariffs-refund"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repeatedly mooted stimulus checks}}}\uc2  for Americans, funded by tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Kicking off a nationwide tour designed to build the narrative of a \u8220\'20\'1Cremarkable turnaround\u8221\'20\'1D, Trump appeared at a rally in Mount Pocono, Pennsylvania, last week in front of a vast sign that read: \u8220\'20\'1CLOWER PRICES BIGGER PAYCHECKS\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Is a golden age really about to take hold? Trump and his officials have an almost messianic belief the economy will step up a gear in the first quarter of next year, \uc2 Axios{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2025/12/15/trump-economy-2026-income-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  earlier this week, citing advisers to the administration.\par \par \uc2 This confidence is said to be driven by projections that stimulus from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/01/what-is-in-big-beautiful-bill-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 massive One Big Beautiful Bill Act tax-and-spending legislation}}}\uc2  will deliver a significant boost to household finances, and spur business investment.\par \par \uc2 Outside the administration, economists are skeptical.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost lower income Americans will be hurt (net) by these policies. And you need to add the increase in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/14/aca-obamacare-expires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 healthcare premiums}}}\uc2  on top of this,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Simon Johnson\uc2 , a Nobel prize-winning economist and professor at MIT\u8217\'20\'19s Sloan School of Management. \u8220\'20\'1CHard to see how 2026 will be better for most people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The US economy is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.imf.org/en/publications/weo/issues/2025/10/14/world-economic-outlook-october-2025#Projections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forecast}}}\uc2  to grow by 2% this year, down from 2.8% in 2024. \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , chief US economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, expects only a \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty small fiscal boost\u8221\'20\'1D from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies next year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn addition, households\u8217\'20\'19 relatively low confidence suggests many of them will save the windfall from larger-than-usual tax refunds in the spring,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tombs. \u8220\'20\'1CAccordingly, we expect GDP growth of about 2% again next year, well short of the administration\u8217\'20\'19s hopes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump remains bullish. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re poised for an economic boom the likes of which the world has never seen,\u8221\'20\'1D he assured the nation this week.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans have yet to see this boom. But they\u8217\'20\'19ve certainly heard a lot about it.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251222elck001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
