\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Wednesday briefing: What\u8217\'20\'19s at stake for the BBC as it faces further funding questions\b0\par\par\uc2 Karen McVeigh \par \uc2 2,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2025\par 06:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In today\u8217\'20\'19s newsletter: The BBC is bracing for a high-stakes legal fight after the US president sues over an edited Panorama clip, and questions mount about the broadcaster\u8217\'20\'19s future funding and political vulnerability\par \par \uc2 Good morning. On Tuesday, the UK woke to the news Donald Trump had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/15/trump-bbc-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed his threatened $10bn lawsuit}}}\uc2  against the BBC, alleging that the corporation \u8220\'20\'1Cintentionally, maliciously and deceptively\u8221\'20\'1D defamed him. Later that day, the government launched a review of how the beleaguered corporation will be funded into the 2030s \u8211\'20\'13 they will ask the public for their views on options, including a subscription service or adverts, amid a drop in licence holders.\par \par \uc2 While there remains a lack of consensus over the latter issue \u8211\'20\'13 in itself enough to worry BBC executives over its future \u8211\'20\'13 few would argue that licence fee payers should be funding a multimillion pound compensation claim to the increasingly litigious US president.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, over an edit of a Trump speech contained in a BBC Panorama documentary shown shortly before the 2024 US election, has sparked a public relations crisis for the broadcaster. On Tuesday, the BBC repeated its commitment to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/dec/16/bbc-donald-trump-lawsuit-should-be-dismissed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defend the lawsuit}}}\uc2 . It has previously acknowledged that the edit of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech before the January 6 riots, was an \u8220\'20\'1Cerror of judgment\u8221\'20\'1D and apologised to the president, but has rejected his demands for compensation and has insisted there was no legal basis for a defamation claim.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from the row led to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resignation of both Tim Davie}}}\uc2 , the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s director-general and the head of news, Deborah Turness, in November.\par \par \uc2 For today\u8217\'20\'19s newsletter, I spoke to Haroon Siddique, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s legal affairs correspondent, to dig into what the case means for media freedom, what is at stake \u8211\'20\'13 and who is likely to win. First, today\u8217\'20\'19s headlines.\par \par \uc2 Five big stories\par \par \uc2 * Australia\par \par \uc2 | The alleged Bondi attacker who survived a shootout with police has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/dec/17/bondi-terror-attack-alleged-shooter-charged-after-15-killed-at-hanukah-celebration-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged with 59 offences}}}\uc2 , including 15 counts of murder, after waking from his coma on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 European Union\par \par \uc2 | An agreement to rejoin Erasmus \u8211\'20\'13 the EU\u8217\'20\'19s student exchange programme \u8211\'20\'13 is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/dec/16/uk-to-rejoin-eu-erasmus-student-exchange-programme"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected to be announced on Wednesday}}}\uc2  as part of the \uc2 UK government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s drive towards closer relations with Brussels.\par \par \uc2 * Venezuela\par \par \uc2 | Donald Trump has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/16/trump-orders-blockade-oil-tankers-venezuela"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered \u8220\'20\'1Ca total and complete\u8221\'20\'1D blockade}}}\uc2  of all sanctioned oil tankers entering and leaving Venezuela, ramping up pressure on the country\u8217\'20\'19s authoritarian leader Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro.\par \par \uc2 * UK news\par \par \uc2 | A former Royal Marine is starting a 21-year jail term {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/dec/16/man-who-drove-into-liverpool-fc-parade-jailed-paul-doyle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for mowing down}}}\uc2  dozens of Liverpool football fans in a \u8220\'20\'1Ctruly shocking\u8221\'20\'1D act that \u8220\'20\'1Cdefies ordinary understanding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * US news\par \par \uc2 | Nick Reiner has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2025/dec/16/rob-reiner-son-nick-reiner-charged"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged with two counts of first-degree murder}}}\uc2  in the killing of his parents, the acclaimed actor and director Rob Reiner and the photographer Michele Singer Reiner, authorities announced on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In depth: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m suing the BBC for putting words in my mouth\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In a complaint filed on Monday evening in a court in Florida, Donald Trump sought $5bn in damages each on two counts: alleging that the BBC defamed him, and that it violated a Florida law that bars deceptive and unfair trade practices.\par \par \uc2 Before Trump filed the lawsuit, lawyers for the BBC had given a response to the president\u8217\'20\'19s claims. There was no malice in the edit, they said, and Trump was not harmed by the programme as he was re-elected shortly after it aired. The BBC did not have the rights to, and did not distribute, the Panorama programme on its US channels, they also said, and that, while the documentary was available on BBC iPlayer, it was restricted only to viewers in the UK.\par \par \uc2 In the lawsuit, Trump cites agreements the BBC had with other distributors, in particular one with a third-party media corporation that allegedly had licensing rights to the documentary outside the UK. It also claims people in Florida may have accessed the programme through the streaming service BritBox, or using a virtual private network (VPN) to mask their location. The BBC has not yet responded to these claims.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 What did the edit show?\par \par \uc2 In his speech on 6 January 2021, before the infamous violence in Washington, Trump told a crowd: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to walk down to the Capitol, and we\u8217\'20\'19re going to cheer on our brave senators and congressmen and women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than 50 minutes later, he said in the speech: \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we fight. We fight like hell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the Panorama programme, an edit showed him as saying: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to walk down to the Capitol \u8230\'20\'26 and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be there with you. And we fight. We fight like hell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The BBC acknowledged the edit had given \u8220\'20\'1Cthe mistaken impression\u8221\'20\'1D Trump had \u8220\'20\'1Cmade a direct call for violent action,\u8221\'20\'1D but disagreed that there was basis for a claim for defamation.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the president told reporters at the Oval Office: \u8220\'20\'1CIn a little while, you\u8217\'20\'19ll be seeing I\u8217\'20\'19m suing the BBC for putting words in my mouth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 What are the key elements of the case?\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit claims the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Cintentionally and maliciously sought to fully mislead\u8221\'20\'1D its viewers around the world by splicing together two separate parts of his speech. It also claims the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Cpurposefully omitted\u8221\'20\'1D a part of the president\u8217\'20\'19s statement calling on marchers to \u8220\'20\'1Cpeacefully\u8221\'20\'1D make their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 Unlike in the UK, public figures in the US are held to a higher standard of proof in defamation cases than the general public \u8211\'20\'13 they must prove \u8220\'20\'1Cactual malice\u8221\'20\'1D, says Haroon Siddique. Under US law, \u8220\'20\'1Cactual malice\u8221\'20\'1D does not mean spite or ill will, but whether the publisher knew the statement was false or recklessly disregarded the truth. The claimant must also show that they suffered injury as a result of the libel. (Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers say the BBC caused him \u8220\'20\'1Creputational and financial harm\u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 In court, lawyers for the BBC could argue against this and say that the content in question was part of a documentary that also featured pro-Trump voices, Haroon says. They could also question whether the president had actually suffered harm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe two questions are whether you could get access to this particular programme,\u8221\'20\'1D Haroon says, \u8220\'20\'1Cand secondly \u8211\'20\'13 if you could get access \u8211\'20\'13 how many people actually watched it, which I imagine wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be that high.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In assessing reputational harm, the \u8220\'20\'1Cnumbers of people who watched is a relevant factor,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it\u8217\'20\'19s only a handful, how can you prove reputational harm? It is not straightforward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mark Stephens, a media lawyer, said back in November: \u8220\'20\'1CTrump\u8217\'20\'19s reputation has already been battered by nine judicial findings, some congressional hearings, global coverage of January 6, and he faces ongoing civil lawsuits in Washington itself, and also a special counsel report alleging a criminal scheme to make or to have civil insurrection.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo proving that Panorama caused additional serious harm is a bit of a stretch. If it can be shown by the BBC that his reputation was already in tatters on this issue, blaming the BBC for the wreckage of that is a bit of a tough sell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Has Trump\u8217\'20\'19s anti-media campaign gone\par \par \uc2 global?\par \par \uc2 The president\u8217\'20\'19s contentious relationship with the US media has led to a catalogue of disputes and legal battles since his re-election in November. ABC, owned by \uc2 Disney\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/dec/14/abc-george-stephanopoulos-trump-15-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to pay}}}\uc2  $15m as part of a settlement over a defamation lawsuit he brought after comments made by the anchor George Stephanopoulos.\par \par \uc2 In July, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/jul/02/paramount-settles-with-trump-for-16m-over-60-minutes-interview-with-kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 also reached}}}\uc2  a $16m settlement with Paramount, the parent company of CBS News, over what he claimed was false editing of a pre-election interview with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Trump had filed a $10bn lawsuit against the company in October, one of a string of legal actions against US media conglomerates over what the US president maintains was biased, incorrect or \u8220\'20\'1Cfake\u8221\'20\'1D news reporting.\par \par \uc2 It does seem to demonstrate an escalation of the anti-media campaign by Trump, says Haroon. \u8220\'20\'1CAs far as I know, this is the first foreign broadcaster or press that he\u8217\'20\'19s taken legal action against.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 What impact could it have on the BBC?\par \par \uc2 In an interview on BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme, \uc2 Christopher Ruddy\uc2 , CEO of the rightwing American media company \uc2 Newsmax\uc2 , said he did not think it would be good for the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s public perception to go ahead with the case, and predicted that the case could settle for $10m (\u163\'00\'A37.5m). Ruddy estimated the cost of the litigation for the BBC would be between $50m and $100m.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust having the case go on, probably the BBC would lose in the sense of public perception,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. And Ruddy added that, in a case like this, the plaintiff would have \u8220\'20\'1Cvery significant powers of discovery\u8221\'20\'1D; for example, emails and conversations and private things said by BBC executives about the president that \u8220\'20\'1Cmay not be flattering and may have shown an intent to harm him in the production of this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Haroon says that while discovery always has \u8220\'20\'1Cthe potential to be embarrassing\u8221\'20\'1D, it would be \u8220\'20\'1Csurprising\u8221\'20\'1D to have a BBC executive say anything, let alone think that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey could take Trump down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not how the world works. How much does the BBC have the ability to impact the world\u8217\'20\'19s view of Trump anyway? That\u8217\'20\'19s part of the case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 How could the case impact US/UK relations?\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said on Tuesday that any legal action was a matter for the corporation, but the government defended \u8220\'20\'1Cthe principle of a strong, independent BBC as a trusted, relied-upon national broadcaster, reporting without fear or favour.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starmer has gone to great lengths to cultivate a relationship with Trump. One might imagine that, in the grand scheme of diplomatic relations, this doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter, says Haroon. \u8220\'20\'1CBut, at the same time, we know that Trump gets easily insulted, which can magnify the importance of things other political leaders would not necessarily see as important.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo it\u8217\'20\'19s very difficult to guess how it will play out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What else we\u8217\'20\'19ve been reading\par \par \uc2 * 43% of 25-year-olds in the UK are adults living with their parents, a statistic to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2025/dec/16/full-nesters-adult-children-leave-home-parents-families"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike fear}}}\uc2  into the hearts of those of us with teenagers. Martin\par \par \uc2 * I was moved by the calm with which \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2025/dec/16/ilhan-omar-donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Somali-born US representative}}}\uc2  for Minnesota, deals with the highest level of death threats of any congressperson. Karen\par \par \uc2 * I worry rejecting AI use in music creation will eventually look as Canute-like as the musicians union trying to ban synths in 1982. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2025/dec/16/musicians-are-deeply-concerned-about-ai-so-why-are-the-major-labels-embracing-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eamonn Forde is erudite here}}}\uc2  on the current state of affairs. Martin\par \par \uc2 * I enjoyed Alex Needham\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2025/dec/16/ben-whishaw-interview-peter-hujar-day"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview}}}\uc2  with Ben Whishaw, about his portrayal of a Manhattan photographer who photographed a long gone \u8220\'20\'1Cqueer New York bohemia lost by aids\u8221\'20\'1D in Ira Sach\u8217\'20\'19s film, Peter Hujar\u8217\'20\'19s Day. Karen\par \par \uc2 * Social data scientist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laurenleek.substack.com/p/how-google-maps-quietly-allocates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lauren Leek has done a deep dive}}}\uc2  into how restaurant recommendations in \uc2 Google\uc2  Maps may be skewing the market and algorithmically making and breaking businesses. Martin\par \par \uc2 Sport\par \par \uc2 Cricket\par \par \uc2 | Usman Khawaja proved reports of his demise as an international cricketer were greatly exaggerated as he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2025/dec/17/australia-vs-england-live-ashes-third-3rd-test-day-one-aus-v-eng-cricket-scores-updates-adelaide-oval"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scored a defiant half-century}}}\uc2  on Wednesday to steady Australia on day one of the third Ashes test against England.Football | Chelsea\u8217\'20\'19s substitutes made the difference Tuesday as wingers Alejandro Garnacho and Pedro Neto scored second-half goals to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/dec/16/cardiff-chelsea-carabao-cup-quarter-final-match-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 secure a 3-1 win}}}\uc2  at third-tier Cardiff City and a place in the Carabao Cup semi-finals.Football | amid backlash against exorbitant prices for the 2026 World Cup, Fifa on Tuesday announced that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/dec/16/fifa-world-cup-pricing-tier-supporters-entry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it had created a new tier of tickets}}}\uc2  specifically for supporters of the involved teams for each game, with prices capped at $60 per ticket for every match of the tournament, including the final.\par \par \uc2 The front pages\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  leads with \u8220\'20\'1CUK to rejoin EU Erasmus programme for students\u8221\'20\'1D. The Times has \u8220\'20\'1CReturn of UK student exchange with Europe\u8221\'20\'1D. The i launches a new campaign under the headline \u8220\'20\'1CSilicosis scandal: ban killer kitchen dust to save lives\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 The Financial Times\uc2  reports \u8220\'20\'1CLabour hails sweeping workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights shift as flagship bill clears final hurdle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Mirror looks at Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit against the BBC with \u8220\'20\'1CBBC comes out fighting\u8221\'20\'1D. The Telegraph reports \u8220\'20\'1CFree TV licences for benefit claimants\u8221\'20\'1D, while the Mail follows the same story with \u8220\'20\'1CFree BBC if you live on Benefits Street\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Today in Focus\par \par \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on Somali-Americans\par \par \uc2 The congresswoman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2025/dec/17/ilhan-omar-donald-trump-attacks-on-somali-americans-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2025/dec/17/ilhan-omar-donald-trump-attacks-on-somali-americans-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 David Smith}}}\uc2  about how it feels to be targeted by the world\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful man. But why has Donald Trump gone after her and the Somali community?\par \par \uc2 Cartoon of the day | Ella Baron\par \par \uc2 The Upside\par \par \uc2 A bit of good news to remind you that the world\u8217\'20\'19s not all bad\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe words people use to describe winter include \u8216\'20\'18bleak\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18dark\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18barren\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18cold\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18miserable\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18depressing\u8217\'20\'19 \u8230\'20\'26 We can\u8217\'20\'19t expect to keep this language and appreciate the joys of winter,\u8221\'20\'1D says Stephanie Fitzgerald, a chartered clinical psychologist. She now feels the spirit of the season is better encapsulated by \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18yutori\u8217\'20\'19, a Japanese word that means room to breathe, a sense of space, taking a moment to pause.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/the-gifts-of-winter-9780241779576/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Gifts of Winter}}}\uc2 , is the result of her exploring how to avoid her former dread of this time. \u8220\'20\'1CI fell deeply in love with winter \u8230\'20\'26 It is a captivating and truly gorgeous season,\u8221\'20\'1D she tells Rachel Dixon, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2025/dec/17/beat-winter-blues-advice-socialising-sad-lamps-celebrating-new-year-april"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this piece}}}\uc2  which teaches us how to love it too.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/feb/12/the-upside-sign-up-for-our-weekly-email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up here}}}\uc2  for a weekly roundup of The Upside, sent to you every Sunday\par \par \uc2 Bored at work?\par \par \uc2 And finally, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s puzzles are here to keep you entertained throughout the day. Until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/crosswords/quick/17353"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Quick crossword}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/crosswords/cryptic/29880"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cryptic crossword}}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wordiply.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wordiply}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251217elch001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
