\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 BBC\uc2  to fight Trump\u8217\'20\'19s $10bn lawsuit, saying it should be dismissed\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Savage Media editor \par \uc2 947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2025\par 21:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corporation will argue it did not have rights to air film in US and it did not cause serious reputational harm\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2  is preparing to argue Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s $10bn court case against it should be dismissed, arguing it has no case to answer over the US president\u8217\'20\'19s claims he was defamed by an episode of Panorama.\par \par \uc2 The development comes after Trump filed a 33-page complaint to a Florida court on Monday, accusing the broadcaster of \u8220\'20\'1Ca false, defamatory, deceptive, disparaging, inflammatory and malicious depiction\u8221\'20\'1D of the president in the documentary.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 BBC\uc2  said it would defend itself over the suit. It is understood that the corporation is likely to argue that it did not have the rights to air the documentary in the US, and that the case should therefore be dismissed.\par \par \uc2 The move is an attempt to see the lawsuit off before litigation costs mount. It will be greeted with relief by supporters of the \uc2 BBC\uc2  who have argued that its editorial independence would be called into question if it sought to settle.\par \par \uc2 The Panorama programme, Trump: A Second Chance?, was broadcast before the 2024 US presidential election. It spliced together two parts of the January 6 speech he gave before the attack on the US Capitol that day.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s complaint argues both that the programme defamed him and that it violated the Florida Deceptive and Unfair Trade Practices Act. It says the broadcast caused Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cmassive economic damage to his brand value and significant damage and injury to his future financial prospects\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2  will note that audiences in the US were unable to access the show via iPlayer, the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s streaming platform.\par \par \uc2 The corporation is also arguing that Trump could not have suffered serious reputational harm from the programme because he won the presidential election. In Florida, Trump actually increased his vote share from his previous campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit alleges that Florida residents may have been able to watch the programme using a virtual private network (VPN) or via the international streaming service, BritBox. The \uc2 BBC\uc2  has not responded directly to those claims.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BBC\uc2  board and lawyers are still working closely on the issue. The \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s legal department is guiding the discussions, but the board, together with the \uc2 BBC\uc2  chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , are understood to be closely involved.\par \par \uc2 The suit also claims the documentary was distributed in the US through a company called \uc2 Blue Ant Media Corporation\uc2 . The company is not a defendant in the case.\par \par \uc2 However, a spokesperson for \uc2 Blue Ant Media\uc2  said none of its buyers had aired the Panorama episode in the US, while the version it was distributing did not include the infamous edit, which had been cut out to make the programme shorter.\par \par \uc2 The timing could scarcely be worse for the \uc2 BBC\uc2 , as it searches for a new director general. It comes after the resignation of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 current director general Tim Davie and head of BBC News, Deborah Turness}}}\uc2 , following the fallout from the Panorama edit.\par \par \uc2 It also comes with the government officially launching the process of renewing the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s charter, which will see it fundamentally re-examine whether the corporation should be part-funded by advertising or subscriptions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/03/bbc-accused-selectively-editing-trump-clip-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The spliced clips in the edition of Panorama}}}\uc2  suggested that Trump told the crowd: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to walk down to the Capitol and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be there with you, and we fight. We fight like hell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The words were taken from sections of his speech almost an hour apart.\par \par \uc2 Shah has already personally apologised to Trump and the \uc2 BBC\uc2  has said it gave \u8220\'20\'1Cthe mistaken impression\u8221\'20\'1D that the president had \u8220\'20\'1Cmade a direct call for violent action\u8221\'20\'1D. However, its lawyers have argued it did not defame him.\par \par \uc2 While there were no complaints about the documentary at the time it was broadcast, the edit came to light in a memo by Michael Prescott, a former external adviser to the \uc2 BBC\uc2 . His memo was sent to the \uc2 BBC\uc2  board earlier this year and subsequently leaked to the Daily Telegraph.\par \par \uc2 At one point, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit cites former prime minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  in support of its allegation that the \uc2 BBC\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cinstitutionally biased against President Trump\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo less an authority than the United Kingdom\u8217\'20\'19s former prime minister, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , discussed this bias, the need to hold the \uc2 BBC\uc2  accountable, and the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s pattern of actual malice,\u8221\'20\'1D it states.\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has come under pressure to back up the \uc2 BBC\uc2 . Both the shadow culture secretary, \uc2 Nigel Huddleston\uc2 , and the Liberal Democrat leader, Ed Davey, urged the prime minister to use his relationship with Trump to persuade him not to pursue the legal action.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Kinnock, a health minister, said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cright that the \uc2 BBC\uc2  stand firm\u8221\'20\'1D against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claims of defamation and that he hoped \u8220\'20\'1Cthey will continue to do so\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Chris Ruddy\uc2 , a Trump ally who founded the \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  platform, said it would prove cheaper for the \uc2 BBC\uc2  to settle the case.\par \par \uc2 However, figures close to the \uc2 BBC\uc2  have urged it to fight the legal action. Former BBC Radio controller \uc2 Mark Damazer\uc2  said it would be \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely damaging\u8221\'20\'1D to the \uc2 BBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reputation to offer a settlement.\par \par \uc2 Mark Stephens, an international media lawyer at the firm Howard Kennedy, said he believed the claim was \u8220\'20\'1Cmeritless\u8221\'20\'1D, but that Trump was seeking political, rather than financial, gain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[The \uc2 BBC\uc2 ] will plead substantial truth, no damage to [Trump\u8217\'20\'19s] reputation \u8211\'20\'13 but it will also, at that time, file the motions to dismiss [the lawsuit],\u8221\'20\'1D he predicted. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are lots of legal defects in the complaint.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251216elcg006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
