\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Unemployment rises in US and UK, adding to pressure to cut interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 3,549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2025\par 14:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial news, as US non-farm payrolls rise by 64,000 in November but shrink by 105,000 in OctoberYoung people hit hard as UK unemployment marches upwards\par \par \uc2 2.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 EU to water down landmark ban on new petrol and diesel cars\par \par \uc2 The EU has confirmed it will water down its landmark 2035 ban on the sale of new petrol or diesel cars, yielding to heavy pressure from the car industry and leaders from several EU member states including Germany and Italy.\par \par \uc2 Under current legislation manufacturers were obliged to ensure that 100% of production of cars and vans had zero emissions up to 2035.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Commission\uc2  confirmed on Tuesday that this will now be reduced to 90%, enabling the continued manufacture of a portion of plug-in hybrid electric cars, or even combustion engines beyond 2035.\par \par \uc2 However, in a carrot-and-stick approach, the remaining 10% of assembly line output that is not carbon neutral will need to be compensated by other green measures on the factory floor, including the use of green steel made in Europe or use of biofuels in non-electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 2.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26. on a day in which unemployment has risen on both sides of the Atlantic\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 The US labor market grew by more than expected last month, recovering some of the damage inflicted by the federal government shutdown, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 An estimated 105,000 jobs were lost in October, and 64,000 were added in November, a highly-anticipated report showed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Jobs growth was higher in November than anticipated by many economists, with a consensus forecast of some 40,000 jobs added.\par \par \uc2 But the headline unemployment rate continued to climb \u8211\'20\'13 and hit 4.6%, a four-year high, last month \u8211\'20\'13 amid apprehension around the strength of the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Several economists forecast that the weakness of the jobs market could prompt the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates more rapidly than it expects next year.\par \par \uc2 The rate of UK unemployment rose to a four-year high of 5.1% in the three months to October, as the labour market showed signs of further weakening before last month\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  said the jobless rate was the highest since January 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 but with the pandemic era stripped out, it was the highest since early 2016.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the rise in the jobless rate made it almost certain that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  would cut interest rates when policymakers meet on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The central bank has said it wanted wages growth to fall further before reducing the cost of borrowing again this year. The latest figures showed wage growth excluding bonuses fell to 4.6% in October, from 4.7% the previous month, the lowest since early 2022.\par \par \uc2 Young people in the UK have been hit particularly hard: the number of 18- to 24-year-olds out of work, at 546,000, is the highest since 2015 and up 85,000 on the quarter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people again find themselves at the heart of this downturn, just as they were in the wake of the financial crisis and Covid. Policymakers and employers need to redouble efforts to support them,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nye Cominetti\uc2 , the principal economist at the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2  thinktank.\par \par \uc2 2.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Stocks have opened lower on Wall Street, where the Dow Jones industrial average is down 116 points or 0.24% at 48,300.\par \par \uc2 2.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , chief international economist at ING, says the continuing US job slowdown is keeping pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to make more interest rates cuts.\par \par \uc2 Knightley says:\par \par \uc2 2.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The signs of weakness in the US jobs market could encourage the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates more often in 2026 than expected.\par \par \uc2 Eaarlier this month the Fed released a dot plot of individual forecasts, showing the median prediction of its policymakers is just one further rate cut in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at \uc2 Principal Asset Management\uc2 , suggests that the Fed may make more cuts than that!\par \par \uc2 1.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Lindsay James, investment strategist at \uc2 Quilter\uc2 , warns that today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report is distorted by some seasonal factors:\par \par \uc2 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Chart: US unemployment climbs as payrolls stumble\par \par \uc2 1.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US lost 105,000 jobs in October as economy loses steam\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy lost 105,000 jobs in October, today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report shows.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a sizeable loss of employment, partly caused by jobs being shed across the federal government, helping to push the jobless rate up to 4.6% in November.\par \par \uc2 Daniela Hathorn, senior market analyst at capital.com, says:\par \par \uc2 1.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Many of the new jobs added across America were in healthcare and construction.\par \par \uc2 The BureauforLaborStatistics reports that\par \par \uc2 * In November, health care added 46,000 jobs with job gains in ambulatory health care services (+24,000), hospitals (+11,000), and nursing and residential care facilities (+11,000).\par \par \uc2 * Construction employment grew by 28,000 in November, as nonresidential specialty trade contractors added 19,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 But on the downside\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 * employment edged down in transportation and warehousing (-18,000), reflecting a job loss in couriers and messengers (-18,000).\par \par \uc2 * Federal government employment continued to decrease in November (-6,000).\par \par \uc2 1.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US lost 26,000 jobs in Augusts\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report also shows that more jobs were lost across the US in August than previously realised.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 1.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US unemployment rate hits 4.6%\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added 64,000 jobs in November, according to the latest jobs report, just released.\par \par \uc2 November\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report, which has been delayed by the recent \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown, shows that the US unemployment rate rose to 4.6% from 4.4% in September.\par \par \uc2 Employment rose in health care and construction in November, while federal government continued to lose jobs.\par \par \uc2 Intriguingly, the jobs report also shows there was a spike in layoffs among government staff in October (probably the effect of the Doge programme).\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The pound has touched a two-month high against the US dollar today, as the greenback weakens generally.\par \par \uc2 Sterling traded as high as $1.3439, its highest level since 20 October.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has also slipped to a two-month low against the euro, as traders position themselves ahead of the US jobs report in 12 minutes time\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 12.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 London\u8217\'20\'19s luxury property market is set for its worst year since 2020, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-12-16/london-luxury-home-market-had-worst-year-since-pandemic-in-2025?srnd=homepage-ukhttps://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-12-16/london-luxury-home-market-had-worst-year-since-pandemic-in-2025?srnd=homepage-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This follows a series of tax measures damped sentiment among wealthy and discretionary buyers, and makes 2025 the second year since 2011 in which no sales above \u163\'00\'A350 million ($66.8 million) were recorded, according to data from property company LonRes.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  adds:\par \par \uc2 12.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The clarity created by November\u8217\'20\'19s budget helped to lift the UK economy in December, says Matt Swannell, chief economic advisor to the \uc2 EY\uc2  ITEM Club:\par \par \uc2 He writes:\par \par \uc2 * The UK flash composite Purchasing Managers\u8217\'20\'19 Index (\uc2 PMI\uc2 ) rebounded in December. Swings in corporate sentiment rather than genuine changes in private sector activity can often drive the survey\u8217\'20\'19s outturns, so reduced domestic uncertainty after the Budget is likely to have played a major role in today\u8217\'20\'19s data. Moreover, the outlook for the private sector in 2026 remains relatively weak.\par \par \uc2 * The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) is likely to cut rates later this week, even if the vote is a closer call than markets expect. With today\u8217\'20\'19s survey suggesting that private sector momentum remains modest and businesses are reflecting higher input costs in prices, the MPC will likely remain deeply divided as we move into next year.\par \par \uc2 11.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC Green Paper considers advertising and subscription options\par \par \uc2 BBC viewers could have to subscribe to a Netflix-style service to watch hit shows such as Traitors and Strictly Come Dancing, and start seeing advertising for the first time, under ambitious options unveiled by ministers to enable the corporation to grow commercial revenues.\par \par \uc2 The culture secretary, \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2 , unveiled the potential proposals in the government\u8217\'20\'19s green paper consultation formally launching the process to renew the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s charter for ten years from 2028.\par \par \uc2 The initial consultation, which will run for 12 weeks before a white paper is published, aims to put the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Con a sustainable financial footing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think there is scope for the BBC to further increase its commercial revenue,\u8221\'20\'1D the government said in the green paper. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are considering a range of options with increasing ambition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The paper outlines potential options from targeted advertisements on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bbc.co.uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bbc.co.uk}}}\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cfull advertising across all BBC platforms\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ministers have also raised the prospect of introducing a \uc2 Netflix\uc2 -style \u8220\'20\'1Ctargeted top-up subscription service\u8221\'20\'1D which could be introduced for access to \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric BBC content\u8221\'20\'1D, or be rolled out more expansively to put \u8220\'20\'1Ccommercial programmes behind a paywall\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The government said:\par \par \uc2 However, the government admitted that there would be challenges determining which genres should continue to be offered outside a paywall, such as British dramas like Waterloo Road or major sporting events involving the home nations.\par \par \uc2 The government said that if it were to allow advertising to run across all BBC services, or introduce a broad subscription service, there would be a reduction in the licence fee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese options could further supplement BBC public funding to facilitate greater investment in content and/or enable the cost of the licence fee for households to be managed,\u8221\'20\'1D the government said.\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s income totalled \u163\'00\'A35.9bn last year but funding has fallen nearly 40% in real-terms since 2010, due to factors including several years of freezes or below inflation licence fee funding settlements, and being forced to take on costs for running the World Service.\par \par \uc2 A further 300,000 households stopped paying the licence fee last year - which along with payment evasion meant the corporation lost out on \u163\'00\'A31bn - and by the end of the decade the BBC will lose more than 1 million more paying households, according to the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAudiences now have much greater choice in what media they consume, and how and when they do so,\u8221\'20\'1D the government said, adding:\par \par \uc2 The government said that it will look at options to reform the \u163\'00\'A3174.50 licence fee to \u8220\'20\'1Cimprove its sustainability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear what price a subscription service would be set at, \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s cheapest package, which includes advertising, costs \u163\'00\'A35.99 a month.\par \par \uc2 10.27am GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/dec/16/uk-jobs-market-unemployment-rises-wage-growth-slows-us-payrolls-economics-business-live-news-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-694127108f08c2ef1a3c2f50#block-694127108f08c2ef1a3c2f50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 This morning\u8217\'20\'19s rebound in the UK PMI survey}}}\uc2  suggests the economy is enjoying a post-budget bounce, says Thomas Pugh, chief economist at audit, tax and consulting firm RSM UK.\par \par \uc2 But there is still a danger that the economy shrinks in the current quarter, he warns.\par \par \uc2 Pugh explains:\par \par \uc2 9.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK private sector growth accelerates as optimism rises after budget\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: UK business growth is accelerating this month, thanks to a rise in new businesses, as the uncertainty created in the build-up to November\u8217\'20\'19s budget fades.\par \par \uc2 The latest poll of UK purchasing managers shows that business activity growth across the UK private sector economy regained momentum, supported by the strongest upturn in new work since October 2024.\par \par \uc2 Data firm S&P Global reports that business activity accelerated in both the manufacturing and service sectors\par \par \uc2 This lifted its global flash UK PMI composite output index up to 52.1 in December, from 51.2 in November, which it says shows \u8220\'20\'1Ca moderate increase in output levels\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PMI\uc2  report also shows \u8220\'20\'1Ca modest recovery in business activity expectations for the year ahead\u8221\'20\'1D, with business optimism was the second-highest since October 2024.\par \par \uc2 Chris Williamson, chief business economist at \uc2 S&P Global Market Intelligence\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 However\u8230\'20\'26 today\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 PMI\uc2  report shows that firms continued to cut staffing numbers \u8211\'20\'13 employment decreased for the fifteenth successive month.\par \par \uc2 Williamson says firms are worried about rising staff costs:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Over in the eurozone, private sector growth has hit a three-month low.\par \par \uc2 Data provider S&P Global reports that its flash eurozone composite \uc2 PMI\uc2  output index has dropped to 51.9, down from November\u8217\'20\'19s 52.8.\par \par \uc2 More happily, any reading over 50 shows growth, and December\u8217\'20\'19s data shows that Eurozone business activity has completed a full calendar year of growth for the first time since the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 9.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 Oil hits seven-month low below $60 on Ukraine peace deal hopes\par \par \uc2 In the markets, hopes of a Ukraine peace deal have pushed the oil price down to its lowest level in seven months.\par \par \uc2 The price of a barrel of Brent crude has dropped by 1% to $59.90, its lowest since early May.\par \par \uc2 The drop comes after European leaders said they were ready to lead a \u8220\'20\'1Cmultinational force\u8221\'20\'1D in Ukraine as part of a US proposal for a peace agreement between Russia and Ukraine\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the leaders of the UK, France, Germany and eight other European countries said troops from a \u8220\'20\'1Ccoalition of the willing\u8221\'20\'1D with US support could \u8220\'20\'1Cassist in the regeneration of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s forces, in securing Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s skies, and in supporting safer seas, including through operating inside Ukraine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The proposal was part of a new package of security guarantees, backed by the White House, that could mark a breakthrough in reaching a peace deal between Moscow and Kyiv, US and European leaders have said.\par \par \uc2 But, significant differences remained over the future status of the Ukrainian territories occupied by Russia.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a peace deal has pushed down shares in defence companies this morning; BAESystems (-2.2%) are the top faller on the FTSE 100 share index.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Young people are being badly affected by the weaking jobs market, points out Stephen Evans, chief executive at Learning and Work Institute (L&W):\par \par \uc2 8.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Thursday interest rate cut 'highly likely' as jobs market slows\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report bolsters the case for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to cut interest rates on Thursday, and again in 2026, economists say.\par \par \uc2 With unemployment its highest in almost four years, and wage growth slowing quickly, the City is confident the Bank will cut interest rates from 4% to 3.75% at its final meeting of the year.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the Bank split 5-4 when it decided not to cut rates, so only one of those five policymakers need to switch\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Richard Carter, head of fixed interest research at \uc2 Quilter Cheviot\uc2 , says today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figures make a cut seem all the more likely.\par \par \uc2 ING\u8217\'20\'19s James Smith predicts a cut on Thursday, and two more next year:\par \par \uc2 Philip Shaw\uc2  of Investec points out that pay growth, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s favoured measure, slowed \u8211\'20\'13 with private sector earnings ex-bonuses slipping to 3.9% in the three months to October from 4.2% in the three months to September.\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at \uc2 XTB\uc2 , also cites the private sector wage growth figures as a reason to cut:\par \par \uc2 8.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 : Peak Budget uncertainty hits labour market\par \par \uc2 There are worrying signs in the labour market continue as we head towards Christmas, warns Sanjay Raja, chief uk economist at Deutsche Bank Research.\par \par \uc2 Raja says:\par \par \uc2 8.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 ING: Public sector employment has fallen for three consecutive months\par \par \uc2 ING\u8217\'20\'19s UK economist JamesSmith has spotted that government hiring is no longer supporting the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 He writes:\par \par \uc2 8.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Payrolls fall by 193,737 since Labour took power\par \par \uc2 Pat McFadden\uc2  may blame the jobs data on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe scale of the challenge\u8221\'20\'1D Labour inherited, but today\u8217\'20\'19s report also shows that payrolls have been shrinking since the government took over.\par \par \uc2 In July 2024, the number of payrolled employees peaked at 30,450,219.\par \par \uc2 The ONS estimates that in November, there were 30,256,482 \u8211\'20\'13 or 193,737 fewer than when \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  and \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  moved into Downing Street.\par \par \uc2 Hannah Slaughter, senior economist at the ResolutionFoundation, says policymakers must take action:\par \par \uc2 7.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 ICAEW\uc2 : Budget speculation and slumping economy hurt jobs market\par \par \uc2 Suren\par \par \uc2 Thiru, \uc2 ICAEW\uc2  economics director, warns that the UK jobs market faces \u8216\'20\'18a harsh winter\u8217\'20\'19, and blamed the flood of speculation ahead of last months\u8217\'20\'19s budget:\par \par \uc2 7.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 IoD\uc2 : It's the government's fault\par \par \uc2 The Institute of Directors has laid the blame for rising unemployment firmly at the government\u8217\'20\'19s door, calling today\u8217\'20\'19s labour market figures an indictment of its employment policies.\par \par \uc2 Alex Hall-Chen, principal policy advisor for employment at the Institute of Directors:\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, several business groups (not including the \uc2 IoD\uc2 ) urged Conservative peers to stop blocking Labour\u8217\'20\'19s workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights bill, following a deal under which new workers would get protection against unfair dismissal after six months, not from day one of employment as previously proposed.\par \par \uc2 Hall-Chen says more changes are needed:\par \par \uc2 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 McFadden: today\u8217\'20\'19s figures underline the scale of the challenge we\u8217\'20\'19ve inherited\par \par \uc2 The Work and Pensions Secretary has blamed the rise in unemployment on the \u8220\'20\'1Cscale of the challenge\u8221\'20\'1D Labour inherited when it took power 16 months ago.\par \par \uc2 Pat\par \par \uc2 McFadden said:\par \par \uc2 7.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 29,733 people at risk of redundancy in November\par \par \uc2 The spectre of redundancy is stalking the UK jobs market.\par \par \uc2 The ONS reports that 29,733 employees are at risk of redundancy, based on the HR1 firms which employers must file if they\u8217\'20\'19re planning to lay at least 20 staff off.\par \par \uc2 The number of people made redundant in August-October rose to to 5.3 per 1,000 employees, which is an increase on a quarterly basis and also over the last year\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Inflation continues to take a large bite out of pay growth.\par \par \uc2 Using the Consumer Prices Index, annual real regular pay growth (ie, excluding bonuses) was 0.9% in August to October 2025, which is slightly up on the previous three-month period (0.8%).\par \par \uc2 But annual real total pay growth (including bonuses) fell to 1.0% in August to October 2025, which is slightly down on the previous three-month period (1.1%). That\u8217\'20\'19s the lowest reading since May to July 2023.\par \par \uc2 7.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK unemployment rose by 158,000 in the August-October quarter, to a total of 1.832m, lifting the jobless rate to 5.1%.\par \par \uc2 The number of people in employment dipped by 16,000 in the quarter to 34.226m, which pushed the UK employment rate down to 74.9%.\par \par \uc2 7.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Public sector pay outstrips private sector\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report also shows the wages in the UK public sector are rising nearly twice as fast as in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Annual average regular earnings growth was 7.6% for the public sector and 3.9% for the private sector in August to October.\par \par \uc2 This is partly due too some public sector pay rises being paid earlier in 2025 than in 2024, causing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.investopedia.com/terms/b/base-effect.asp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a base effect}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ONS director of economic statistics \uc2 Liz McKeown\uc2  explains:\par \par \uc2 7.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK unemployment rate hits 5.1%\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Jobs reports from from both sides of the Atlantic are in focus today, as we get our final health check on the labour markets in the UK and the US.\par \par \uc2 And the breaking news is that Britain\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate has hit a new four-year high, as firms continue to cut jobs.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate has risen to 5.1% in the August-October period, up from 5% a month ago, to its highest level since the three months to January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rose, again, as the number of people on company payrolls fell; by 149,000 between October 2024 and October 2025, and by 22,000 in October alone.\par \par \uc2 The ONS also estimates that payrolled employees for November 2025 decreased by 171,000 on the year, and by 38,000 (0.1%) on the month, to 30.3 million.\par \par \uc2 And with the jobs market cooling, wage growth has slowed again too.\par \par \uc2 Average earnings, excluding bonuses, rose by 4.6% in the quarter, down from 4.7% a month ago. Total pay growth (including bonuses) slowed to 4.7% from 4.9%.\par \par \uc2 That weakening in pay growth may encourage the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to cut interest rates on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ONS director of economic statistics \uc2 Liz McKeown\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 Later today we\u8217\'20\'19ll finally get the delayed US non-farm payroll report for November, showing how many jobs were created last month. This report was held up by the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown, which also meant October\u8217\'20\'19s report was cancelled.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am GMT: UK labour market report\par \par \uc2 * 9am GMT: Eurozone flash \uc2 PMI\uc2  report for December\par \par \uc2 * 9.30am GMT: UK flash \uc2 PMI\uc2  report for December.\par \par \uc2 * 10am GMT: Eurozone trade balance report for October\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm GMT: US non-farm payroll jobs report for November\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm GMT: US retail sales for October\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251216elcg001me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
