\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 A hurricane destroyed their homes in Jamaica. Now they fear losing the jobs they rely on in the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 935 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2025\par 14:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of Jamaican workers who come to the US on an H-2A visa say they aren\u8217\'20\'19t sure if they\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to return from one year to the next\par \par \uc2 Farm worker Owen Salmon has picked apples in upstate New York for almost a decade, some 1,500 miles (2,400km) from home. In the midst of harvest season this year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/oct/28/hurricane-melissa-makes-landfall-in-jamaica"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hurricane Melissa}}}\uc2 , a record-breaking category 5 hurricane, made landfall in Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was terrifying,\u8221\'20\'1D said Salmon, whose wife and two children were at home near Black River, a town on the country\u8217\'20\'19s south-western coast. \u8220\'20\'1CFor days, I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t hear from them. When I finally did, I heard my roof was completely gone. My wife and kids had to run for their lives, but thank God they\u8217\'20\'19re alive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2025/nov/15/hurricane-melissa-a-real-time-case-study-of-colonialisms-legacies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  90,000 households and 360,000 people have been affected by the damage wrought by Hurricane Melissa in Jamaica. Salmon is one of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jaclo.org/jamaica-among-top-suppliers-of-english-speaking-farm-workers-to-the-us/#:~:text=Jamaica%20continues%20to%20lead,the%20programme%20in%20recent%20years."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roughly 5,000}}}\uc2  Jamaican workers who come to the US each year on an H-2A visa.\par \par \uc2 The H-2A guest worker visa program allows US farms and agricultural firms to hire foreign workers for temporary jobs. It plays a significant role in the US\u8217\'20\'19s agriculture industry, which remains reliant on overseas labor at crucial points on the calendar: last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://farmdocdaily.illinois.edu/2025/07/the-growing-role-of-h-2a-workers-in-us-agriculture.html#:~:text=The%20program%20plays%20a%20critical,%2C%20and%20Rutledge%2C%202024)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 380,000 workers}}}\uc2  were authorized for H-2A visas, about 15% of the US agricultural workforce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur boss, he would tell us he likes the H-2A program because you can be there this year and next year he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to bring us back,\u8221\'20\'1D said Salmon, who works at Wafler Farms in Wolcott, New York. \u8220\'20\'1CHe threatened us if we didn\u8217\'20\'19t work fast, and there was nobody to stop him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDozens of workers\u8221\'20\'1D who \u8220\'20\'1Cdid nothing wrong\u8221\'20\'1D have not been able to return from one year to the next, Salmon claimed. Wafler Farms denied this, claiming they have never not recalled workers but are \u8220\'20\'1Crequired to actively recruit US workers and are required to offer any positions to them before we hire a worker through the H-2A program\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Salmon is one of several New York farm workers represented by the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 , which has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/help-jamaican-farm-workers-rebuild-homes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched a fundraiser}}}\uc2  to rebuild their homes, and those of other union workers hit by the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI came home to see what was going on, and everything was gone. No house, nothing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Damian, a H-2A farm worker from Jamaica who has worked at Cahoon Farms in Wolcott, New York. Apprehensive about his US work status, he declined to provide his surname. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s something that took 20 to 30 years to build, and in just a few hours, it\u8217\'20\'19s gone now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A week before the storm, Damian\u8217\'20\'19s wife had a baby. He said he has struggled to care for his family since flying home in the aftermath of the hurricane. \u8220\'20\'1CHere, if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have a job, what are you supposed to do? I\u8217\'20\'19m just hoping for the best,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s terrible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Marley, who also declined to provide his surname, has worked at Cahoon Farms in New York since 2018, alongside Damian. He was there when the storm hit Jamaica, where his mother and children live.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was just hoping and praying that I could reach home before the storm,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marley. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I heard my home was destroyed, I was devastated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He has since made it back to Jamaica, but has been unable to get in touch with dozens of friends and co-workers.\par \par \uc2 Reports of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://radiojamaicanewsonline.com/local/reports-of-price-gouging-to-be-investigated-pm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 price gouging}}}\uc2  have materialized since the hurricane. Marley said his family has grappled with price hikes on food, with several staples such as flour still unavailable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not easy,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CMost of my friends, I can\u8217\'20\'19t get in touch with them, and I still feel for them. And I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to be comfortable till I hear from all of those guys.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Most of the roofs on homes in Salmon\u8217\'20\'19s community in the Black River area of Jamaica are gone, Salmon said. Building materials are expensive \u8211\'20\'13 and have soared in cost since the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Every year, he said, farm workers from Jamaica leave their homes and families to work on US farms, often for low wages and in poor working conditions.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Salmon and his co-workers unionized. A Wayne county judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fingerlakes1.com/2025/12/09/wayne-judge-backs-farmworkers-union-at-wafler-farms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently upheld}}}\uc2  the New York state labor board decision to certify the union, as the employer had argued that H-2A guest workers couldn\u8217\'20\'19t unionize while refusing to recognize the union.\par \par \uc2 Following the ruling, Wafler Farms has \u8220\'20\'1Cbegun coordinating with the UFW to move forward with the implementation of the bargaining agreement\u8221\'20\'1D, a spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRegarding Hurricane Melissa, we recognize the devastating impact it had on Jamaica and its people, with widespread damage to homes across the island,\u8221\'20\'1D they added. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the aftermath of the storm, Wafler Farms made donations to several reputable humanitarian organizations actively involved in housing recovery and community support.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe also shared information about these organizations with our workers so they could access support if they or their families were affected. We understand that some workers created a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  campaign upon returning to Jamaica to further assist with rebuilding efforts. We encourage anyone who wishes to contribute to do so through the avenue they feel will best support the recovery process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cahoon Farms did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251215elcf0030j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
