\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Most people aren\u8217\'20\'19t fretting about an AI bubble. What they fear is mass layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,475 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2025\par 13:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence could make income inequality even worse and create a new underclass. Governments and society must take action\par \par \uc2 Nowadays there seems to be nonstop discussion about AI, with much of the conversation focused on whether there\u8217\'20\'19s a speculative bubble or whether the chipmaker \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  is really worth $5tn or whether \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  will beat its rivals in developing new generations of artificial intelligence. But the vast majority of Americans \u8211\'20\'13 just like the vast majority of Europeans and Asians \u8211\'20\'13 couldn\u8217\'20\'19t care less about those things.\par \par \uc2 Their big concern is whether AI is going to cause huge layoffs and create a disastrous job market, especially for younger workers. \uc2 Dario Amodei\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 , a leading AI company, fed those fears when he said that AI could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wipe out half of all entry-level white-collar jobs}}}\uc2  in the next one to five years and increase unemployment in the US to 10% to 20%. In October, Bernie Sanders, the top Democrat on the Senate education and labor committee, issued a report saying AI and automation could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-releases-report-on-big-tech-oligarchs-war-against-workers-warns-ai-could-eliminate-nearly-100-million-u-s-jobs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 replace up to 97m jobs}}}\uc2  in the US over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 Such predictions fuel worries that AI will make today\u8217\'20\'19s enormous income inequality even worse as the already wealthy investors in AI grow even wealthier while millions of workers lose their jobs and perhaps form a new underclass struggling to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=McYBgZrORi4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent panel discussion}}}\uc2  I moderated, \uc2 Daron Acemoglu\uc2 , an MIT economist and winner of the 2024 Nobel prize in economic sciences, said there were essentially two routes for developing AI: an anti-worker route and a pro-worker route. He voiced dismay that tech companies were focused on the anti-worker route \u8211\'20\'13 a route that aims to develop AI in ways that maximize automation and maximize job reductions.\par \par \uc2 In that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=McYBgZrORi4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 panel discussion}}}\uc2  at the City University Graduate Center in New York, Acemoglu said AI could take \u8220\'20\'1Cvery different directions, and which direction we choose is going to have great consequences in terms of its labor market impact\u8221\'20\'1D. He said that today\u8217\'20\'19s AI \u8220\'20\'1Ccraze is really an automation agenda\u8221\'20\'1D which \u8220\'20\'1Cis going to eliminate more and more jobs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Acemoglu called for \u8220\'20\'1Ca different future\u8221\'20\'1D with \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-worker AI\u8221\'20\'1D. In his view, it would be far better if society and government could get tech companies to develop AI in ways that, instead of maximizing layoffs, increase workers\u8217\'20\'19 skills so that workers become more capable and valuable and employers are eager to keep them. That way AI would result in far fewer job losses.\par \par \uc2 Acemoglu said pro-worker AI would be far better for productivity, social cohesion and holding down income inequality. He acknowledged, however, that pro-worker AI is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot so good for the business models of the big tech companies\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 their models seek to maximize profits and automation.\par \par \uc2 To get AI companies to embrace a pro-worker approach will undoubtedly take considerable pressure from government and society. The Biden White House held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/readout-white-house-listening-session-with-union-leaders-advancing-responsible-artificial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discussions with labor leaders}}}\uc2  about how to make AI less harmful to workers. The Biden administration and various Biden-era agencies adopted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/protecting-and-empowering-workers-in-an-age-of-artificial-intelligence-lessons-from-the-biden-harris-administration/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several pro-worker AI policies}}}\uc2  (for instance, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMKF8Mukec-Vn_x1rfZNvfLNYiWaQSxp/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 limit harmful AI surveillance}}}\uc2 ), but his administration never issued recommendations or regulations to steer AI\u8217\'20\'19s future in a way that would minimize layoffs. Perhaps that would have happened in a second Biden term.\par \par \uc2 Three days after returning to the White House, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 with tech billionaires having helped finance his campaign and inauguration \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2025/01/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-takes-action-to-enhance-americas-ai-leadership/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rescinded Biden\u8217\'20\'19s modest efforts}}}\uc2  to make AI less harmful. Trump essentially gave the green light to AI companies to pursue whatever strategies they wanted, workers and the public be damned. On Thursday, he issued an executive order aiming to block any state laws restricting AI.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump administration has really changed the trajectory of the conversation about AI,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amanda Ballantyne, until recently the director of the AFL-CIO\u8217\'20\'19s Technology Institute. (The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  is the main US labor federation.) \u8220\'20\'1CI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t say we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a great trajectory right now for what AI will mean for workers. We\u8217\'20\'19re living through a live-action experiment as tech companies and employers try to figure out how to use this new technology, with many people concerned that it will be destabilizing and bad for workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It would be great if the path forward for AI could follow Germany and Scandinavia\u8217\'20\'19s model of having industry, labor and government work together to develop policies that help business and workers alike. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in new territory with AI technology,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ballantyne, now a senior fellow at New America, a progressive thinktank. \u8220\'20\'1CWe should take lessons from deindustrialization in the United States, where our anemic policy response to the negative effects of free trade left many working people behind. We can, and absolutely should, use the government to incentivize the development of pro-worker AI and smart regulations, as we\u8217\'20\'19ve done previous times in history.\u8221\'20\'1D Ballantyne pointed to Franklin Roosevelt\u8217\'20\'19s program that led to the electrification of hundreds of backwards rural communities, helping to improve living conditions and spur economic development.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D Ballantyne said, \u8220\'20\'1Cshould make this a core part of their platform, that it\u8217\'20\'19s possible to use government to create the kind of economy we want\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in this case, to incentivize tech companies to develop AI in more pro-worker, less destructive ways.\par \par \uc2 Four decades ago, Wassily Leontief, an \uc2 NYU\uc2  economics professor who won a Nobel prize for economics, hypothesized, somewhat playfully, about a future with so much automation that just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/2015/05/25/robots-coming-robots-coming/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one factory worker would be left}}}\uc2 , and that worker would turn a switch, and all the world\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturing would get done. Leontief worried that the factory owners would get all the benefits and workers would get pauperized.\par \par \uc2 He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://conversableeconomist.com/2016/08/22/automation-and-job-loss-leontief-in-1982/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked two crucial questions}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CWho will get the benefit? How will the income be distributed?\u8221\'20\'1D Those questions are arguably more relevant today.\par \par \uc2 With this in mind, the US and other wealthy nations should move quickly to adopt targeted policies that protect workers from the anticipated negative effects of AI, especially massive layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The government should arrange for greatly expanded efforts to retrain laid-off workers and get them back into the workforce \u8211\'20\'13 one option would be to make community colleges free. It would be terrible for those workers \u8211\'20\'13 and society \u8211\'20\'13 if AI results in millions of workers becoming unemployed for years.\par \par \uc2 If AI causes massive layoffs, that could mean that millions of workers and families lose a vital necessity: health coverage. With all the job churn AI is likely to cause, we should jettison our system in which workers receive health coverage through their job. We should instead embrace a system with guaranteed health coverage for everyone, perhaps in the form of Medicare for All.\par \par \uc2 As AI takes over more and more of workers\u8217\'20\'19 tasks, employers should move to a four-day workweek \u8211\'20\'13 with workers continuing to receive the same salaries \u8211\'20\'13 as a way to spread the work, reduce layoffs and give workers some of the gains from AI.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/universal-basic-income-ai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Some tech executives}}}\uc2  have called for universal basic income (of perhaps $1,000 a month) so that everyone receives a minimum income in case there are huge layoffs. Unfortunately, most UBI proposals would give an inadequate amount, perhaps $12,000 a year, to those most in need, while giving that same amount to tens of millions who are working and don\u8217\'20\'19t need it. To my mind, a more generous unemployment insurance system, with higher weekly benefits and more weeks of benefits, would be better and fairer than UBI.\par \par \uc2 All this will require higher taxes. As AI makes the very rich even richer \u8211\'20\'13 giving them even more money that they don\u8217\'20\'19t need and don\u8217\'20\'19t know what to do with \u8211\'20\'13 lawmakers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t hesitate to increase taxes on the ultra-rich to finance an improved safety net that includes universal health coverage, beefed-up retraining and expanded unemployment insurance.\par \par \uc2 Last but not least, we should ensure that workers have a voice in developing AI so it isn\u8217\'20\'19t focused exclusively on helping tech companies maximize profits, automation and layoffs. Biden was dead serious about giving workers a voice in shaping AI, but Trump seems to have little interest in that idea. The billionaires and tech bros who have his ear (and are helping finance his gilded ballroom) despise labor unions and aren\u8217\'20\'19t keen on giving workers an effective voice.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, once again, is there needs to be a bottom-up movement, this time to pressure lawmakers and tech companies to give workers a say in developing AI and to create a stronger safety net.\par \par \uc2 * Steven Greenhouse is a journalist and author, focusing on labour and the workplace, as well as economic and legal issues\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251212elcc0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
