\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 ICE is using smartwatches to track pregnant women, even during labor: \u8216\'20\'18She was so afraid they would take her baby\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Johana Bhuiyan \par \uc2 2,448 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2025\par 01:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pregnant immigrants in ICE monitoring programs are avoiding care, fearing detention during labor and delivery\par \par \uc2 In early September, a woman, nine months pregnant, walked into the emergency obstetrics unit of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2  hospital. Though the labor and delivery staff caring for her expected her to have a smooth delivery, her case presented complications almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 The woman, who was born in central Asia, checked into the hospital with a smartwatch on her wrist, said two hospital workers who cared for her during her labor, and whom the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  is not identifying to avoid exposing their hospital or patients to retaliation.\par \par \uc2 The device was not an ordinary smartwatch made by \uc2 Apple\uc2  or \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , but a special type that \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  (ICE) had mandated the woman wear at all times, allowing the agency to track her. The device was beeping when she entered the hospital, indicating she needed to charge it, and she worried that if the battery died, ICE agents would think she was trying to disappear, the hospital workers recalled. She told them that, just days earlier, she had been put on a deportation flight to Mexico, but the pilot refused to let her fly because she was so close to giving birth.\par \par \uc2 The woman\u8217\'20\'19s fear only grew from there, according to the hospital workers. Her delivery wasn\u8217\'20\'19t progressing the way the care team hoped, and she needed a C-section, a procedure that requires doctors to use a cauterizing tool to minimize bleeding. To prevent possible burning and electrocution, patients are instructed to take off all jewelry or metals before the surgery. The mandatory watch had no way to be easily removed, nor was information about whether it would be safe to wear during the procedure readily available. Hospital staff didn\u8217\'20\'19t know how to contact ICE to ask what to do. When hospital staff told the woman they might have to cut the smartwatch off, she panicked, the workers said.\par \par \uc2 Staff eventually did remove the device, and ICE agents did not show up at the hospital during the delivery. The nurses said they do not know what happened to the woman after she left the hospital with her baby.\par \par \uc2 The woman was one of three pregnant patients wearing a location-tracking smartwatch whom these two workers encountered in their ER in the last few months, they said.\par \par \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  and alternative to detention\par \par \uc2 The watches are built and operated by \uc2 BI Inc\uc2 , a company specializing in monitoring tech that runs the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s largest immigrant surveillance operation. The program, Alternative to Detention (ATD), allows select immigrants to await their day in court at home rather than in detention, provided they subscribe to intense monitoring.\par \par \uc2 When immigrants are enrolled in ATD, they are assigned one or more types of supervision. Some have to wear an ankle monitor, some a smartwatch. Some are required to complete regularly scheduled facial recognition scans at their home using a \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  app, while others are mandated to go into a \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  or ICE office for regular in-person check-ins.\par \par \uc2 The smartwatch, officially called the VeriWatch, was introduced two years ago by \uc2 BI Inc.\uc2  It was first piloted under the Biden administration and framed as a more discreet alternative to the less digitally equipped ankle monitor, which BI also manufactures and supplies to ICE. As the{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/08/us-immigrants-isap-ice-bi-ankle-monitor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Guardian previously reported}}}\uc2 , immigrants wearing the ankle monitors have complained about the stigma that comes with wearing the conspicuous device as well as physical pain caused by the monitors, including electric shocks and cuts from devices that are strapped on too tightly.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 200,000 people are currently enrolled in the program, and many of them have become increasingly fearful of being considered out of compliance as the Trump administration works to deport immigrants en masse. There have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/mercy-families-plead-migrants-arrested-routine-dhs-check/story?id=122528525"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several cases}}}\uc2  of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SPtJ7xmZbPw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people in the program}}}\uc2  showing up to a mandated, regular in-person check-in with immigration officials, believing they will continue in the ATD program, only to be detained.\par \par \uc2 All three women encountered by the Colorado hospital staff were reluctant to take their monitors off, fearing that doing so would trigger an alert to ICE or \uc2 BI Inc\uc2 , the staff said, even if removing the device was deemed medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 One of the women went into the ER for a C-section and was diagnosed with pre-eclampsia, a complication that can cause significant swelling. Staff were worried her smartwatch would cut off her circulation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe was in tears about it. She had this deep fear that ICE was going to come to the hospital and take her baby,\u8221\'20\'1D one of the staff said. The hospital worker\u8217\'20\'19s shift ended before the patient underwent the C-section. They said they do not know whether the staff who took over the patient\u8217\'20\'19s case convinced her to cut off the watch.\par \par \uc2 The confusion and fear surrounding the wrist monitor caused delays in the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s ability to provide adequate and necessary care for these women, the workers said, though the patients delivered their babies safely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWaiting and trying to figure these things out even when things are not super emergent can cause something emergent to happen,\u8221\'20\'1D one of the workers said. \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes in birth, doing a C-section 20 minutes before something bad happens can prevent it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The workers pointed out that when they treat patients wearing a monitor issued by the state department of corrections, there is a protocol in place to remove it before medical procedures.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s chaotic crackdown\par \par \uc2 Hospital staff from across the US who spoke to the Guardian say the confusion brought on by monitoring devices is just one of several ways Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration crackdown is affecting medical care, and comes as immigrant patients are increasingly fearful of seeking out treatment.\par \par \uc2 One of the staff at the Colorado hospital said she\u8217\'20\'19s had at least three pregnant patients show up for their first-ever prenatal appointment between 34 and 38 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 well into their third trimester and long after pregnant women are recommended to begin going to consistent doctor appointments.\par \par \uc2 In California, hospital workers have also noticed a drop this year in immigrants not just seeking emergency care but also showing up for regular doctor visits or vaccinations, according to the \uc2 California Nurses Association\uc2  president, Sandy Reding.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CObviously it has a cascading effect,\u8221\'20\'1D Reding said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t see your doctor regularly then the outcomes are worse and you wait until you have a crisis to go to the ER.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, CommunityHealth, one of the largest volunteer-based health centers in the country, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenationshealth.org/content/55/4/1.2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documented}}}\uc2  an overall drop in visits per patient and patient retention between 2024 and 2025 due to immigration enforcement activity in the city. In June, the organization observed a 30% dip in patients showing up for their appointments and around a 40% drop in patients picking up their medication since Trump took office.\par \par \uc2 Neither ICE nor \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  responded to requests for comment. ICE {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/08/us-immigrants-isap-ice-bi-ankle-monitor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previously}}}\uc2  told the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  that there is no evidence the ankle monitors have caused physical harm and that the ATD program was effective at increasing court appearance rates among immigrants facing removal.\par \par \uc2 Vague procedures, concrete problems\par \par \uc2 The lack of procedure to have ankle or wrist monitors removed in medical emergencies has affected more than just pregnant women. In one July 2025 case, ICE responded to a man\u8217\'20\'19s request to remove his ankle monitor because of a medical issue by detaining him, according to a court petition filed on his behalf by immigrant rights group Amica, which the Guardian reviewed.\par \par \uc2 The man came to the US from Bangladesh to seek political asylum, and was told he had to wear an ankle monitor while his claim was pending. Suffering from nerve damage in one leg, he obtained a note from a medical clinic requesting the monitor be removed. His lawyer sent the note to the ICE officer on the case but never heard back. During his first check-in at the BI offices, the man brought the medical note to the \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  employee assigned to the case, who suggested the man might be able to move the ankle monitor to his other leg. But after the man\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer called ICE to inquire about moving the ankle monitor, the BI case manager informed the man that ICE officers were coming to the BI office to speak with him. They arrested and detained the man, according to the petition.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe explained that he was just asking for the ankle monitor to be put on the other leg, and the officer told him it was \u8216\'20\'18too late\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D the petition reads.\par \par \uc2 In 2009, ICE {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ice.gov/doclib/foia/policy/memoGPS_MonitoringMedical_09.14.2009.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discontinued}}}\uc2  the use of ankle monitors for pregnant women and people whose medical conditions made it \u8220\'20\'1Cinappropriate\u8221\'20\'1D to wear them. But former \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  staff as well as immigrants rights groups Amica and \uc2 American Friends Service Committee\uc2  said they are concerned that these exceptions are not always enforced. That exception also doesn\u8217\'20\'19t apply to smartwatches, a June 2025 ICE memo shows.\par \par \uc2 The ICE memo instructs agency staffers to put ankle monitors on anyone enrolled in ATD. Dawnisha M Helland, an ICE acting assistant director in the management of non-detained immigrants, wrote that the only group who would not be given ankle monitors were pregnant women. Instead, pregnant{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/immigration/2025/07/24/ice-check-in-ankle-monitor-immigrants/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  women in ATD would wear the smartwatch}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though it resembles a typical consumer smartwatch, the VeriWatch is not less restrictive than the ankle monitor. Like the ankle monitor, the wrist watch can\u8217\'20\'19t be removed by the person wearing it. ICE had the option of using a removable version of the watch, according to a 2023{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sam.gov/opp/569cef7239f14fb7a7a4ca98559d3866/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  request for information}}}\uc2  DHS published. The agency chose a different direction; it currently only uses a watch that cannot be removed except by an ICE or authorized BI agent, according to two former DHS officials and two former BI employees.\par \par \uc2 Immigrants in the program are not told what to do with their ankle or wrist monitors in case of medical emergencies, and BI staff were not authorized to approve the removal of the monitors without first speaking to ICE, the two former \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  staff members recalled.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s not always time in emergency cases to wait for approval from ICE to cut off the monitors, the Colorado hospital workers said. One of the Colorado staff said they\u8217\'20\'19re deeply concerned about how this unremovable watch will continue to impact vulnerable pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re looking at people who literally can\u8217\'20\'19t speak up, who have no legal resources, who are not American citizens, and are pregnant. They\u8217\'20\'19re asking themselves what they can get away with in terms of violating civil liberties for these patients,\u8221\'20\'1D the employee said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the true pilot program: how far can they overreach?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Internal alarm\par \par \uc2 Healthcare workers are not the only ones sounding the alarm over surveillance\u8217\'20\'19s interference with medical care. Two former \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  officials told the Guardian that the lack of protocols for immigrants surveilled under ATD with exigent medical issues is a symptom of a larger issue with the way \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  and ICE run the program. As the Guardian {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/07/us-immigration-surveillance-ice-bi-isap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previously reported}}}\uc2 , immigrants surveilled under ATD and \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  employees alike have long complained that the program is highly discretionary. They said that many of the decisions about how, why or how long a given person was mandated to wear an ankle monitor or a smartwatch was left to individual caseworkers.\par \par \uc2 BI Inc\uc2 , which started off as a cattle monitoring company, and its parent company the \uc2 Geo Group\uc2 , which develops detention centers, private prisons and rehabilitation facilities, have been given the exclusive DHS contract to operate all aspects of the ATD program since its inception in 2004. That\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sam.gov/opp/569cef7239f14fb7a7a4ca98559d3866/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 despite previous attempts}}}\uc2  by ICE leadership under Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration to break the contract up into three parts rather than awarding the entirety of the contract to \uc2 Geo Group\uc2 , a company that has served as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pogo.org/investigations/private-prison-giant-hired-ice-detention-chief"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 landing spot}}}\uc2  for former ICE and DHS officials.\par \par \uc2 At its peak, \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  monitored approximately 370,000 immigrants under the Biden administration as part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/changing-border-policies-and-apprehensions-whats-the-relationship/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 policy}}}\uc2  that put every {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://immpolicytracking.org/policies/report-that-ice-ends-family-case-managment-program-a-detention-alternative-program/#/tab-policy-documents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 head of household}}}\uc2  crossing the border on ATD. The tally decreased in 2025 to about 180,000 people, due in part to high costs of putting so many people on ATD, former DHS officials said. As Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second administration supercharged immigration enforcement and greenlit a $150bn surge in funding for ICE, though, \uc2 Geo Group{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/feb/27/migrant-surveillance-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executives expressed}}}\uc2  confidence they could reach the same height by the second half of 2025. The goal, the executives have said, is to monitor all 7.5 million people listed on the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s non-detained docket, the list of non-citizens who have not been detained but are subject to removal.\par \par \uc2 However, the Trump administration has focused on deportation and detention rather than monitoring, and the number of immigrants enrolled in ATD and wearing ankle monitors or other GPS tracking devices has hovered around 180,000, much to the dismay of \uc2 Geo Group\uc2  executives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow the count has been fairly stable, which is a little disappointing, obviously,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 George Zoley\uc2 , the \uc2 GEO Group\uc2  founder and executive chair of the board, said during the company\u8217\'20\'19s November {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4840152-the-geo-group-inc-geo-q3-2025-earnings-call-transcript"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earnings call}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ICE awarded another two-year-contract to \uc2 BI Inc\uc2  to manage ATD in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://investors.geogroup.com/news-releases/news-release-details/geo-group-awarded-contract-us-immigration-and-customs-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September}}}\uc2 . Executives have said they\u8217\'20\'19re pleased that the agency is prioritizing using the company\u8217\'20\'19s more expensive ankle monitors on those immigrants already in ATD rather than the more cost-effective tools like the company\u8217\'20\'19s facial recognition app, Smart Link.\par \par \uc2 Under the Biden administration, several departments within DHS {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://purl.stanford.edu/gp176vn6331"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attempted to address the lack}}}\uc2  of consistent policy around how ICE should run ATD. In December 2022, DHS {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ice.gov/news/releases/ice-hosts-alternatives-detention-program-symposium"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hosted}}}\uc2  100 non-governmental organizations as well as members of academia and private industry to discuss how to bring more \u8220\'20\'1Cuniform standards to govern\u8221\'20\'1D ATD. That two-year effort to draft guidelines in a document, initially titled Non-Detained Management Standards, was ultimately scuttled by ICE and BI, said Scott Shuchart, a former assistant director for regulatory affairs and police at ICE under the Biden administration. Another former DHS official confirmed his account. The draft standards were never made public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe program is really structured for the benefit of BI and not for the benefit of the non-citizens who were going to be managed through it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shuchart. \u8220\'20\'1CTherefore ERO [ICE\u8217\'20\'19s enforcement and removal arm] was extremely resistant to bring rationalization and consistent policy into it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251210elca003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
