\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Liberal candidate denies law firm\u8217\'20\'19s claims of bullying and using staff to pursue political ambitions\b0\par\par\uc2 Nino Bucci and Benita Kolovos \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2025\par 23:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Grant Hutchinson denies allegations made in court by current and former partners prior to his preselection for Victorian seat of CroydonGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Liberal candidate for an ultra-marginal seat is in a legal fight with his own law firm, accused by his partners of bullying staff and using business resources to further his political ambitions.\par \par \uc2 Grant Hutchinson \u8211\'20\'13 who has denied the allegations against him \u8211\'20\'13 was preselected for the seat of Croydon in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s east last month. It is one of the Liberals\u8217\'20\'19 most marginal electorates, with a gap of only 1.2%, and the fate of such outer suburban seats will be vital to the Coalition at the November 2026 election.\par \par \uc2 It can be revealed that nearly six months before his preselection, Hutchinson and his wife won a supreme court injunction against a bid to remove them from Hutchinson Legal, which is run \u8220\'20\'1Cas a form of Christian ministry\u8221\'20\'1D, according to documents provided by the court to Guardian Australia.\par \par \uc2 That case triggered a counterclaim from current and former partners, who say the Hutchinsons should be terminated from the firmbecause of alleged breaches of the partnership agreement, including by \u8220\'20\'1Cengaging in another profession\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 They have accused him of several incidents of bullying \u8211\'20\'13 including allegedly threatening and detaining one of his lawyers \u8211\'20\'13 along with using the firm, its staff and a defamation case for political activities.\par \par \uc2 In court documents \u8211\'20\'13 filed before his preselection \u8211\'20\'13 it is claimed his conduct caused six people to leave the practice. One of those was a female lawyer who it is alleged made a complaint against Hutchinson, and has allegedly been off work for several months because of a workplace injury. In court documents Hutchinson denies the claims.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed&CMP=emailbutton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Sign up: AU Breaking News email }}}\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson is also accused of loudly striking an office window to get the lawyer\u8217\'20\'19s attention, rudely gesturing, and then using offensive and crass language towards her in October last year. Hutchinson says the pair had an interaction but otherwise denies the claim.\par \par \uc2 According to the counterclaim, during a meeting with the lawyer in January, Hutchinson questioned her about a complaint she made about the firm\u8217\'20\'19s workload and his \u8220\'20\'1Cavailability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At that meeting, he allegedly \u8220\'20\'1Cintimidated and threatened\u8221\'20\'1D her, deprived her of her liberty and \u8220\'20\'1Cfreedom to move\u8221\'20\'1D, and caused her to become fearful and distressed, according to court documents, which do not provide any further detail.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson denies this, saying only that the pair met and discussed the complaint.\par \par \uc2 In court documents, Hutchinson agrees that an internal complaint about that meeting was subsequently filed by the lawyer \u8211\'20\'13 who left work in March after filing a WorkCover application \u8211\'20\'13 but denies its accuracy or that there was a legitimate basis for it.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Political activity\u8217\'20\'19 denied\par \par \uc2 The counterclaim against Hutchinson makes accusations about his involvement with the Liberal party, with nine alleged examples since 2013 outlined. These are described as \u8220\'20\'1Cengaging in another profession\u8221\'20\'1D, in breach of the partnership agreement.\par \par \uc2 He is also accused of starting a defamation case in 2021 that was related to his political activity, but Hutchinson denies this.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson is alleged to have carried on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe political activity\u8221\'20\'1D by using the practice to meet people, using his work email, and using his personal assistant and other staff. He is also alleged to have been absent during work hours to \u8220\'20\'1Ccarry on the political activity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In court documents, Hutchinson does not admit he held the positions or engaged in the political activities outlined in the claim, and says they were not a \u8220\'20\'1Cprofession\u8221\'20\'1D. He also denies any of the firm\u8217\'20\'19s resources were used in this way.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson and Con Nottas, the firm\u8217\'20\'19s managing partner who is also one of the partners involved in the legal dispute, were both contacted for comment.\par \par \uc2 According to an order made by Justice Richard Attiwill last month, mediation in the matter must be completed by 20 March 2026, ahead of a directions hearing on 27 March.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia understands the Liberal party was aware of the case prior to Hutchinson\u8217\'20\'19s preselection, although a spokesperson declined to confirm this.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot comment on matters before the court,\u8221\'20\'1D the party spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 The current MP for Croydon, David Hodgett, is retiring. Hutchinson was formally endorsed by the party in late November.\par \par \uc2 Before his preselection, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/oct/04/victorian-liberal-party-reselections-unusual-step-extending-deadline-search-for-future-mps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Liberal party executive extended the preselection period}}}\uc2  to allow more candidates to come forward in four seats.\par \par \uc2 Croydon was considered \u8220\'20\'1Cwinnable\u8221\'20\'1D by Labor, with some party members encouraged by the results in the overlapping federal seat of Deakin, which they unexpectedly won in May.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251209elc9006el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
