\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not going anywhere\u8217\'20\'19: how unionization \u8216\'20\'18whirlwind\u8217\'20\'19 set stage for historic \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 2,365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2025\par 22:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Four years after workers at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  store in upstate New York became the first to unionize, hundreds of outlets followed \u8211\'20\'13 defying intense resistance from the coffee chain. What happened next?\par \par \uc2 Thousands of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas are on strike across the US, warning the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest coffee chain to brace for the \u8220\'20\'1Clongest and biggest\u8221\'20\'1D bout of industrial action in its history.\par \par \uc2 Barely a year after \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO, tried to draw a line under bitter divisions between its management and unionized workers, pledging to \u8220\'20\'1Cengage constructively\u8221\'20\'1D with them, the American coffee giant is now grappling with an escalating strike during its lucrative holiday trading season.\par \par \uc2 About 2,500 workers are striking across 85 cities and 120 stores \u8211\'20\'13 and urging customers to steer clear. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  claims less than 1% of its coffee houses have experienced disruption due to the industrial action.\par \par \uc2 But the union, Starbucks Workers United, which represents 11,000 baristas at more than 550 stores, is threatening to escalate the strike far beyond its current footprint unless executives make concessions during contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Four years after the first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 -owned US store voted to form a union, defying intense resistance from the company, relations between the two sides have deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s still shocking to me to wake up and have them every day still fighting us the way that they\u8217\'20\'19re fighting us,\u8221\'20\'1D Michelle Eisen, spokesperson for Starbucks Workers United, told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause we have proven time and time again that we\u8217\'20\'19re not going anywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 For decades, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , founded in the 1970s, and taken over in the 1980s by \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , who built it into the global coffee colossus it is today, successfully fought off unionization.\par \par \uc2 The chain dubbed its workers \u8220\'20\'1Cpartners\u8221\'20\'1D, and promoted a package of \u8220\'20\'1Cindustry-leading\u8221\'20\'1D benefits, including healthcare coverage and education costs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not an anti-union person. I am pro-\uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , pro-partner, pro-\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  culture,\u8221\'20\'1D Schultz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/04/09/starbucks-ceo-howard-schultz-has-history-of-opposing-unions.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told employees}}}\uc2  in 2022. \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t get here by having a union.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By then, however, cracks had already started to appear in the dam. The previous year baristas at a store in Buffalo, New York, voted 19-8 in favor of unionizing \u8211\'20\'13 setting the stage for hundreds of outlets across the US to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Eisen started working at the Buffalo store in August 2010, to supplement her job as a production stage manager, in between theater shows.\par \par \uc2 She was a longtime \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  customer, and trusted its reputation for treating workers well. And for years, Eisen felt valued as an employee, receiving regular wage increases and working around her production schedule.\par \par \uc2 But things started to change in about 2016, according to Eisen. \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw our benefits costs go up significantly. Those twice-a-year raises went away completely,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of a sudden, we were getting one cost of living raise at the beginning of the calendar year, which, most of the time, was significantly less than the raises previous to that. And that\u8217\'20\'19s really when we started to see the staffing levels slowly start to decline in these stores.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Four years later, things \u8220\'20\'1Creally, really, really came to a head\u8221\'20\'1D, Eisen said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores stayed open when Covid hit in 2020. Baristas were startled to encounter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/may/26/starbuck-employees-intense-work-customer-abuse-understaffing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increasingly aggressive and confrontational}}}\uc2  customers, and some workers felt their pay was too low for what they believed had become more intense work in deteriorating conditions.\par \par \uc2 Eisen, whose theater job was shut down due to the pandemic, took on as many hours as she could at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , but still struggled to make ends meet. After 11 years at the company, she was only making a few cents more an hour than a new hire. In 2021, she considered quitting.\par \par \uc2 But then she started speaking with co-workers about organizing a union. A group of about 50 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas in the Buffalo area {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/28/starbucks-workers-buffalo-ny-organizing-union-first-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went public}}}\uc2  with their plan in August 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo me, it was a no-brainer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eisen. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause it gave an opportunity to try to address some of these problems, and fix them from the inside. And I was hoping, beyond hope, that it would work, we would be successful, and I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to leave. Because I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  executives did not see the move as a no-brainer. Managers and corporate executives, including the chain\u8217\'20\'19s then North American president, \uc2 Rossann Williams\uc2 , and Schultz, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/nov/23/starbucks-aggressive-anti-union-effort-new-york-stores-organize"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 descended}}}\uc2  on Buffalo in an effort to stop the effort to unionize.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo partner [employee] has ever needed to have a representative seek to obtain things we all have as partners at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Schultz wrote in a letter to workers. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I am saddened and concerned to hear anyone thinks that is needed now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey waged an absolutely vicious union-busting campaign,\u8221\'20\'1D claimed Eisen. \u8220\'20\'1CThey still are, but it started there in Buffalo, from the very beginning. And against all odds, in spite of that, my store was able to win their union election on December 9, 2021 and became the first [unionized] store. And then it was kind of a whirlwind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Since that first victory, mobilizing workers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unionelections.org/data/starbucks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won more than 650 union elections}}}\uc2 , and lost about 120.\par \par \uc2 The chain has since moved to closed 59 unionized stores, according to Starbucks Workers United. Others are still awaiting for results to be certified.\par \par \uc2 As as a wave of mobilization started to sweep the firm\u8217\'20\'19s ranks in early 2022, its top tier was overhauled. Three months after the Buffalo vote, \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/mar/16/starbucks-howard-schultz-ceo-again-us-workers-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abruptly announced}}}\uc2  its CEO, \uc2 Kevin Johnson\uc2 , was standing down, and Schultz, its veteran boss, would return for a third stint in charge.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  were under pressure, costs were rising as Covid continued to disrupt supply chains, and \u8211\'20\'13 while the firm continued to generate billions of dollars in sales every quarter \u8211\'20\'13 customers were spending less time inside its stores.\par \par \uc2 Schultz was tasked with turning around a business many on Wall Street felt was losing its way. Many of his freshly unionized baristas agreed. But Schultz declined to work with Starbucks Workers United, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/live/2022/06/09/business/inflation-starbucks-guns-dealbook?smid=url-share#whats-happening-in-america-is-much-bigger-than-starbucks-chief-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declaring}}}\uc2  he would never embrace the union, and offering new benefits and pay increases to non-union workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was just infuriating that we were being bullied in this way, and made to feel like we were doing something wrong, because we were trying to hold the company accountable and trying to make them better,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eisen.\par \par \uc2 A new CEO, \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2 , was tapped later that year. Unionized baristas started to take action, including by demonstrating on the chain\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cred cup day\u8221\'20\'1D holiday in November 2022 and 2023.\par \par \uc2 Relations slowly improved. In early 2024, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Starbucks Workers United agreed a new framework for collective bargaining agreements, sparking hope of a first union contract by the end of that year. Workers at unionized stores were granted the benefits that had been given to non-union workers in 2022.\par \par \uc2 But the chain\u8217\'20\'19s business woes appeared to worsen, with competition mounting and footfall declining. Narasimhan was ousted after 16 months as CEO last year, and replaced by \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the Chipotle boss.\par \par \uc2 Bargaining stuttered to a halt following Niccol\u8217\'20\'19s arrival, according to the union. \u8220\'20\'1CThe company picked right back up where they left off at the end of 2023, when it came to violating workers rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eisen. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we still don\u8217\'20\'19t have a contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Niccol moved fast in an effort to turn around the chain, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/07/starbucks-brian-niccol-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launching}}}\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1CBack to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D campaign, aimed at reversing declining sales. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/21/starbucks-ceo-backlash-super-commute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced criticism}}}\uc2  after it emerged he would commute from his home in Newport Beach, California, to the firm\u8217\'20\'19s Seattle headquarters, rather than relocate.\par \par \uc2 Progress at the bargaining table, meanwhile, stalled. Niccol remained mum on the union, and its pursuit of a first union contract.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe changing of CEOs within the last five years alone has kind of shown that this company has a lot it\u8217\'20\'19s still figuring out,\u8221\'20\'1D Zarian Antonio Pouncy, who has worked for 11 years as a barista in Las Vegas, told the Guardian. His store unionized in late 2023.\par \par \uc2 Pouncy said: \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of listening to the partners \u8211\'20\'13 listening to those on the forefront, dealing with the business every day \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re relying too much on leadership in their opinion, and AI, algorithms and numbers, to kind of show them how business works.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Julie Su has been watching closely. As US deputy secretary of labor, and acting labor secretary, between 2021 and 2025, she helped oversee the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to reinvigorate the US labor movement \u8211\'20\'13 as thousands of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas voted to unionize.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen workers choose a union, they deserve a contract,\u8221\'20\'1D Su told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CToo often, it takes a long time to get one because the employer uses delay as a weapon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe time to a first contract is used to punish workers for unionizing, to undermine the effort, or to send a message that there is no benefit to joining a union because nothing changes at work for years. Usually all of the above. This is unacceptable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On average, it takes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/bloomberg-law-analysis/analysis-now-it-takes-465-days-to-sign-a-unions-first-contract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 15 months}}}\uc2  for a union to secure a first contract with an employer, often due to delays and obstruction by management. At \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , nearly 48 months have passed since the Buffalo store voted to unionize.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe other way this operates as a specific tool for union-busting in the case of a company like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is the company tries to wait out its employees, hoping that they leave before a contract is reached,\u8221\'20\'1D added Su.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/28/will-starbucks-union-busting-stifle-a-union-rebirth-in-the-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2 hundreds of unfair labor practice (ULP) charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . Dozens of union leaders have been fired, allegedly in retaliation for their union support. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  denied this in all cases, although several workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/03/starbucks-unions-fired-workers-new-york-city-labor-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won reinstatements}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tensions simmered. Starbucks Workers United claimed baristas at several hundred stores walked off the job last December. And in April, union delegates voted to reject a contract proposal from the company which would have guaranteed annual raises of at least 2%, dismissing the increase as \u8220\'20\'1Cnot good enough\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 On 13 November \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1CRed Cup Day\u8221\'20\'1D, inside the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  empire \u8211\'20\'13 more than a thousand US workers walked off the job, demanding the chain present a \u8220\'20\'1Cfair\u8221\'20\'1D contract. The chain played down the strike\u8217\'20\'19s impact.\par \par \uc2 The union escalated. A week later, the action was expanded to 65 cities and 95 stores, with 2,000 workers on strike. Starbucks Workers United also staged a blockade of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 largest distribution center on the US east coast, in York, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 On 24 November, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers rallied outside the company\u8217\'20\'19s corporate office in Newport Beach, Niccol\u8217\'20\'19s part-time base, to demand the company finish the contract.\par \par \uc2 On 28 November, the strike was expanded further. About 2,500 baristas across 120 stores are now involved, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe company has been stonewalling us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Diego Franco, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista for six years in Chicago. \u8220\'20\'1CMy store chose to go out on Red Cup Day, the busiest sales day for the company, because we were frankly fed up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t expect us to continue to come into work, to continue to push policies and procedures that don\u8217\'20\'19t actually fix any of the problems that we face at work \u8211\'20\'13 not just for my co-workers, but for the customers and our regulars. I would much rather stand outside in the cold and be on strike than know that I\u8217\'20\'19m showing up to work where I\u8217\'20\'19m constantly being disrespected by our CEO, and by upper management.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Contacted for comment, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  played down the impact of the strike so far, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.starbucks.com/press/2025/a-record-setting-start-to-the-holidays/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  to have seen record holiday sales so far this year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we\u8217\'20\'19ve said, 99% of our 17,000 US locations remain open and welcoming customers \u8211\'20\'13 including many the union publicly stated would strike but never closed or have since reopened,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jaci Anderson, a spokesperson for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CRegardless of the union\u8217\'20\'19s plans, we do not anticipate any meaningful disruption.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the union is ready to return to the bargaining table, we\u8217\'20\'19re ready to talk. The facts are clear: \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  offers the best job in retail, with pay and benefits averaging $30 per hour for hourly partners. People choose to work here and stay here \u8211\'20\'13 our turnover is less than half the industry average, and we receive more than a million job applications every year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A growing number of progressive political leaders are unpersuaded by such reassurances. More than 100 members of Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laborcaucus.house.gov/media/in-the-news/more-than-100-lawmakers-push-starbucks-to-resume-union-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  letters demanding \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  return to negotiations with the union and finish the contract.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile workers are on strike, I won\u8217\'20\'19t be buying any \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , and I\u8217\'20\'19m asking you to join us,\u8221\'20\'1D New York\u8217\'20\'19s mayor-elect, Zohran Mamdani, wrote on social media. His transition co-chair, Lina Khan, and incoming first deputy mayor Dean Fuleihan have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/ZohranKMamdani/posts/my-transition-co-chair-lina-khan-and-first-deputy-mayor-dean-fuleihan-joined-sta/1233828831889266/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stood}}}\uc2  on picket lines. Mamdani appeared on a picket line with Senator Bernie Sanders on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Seattle\u8217\'20\'19s mayor-elect, Katie Wilson, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3XzEg9Astkw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared}}}\uc2  on a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  picket line hours after her acceptance speech, where she told workers and supporters: \u8220\'20\'1CBaristas are the heart and soul of this company, and they deserve better than empty promises and corporate union-busting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Eisen left \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in May, after 15 years. She currently serves as the principal spokesperson for Starbucks Workers United.\par \par \uc2 She said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis company is being run into the ground, and these unionized workers are the only ones standing up and saying, \u8216\'20\'18Hey, what are you doing? Our cafes are not places that people want to come spend time any more. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to come in and spend their money here because of the way you are running this business. You have to start investing in us if you want to see this company turned around.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers are done, and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to escalate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to be on the unfair labor practice strike, and they are prepared to make this the longest and biggest strike in company history if the company doesn\u8217\'20\'19t return to resolve these remaining issues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251202elc20030f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
