\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 More than 1,000 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers warn rapid AI rollout threatens jobs and climate\b0\par\par\uc2 Varsha Bansal \par \uc2 1,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2025\par 13:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers say the firm\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18warp-speed\u8217\'20\'19 approach fuels pressure, layoffs and rising emissions\par \par \uc2 More than 1,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  employees have signed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazonclimatejustice.org/open-letter?ms=nai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an open letter}}}\uc2  expressing \u8220\'20\'1Cserious concerns\u8221\'20\'1D about AI development, saying that the company\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Call-costs justified, warp speed\u8221\'20\'1D approachto the powerful technology will cause damage to \u8220\'20\'1Cdemocracy, to our jobs, and to the earth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The letter, published on Wednesday, was signed by the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers anonymously, and comes a month after \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2025/10/28/business/amazon-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass layoff}}}\uc2  plans as it increases adoption of AI in its operations.\par \par \uc2 Among the signatories are staffers in a range of positions, including engineers, product managers and warehouse associates.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting broader AI concerns across the industry, the letter was also supported by more than 2,400 workers from companies including \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The letter contains a range of demands for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , concerning its impact on the workplace and the environment. Staffers are calling on the company to power all its data centers with clean energy, make sure its AI-powered products and services do not enable \u8220\'20\'1Cviolence, surveillance and mass deportation\u8221\'20\'1D, and form a working group comprised of non-managers\u8220\'20\'1Cthat will have significant ownership over org-level goals and how or if AI should be used in their orgs, how or if AI-related layoffs or headcount freezes are implemented, and how to mitigate or minimize the collateral effects of AI use, such as environmental impact\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The letter was organized by employees affiliated with theadvocacy groupAmazon Employees for Climate Justice. One worker who was involved in drafting the letter explained that workers were compelled to speak out because of negative experiences with using AI tools in the workplace, as well as broader environmental concerns about the AI boom. The staffers, the employee said, wanted to advocate for a better way to develop, deploy and use the technology.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI signed the letter because of leadership\u8217\'20\'19s increasing emphasis on arbitrary productivity metrics and quotas, using AI as justification to push myself and my colleagues to work longer hours and push out more projects on tighter deadlines,\u8221\'20\'1D said a senior software engineer, who has been with the company for over a decade, and requested anonymity due to fear of reprisal.\par \par \uc2 Climate goals\par \par \uc2 The letter accuses \uc2 Amazon\uc2  of \u8220\'20\'1Ccasting aside its climate goals to build AI\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Like other companies in the generative AI race, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has invested heavily in building new data centers to power new tools \u8211\'20\'13 which are more resource intensive and demand high amounts of electricity to operate. The company plans to spend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/professional/insights/data/amazon-bets-150-billion-on-data-centers-required-for-ai-boom/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $150bn on data centers in the next 15 years}}}\uc2 , and just recently said it will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-15-billion-indiana-data-centers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invest $15bn}}}\uc2  to build data centers in northern Indiana and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/amazon-3-billion-mississippi-data-center-investment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least $3bn for data centers in Mississippi}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The letter claims that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual emissions have \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazonclimatejustice.org/unsustainability-report.pdf?ms=cjp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grown roughly 35%}}}\uc2  since 2019\u8221\'20\'1D, despite the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sustainability.aboutamazon.com/climate-solutions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 company\u8217\'20\'19s promise}}}\uc2  in 2019 to achieve net zero carbon emissions by 2040. It warns many of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s investments in AI infrastructure will be in \u8220\'20\'1Clocations where their energy demands will force utility companies to keep coal plans online or build new gas plants\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18AI\u8217\'20\'19 is being used as a magic word that is code for less worker power, hoarding of more resources, and making an uninformed gamble on high energy demand computer chips magically saving us from climate change,\u8221\'20\'1D said an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  customer researcher, who requested anonymity out of fear of retaliation for speaking out. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we can build a climate saving AI \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s awesome! But that\u8217\'20\'19s not what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is spending billions of dollars to develop. They are investing fossil fuel energy draining data centers for AI that is intended to surveil, exploit, and squeeze every extra cent out of customers, communities, and government agencies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson Brad Glasser pushed back on employees\u8217\'20\'19 claims and pointed toward the company\u8217\'20\'19s climate goals. \u8220\'20\'1CNot only are we the leading data center operator in efficiency, we\u8217\'20\'19re the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest corporate purchaser of renewable energy for five consecutive years with over 600 projects globally,\u8221\'20\'1D said Glasser. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve also invested significantly in nuclear energy through existing plants and new SMR technology\u8211\'20\'13these aren\u8217\'20\'19t distractions, they\u8217\'20\'19re concrete actions demonstrating real progress toward our Climate Pledge commitment to reach net-zero carbon across our global operations by 2040.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 AI for productivity\par \par \uc2 The letter also includes strict demands around the role of AI in the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workplace, demands that, staffers say, arose out of challenges employees are experiencing.\par \par \uc2 Three \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees who spoke to the Guardian claimed that the company is pressuring them to use AI tools for productivity, in an effort to increase output. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m getting messaging from my direct manager and [from] of all the way up the chain, about how I should be using AI for coding, for writing, for basically all of my day-to-day tasks, and that those will make me more efficient, and also that if I don\u8217\'20\'19t get on board and use them, that I\u8217\'20\'19m going to fall behind, that it\u8217\'20\'19s sort of sink or swim,\u8221\'20\'1D said a software engineer who has been with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for over two years, requesting anonymity due to fear of reprisal.\par \par \uc2 The worker added that just weeks ago she was told by her manager that they were \u8220\'20\'1Cexpected to do twice as much work because of AI tools\u8221\'20\'1D, and expressed concern that the output expected demanded with fewer people is unsustainable, and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe tools are just not making up that gap.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The customer researcher echoed similar concerns. \u8220\'20\'1CI have both personally felt the pressure to use AI in my role, and hear from so many of my colleagues they are under the same pressure \u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll the while, there\u8217\'20\'19s no discussion about the immediate effects on us as workers \u8211\'20\'13 from unprecedented layoffs to unrealistic expectations for output.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The senior software engineer said that the adoption of AI has had imperfect outcomes. He said that most commonly, workers are pressured to adopt agentic code generation tools: \u8220\'20\'1CRecently I worked on a project that was just cleaning up after a high-level engineer tried to use AI to generate code to complete a complex project,\u8221\'20\'1D said this worker. \u8220\'20\'1CBut none of it worked and he didn\u8217\'20\'19t understand why \u8211\'20\'13 starting from scratch would have actually been easier.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  did not respond to questions about the staffers\u8217\'20\'19 workplace critiques about AI use.\par \par \uc2 Workers emphasized they are not against AI outright, rather they want it to be developed sustainably and with input from the people building and using it. \u8220\'20\'1CI see \uc2 Amazon\uc2  using AI to justify a power grab over community resources like water and energy, but also over its own workers, who are increasingly subject to surveillance, work speedups, and implicit threats of layoffs,\u8221\'20\'1D said the senior software engineer. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a culture of fear around openly discussing the drawbacks of AI at work, and one thing the letter is setting out to accomplish is to show our colleagues that many of us feel this way and that another path is possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251128elbs003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
