\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Claims of systemic problems with BBC news coverage disputed by former adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Savage Media editor \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2025\par 06:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Caroline Daniel tells MPs leaked memo by Michael Prescott that sparked resignations at BBC did not provide full picture\par \par \uc2 Claims of \u8220\'20\'1Cserious and systemic problems\u8221\'20\'1D in the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s coverage of issues including Donald Trump, Gaza and trans issues \u8211\'20\'13 which led to the resignation of its director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have been disputed by a former adviser to the corporation.\par \par \uc2 Deborah Turness, the head of \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , also resigned after the allegations by Michael Prescott, a PR executive and former independent external adviser to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editorial guidelines and standards committee (EGSC).\par \par \uc2 Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s memo, sent to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s board and eventually leaked to the Daily Telegraph, contained a series of claims of bias in relation to issues including Trump and racial diversity.\par \par \uc2 He claimed the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s leaders had ignored his concerns or not properly followed through on mistakes.\par \par \uc2 However, speaking to the Commons culture, media and sport committee, an adviser who served alongside Prescott as an external EGSC adviser said Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s view did not provide the full picture and was his \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal account\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Caroline Daniel, a PR executive and former journalist, said she believed the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Ctook issues of impartiality extremely seriously\u8221\'20\'1D throughout the period cited by Prescott.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a healthy debate and also a very robust debate,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in my view, was the BBC willing to have a proper conversation, debate and actually take action? In my view, yes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Also appearing before MPs, Prescott said he did not believe the BBC was \u8220\'20\'1Cinstitutionally biased\u8221\'20\'1D, but said he believed the BBC repeatedly failed to properly follow up issues raised at the EGSC.\par \par \uc2 However, Daniel disagreed. \u8220\'20\'1CThe real issue is the culture of the BBC in terms of the level of attention paid to these issues,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view, the fact that we had a robust committee to debate issues of impartiality \u8230\'20\'26 was actually really significant. I think there are few organisations when you actually have that level of auditing internally on a regular basis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She also disputed the account of the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s US election coverage portrayed in Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s memo. \u8220\'20\'1CMichael\u8217\'20\'19s account is a personal account rather than a comprehensive review of everything that was covered in the committee,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Prescott said his memo was a \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable edit\u8221\'20\'1D of concerns raised, but conceded it was \u8220\'20\'1Cunbalanced\u8221\'20\'1D because it did not include examples of where an internal review had praised the coverage.\par \par \uc2 Claims of political pressure over the memo from within the BBC have focused attention on \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 , the board member who was Theresa May\u8217\'20\'19s communications chief in Downing Street.\par \par \uc2 Appearing before the committee, Gibb said he had no intention of resigning, despite BBC staff and some MPs calling for him to leave the corporation because of his political links. He said Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s memo had \u8220\'20\'1Cbeen weaponised and I have been a victim of that weaponisation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Gibb ridiculed the idea of \u8220\'20\'1Ca coup\u8221\'20\'1D from the right. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is up there as one of the most ridiculous charges,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s complete nonsense. It\u8217\'20\'19s also deeply offensive to fellow board members.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone who knows me knows that I\u8217\'20\'19m hugely impartial,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI have friends across the political divide. I have impartiality through my bones.\u8221\'20\'1D He said he did not think the BBC was institutionally biased.\par \par \uc2 Prescott confirmed he was friends with Gibb and that Gibb had had a role in his appointment, but added that the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s HR department was also involved. He said his memo was all his own work, though based on research by the EGSC\u8217\'20\'19s adviser, the longtime BBC journalist David Grossman.\par \par \uc2 Prescott insisted he had no political bias, as MPs suggested all his criticisms appeared to come from the right. He said he was a \u8220\'20\'1Ccentrist dad\u8221\'20\'1D and that he was \u8220\'20\'1Cno soul mate\u8221\'20\'1D of Gibb.\par \par \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair, also came under pressure for his own role in the crisis, with accusations that delays in the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s response left the corporation open to attack.\par \par \uc2 He said he had wanted to make sure the board was united and that they were right in understanding the issues. Shah defended Gibb, saying he was interested in accuracy and impartiality. He also said he tried to stop Davie resigning.\par \par \uc2 The most serious issue to emerge from Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s memo was an accusation that the BBC had selectively edited a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  speech to make it appear clearer that he encouraged the US Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 Prescott said that President Trump was \u8220\'20\'1Cprobably not\u8221\'20\'1D defamed by the Panorama edit, over which the president is threatening to sue.\par \par \uc2 Shah and Caroline Thomson, another BBC board member appearing before MPs, said Turness and \uc2 BBC News\uc2  wanted to apologise for the edit. However, they said they were among the board members who wanted \uc2 BBC News\uc2  to go further in stating the show was misleading about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s role in the Capitol riots.\par \par \uc2 Shah also rejected allegations from a fellow board member, who has since resigned, that he did not properly consult all board members in the run-up to the resignations of Davie and Turness. Shah said he called tech investor \uc2 Shumeet Banerji\uc2  on the weekend of the resignations to discuss the situation.\par \par \uc2 Thomson said Shah had \u8220\'20\'1Cthe unanimous support\u8221\'20\'1D of the current board.\par \par \uc2 But speaking later on the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s World Tonight, committee chair Caroline Dinenage was critical of Shah\u8217\'20\'19s evidence, saying that she and her fellow MPs were not \u8220\'20\'1Cwildly enthused that the board is in safe hands\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dinenage, a Conservative, added that Shah\u8217\'20\'19s evidence was \u8220\'20\'1Cwishy-washy\u8221\'20\'1D and said: \u8220\'20\'1CThere wasn\u8217\'20\'19t a huge sense that there was grip at the heart of the BBC governance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shah confirmed the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s report that the BBC wants its new director general to have a deputy responsible for its journalism, to make it a more manageable job.\par \par \uc2 The BBC also confirmed it was reviewing how the EGSC works and its composition. Some BBC insiders believe Gibb had too much power on the body, which recently had only four members.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251124elbo005v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
