\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy added more jobs than forecast in September, after shock losses in August \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 3,896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par 06:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US non-farm payrolls rose by 119,000 in September, but new data also shows economy shed jobs in August\par \par \uc2 6.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Update: the relief rally did not last.\par \par \uc2 Fears of a growing bubble around the artificial intelligence frenzy resurfaced by the close of trading on Thursday as leading US stock markets fell.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark S&P 500 closed down 1.6%, and the Dow Jones industrial average closed down 0.8% in New York. The tech-focused Nasdaq Composite closed down 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 3.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Global stock markets are rising after the US economy added more jobs than forecast in September, and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  beat Wall Street expectations.\par \par \uc2 The US market is sharply higher, with the DowJonesindustrialaverage now up 692 points or 1.5% at 46,830.\par \par \uc2 Investors pushed the market higher after new data showed the US jobs market added 119,000 jobs in September.\par \par \uc2 Amid heightened uncertainty surrounding the strength of the US economy, the much-anticipated reading was higher than the 51,000 jobs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://groundworkcollaborative.org/news/some-thoughts-on-the-labor-market/#:~:text=As%20Kolko%20convincingly%20shows%2C%20this,breakthrough%20is%20coming%20anytime%20soon."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected}}}\uc2  by analysts to be added in September.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, meanwhile, ticked up from 4.3% to 4.4%: its highest level since 2021. And a previous estimate for jobs growth in August was downgraded \u8211\'20\'13 revealing an unexpected decline in the US workforce.\par \par \uc2 Stocks had already rallied across Asia-Pacific markets overnight, and in Europe. In London, the FTSE 100 is now up 83 points or 0.9% at 9591 points.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  are up 4% after the chipmaker dismissed fears of an AI bubble, beat forecasts for sales growth, and gave an upbeat outlook.\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Jensen Huang\uc2  insisted that the artificial intelligence transformation had legs, telling analysts:\par \par \uc2 2.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  shares rally after results\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  have jumped by 3.9% in early trading, to $193.83, after beating Wall Street forecasts last night.\par \par \uc2 As covered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/20/markets-rally-nvidia-results-ai-bubble-us-jobs-report-ftse-pound-dollar-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-691eb6628f0888d27c1da9b4#block-691eb6628f0888d27c1da9b4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the introduction}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  also predicted continued strong sales growth.\par \par \uc2 Jensen Huang\uc2 , founder and CEO of Nvida, told analysts last night:\par \par \uc2 2.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street rallies after jobs report and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  results\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has jumped at the start of trading, as traders hail \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s strong results last night and today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks are rallying strongly, after \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  cooled fears of a bursting AI bubble by beating expectations last night.\par \par \uc2 The news that more jobs were added across America in September than expected has also bolstered confidence about the health of the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Reuters helpfully has the details:\par \par \uc2 2.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Isaac Stell, investment manager at Wealth Club, is also confident the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  won\u8217\'20\'19t deliver a pre-Christmas rate cut:\par \par \uc2 2.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Earnings growth slows\par \par \uc2 Average earnings growth slowed in September, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows, in a blow to US workers.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls rose by 9 cents, or 0.2%, to $36.67 in September, down from 0.3% growth in August.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 2.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at \uc2 Principal Asset Management\uc2 , says today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report is \u8220\'20\'1Cunlikely to tip the balance to a December cut\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Shah explains:\par \par \uc2 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Market expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates next month are rising, reports \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB.\par \par \uc2 A cut still isn\u8217\'20\'19t seen as likely, though.\par \par \uc2 Brooks explains:\par \par \uc2 1.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Some more timely employment data has also been released, showing that the number of Americans filing new applications for unemployment benefits fell last week.\par \par \uc2 The number of fresh \u8216\'20\'18initial claims\u8217\'20\'19 for state unemployment benefits dropped 8,000 to 220,000 in the week ending on 15 November, the Labor Department has reported.\par \par \uc2 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Lindsay James, investment strategist at \uc2 Quilter\uc2 , says today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report is better than expected, meaning it is unlikely to prompt the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  into cutting interest rates in December.\par \par \uc2 James says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  has confirmed that the October print will not be released as the data collection did not take place during the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 That suggests today\u8217\'20\'19s release will be the final official labour market update before the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s meeting on 10 December.\par \par \uc2 1.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US unemployment rate rises to 4.4%\par \par \uc2 The jobless rate across the US has risen higher than expected too.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows the unemployment rate rose to 4.4% in September, up from 4.3% in August.\par \par \uc2 The number of people classed as unemployed rose to 7.603m, up from 7.384m in August.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 1.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US September jobs report beats forecasts, August a shocker though\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added more jobs than forecast in September, as America\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market picked up after a summer lull.\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s official employment report, delayed since the start of October by the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown, shows that nonfarm payroll employment rose by 119,000 in September.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s more than twice as many jobs as expected, thanks to gains in health care, food services and drinking places, and social assistance. Job losses occurred in transportation and warehousing and in federal government, though.\par \par \uc2 But there\u8217\'20\'19s bad news too. The jobs reports from July and August have been revised down, to show that the US economy actually lost jobs in August (!).\par \par \uc2 The BureauforLaborStatistics explains:\par \par \uc2 1.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The market\u8217\'20\'19s focus today is on the September payroll report, says Mohit Kumar of investment bank Jefferies:\par \par \uc2 1.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US jobs report: a preamble\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re about to, finally, get some official insight into how the US jobs market fared at the end of the summer.\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll is due to be released at the bottom of the hour, almost seven weeks late, due to the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Economists have predicted that the report will show that hiring picked up after a slowdown in August. Payroll growth is forecast to have risen to around 50,000 in September, more than doubling the August increase of just 22,000.\par \par \uc2 The health of the US labor market is a key factor determining whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut US interest rates next month.\par \par \uc2 So while the data is rather stale, it is also important for the markets.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Innes, managing partner at SPI Asset Management, explains:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US retail giant \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has raised its annual forecasts for the second time this year, and reported another strong quarter of online sales.\par \par \uc2 The company reported growth in US comparable sales, which includes online and stores, of 4.5% for the August through October period, above estimates for 3.8% growth.\par \par \uc2 It is now forecasting that annual net sales to rise 4.8% to 5.1%, up from a prior target of 3.75% to 4.75%.\par \par \uc2 12.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2 : AI capex to almost double to $4.7trn\par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence spending commitments could almost double, compared to existing plans, over the next five years, Swiss bank \uc2 UBS\uc2  has forecast.\par \par \uc2 In its 2026 outlook, released this morning, \uc2 UBS\uc2  predicts that AI capital expenditure (capex) will remain a driver of near-term growth in the markets, pointing to the opportunities in both \u8216\'20\'18agentic\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18physical\u8217\'20\'19 AI.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  predicts that $4.7trn will be spent on global AI capex between 2026 and 2030, with $2.4trn already planned based on more than 40 announcements disclosed this year alone.\par \par \uc2 It expects $571bn of this spending to come in 2026 (up from a previous forecast of \u163\'00\'A3500bn).\par \par \uc2 For momentum to continue, tech leaders and investors must believe future demand justifies today\u8217\'20\'19s significant investments, \uc2 UBS\uc2  point out, adding:\par \par \uc2 11.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK factory output slumps\par \par \uc2 Just in: Output at UK factories has fallen at the fastest pace since August 2020 over the last quarter, a new poll has found.\par \par \uc2 The CBI\u8217\'20\'19s latest Industrial Trends Survey (ITS) had found that manufacturing output volumes fell in the three months to November, with bosses forecasting a similar decline over the next three months.\par \par \uc2 The poll, which measures how many factories reported higher sales vs lower, found:\par \par \uc2 * Output volumes fell at an accelerated pace in the three months to November (weighted balance of -30%, from -16% in the quarter to October), which saw the sharpest decline since the three months to August 2020. Manufacturers expect output volumes to fall at a similar pace in the three months to February (-30%).\par \par \uc2 The last three months includes the shutdown at \uc2 Jaguar Land Rover\uc2  in September, which pulled down UK growth that month.\par \par \uc2 Total order books were stable at a historically weak level in November, the CBI adds, with export order books still well below average.\par \par \uc2 Ben Jones\uc2 , CBI lead economist, says:\par \par \uc2 10.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a chart showing how the FTSE 100 share index has broken out of a five-day losing run this morning:\par \par \uc2 10.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 CLA president blasts Labour's rural economic policy\par \par \uc2 Gavin Lane, the new president of the \uc2 Country, Land and Business Association\uc2 , has delivered a blistering attack on Labour\u8217\'20\'19s rural economic policy, as Emma Reynolds the Defra secretary watches on. She looks unimpressed, my colleague Helena Horton reports from the CLA\u8217\'20\'19s annual conference.\par \par \uc2 Reynolds is due to speak but is not taking questions from the press or live questions from the audience. Defra requested the CLA send her questions from members in advance, presumably so officials could help her write answers to them.\par \par \uc2 Lane said of the changes to inheritance tax which mean a large number of farmers will find it difficult to hand down their businesses to their children:\par \par \uc2 He accused Labour of having \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomic theories about wealth inequality without grasping the breaking up family businesses can destroy the very stability that we need to solve national challenges.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lane said Labour was pursuing business consolidation and courting big international companies rather than supporting small and medium-sized businesses which have seen increased taxes and costs over the past year. Unemployment is up, he added, and investment is down in the rural economy. He added that farmers are needed to meet Labour\u8217\'20\'19s targets to build reservoirs and clean energy such as solar farms and onshore wind.\par \par \uc2 He added:\par \par \uc2 Lane said that farmers and landowners are not faceless corporations and that the people in the room \u8220\'20\'1Clive above the shop\u8221\'20\'1D and care about their local communities.\par \par \uc2 Lane added:\par \par \uc2 10.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dr Martens to raise US prices due to Trump tariffs\par \par \uc2 Dr Martens is set to put prices up on some items in the US in January as the British bootmaker attempts to offset the impact of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s import tariffs which it said amounted to between \u163\'00\'A37m and \u163\'00\'A39m.\par \par \uc2 The company is also shifting production for US-destined boots and shoes from Laos to Vietnam where the tariff rate is 20% - half that in its neighbouring country. Such measures are expected to mitigate half the impact of the tariffs by 2027.\par \par \uc2 Ije Nwokorie\uc2 , the chief executive of DrMartens, told \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  that DrMartens had not put prices up in the US for three years and it would be ensuring it \u8220\'20\'1Cmaintains competitive advantage\u8221\'20\'1D there with careful adjustments on certain lines.\par \par \uc2 He added that the broadening of Dr Marten\u8217\'20\'19s product assortment - with more shoes, and new boot styles including a new welly ready for next year\u8217\'20\'19s festival season, had helped give a kick to wholesale sales in the US - which rose for the first time in several years in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 Nwokorie said the UK was not trading significantly differently to other European markets at present with the consumer \u8220\'20\'1Ccautious right now\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Clooking for deals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said DrMarten\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to reduce discounting in a quite promotional environment had affected the sales line but it was benefiting from a \u8220\'20\'1Cflight to quality\u8221\'20\'1D and people trading down from luxury brands with shoe sales up 33%. \u8220\'20\'1CThe consumer is still buying products that meet their needs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 9.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Chris Beauchamp\uc2 , chief market analyst at IG, reports that \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s results have steadied market sentiment, while also giving bearishly-inclined investors something to get their teeth into.\par \par \uc2 He writes:\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Burry (famously portrayed in The Big Short) has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0XyGQ8FjuE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted a chart}}}\uc2  showing all the interlinked deals between AI players:\par \par \uc2 9.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Brazil denies planning to poison \uc2 EU\par \par \uc2 Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the \uc2 EU\uc2  has hit out the \u8220\'20\'1Cfake news\u8221\'20\'1D about meat and other food from Latin American countries, saying nobody is trying to \u8220\'20\'1Cpoison\u8221\'20\'1D citizens of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  under the recent trade deal with Mercosur countries that continues to be opposed by farmers.\par \par \uc2 Pedro Miguel da Costa e Silva told a trade conference at the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  that half of his country\u8217\'20\'19s exports to the EU are coffee and soy, neither products produced locally, which go on to support a lot of business in the EU.\par \par \uc2 He said there was also much disinformation about Brazil and other countries in Mercosur such as Argentina flooding Europe with food.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 9.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 China: \uc2 Nexperia\uc2  row not fully resolved\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Chinese ministry of commerce\uc2  has said the dispute over the supply of chips from \uc2 Nexperia\uc2 , the Dutch-based Chinese-owed company, is still not fully resolved.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is still a gap to completely solve the problem,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 Chinese ministry of commerce\uc2  (MOFCOM) said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Nexperia\uc2  has been at the heart of a global slowdown of chip supply after the Dutch government effectively took control of the company in the EU amid concerns the company was moving its intellectual and physical assets to China.\par \par \uc2 MOFCOM said it hopes to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinue to see sincere cooperation\u8221\'20\'1D and an \u8220\'20\'1Cearly settlement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The car manufacturers\u8217\'20\'19 trade body in the EU, \uc2 ACEA\uc2 , yesterday said that although the supply of chips had been restored following Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s decision to lift a ban on chips, supplies remained \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan O\u8217\'20\'19Riordan, \uc2 ACEA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s director of international trade, said a \u8220\'20\'1Cbridging agreement\u8221\'20\'1D was still needed to secure long term supply to the sector, which a few weeks ago said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cdays away\u8221\'20\'1D from halting production.\par \par \uc2 Nexperia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s wafers need input from both the EU and China with the Netherlands producing the wafers which are then sent to the parent plants in China for finishing and global export.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday the Dutch economy minister VincentKarremans said they would lift the order which imposed controls on \uc2 Nexperia\uc2  in the EU as a \u8220\'20\'1Cgesture of goodwill\u8221\'20\'1D to the Chinese. But Wingtech, the \uc2 Nexperia\uc2  Chinese, hit out demanding it permanently rescind the order that placed it under state control and insisting it had done nothing to warrant the Dutch action.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Wingtech said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 9.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Johnson Matthey\uc2  urges Reeves not to increase costs for businesses\par \par \uc2 The boss of FTSE 250 metals group \uc2 Johnson Matthey\uc2  has said that chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  should hold off increasing costs for British businesses ahead of next Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive LiamCondon said that cost inflation was still a major issue for British industry, but that the government should also stick with plans to bring in new renewable energy supplies. He said:\par \par \uc2 Johnson\par \par \uc2 Matthey is investing in a new refinery to recycle platinum group metals in Royston, Hertfordshire. The company is negotiating with the \uc2 UK government\uc2  to qualify for discounts on electricity bills that are reserved for energy-intensive industries. The biggest electricity users receive a 90% discount on extra fees to the grid - paid by other users including households - under the British Industry Supercharger, but it is limited to companies in specific industries.\par \par \uc2 Johnson\par \par \uc2 Matthey revealed a 38% increased in underlying profit this morning, excluding a catalyst technologies business it is selling to US conglomerate \uc2 Honeywell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s main business, producing catalytic converters for petrol and diesel engines, has benefited from a slower-than-expected switch to battery electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Condon said that changes to US and EU rules were \u8220\'20\'1Creally only catching up to the reality of the market\u8221\'20\'1D. He said that consumers had been unwilling to bear \u8220\'20\'1Chorrendous prices\u8221\'20\'1D for electric vehicles, but they were now \u8220\'20\'1Cgetting to the right price point\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 European stock markets are rising across the board, as \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s results brighten the mood.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the situation:\par \par \uc2 * UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100: up 72 points at 9,579, up 0.77%\par \par \uc2 * Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX: up 241 points at 23,402, up 1%\par \par \uc2 * France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC: up 90 points at 8,044, up 1.1%\par \par \uc2 * Spain\u8217\'20\'19s IBEX: up 143 points at 16,031, up 0.9%\par \par \uc2 * Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSEMIB: up 541 points at 43,192, up 1.25%\par \par \uc2 Matt Britzman, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says risk appetite has raced back:\par \par \uc2 8.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D: era of secure global supply chains is over\par \par \uc2 European trade commissioner \uc2 Maro\u353\'01\'61 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D\uc2  has said the decades-old global trading system with secure global supply chains is over.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the most recent battle between the EU and China over the supply of chips for the auto industry, he told a conference in Brussels \u8220\'20\'1Ceverything could be weaponised\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trade he said is the \u8220\'20\'1Cnew tool\u8221\'20\'1D in the trade wars now causing geopolitical waves between China and the US, Europe and the US with import and export bans being imposed at will by Beijing.\par \par \uc2 A \u8220\'20\'1Cwell-functioning \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D with reforms was vital for the future of free trade, he added.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 8.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Games Workshop\uc2  shares surge after trading update\par \par \uc2 Tabletop gaming company \uc2 Games Workshop\uc2  has muscled its way to the top of the FTSE 100 share index, after predicting sales and profits will be higher over the last six months.\par \par \uc2 In a brief trading update, GamesWorkshop told the City:\par \par \uc2 It also declared a dividend of \u163\'00\'A31.00 per share taking dividends declared so far in 2025/26 to \u163\'00\'A33.25 per share (up from \u163\'00\'A31.85 a year ago).\par \par \uc2 This has given GamesWorkshop\u8217\'20\'19s shares a boost \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re up 10% today at \u163\'00\'A3177, nearly a year after being promoted to the FTSE100 share index.\par \par \uc2 Fun fact, they were just \u163\'00\'A331 in January 2019, when my then-colleague Alex Hern wrote the definitive explanation of how, and why, interest in Warhammer 40,000 was booming:\par \par \uc2 8.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 jumps after \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  results\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  relief rally has reached London.\par \par \uc2 After falling for the last five sessions, stocks are higher in early trading. The FTSE 100 share index has gained 56 points, or 0.6%, at the start of trading, to 9564 points.\par \par \uc2 Technology investors are leading the rally, with PolarCapitalTechnologyTrust up 3.45% and ScottishMortgageInvestmentTrust gaining 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s another sign that \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s earnings have reassured investors.\par \par \uc2 Michael Brown\uc2 , senior research strategist at brokerage Pepperstone, says:\par \par \uc2 7.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 JD Sports\uc2  says profits will be at lower end of expectations\par \par \uc2 In the City this morning, retailer \uc2 JD Sports\uc2  has not matched \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s cheery outlook.\par \par \uc2 JD Sports\uc2  has told investors that profits this year will come in towards the lower end of current market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The sports and outdoor wear retailer says it is \u8220\'20\'1Ctaking a pragmatic approach\u8221\'20\'1D to its outlook for this financial year, due to \u8220\'20\'1Cincrementally weaker macro and consumer indicators in recent weeks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 R\u233\'00\'E9gis Schultz\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 JD Sports Fashion\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: \u8203\'20\'0BThe UK was the worst performing market,\uc2 JD Group\uc2  added, as it cautioned over \u8220\'20\'1Cpressures on our core customer demographic, including rising unemployment levels, as well as near-term volatility around consumer sentiment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sales at established stores in the UK slid 3.3% in the three months to 1 November, but sales were also down in the US and EU - by 1.7% and 1.1% respectively - amid similar pressures there as well as a lack of new product launches to draw in the shoppers and the slowdown in the trend for women\u8217\'20\'19s vintage trainers.\par \par \uc2 Aarin Chiekrie, equity analyst at broker HargreavesLansdown said:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 7.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 : \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  results have completely changed the market mood\par \par \uc2 After several weak trading sessions, \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s results have changed the mood in the markets, says Jim Reid, market strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 He told clients this morning:\par \par \uc2 7.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific stocks soar on \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  relief\par \par \uc2 A relief rally has swept across Asian markets today after \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s market-topping earnings cheered investors.\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO JensonHuang\u8217\'20\'19s upbeat forecasts about the future of the AI also helped to send shares roaring higher in Japan, where the Nikkei index is up 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s KOSPI index is up 2%, while Taiwan\u8217\'20\'19s TW50 index has jumped by 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at \uc2 Swissquote\uc2 , says \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  has delivered another set of impressive \u8211\'20\'13 and record-breaking \u8211\'20\'13 results, adding:\par \par \uc2 7.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  shrugs off AI bubble fears\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s one risk event down, one to go, for investors today after \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  calmed nerves with some sizzling financial results.\par \par \uc2 The chipmaker at the heart of the artificial intelligence boom calmed fears of a bursting bubble \u8211\'20\'13 and pushed markets higher \u8211\'20\'13 by beating Wall Street forecasts, and giving a strong forecasts for its future performance.\par \par \uc2 Jensen Huang\uc2 , founder and CEO of Nvida, tried to squish bubble fears, declaring that \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve entered the virtuous cycle of AI\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Huang told analysts last night:\par \par \uc2 Sales are up 62% year-over-year, reflecting the massive demand for its chips to power AI systems. The company reported $51.2bn in revenue fromdata-center sales, beating expectations of $49bn.\par \par \uc2 And crucially for market sentiment, \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  sees faster growth than expected. It is projecting fourth- quarter revenue of around $65bn; analysts had predicted the company would issue guidance of $61bn.\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s shares jumped 5% in after-hours trading. Kyle Rodda, senior financial market analyst at capital.com, calls the results \u8220\'20\'1Cpractically spotless\u8221\'20\'1D, explaining:\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s strong results may calm anxiety that the valuations of companies in the AI revolution have risen dangerously high, leaving the markets vulnerable to a crash. Those worries had heightened after two major investors - SoftBank and PeterThiel \u8211\'20\'13 recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/nov/11/softbank-sells-stake-in-nvidia-as-it-doubles-down-on-openai-bets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sold their stakes in Nvidia}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific markets have rallied today (more on that shortly), and European bourses are set to open higher.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 The second fear which hit share prices in recent days is that US central bankers may not cut interest rates as quickly as hoped.\par \par \uc2 The long-awaited US jobs report for September is finally due to be released today, and should give insight into whether the labour market has continued to cool.\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s Non-Farm Payrolls report is expected to show a rise of 50,000 jobs with the unemployment rate remaining at 4.3%. A weak reading might nudge the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  towards a December rate cut\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 9.30am GMT: ONS data on young people not in education employment or training\par \par \uc2 * 10a, GMT: Eurozone construction data for September\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm GMT: US non-farm payroll report for September\par \par \uc2 * 3pm: US home sales data for October\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251120elbk001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
