\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Klarna\uc2  says AI drive has helped halve staff numbers and boost pay\b0\par\par\uc2 Kalyeena Makortoff Banking correspondent \par \uc2 483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2025\par 18:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 31\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Buy now, pay later firm says pay has risen by 60% with staff numbers mostly cut by attrition and tech investment\par \par \uc2 Klarna\uc2  has claimed that AI-related savings have allowed the buy now, pay later company to increase staff salaries by nearly 60%, but hinted it could slash more jobs after nearly halving its workforce over the past three years.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive \uc2 Sebastian Siemiatkowski\uc2  said headcount had dropped from 5,527 to 2,907 since 2022, mostly as a result of natural attrition, with departing staff replaced by technology rather than by new staff members.\par \par \uc2 The figures add to the impact of an internal artificial intelligence programme, which had steadily reduced its use of outsourced workers including those in customer service, with technology now carrying out the work of 853 full-time staff, up from 700 earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 It meant the company, which was founded in Sweden in 2005, had managed to increase revenues by 108% while keeping operating costs flat. Siemiatkowski told analysts on an earnings call on Tuesday that it was \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty remarkable, and unheard of as a number, among businesses\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He explained that \uc2 Klarna\uc2  has not hired \u8220\'20\'1Cfor a few years\u8221\'20\'1D. However, some of the resulting cost-savings had been used to increase pay for remaining staff, with average compensation \u8211\'20\'13 including employee-related taxes and pension contributions \u8211\'20\'13 rising by 60% over the past three years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have made a commitment to our employees that all of these efficiency gains, and especially the applications of AI, should also, to some degree, come back in their pay cheques so that they are fully \u8230\'20\'26 incentivised [and] aligned with the investors, to drive these changes through the company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Average compensation for each employee has jumped from $126,000 (\u163\'00\'A396,000) in 2022 to $203,000 today, \uc2 Klarna\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Siemiatkowski, who is a shareholder in a number of AI firms including \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Perplexity\uc2  through his family investment firm Flat Capital, said he hoped to continue increasing a metric measuring revenue per employee, suggesting a further reduction in staff numbers in the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re now at $1.1m per employee, and we hope to continue to do that acceleration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Siemiatkowski warned this week against costly investments in datacentres to power AI, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/61ce397b-968c-403e-aecf-16d4b2ff08cd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 telling the Financial Times}}}\uc2  that he expected the technology would become more efficient over time.\par \par \uc2 The comments came as \uc2 Klarna\uc2  reported a 26% jump in revenues in the three months to the end of September to $903m, beating analysts\u8217\'20\'19 expectations of $882m.\par \par \uc2 But the Swedish business reported a $95m loss over the period, significantly higher than the $4m loss last year. \uc2 Klarna\uc2  said this was primarily driven by changes to accounting standards that it had to follow in the US, after its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/13/klarna-chooses-new-york-over-london-ipo-by-now-pay-later-flotation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision to list its shares on the New York stock exchange in September.}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251118elbi0050p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
