\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Florida workers with criminal records trapped by temp agencies, report finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2025\par 18:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former prisoners feel \u8216\'20\'18disposable\u8217\'20\'19 after being driven to accept jobs that often pay lower wages and have no benefits\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/florida"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Floridian}}}\uc2  workers with criminal records are driven to accept jobs at temporary staffing agencies, often with lower wages and no benefits, according to a new report.\par \par \uc2 Former prisoners described feeling \u8220\'20\'1Cdisposable\u8221\'20\'1D after finding themselves stuck in temporary jobs, unable to obtain direct employment with companies.\par \par \uc2 The temp industry has become \u8220\'20\'1Cthe default entry point after incarceration\u8221\'20\'1D, Beyond the Bars, a non-profit organization advocating for workers with criminal records, said.\par \par \uc2 More than 70% of individuals coming home from prison in south Florida seek employment through a temp agency at some point within three years, according to field research published in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/60f035bf2ee5504277e4be9d/t/691756c5d3191b4af6748064/1763137239421/The+Temp+Trap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Temp Trap}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some 57% of respondents were unable to find a full-time job paying the minimum wage within a year of their release.\par \par \uc2 Temp agencies and labor pools are often the only employers willing to hire workers with criminal records and provide the flexibility needed to accommodate state or county supervision, curfews, and mandatory parole or court appointments.\par \par \uc2 These agencies profit by charging an employer a fee, while assuming the worker\u8217\'20\'19s compensation and other risks and liabilities. They also often charge steep placement fees that serve as barriers for temp workers being hired permanently.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are warehouses in Florida that are entirely staffed by temp workers. The management is hired by the host employer. The entire line staff is temped out,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maya Ragsdale, co-executive director of Beyond The Bars, and co-author of the report. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat it really does is it suppresses wages across the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers at temp agencies in construction earn about $6.47 less per hour, and temp workers in warehouses about $3.38 less per hour than permanent direct hires, the report said: equivalent to $13,458 and $7,030 in lost income per year, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Around 6.2 million people in Florida \u8211\'20\'13 about 36% of the state\u8217\'20\'19s population \u8211\'20\'13 have a criminal record. Over 164,000 people in Florida are under some form of state supervision, not including county supervision. On any given day, 157,000 people are incarcerated in Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state prisons and another 55,763 people are incarcerated in Florida county jails.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBefore I went to jail, I was a manager at \uc2 Office Depot\uc2 . When I came out, I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get the kind of jobs I had before,\u8221\'20\'1D Felix, a member of Beyond the Bars, who said received a packet listing temp agencies before they were released, told the authors of the report. \u8220\'20\'1CProbation required a paycheck, and I had court fees due. With better jobs closed off because of my record, I ended up at a temp and labor pool \u8211\'20\'13 doing construction, which I\u8217\'20\'19d never done before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter incarceration, I was placed as a temp on a construction site and ended up working for the same company for nine years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cam. \u8220\'20\'1CI did the same job as direct employees, sometimes even more, but because of my record, they never offered me a permanent position. No benefits, no security \u8211\'20\'13 just years of labor, only to be treated like I was disposable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A 2022 survey of temp workers by the National Employment Law Project found 24% reported their employers engaged in some form of wage theft, 17% reported experiencing a work-related injury or illness, and 41% reported that they personally covered their own healthcare costs. In addition, 71% of temp workers reported experiencing some form of retaliation for raising workplace issues with management.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the Bars is calling for changes to probation requirements; more stringent standards and regulations of temp agencies and host employers; improved protections and organizing rights for workers at temp agencies; expansion of training programs, apprenticeships, and union jobs; and a crackdown on abuse of subsidies of these temp systems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things that has been really challenging for members of our organization is that labor pools kind of pop up and close very regularly. We have done a survey of all the temp agencies in south Florida last year, and then this year, we took that same list and looked through it again, and only like 50% of them are still open,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ragsdale. \u8220\'20\'1CI think one of the key reasons is the fact they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to avoid their insurance rates going up when there\u8217\'20\'19s worker complaints.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251118elbi002jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
