\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Red cup rebellion\u8217\'20\'19: striking \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas urge customers to stay away\b0\par\par\uc2 Marina Dunbar in New York \par \uc2 863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par 16:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Brooklyn, workers walked off the job in search of fair pay and conditions \u8211\'20\'13 and said many customers were in support\par \par \uc2 At a popular \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Brooklyn\u8217\'20\'19s Clinton Hill, hundreds of people \u8211\'20\'13 including workers, union allies and community supporters \u8211\'20\'13 filled the sidewalks. In 40F (4.4C) weather, picketers held signs, marched, and chanted \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s disgusting? Union-busting!\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CNo contract, no coffee!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than a thousand \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers across the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/nov/13/starbucks-workers-launch-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked off}}}\uc2  the job on Thursday in over 40 cities, marking one of the largest coordinated actions yet by the rapidly growing union movement inside the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest coffee chain.\par \par \uc2 The strike, timed to coincide with the company\u8217\'20\'19s lucrative \u8220\'20\'1Cred cup day\u8221\'20\'1D festivities, is designed to pressure \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  back to the bargaining table after months of stalled contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 In Clinton Hill, many potential customers who stopped by for coffee were successfully deterred, choosing instead to support the strike. Those who chose to enter anyway were met with boos.\par \par \uc2 Kaari Harsila, a 21-year-old shift supervisor at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and one of the lead organizers of the rally, said the strike was among their biggest actions yet. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re changing red cup day into the red cup rebellion to show \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  that we are serious about our demands,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Workers were heartened by the response. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we have had a great turnout,\u8221\'20\'1D said Harsila. \u8220\'20\'1CI am honestly so impressed by all of the support that we\u8217\'20\'19re getting from the community and from people that come here and we are able to turn away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than half of potential customers, she estimated, refused to cross the picket line. Many of those protesting, she explained, were \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, joined by \u8220\'20\'1Ca bunch of allies that have come out from other unions\u8221\'20\'1D and local supporters.\par \par \uc2 Inside the store, however, Harsila said \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had brought in managers and higher-level supervisors to keep operations running. \u8220\'20\'1CThey brought in the regional and the district manager to work because they are scared. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to close it down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jacob Muldoon, 25, who previously worked at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and now works at the delivery giant UPS, said he came because he knows firsthand what a union can deliver. \u8220\'20\'1CI used to be a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  worker as well \u8230\'20\'26 and I really understood those conditions, those early mornings, and that bad pay,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI know what a good union contract does. I saw my pay go up $8.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Muldoon said the benefits he now receives at UPS, including free healthcare, could transform life for baristas. \u8220\'20\'1CI hope that they get the same kind of benefits like that, especially now with everything becoming so expensive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s looking good out here,\u8221\'20\'1D said Edwin Augustly, a 50-year-old member of Local 79 who currently works at John F Kennedy airport. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty empty in the store right now. I live in the neighborhood, and this \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is pretty much always full. And today it\u8217\'20\'19s not like that at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New York assembly member Claire Valdez, who previously chaired \uc2 UAW\uc2  Local 2110 at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , told workersthat their struggle resonated far beyond \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have lost count of the number of times I\u8217\'20\'19ve been in a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  picket line, and I\u8217\'20\'19m never not proud to do it,\u8221\'20\'1D Valdez said. She praised baristas for fighting not only for themselves, but broader causes. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you fight for your trans co-workers to have healthcare, that is everyone\u8217\'20\'19s fight. When you organize for Palestinian human rights, that is everyone\u8217\'20\'19s fight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , spoke about the struggle for fair pay. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy is it that these gazillionaires think it is OK to nickel-and-dime baristas?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to make sure that the people who do the work to make America what it is get treated with respect and dignity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United announced last week that workers had voted to authorize an open-ended unfair labor practice (ULP) strike. The union had spent months demanding that managers consider new proposals to improve staffing and pay, and resolve hundreds of ULP charges filed by the union against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  throughout its organizing campaign.\par \par \uc2 Since 2021, more than 650 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores have unionized, despite pushback from management. Contract talks broke down earlier this year, after the union rejected \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 economic proposals.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointed\u8221\'20\'1D that Workers United had voted to strike, rather than continue bargaining, but insisted the \u8220\'20\'1Cvast majority\u8221\'20\'1D of stores would be unaffected by the action.\par \par \uc2 In Brooklyn, however, business certainly did seem affected, at least when it comes to the number of customers walking into the store on Lafayette Avenue. For Harsila, seeing people choose to turn away from their daily \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to support the union was inspiring, a sign of a successful strike.\par \par \uc2 With three nearby stores already closed, the store had been \u8220\'20\'1Cvery busy\u8221\'20\'1D in recent months, she said, but not today. \u8220\'20\'1CI am so happy to say that it has been a lot slower than it has been in the previous days.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251114elbe003v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
