\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 MPs and BBC staff call for \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  to leave broadcaster\u8217\'20\'19s board\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Savage Media editor \par \uc2 776 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par 20:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s appointee is accused of pushing claims of institutional bias at the corporation after shock resignations\par \par \uc2 MPs and BBC staff members have called for \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  to be removed from the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s board as outgoing director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  hit out at the \u8220\'20\'1Cweaponisation\u8221\'20\'1D of criticisms of the broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 In an online meeting with Davie, staff questioned the position of Gibb, Theresa May\u8217\'20\'19s former communications chief who was appointed during Boris Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s time as prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Several said Gibb and all political appointees should be removed from the body. It has been claimed that Gibb pushed accusations of institutional bias that preceded the shock resignation of Davie and Deborah Turness, the head of \uc2 BBC News\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Their departures over the weekend followed accusations of bias made in a memo by Michael Prescott, a former independent external adviser to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editorial guidelines and standards committee. He left that role in the summer.\par \par \uc2 His memo was leaked to the Daily Telegraph last week and reported over the course of several days.\par \par \uc2 It included criticism of the way Panorama broadcast edited footage of a Donald Trump speech, which has since led the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/10/trump-bbc-legal-action"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US president to threaten a $1bn legal action}}}\uc2 . Prescott made a series of other claims of a more general liberal bias on issues such as trans rights and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 While the BBC has acknowledged failings, concern has grown over Gibb\u8217\'20\'19s position on the board amid suggestions that he played a role in both pushing Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s claims and in Prescott being awarded the advisory role. Both Gibb and Prescott have now been summoned to give evidence to the Commons culture, media and sport committee.\par \par \uc2 Thanking staff for their support at the meeting, Davie reassured them that the narrative around the corporation \u8220\'20\'1Cwill not just be given by our enemies\u8221\'20\'1D after a week in which senior politicians have accused the BBC of systemic bias in its reporting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do hear everyone when we have to be very clear and stand up for our journalism,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in a unique and precious organisation. I see the free press under pressure. I see the weaponisation. I think we\u8217\'20\'19ve got a fight for our journalism. I\u8217\'20\'19m really proud of our work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staff watching Davie\u8217\'20\'19s address tried to raise the issue of Gibb\u8217\'20\'19s place on the board. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy is \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  still on the board,\u8221\'20\'1D asked one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe board needs independent oversight free from political interference,\u8221\'20\'1D said another. \u8220\'20\'1CUntil \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  and any other political appointee are removed, we cannot possibly be truly trusted to be an unbiased organisation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another said: \u8220\'20\'1CI find \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s continued presence at the BBC to be incredibly demoralising. It feels as if he is fighting against and undermining the work we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 MPs also called for Gibb to be removed. Labour MP Sarah Owen said it was time to review his place on the board.\par \par \uc2 Anna Sabine, the Liberal Democrat culture spokesperson, said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cobvious to everyone that this issue is being weaponised by those who want to undermine the BBC and who would profit from its demise\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CSerious concerns remain over the conduct of Sir \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  during his tenure on the BBC board,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, backing his removal.\par \par \uc2 Pete Wishart\uc2 , from the SNP, also called for his departure.\par \par \uc2 However, Conservative MP Sir Julian Lewis said the \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis had nothing to do with \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and was entirely down to the mistakes the BBC had made. A corporation spokesperson said Gibb was one voice on a board of 13 people, which had a \u8220\'20\'1Ccross-section of views\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The culture secretary, \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2 , said there was a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrict legal threshold that must be met before dismissal of a board member\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that she could not remove Gibb. She condemned attempts to launch a \u8220\'20\'1Csustained attack\u8221\'20\'1D on the BBC.\par \par \uc2 Suggestions of a political effort to pressure the BBC from the right have been denied. In his letter, Prescott said his criticisms \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not come with any political agenda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In the all-staff meeting, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair, said any idea of a rightwing coup to destabilise the BBC from within was \u8220\'20\'1Cfanciful\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Board members] have different points of view,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t want a board that\u8217\'20\'19s guilty of groupthink, but the opposite of groupthink is different points of view. That makes the job of the chair challenging to try and organise and get consensus, but it\u8217\'20\'19s important that people have a diversity of opinion on the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251111elbb0060q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
