\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  resigns as BBC director general after accusations of \u8216\'20\'18serious and systemic\u8217\'20\'19 bias in coverage\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Savage Media editor \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par 17:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Davie says departure is \u8216\'20\'18my own decision\u8217\'20\'19, while head of news also quits. Insiders say it \u8216\'20\'18feels like a coup\u8217\'20\'19 by broadcaster\u8217\'20\'19s enemiesBBC bias row: how a week of hostile headlines from rightwing media led to resignations\par \par \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s director general, and the head of \uc2 BBC News\uc2  have resigned after a former adviser to the corporation accused it of \u8220\'20\'1Cserious and systemic\u8221\'20\'1D bias in its coverage of issues including Donald Trump, Gaza and trans rights.\par \par \uc2 In an announcement that caused shock within the corporation, Davie said his departure was \u8220\'20\'1Centirely my decision\u8221\'20\'1D and it comes as the BBC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/why-is-the-bbc-expected-to-apologise-over-a-donald-trump-speech-edit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prepares to apologise}}}\uc2  for the way it edited a Trump speech.\par \par \uc2 Deborah Turness, the head of \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , has also resigned. Sources within that department described a mood of dismay at Turness\u8217\'20\'19s exit. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like a coup,\u8221\'20\'1D one said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the result of a campaign by political enemies of the BBC.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/11/the-bbc-is-flawed-but-fundamentally-truthful"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The BBC is flawed, but fundamentally truthful}}}\par \par \uc2 It is a huge moment for the corporation, as it heads into critical talks with the government over its future and funding. The hunt is now on to fill two of the biggest roles in British media.\par \par \uc2 The BBC chairman, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , is also expected to apologise for the way the speech by Donald Trump was edited for Panorama.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/nov/09/bbc-attack-trump-telegraph-tories-tim-davie-resignation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The BBC is facing a coordinated, politically motivated attack. With these resignations, it\u8217\'20\'19s given in | Jane Martinson}}}\par \par \uc2 The Commons culture, media and sport select committee had set a Monday deadline for the BBC to respond to the claims made by Michael Prescott, a former independent external adviser to its editorial guidelines and standards committee. He left that role in the summer.\par \par \uc2 Prescott criticised the editing of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech in an edition of Panorama, which spliced together clips from an address the US president made on 6 January 2021. The edit suggested Trump told the crowd: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to walk down to the Capitol and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be there with you, and we fight. We fight like hell.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The words were taken from sections of his speech almost an hour apart. The incident has led to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/08/bbc-100-fake-news-says-donald-trumps-press-secretary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticism of the BBC by Karoline Leavitt, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s press secretary}}}\uc2 , who described the corporation this weekend as \u8220\'20\'1C100% fake news\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cpropaganda machine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night Leavitt appeared to celebrate the departures, referring on X to her complaints about the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s treatment of the US president\u8217\'20\'19s speech.\par \par \uc2 Trump posted on his Truth Social platform that \u8220\'20\'1Cvery dishonest people\u8221\'20\'1D had \u8220\'20\'1Ctried to step on the scales of a Presidential Election\u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1COn top of everything else, they are from a Foreign Country, one that many consider our Number One Ally. What a terrible thing for democracy!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-the-marketing-man-who-became-the-bbcs-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim Davie: the marketing man who became the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s director general}}}\par \par \uc2 In a note to staff, Davie said he had retained the \u8220\'20\'1Cunswerving and unanimous support\u8221\'20\'1D of the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s board and its chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , throughout. However, he said the current accusations had contributed to his decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLike all public organisations, the BBC is not perfect, and we must always be open, transparent and accountable,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile not being the only reason, the current debate around \uc2 BBC News\uc2  has understandably contributed to my decision. Overall the BBC is delivering well, but there have been some mistakes made and as director general I have to take ultimate responsibility.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Davie\u8217\'20\'19s departure comes after a torrid year in which the BBC has been criticised over the comments of its former presenter Gary Lineker, the livestreaming of a Glastonbury festival performance of the rap punk duo Bob Vylan, and criticisms over a Gaza documentary.\par \par \uc2 The departures of Davie and Turness appear to be an attempt to protect the BBC from further attacks following Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s criticisms, which he made in a letter to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s board.\par \par \uc2 Shah said Davie\u8217\'20\'19s departure was a \u8220\'20\'1Csad day\u8221\'20\'1D for the BBC. \u8220\'20\'1CHe has had the full support of me and the board throughout,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, I understand the continued pressure on him, personally and professionally, which has led him to take this decision today. The whole board respects the decision and the reasons for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Prescott letter, which was passed to the Daily Telegraph, also condemned BBC Arabic for using contributors who had expressed antisemitic views. It included the views of a contributor who had previously stated online that Jews should be burned \u8220\'20\'1Cas Hitler did\u8221\'20\'1D. The BBC has previously said he should not have been featured in the way he was.\par \par \uc2 Another featured contributor had described Jews online as \u8220\'20\'1Cdevils\u8221\'20\'1D. The BBC said in May that the person had been barred from being a contributor in future.\par \par \uc2 The culture secretary, \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2 , had said she was confident that Shah and Davie were \u8220\'20\'1Ctreating this with the seriousness that this demands\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In her departure note to staff, Turness said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe ongoing controversy around the Panorama on President Trump has reached a stage where it is causing damage to the BBC \u8211\'20\'13 an institution that I love.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the CEO of \uc2 BBC News\uc2  and current affairs, the buck stops with me. In public life leaders need to be fully accountable, and that is why I am stepping down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, she used her departure statement to push back on Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s allegations of general bias at the corporation. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile mistakes have been made, I want to be absolutely clear recent allegations that \uc2 BBC News\uc2  is institutionally biased are wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Liberal Democrat leader, Ed Davey, said the corporation needs \u8220\'20\'1Cto turn a new leaf\u8221\'20\'1D, the Conservative party leader, \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2 , called for \u8220\'20\'1Ctop-to-bottom\u8221\'20\'1D reform and Reform UK\u8217\'20\'19s leader, \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2 , said it was the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clast chance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 While many BBC figures believe Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s letter refers to some genuine errors, there is also concern it is being used by its political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Some BBC figures point to an effort to shift the corporation politically, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/nov/07/boris-johnson-trying-to-undermine-bbc-leadership-insiders-fear-after-leak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dating back to Boris Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s time in government}}}\uc2 . \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has been told Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s appointment as an external adviser had been pushed by the BBC board member \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 , Theresa May\u8217\'20\'19s former communications chief who helped set up the rightwing broadcaster \uc2 GB News\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gibb was first placed on the BBC board during Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s time in government. Gibb and Prescott have previously been reported as being friends. Gibb was on a four-person interview panel that appointed Prescott.\par \par \uc2 Johnson told the Guardian that any suggestion of an attempt to undermine the BBC was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete and utter bollocks\u8221\'20\'1D. Prescott said in his letter that his criticisms \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not come with any political agenda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251109elb9003ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
