\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 With his threat of a $1bn lawsuit against BBC, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s assault on the media goes global\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeremy Barr in Washington \par \uc2 1,033 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par 17:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US president\u8217\'20\'19s latest media target comes from across the pond as he continues to scrutinize coverage he does not likeUS politics live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  has, for years, used legal threats and lawsuits to pressure news companies who put out coverage he does not like. After his return to power, a string of US broadcasters and tech firms have paid tens of millions of dollars to settle such cases.\par \par \uc2 The president has now gone global with this campaign, crossing the pond to threaten the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/bbc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BBC}}}\uc2  with a $1bn lawsuit over an episode of the Panorama documentary program that aired more than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer representing Trump accused the BBC of \u8220\'20\'1Cdefamatory, disparaging and inflammatory statements\u8221\'20\'1D in stitching together Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments from his 6 January 2021 speech in Washington DC to make it sound like he was encouraging his supporters to \u8220\'20\'1Cfight like hell\u8221\'20\'1D at the US Capitol, hours before a deadly insurrection unfolded.\par \par \uc2 Already, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two top BBC executives have resigned}}}\uc2  over the controversy. A lawyer for Trump said the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s broadcast has caused Trump \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelming financial and reputational harm\u8221\'20\'1D and suggested it violated Florida law \u8211\'20\'13 even though BBC iPlayer, the main streaming platform that carries Panorama, and BBC One, the main TV channel that broadcasts it, are not available in the US.\par \par \uc2 The BBC has said it will review the correspondence from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s legal team \u8220\'20\'1Cand respond directly in due course\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The saga is only the latest chapter in a campaign meant to keep media institutions that cover Trump on their toes. Often, legal letters sent to media companies on his behalf have not actually led to lawsuits \u8211\'20\'13 though many journalists say they have contributed to a chilling effect on coverage.\par \par \uc2 But Trump has also followed through on several lawsuits, and since his re-election one year ago, a series of media and tech companies have chosen to take the easy way out by agreeing to significant settlements. Several of those companies have business before his administration.\par \par \uc2 In July, Paramount, parent company of CBS News, chose to settle a case that Trump had filed in the state of Texas arguing that the company had violated consumer protection laws by misleadingly editing a 60 Minutes interview of then vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Many legal experts viewed the case as easily winnable for Paramount, considering the unrelated statute he sued under \u8211\'20\'13 and that Trump could not credibly claim to have been harmed by the segment since he defeated Harris in the election.\par \par \uc2 But company leadership viewed the lawsuit as an unnecessary distraction, particularly as it sought the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s approval of a merger with \uc2 Skydance Media\uc2 . Paramount {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/jul/02/paramount-settles-with-trump-for-16m-over-60-minutes-interview-with-kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimately paid $16m}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump also won a settlement last year from ABC, owned by \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which he had sued over comments made by anchor George Stephanopoulos. ABC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/dec/14/abc-george-stephanopoulos-trump-15-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to pay $15m}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When combining Trump\u8217\'20\'19s settlements with ABC, \uc2 CBS\uc2  and cases against both \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent company \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , which is owned by \uc2 Google\uc2 , he has racked up over $80m in agreements. Most of this money is slated to go toward the building of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidential library, rather than to him personally.\par \par \uc2 Now the BBC is in his sights. Unlike \uc2 CBS\uc2 , owned by \uc2 Paramount Skydance\uc2 , and ABC, owned by \uc2 Disney\uc2 , the BBC is not part of a complicated corporate empire: it is independent, although its unique structure as a publicly funded organization invites intense scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Because of the timing of the Panorama broadcast, one week before the 5 November 2024 presidential election, a spokesperson for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s legal team accused the BBC of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Celection interference\u8221\'20\'1D, a similar charge to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  case. \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump will continue to hold accountable those who traffic in lies, deception and fake news,\u8221\'20\'1D they told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The legal letter cites the actual malice standard necessary to win defamation cases in the US. To meet that bar, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s team would have to prove that someone with authority at the BBC knew that the edited package falsely portrayed Trump to have encouraged violence, and chose not to act on that knowledge.\par \par \uc2 David A Logan, professor emeritus at the Roger Williams School of Law in Rhode Island, said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s allegations against the BBC closely \u8220\'20\'1Ctrack\u8221\'20\'1D those he made against \uc2 CBS\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 though in that case, \uc2 CBS\uc2  had merely used two different parts of the same answer in separate broadcasts, rather than pulling together comments from opposite ends of the Harris interview.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am reluctant to say which is worse journalism,\u8221\'20\'1D Logan said, but he noted that the resignations of BBC director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and chief executive of \uc2 BBC News\uc2  Deborah Turness \u8220\'20\'1Csignals that the BBC thinks it\u8217\'20\'19s plenty bad behavior\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mark Stephens, an international media lawyer at the firm Howard Kennedy, said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s team likely targeted the US in which to potentially file the case because the statute of limitations \u8211\'20\'13 one year from the date of the broadcast, which was 28 October 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 has passed in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Still, he said that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s team would face \u8220\'20\'1Cjurisdictional hurdles\u8221\'20\'1D if it filed the lawsuit. Because the program was not easily viewable in Florida, Stephens said it could be challenging to prove that someone in the state had seen it. \u8220\'20\'1CThe question I would ask myself early on is: how can someone in Florida think the worst of Donald Trump if they haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen the publication?\u8221\'20\'1D (The legal letter noted the segment in question has been distributed \u8220\'20\'1Cthrough various digital mediums\u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 If Trump chooses to sue, Stephens said the case would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/10/why-bbc-resignations-trump-speech-edit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bring renewed attention to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments}}}\uc2 , and any role he might have played in fomenting the violence of January 6. (Trump claims he did no such thing.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf he sues, he opens a Pandora\u8217\'20\'19s box, and in that Pandora\u8217\'20\'19s box is every damning quote he\u8217\'20\'19s ever uttered about January 6,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stephens. \u8220\'20\'1CSo this isn\u8217\'20\'19t the hill to die on, in my view. It\u8217\'20\'19s a legal cliff edge, and if he jumps, there\u8217\'20\'19s a high chance he\u8217\'20\'19ll fall.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251111elbb001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
