\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Tech shares slide after SoftBank sells \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  stake; UK interest rate cut expected in December \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 4,048 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2025\par 17:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial newsAnalysis: Rising unemployment could affect budget, interest rates, pay and more\par \par \uc2 5.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 And finally\u8230\'20\'26 here\u8217\'20\'19s our news story on SoftBank\u8217\'20\'19s decision to sell its stake in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s some of the other major news today:\par \par \uc2 5.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street is not sharing the festive mood in London.\par \par \uc2 The NasdaqComposite is down around 0.7% after this morning\u8217\'20\'19s trading session.\par \par \uc2 Here are the top fallers:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Applovin Corp\uc2 : -10.1%\par \par \uc2 * Micron\par \par \uc2 Technology: -4.072%\par \par \uc2 * NVIDIA\par \par \uc2 Corp: -3.509%\par \par \uc2 * Marvell\par \par \uc2 Technology: -3.411%\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Lam Research Corp\uc2 : -3.409%\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Arm Holdings\uc2 : -3.333%\par \par \uc2 4.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 closes at new high\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s stock market has closed at a new high, as investors anticipate a cut to UK interest rates next month.\par \par \uc2 Having scaled the 9,900 point mark for the first time in late-afternoon trading, the FTSE100 slipped back slightly in the closing auction to finish at a new closing high of 9899.6 points. That\u8217\'20\'19s a jump of 112.5 points, or 1.15%, today.\par \par \uc2 Raffi\par \par \uc2 Boyadjian, lead market analyst at TradingPoint, says:\par \par \uc2 Those bets were boosted by the weak jobless data this morning, which showed UK unemployment has hit its highest level since early 2021.\par \par \uc2 With a December rate cut now seen as a 74% chance, shares in UK housebuilders rose today.\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  were the top riser, as investors welcomed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-691333938f0889b4d8c8a99e#block-691333938f0889b4d8c8a99e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its decision to increased its dividend for the first time in eight years and upgraded its profit guidance}}}\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceuticals firm \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  gained 2.5%, hitting a new record high today.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Back in London, the stock market has hit a new record high in late trading.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 has climbed over the intraday high set this morning, and is now trading over 9,900 points for the first time \u8211\'20\'13 at 9,907 points.\par \par \uc2 4.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share price is continuing to dip \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s now down 3.1% at $192.88.\par \par \uc2 2.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 SoftBank\u8217\'20\'19s decision to sell out of \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Ca major event for markets\u8221\'20\'1D, says \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2  investment director Russ Mould.\par \par \uc2 2.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The tech-focused Nasdaq index has dropped by 0.6% in early trading.\par \par \uc2 Marketing firm \uc2 Applovin\uc2  (-5,6%) are the top faller, followed by semiconductor maker Micron (-3.8%) and chip designer ARM (-3.6%).\par \par \uc2 2.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 S&P opens lower as tech shares slide\par \par \uc2 The US stock market has opened slightly lower.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 index dipped by 0.25% at the open, with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  (-2.3%) among the fallers following SoftBank\u8217\'20\'19s sale of its stake in the chipmaker ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-691345e48f08af9e6137fc3d#block-691345e48f08af9e6137fc3d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Fellow tech companies \uc2 Oracle\uc2  (-2.5%) and \uc2 Dell\uc2  (-2.4%) are leading the fallers.\par \par \uc2 As well as concerns over technology valuations, investors will have noted today\u8217\'20\'19s warning that US companies cut jobs at the end of last month ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-69133b208f08af9e6137fba6#block-69133b208f08af9e6137fba6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  shares fall after SoftBank sells stake\par \par \uc2 Shares in chip giant \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  have fallen by over 2% at the start of trading in New York, after one of its major shareholders revealed it had sold its stake.\par \par \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s SoftBank disclosed this morning that it had sold its entire shareholding in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  in October, for $5.83bn.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said SoftBank, a powerful tech investor, appears to be raising money to fund other investments in artificial intelligence groups such as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But\u8230\'20\'26 the sale of the stake in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , which has been very profitable for SoftBank, could fuel concerns that AI valuations have risen too high.\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stock has dropped by 2.3%, to $194.70, but is still up over 45% so far this year (and around 1,300% over the last five years!).\par \par \uc2 SoftBank\u8217\'20\'19s chief financial officer YoshimitsuGoto told investors that the salw as part of the firm\u8217\'20\'19s strategy for \u8220\'20\'1Casset monetization,\u8221\'20\'1D explaining:\par \par \uc2 2.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The jump in the UK unemploymen rate is a blow to the Government\u8217\'20\'19s tax take.\par \par \uc2 Robert Salter, a director at audit, tax and business advisory firm BlickRothenberg, explains:\par \par \uc2 1.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 ADP: US companies shed jobs last month\par \par \uc2 Just in: We also have a weak-looking jobs report from the US.\par \par \uc2 Not an official report (the shutdown isn\u8217\'20\'19t over yet!). But private payroll operator ADP has crunched its latest data, and found that in the four weeks to 25 October, private employers shed an average of 11,250jobs a week.\par \par \uc2 That suggests the labor market struggled to produce jobs consistently during the second half of the month, ADP say.\par \par \uc2 This implies a weak jobs market \u8211\'20\'13 which might spur the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  into cutting US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also surprising, as ADP reported last week that private sector employment increased by 42,000 jobs in October.\par \par \uc2 This apparent contradiction could be caused by a sharp deterioration in the jobs market towards the end of October (after the monthly data was collected).\par \par \uc2 1.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has slipped back from this morning\u8217\'20\'19s record high, as investors anticipate a weak start to trading in New York.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures indicate the S&P500 share index could drop by 0.25%, while the Nasdaq tech index is on track for a 0.4% drop.\par \par \uc2 Concerns around elevated technology valuations appear to have resurfaced, while markets watch whether the longest government shutdown in US history is ending.\par \par \uc2 1.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  shares at two-year high after dividend boost\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  has increased its dividend for the first time in eight years and upgraded its profit guidance, sending shares in the telecoms operator to a more than two-year high.\par \par \uc2 The company, which completed a \u163\'00\'A316.5bn merger of its UK operations with rival Three earlier this year, was boosted by a return to revenue growth in its biggest market, Germany, which has been struggling for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose about 6% on Tuesday, making \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  the biggest riser in the FTSE 100 ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6912eced8f08a98bc7592e6b#block-6912eced8f08a98bc7592e6b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lifting it to that new record}}}\uc2 ), as the company said it would increase its dividend for the first time since 2018.\par \par \uc2 Margherita Della Valle\uc2 , who has been carrying out a major transformation programme since being appointed as the company\u8217\'20\'19s first female chief executive two years ago, said that \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  is looking to move towards a \u8220\'20\'1Cprogressive dividend policy\u8221\'20\'1D to increase returns to shareholders annually.\par \par \uc2 The group slashed its dividend by about 40% in May 2019 after the cost of buying mobile 5G spectrum caused its debt to balloon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been a long time since this happened at \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  and we are pleased that we are able to share this with our investors,\u8221\'20\'1D DellaValle said.\par \par \uc2 The new dividend policy will deliver an expected increase of 2.5% in the year to the end of March.\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  said that it now expects to deliver the upper range of guidance for full year earnings of between \u8364\'20\'AC11.3bn and \u8364\'20\'AC11.6bn, and adjusted free cash flow of \u8364\'20\'AC2.4bn to \u8364\'20\'AC2.6bn.\par \par \uc2 In Germany, where \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  has lost half of its TV customers between July 2024 and March this year due to a law change that gave customers living in housing association properties the right to choose their own TV provider, the company added 90,000 new customers in the first half of its current financial year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe group had quite simply been fighting fires on too many fronts while dealing with an increasingly onerous debt burden, leading to the need for a significant transformation,\u8221\'20\'1D said RichardHunter, head of markets at InteractiveInvestor. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat is now emerging is a smaller and less geographically diverse, but more focused operation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\par \par \uc2 UK is currently embroiled in a \u163\'00\'A378m legal claim with more than 60 of its franchise operators.\par \par \uc2 Claimants have accused the company of \u8220\'20\'1Cunjustly enriching\u8221\'20\'1D itself by cutting commission rates paid to franchisees which many have said have led to them running up huge personal debts and fearing for their livelihoods or homes.\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  has been offering settlements to some former franchise partners who are not involved in the high court claim.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs you know we have touched on this before,\u8221\'20\'1D said DellaValle, in response to a question about the status of the legal action and settlement offers.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for VodafoneUK said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Vodafone\uc2  continues to defend all allegations against them in this commercial dispute.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Kate Nicholls\uc2 , chair of UKHospitality, has firmly blamed \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget a year ago for the downturn in the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Nicholls warns that hospitality firms are businesses are reducing hours, cancelling investment, raising prices, or even closing down, as well as cutting jobs.\par \par \uc2 12.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Disabled people are bearing the brunt of the weakening UK job market, \uc2 charity Scope\uc2  is warning today.\par \par \uc2 Scope, the disability equality charity, has analysed today\u8217\'20\'19s UK labour market report and spotted that the number of disabled people in employment fell by 349,000 between July-September 2024 and July-September 2025.\par \par \uc2 This means the Disability Employment Gap (DEG) (comparative employment rates of disabled and non-disabled people) rose to 30.1% points in the third quarter of 2025, up from 27.5% points a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 They also report that disabled people are more than twice as likely to be unemployed as non-disabled people:\par \par \uc2 * Employment rates for disabled people dropped to 52.3% (compared to 54.5% in the third quarter of 2024) while for non-disabled individuals improved, reaching 82.5% compared to 81.9% in the third quarter of 2024.\par \par \uc2 * Unemployment among disabled individuals has been rising since the first quarter of 2024. And it continued to do so reaching 9.1% for disabled people in the third quarter of this year (compared to 6.7% same quarter in 2024).\par \par \uc2 * Inactivity rates, after stability for few months, began to increase among disabled people from 41.6% in July-September 2024 to 42.5% in same quarter in 2025. This is not the case for non-disabled people, for which inactivity rates have continued to fall.\par \par \uc2 James Taylor, executive director of strategy at Scope, says:\par \par \uc2 Huge numbers of disabled people want to work but are denied the opportunity, Taylor adds:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policymaker Megan Greene has indicated that this morning\u8217\'20\'19s weak UK unemployment data won\u8217\'20\'19t prompt her to vote for interest rate cuts next month.\par \par \uc2 Greene is one of the more hawkish members of the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee, and one of the five who voted to leave rates unchanged last week.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a \uc2 UBS\uc2  conference in London, Greene said she believed the labour market is past the worst and warned that firms are predicting more hefty wage increases, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Greene explaine:\par \par \uc2 She did warn, though, that the rise in the unemployment rate to 5% was a blow for the economy, saying:\par \par \uc2 11.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 US small business optimism falls\par \par \uc2 Over in the US, small business owners have grown more pessimistic about their prospects.\par \par \uc2 The NFIB Small Business Optimism Index declined 0.6 points in October to 98.2, with small firms reporting falling sales and a struggle to hire workers.\par \par \uc2 The Uncertainty Index fell 12 points from September to 88, the lowest reading of this year.\par \par \uc2 NFIB\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Bill Dunkelberg\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The report found:\par \par \uc2 * Thirty-two percent (seasonally adjusted) of all owners reported job openings they could not fill in the current period, unchanged for the second consecutive month. Before August, the last time unfilled job openings hit 32% was in December 2020.\par \par \uc2 * In October, 27% of small business owners cited labor quality as their single most important problem, up 9 points from September and the highest level since the record high of 29% in November 2021. Labor quality ranked as the top problem and was 11 points higher than taxes, which ranked second.\par \par \uc2 * A net negative 13% of all owners (seasonally adjusted) reported higher nominal sales in the past three months, down 6 points from September.\par \par \uc2 11.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a cut in UK interest rates next month has driven up shares in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s housebuilders.\par \par \uc2 Berkeley Group\uc2  (+2%) are among the top risers on the FTSE100 index this morning, with \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  (+1.6%) and BarrattRedrow (+1.6%) both higher.\par \par \uc2 Chris Beauchamp\uc2 , chief market analyst at IG, explains:\par \par \uc2 10.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 Over in Germany, investor morale has fallen unexpectedly this month.\par \par \uc2 The ZEW economic research institute\u8217\'20\'19s economic sentiment index has dropped to 38.5 points from 39.3 points in October, dashing hopes of a rise to 41.\par \par \uc2 ZEW president AchimWambach says:\par \par \uc2 10.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 73% chance of rate cut in December\par \par \uc2 The odds of a UK interest rate cut next month have risen towards 75% today, after unemployment jumped to 5% and wage growth slowed.\par \par \uc2 The money markets now indicate there is a 73.6% chance of a rate cut in December, to 3.75%, and just a 26.4% prospect that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  leaves borrowing costs on hold again, at 4%.\par \par \uc2 Before this morning\u8217\'20\'19s labour market report, a December cut was seen as a 61.5% chance.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Japanese bank \uc2 Nomura\uc2  say:\par \par \uc2 * Today\u8217\'20\'19s labour market report was weaker than expected, with the unemployment rate rising above consensus expectations to 5.0% in Q3, and private sector regular pay growth soft at 0.2% m-o-m in September.\par \par \uc2 * This follows soft labour market data in the previous report. However, policymakers left Bank Rate unchanged at the November MPC meeting last week, citing the need for an accumulation of evidence to support further policy loosening.\par \par \uc2 * Today\u8217\'20\'19s data support a December \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  (\uc2 BoE\uc2 ) rate cut, but we acknowledge that there are several more data releases, and also the budget, before then. We think these releases will add to reasoning for another 25bp cut at the next policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 9.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK grocery inflation slows as supermarkets ramp up promotions\par \par \uc2 UK grocery inflation has slowed, in a boost to households.\par \par \uc2 Grocery price inflation slowed to 4.7% in October, data from Worldpanel by Numerator shows, as consumers bought more on deals.\par \par \uc2 Just under 30% of grocery spending was on promoted items last month, they say, as consumers cut back ahead of the budget.\par \par \uc2 Fraser McKevitt\uc2 , head of retail and consumer insight at Worldpanel, explains:\par \par \uc2 9.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 Back in the City, shares in \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  have hit a fresh record high.\par \par \uc2 The move cements \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s position as the largest UK-listed stock by market value, with its value now approaching \u163\'00\'A3210bn.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the company announced stronger-than-expected quarterly earnings.\par \par \uc2 But there is also doubt over its long-term commitment to the London market, after it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/sep/29/astrazeneca-plans-new-york-stock-exchange-listing-but-isnt-quitting-london"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decided in September to also list its shares directly on the New York Stock Exchange}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 9.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump 'working on' a trade deal with Switzerland\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump has said he is \u8220\'20\'1Cworking on a deal\u8221\'20\'1D to reduce the punitive 39% tariff he slapped on Switzerland in August, with expectations a deal aligning the country with the \uc2 EU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 15% could be sealed within the fortnight.\par \par \uc2 Richemont and SwatchGroup shares rose on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland has been hit with one of the highest tariffs in the world baffling the country\u8217\'20\'19s leaders and industries.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re working on a deal to get their tariffs a little bit lower,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said. \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t set any number, but we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be working on something to help Switzerland,\u8221\'20\'1D he added on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the WatchesofSwitzerland group have rusen by more than 7% in early trading, while \uc2 Swatch\uc2  gained 4.2% and Richemont up 2%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe talks are ongoing and we do not comment further,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesman for the Swiss Department of Economic Affairs said.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: A Swiss source has told Reuters that a deal could come as early as Thursday or Friday this week\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK investors pull money from global equity funds at record pace\par \par \uc2 Although the London stock market is at a new peak, we also have new data showing that UK investors have been pulling cash out of global share funds at a record pace.\par \par \uc2 Global funds network \uc2 Calastone\uc2  has reproted that budget concerns and soaring share prices drove record outflows from equity funds in October.\par \par \uc2 They have found that UK investors pulled \u163\'00\'A33.63bn out of equity funds in October, the fifth month of selling in a row \u8211\'20\'13 the longest stint of selling since the Brexit referendum in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Since June, they say, \u163\'00\'A37.36bn has left equity funds, also a record.\par \par \uc2 Calastone\uc2  also reports:\par \par \uc2 * All equity categories saw net selling; UK funds lost \u163\'00\'A31.22bn in October alone.\par \par \uc2 * Global, North American and technology funds also saw heavy withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 * Investors shifted some of the capital to lower-risk assets, with a record \u163\'00\'A3955m into money market funds and \u163\'00\'A3589m into fixed income.\par \par \uc2 Edward\par \par \uc2 Glyn, head of global markets at \uc2 Calastone\uc2  says two forces are driving investor behaviour:\par \par \uc2 8.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Victoria Scholar, head of investment at interactive investor, points out that London\u8217\'20\'19s stock market is outpacing the rest of Europe this morning:\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx600 is up 0.6% this morning, with Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX up 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 8.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 Market Report: UK wages pave way for rate cuts, US dip buyers\u8217\'20\'19 cheer\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Matt Britzman, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , to explain why the London stock market has hit a new peak today:\par \par \uc2 8.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 hits fresh record high\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a#block-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The weakening UK jobs market}}}\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t deterred traders from driving the UK stock market to a new record high.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 index of blue-chip shares, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/10/stock-markets-rally-us-shutdown-breakthrough-ftse-currencies-business-live-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closed at a record high last night}}}\uc2 , has jumped further at the start of trading.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE100 is up almost 1% in early trading, gaining another 97 points to hit a fresh intraday high of 9,884 points.\par \par \uc2 Mobile network operator \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  are the top riser, up 4.4%, after raising its full-year outlook for earnings and cash flow this morning.\par \par \uc2 Advertising group \uc2 WPP\uc2  (+2.9%) and pharmaceuticals giant \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  (+2.2%) are also in the top risers, along with UK housebuilders who would benefit from interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 There is some general relief in the financial markets that the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown is close to ending, which lifted share prices yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The weakness in the pound this morning, as traders anticipate interest rate cuts, will also push up the share prices of multinational firms listed in London (as it make their overseas earnings more valuable in sterling terms).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State for Work and Pensions, PatMcFadden, has insisted the government is taking steps to help people into work:\par \par \uc2 7.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank: Labour market data strengthens case for a Christmas rate cut\par \par \uc2 Sanjay Raja. DeutscheBank\u8217\'20\'19s chief UK economist, says this morning\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report strengthens the case for a Christmas rate cut, adding:\par \par \uc2 7.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  expected to cut interest rates as jobs market weakens\par \par \uc2 Several economists are predicting the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  could cut interest rates as soon as December, following this morning\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report.\par \par \uc2 And looking further ahead, the money markets are now indicating they expect 65 basis points of \uc2 BoE\uc2  rate cuts by the end of next year, up from 55 bps on Monday. That means two quarter-point cuts by December 2026 are fully priced in, with a third now more likely.\par \par \uc2 Suren\par \par \uc2 Thiru, economics director at \uc2 ICAEW\uc2  (the \uc2 Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and Wales\uc2 ), reckons the odds of a rate cut next month have risen, now that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a#block-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unemployment has jumped to 5%}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6912e5218f08a98bc7592e46#block-6912e5218f08a98bc7592e46"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wage growth has slowed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Richard Carter, head of fixed interest research at \uc2 Quilter Cheviot\uc2 , points out that \uc2 BoE\uc2  governor \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  is the \u8216\'20\'18swing voter\u8217\'20\'19 on its Monetary Policy Committee, who could be swayed into creating a majority for a cut:\par \par \uc2 Thomas Pugh, chief economist at audit, tax and consulting firm RSM UK, points out that problems at the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  do make today\u8217\'20\'19s data somewhat unreliable\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 The business tax rises announced in last year\u8217\'20\'19s budget, and uncertainty over what the chancellor will announce this time, are being blamed for the rise in UK unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Isaac Stell, investment manager at tax advisory service Wealth Club, calls it a \u8220\'20\'1Cself-inflicted wound\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Ashley Webb, UK economist at CapitalEconomics, also blames the rise in employer national insurance contributions for workforce cuts:\par \par \uc2 7.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Real wage growth slows too\par \par \uc2 Inflation is taking a large chunk out of UK pay growth.\par \par \uc2 While regular pay growth was 4.6% in July to September, that falls to just 0.8% in real terms once you adjust for CPI inflation.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s down from 0.9% in the previous three-months.\par \par \uc2 It was last lower than 0.8% in June to August 2023, when it was 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 7.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Public sector pay outpaces private sector\par \par \uc2 Wage growth in the public sector was much more rapid than in the private sector over the summer, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows.\par \par \uc2 Annual average regular earnings growth was 6.6% for the public sector in July to September, compared with 4.2% for the private sector.\par \par \uc2 The ONS cautions that the public sector annual growth rate is affected by some public sector pay rises being paid earlier in 2025 than in 2024, which caused {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.ons.gov.uk/2021/05/19/beware-base-effects/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a base effect}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reminder: overall regular pay growth slowed to 4.6%, which is the lowest since February to April 2022.\par \par \uc2 7.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 This morning\u8217\'20\'19s labour market report shows a jump in unemployment among men in the last couple of months.\par \par \uc2 While the overall jobless rate has risen to 5% in July-September, up from 4.7% in April-June, there\u8217\'20\'19s a clear gender split. Among men, joblessness is up from 4.8% to 5.4%, while among women it\u8217\'20\'19s unchanged at 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 The ONS says:\par \par \uc2 7.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 Sterling drops after UK jobs report\par \par \uc2 The pound has dropped by half a cent against the US dollar, as traders react to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/11/pound-falls-uk-unemployment-four-year-high-stock-markets-ftse-100-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a#block-6912da4d8f08a98bc7592e1a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the jump in UK unemployment}}}\uc2 , and the slowdown in wage growth.\par \par \uc2 This has pushed sterling down to $1.3123, and indicates the City may expect the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to respond by lowering interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Bank narrowly voted to leave borrowing costs unchanged, by fivevotes to four.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK unemployment hits 5%, first time since 2021\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The weakening in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market has gathered pace, with new data showing the jobless rate has hit its highest level since the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The UK unemployment rate has jumped to 5% in the July to September quarter, the highest level since February 2021. That\u8217\'20\'19s up from 4.8% a month earlier, and higher than expected.\par \par \uc2 In another blow, the OfficeforNationalStatistics estimate that the number of employees on payrolls fell by 32,000 in September, and by another 32,000 in October to 30.3 million.\par \par \uc2 And with the jobs market cooling, wage growth slowed too. Annual growth in employees\u8217\'20\'19 average earnings slipped to 4.6% in the quarter, down from 4.7% in the three months to August.\par \par \uc2 ONS director of economic statistics \uc2 Liz McKeown\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 This jump in unemployment, and slowing wages, will heighten concerns that the economy is cooling \u8211\'20\'13 as \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  prepares her budget due on 26 November \u8211\'20\'13 and could spur the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to cut interest rates again as soon as December.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am GMT: UK labour market report.\par \par \uc2 * 8am GMT: UK grocery inflation report\par \par \uc2 * 10am GMT: ZEW eurozone economic confidence index\par \par \uc2 * 11am GMT: US NFIB Business Optimism Index\par \par \uc2 * 2.30pm GMT: Business Secretary Peter Kyle makes first appearance at Business and Trade committee\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251111elbb001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
