\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump threatens BBC with $1bn lawsuit as chair says speech edit was \u8216\'20\'18error of judgment\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Nadeem Badshah (now); Frances Mao, Yohannes Lowe and Vicky Graham (earlier) \par \uc2 8,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2025\par 20:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 BBC chair \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  apologises for way in which speech from US president was edited after corporation\u8217\'20\'19s two most senior executives resign. This liveblog is now closedFull report: BBC board member with Tory links \u8216\'20\'18led charge\u8217\'20\'19 in systemic bias claims, say insiders\par \par \uc2 7.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A summary of today's developments\par \par \uc2 * Deborah Turness, the outgoing News CEO, has defended the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s journalism, dismissing President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attack that BBC journalists are corrupt. \u8220\'20\'1COur journalists aren\u8217\'20\'19t corrupt. Our journalists are hard-working people who strive for impartiality. And I will stand by their journalism,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no institutional bias. Mistakes are made, but there\u8217\'20\'19s no institutional bias.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/10/bbc-resignations-internal-coup-says-former-sun-editor-david-yelland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The chair of the BBC, Samir Shah, releases the long-awaited letter addressing journalistic errors}}}\uc2 , including most crucially, the Panorama documentary which spliced together different parts of Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 speech. Shah accepts that the edit gave \u8220\'20\'1Cthe impression of a direct call for violent action\u8221\'20\'1D and was \u8220\'20\'1Can error of judgment\u8221\'20\'1D. He apologises on behalf of the BBC. He also details action taken to address errors made on other stories including Gaza coverage.\par \par \uc2 * Immediately after the letter\u8217\'20\'19s release, the BBC also airs an interview with Shah where he rejects allegations of \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic bias\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Canti-Israel bias\u8221\'20\'1D in the newsroom and says the board is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot overly political\u8221\'20\'1D. This comes amid reports, including by the Guardian, of a political campaign orchestrated by enemies within the BBC to bring it down. Shah says he and the board did not want Davie to resign.\par \par \uc2 * US President Donald Trump threatens in a letter to sue the BBC. His lawyers claim that Trump, who was indicted by Congress for efforts to overturn the 2020 vote, has been defamed by the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s Panorama episode. They issue a Friday deadline to the BBC, demanding the news organisation make amends or they will launch a $1bn lawsuit. The BBC says it is reviewing the letter.\par \par \uc2 * Downing Street says the BBC is not corrupt not institutionally biased and plays a vital role in the age of disinformation. The Conservatives and Reform said the BBC has shown bias.\par \par \uc2 * The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s deputy head of news, now temporarily in charge of editorial decisions, sends out an all-staff email announcing a pan-BBC call tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/10/bbc-board-member-tory-links-led-charge-systemic-bias-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A BBC board member with links to the Conservative party \u8220\'20\'1Cled the charge\u8221\'20\'1D in pressuring}}}\uc2  the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s leadership over claims of systemic bias in coverage of Donald Trump, Gaza and transgender rights, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  has been told. Sources said that \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 , Theresa May\u8217\'20\'19s former communications chief who was appointed to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s board during Boris Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s administration, amplified the criticisms in key board meetings that preceded the shock resignation of the director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , and the head of \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , Deborah Turness. In an article for the Guardian, the Liberal Democrat leader, Ed Davey, called for Gibb to be removed from the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s board before the search for a new director general begins.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 7.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 On a similar theme to the first minister\u8217\'20\'19s comments, the Lib Dem leader Ed Davey has penned a piece for the Guardian about \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s position on the BBC board.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/nov/10/bbc-enemies-attack-robbie-gibb-board"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The first step towards saving our precious BBC: remove Robbie Gibb from the board | Ed Davey}}}\par \par \uc2 7.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 When Deborah Turness, the now departed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/bbc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BBC}}}\uc2  News chief, was first invited to a meeting with the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s board a few weeks ago, there was little to suggest it would be a particularly significant encounter.\par \par \uc2 But instead of a routine meeting, she came under attack over an item added to the agenda.\par \par \uc2 The lengthy confrontation that followed set off an extraordinary series of events that ultimately ended in her resignation and that of the BBC director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and raised questions about politically motivated interference with the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s news operation.\par \par \uc2 The board wanted to discuss a letter \u8211\'20\'13 and accompanying 8,000-word memo \u8211\'20\'13 it had received from Michael Prescott, a former external adviser to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editorial guidelines and standards committee (EGSC), who was making broad claims of bias at the organisation.\par \par \uc2 The claims, which would later be leaked to the Telegraph and reported prominently over a week, had been sent to the board\u8217\'20\'19s chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , and the rest of its members.\par \par \uc2 There were some serious accusations in the claims made by Prescott, once the political editor of the \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 -owned \uc2 Sunday Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most notably, he described how Panorama had edited together two parts of a Donald Trump speech without informing viewers. Other accusations were made over its reporting on Gaza and trans rights.\par \par \uc2 However, the criticisms were all made from the same political perspective: that the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s reporting on such issues was too liberal and that it had ignored such concerns. Sources said that at the meeting, Turness was \u8220\'20\'1Con the rack\u8221\'20\'1D for more than an hour as Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s criticisms were laid out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake, this was a coup,\u8221\'20\'1D said a BBC source.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/ng-interactive/2025/nov/10/make-no-mistake-this-was-a-coup-the-extraordinary-downfall-of-the-bbcs-top-bosses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Make no mistake \u8211\'20\'13 this was a coup\u8217\'20\'19: the extraordinary downfall of the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s top bosses}}}\par \par \uc2 7.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s position on the BBC board is being called into question amid bias accusations at the broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 Gibb served as director of communications for Theresa May when she was in Downing Street before his appointment to the broadcaster\u8217\'20\'19s board.\par \par \uc2 The former broadcast journalist served in Number 10 for two years under the former Conservative prime minister and his appointment to the board of the BBC in 2021 was met with criticism.\par \par \uc2 Scotland\u8217\'20\'19s first minister \uc2 John Swinney\uc2  said his position \u8220\'20\'1Cfuels that doubt and debate about the independence and the impartiality of the BBC\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to LBC on Monday, first minister and SNP leader Swinney said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2  is a very clearly affiliated party political figure on the board of the BBC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the BBC wants to be viewed as an impartial organisation that\u8217\'20\'19s authoritative, that reflects the independence of commentary, then I think \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s position is untenable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the PA news agency, he added: \u8220\'20\'1CI quite understand the concerns that are being expressed, given the role and the contribution of some of the members of the BBC board.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhoever it is in the BBC has got to be able to live up to the standards of impartiality, independence and to be able to give that authoritative commentary and contribution to public service broadcasting, because that\u8217\'20\'19s what we all rely upon the BBC to provide.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 6.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Gotcha! The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s enemies have taken two scalps and inflicted maximum damage. The shock resignation of the director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , and the head of news, Deborah Turness, make it look as if the BBC accepts that it does indeed suffer from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cserious and systemic\u8221\'20\'1D bias}}}\uc2  in its coverage of issues including Donald Trump, Gaza and trans rights. But in this political coup, only the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s sworn ideological foes think a cherrypicked sample of journalistic errors amounts to \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic\u8221\'20\'1D bias. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was indeed a bad mistake to splice together two bits of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech; but it needed a quick apology, not a decapitation. The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/samir-shah"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samir Shah}}}\uc2 , I\u8217\'20\'19m told, tried to persuade Davieto stay to avoid this apparent capitulation to critics: Davie should indeed have stood his ground, not weakened the BBC by walking away.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/nov/10/stand-up-and-defend-bbc-right-resignations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If you care about the BBC, stand up and defend it: this could be the beginning of the end | Polly Toynbee}}}\par \par \uc2 6.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The shadow culture secretary Nigel Huddleston wrote on X: \u8220\'20\'1CThere are many allegations of the BBC failing to deliver on its charter obligations on impartiality - and on it\u8217\'20\'19s failure to abide by its own editorial guidelines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis points to deep rooted problems that go well beyond any one individual.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe BBC must undergo a fundamental review to restore trust and ensure it delivers on its founding principles to inform, educate, and entertain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPublic confidence and the licence fee depends on it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 6.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The editing of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  speech on 6 January 2021 has embroiled the BBC in controversy, led to the resignations of its top leaders, and given fuel to claims of media bias at the storied British broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 A dossier from a former external adviser to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editorial standards committee flagged the edit, among other instances of purported bias in the outlet\u8217\'20\'19s coverage, which was leaked to the Telegraph. As a public sector organization, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/bbc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BBC}}}\uc2  is required to be impartial, though it often faces claims of bias, particularly from the right.\par \par \uc2 In a broadcast of the news show Panorama before the 2024 election, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech was edited to put together two sentences that were actually 54 minutes apart, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/video/2025/nov/10/side-by-side-comparison-of-bbc-edited-trump-speech-from-day-of-capitol-attack-with-original-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 making it appear}}}\uc2  as though he was telling people they would walk to the US Capitol and \u8220\'20\'1Cfight like hell\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 After Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech, thousands of his supporters descended on the US Capitol, some of them entering the building and brawling with police to try to stop the electoral count. Five people died within days, marking a violent start to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 The scandal had provided Trump another avenue to attack the media, this time overseas, and to relitigate the circumstances of the 2020 election and his attempts to overturn the results. He has threatened legal action against the BBC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe TOP people in the BBC, including TIM DAVIE, the BOSS, are all quitting/FIRED, because they were caught \u8216\'20\'18doctoring\u8217\'20\'19 my very good (PERFECT!) speech of January 6th,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote on Truth Social. \u8220\'20\'1CThank you to The Telegraph for exposing these Corrupt \u8216\'20\'18Journalists.\u8217\'20\'19 These are very dishonest people who tried to step on the scales of a Presidential Election. On top of everything else, they are from a Foreign Country, one that many consider our Number One Ally. What a terrible thing for Democracy!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , called the edit an \u8220\'20\'1Cerror in judgement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/10/why-bbc-resignations-trump-speech-edit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editing of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 speech caused controversy \u8211\'20\'13 but what did he really say?}}}\par \par \uc2 6.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Tonia Antoniazzi is organising a letter to be sent to the culture secretary \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2  seeking clarification on whether the BBC board prevented a swift apology going out for the Panorama Trump edit and also why \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  felt the need to state the BBC shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be \u8220\'20\'1Cweaponised\u8221\'20\'1D in his resignation letter.\par \par \uc2 Antoniazzi told the BBC: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not BBC bashing \u8211\'20\'13 I want to protect it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith both the director general and the head of news gone and threats of legal action from President Trump, this is a significant moment for the organisation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s up to us as parliamentarians to stand up for a free press \u8211\'20\'13 the fourth pillar of democracy \u8211\'20\'13 and help preserve the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s journalistic integrity at all costs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CThis transparency is vital to allow the BBC to move on, and enable its news operation to continue its role providing fair, accurate, impartial news in a deeply polarised world, plagued by misinformation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 6.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Key takeaways from events today at the BBC\par \par \uc2 A lot has happened today following the shock resignations of the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s director general Tim Davies and News CEO Deborah Turness on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s go through things chronologically, to show how it all unfolded.\par \par \uc2 * 9:45am GMT: Deborah Turness, the outgoing News CEO, turns up to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s London headquarters on Monday morning where she faces the press pack. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6911b6858f087353b0fc6782#block-6911b6858f087353b0fc6782"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 She defends BBC journalism}}}\uc2 , dismissing President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attack that BBC journalists are corrupt.\par \par \uc2 * \u8220\'20\'1COur journalists aren\u8217\'20\'19t corrupt. Our journalists are hardworking people who strive for impartiality. And I will stand by their journalism,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no institutional bias. Mistakes are made, but there\u8217\'20\'19s no institutional bias.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Midday: The Chair of the BBC, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , releases the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://committees.parliament.uk/publications/50118/documents/270340/default/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long-awaited letter addressing journalistic errors}}}\uc2 , including most crucially, the Panorama documentary which spliced together different parts of Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 speech.\par \par \uc2 * Shah accepts that the edit gave \u8220\'20\'1Cthe impression of a direct call for violent action\u8221\'20\'1D and was \u8220\'20\'1Can error of judgement\u8221\'20\'1D. He apologises on behalf of the BBC. He also details action taken to address errors made on other stories including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6911df568f087ec9f3660c92#block-6911df568f087ec9f3660c92"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gaza coverage.}}}\par \par \uc2 * Immediately after the letter\u8217\'20\'19s release, the BBC also airs an interview with Shah where he rejects allegations of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6911e6f38f08d3736687b113#block-6911e6f38f08d3736687b113"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic bias\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6911efb78f087ec9f3660d3b#block-6911efb78f087ec9f3660d3b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Canti-Israel bias\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  in the newsroom and says the board is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6911eac48f087ec9f3660d18#block-6911eac48f087ec9f3660d18"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cnot overly political\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * This comes amid reports, including by \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/audio/2025/nov/10/were-the-bbc-resignations-politically-orchestrated-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a political campaign orchestrated by enemies within the BBC }}}\uc2 to bring it down. Shah says he and the board did not want Davie to resign.\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-691209628f08a98bc75927eb#block-691209628f08a98bc75927eb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US President Donald Trump threatens in a letter to sue the BBC.}}}\uc2  His lawyers claim that Trump, who was indicted by Congress for efforts to overturn the 2020 vote, has been defamed by the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s Panorama episode. They issue a Friday deadline to the BBC, demanding the news organisation make amends or they will launch a $1 billion lawsuit. The BBC says it is reviewing the letter.\par \par \uc2 * The Labour government and the Lib Dems have at various points today defended the BBC. Downing St says the BBC is not corrupt not institutionally biased and plays a vital role in the age of disinformation. The Conservatives and Reform say the BBC has shown bias.\par \par \uc2 * 4.30pm: The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s deputy head of news, now temporarily in charge of editorial decisions, sends out an all-staff email announcing a pan-BBC call tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 5.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Departures 'due to a political campaign'\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like a coup,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sources from within the News department have told The Guardian. }}}\uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the result of a campaign by political enemies of the BBC.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The crisis was triggered by a Telegraph report on the leaked letter from Michael Prescott detailing errors made by \uc2 BBC News\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prescott was previously an independent adviser on the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s senior editorial guidelines and standards committee (EGSC). He left in summer after three years on the panel, and wrote a letter detailing BBC errors to the BBC board after that.\par \par \uc2 Our Media editor Michael Savage reports that the departures of Davie and Turness appear to be an attempt to protect the BBC from further attacks following Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s criticisms.\par \par \uc2 While many BBC figures believe Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s letter referred to some genuine errors, there is also concern that it has been exploited by political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Some BBC figures have pointed to an effort to shift the corporation politically, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/nov/07/boris-johnson-trying-to-undermine-bbc-leadership-insiders-fear-after-leak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dating back to Boris Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s time in government}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has been told Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s appointment as an external adviser had been pushed by the BBC board member \uc2 Robbie Gibb\uc2 , Theresa May\u8217\'20\'19s former communications chief who helped set up the rightwing broadcaster \uc2 GB News\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gibb was first placed on the BBC board during Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s time in government. Gibb and Prescott have previously been reported as being friends. Gibb was on a four-person interview panel that appointed Prescott.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/nov/10/bbc-enemies-attack-robbie-gibb-board"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The first step towards saving our precious BBC: remove Robbie Gibb from the board | Ed Davey}}}\par \par \uc2 Johnson told the Guardian that any suggestion of an attempt to undermine the BBC was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete and utter bollocks\u8221\'20\'1D. Prescott said in his letter that his criticisms \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not come with any political agenda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair attached Mr Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s letter in his submission to the parliamentary culture and media committee today. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://committees.parliament.uk/publications/50118/documents/270340/default/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 You can read it here.}}}\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile others have also described what happened as politically-motivated. David Yelland, a former Sun editor and now presenter of a BBC podcast, said the departures were \u8220\'20\'1Ca coup\u8221\'20\'1D orchestrated from the inside.\par \par \uc2 He told the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme that Davie and Turness had been systematically undermined by people close to the BBC board over a lengthy period.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC to hold all-staff call tomorrow\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s deputy head of news Jonathan Munro has just sent out an all-staff email acknowledging the crises. An all-BBC call will be held tomorrow, and another meeting for News staff on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFollowing events of the last 24 hours, I\u8217\'20\'19m writing alongside the rest of the News Board to acknowledge how difficult this situation is and reassure you that our vital and valued work providing trusted journalism continues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He says the coming weeks will be hard but \u8220\'20\'1Cour trusted, impartial and agenda-setting journalism is more important than ever and must continue as our audiences expect.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Munro also acknowledges the work of Deborah Turness, the outgoing head of news.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDetails are still being worked through about what happens next. Deborah\u8217\'20\'19s successor will be appointed and we\u8217\'20\'19ll share everything we can as soon as we know it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC concedes Trump video edit gave impression of 'direct call for violence'\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s chair today admitted an \u8220\'20\'1Cerror of judgement\u8221\'20\'1D in the editing of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 speech, which has prompted this defamation threat from the US president.\par \par \uc2 It showed two sections of the speech that were spliced together when they had been delivered an hour apart, and the edit did not note that Trump had also said he wanted his supporters to demonstrate peacefully.\par \par \uc2 Critics had said the edit made it look like Trump had encouraged the US Capitol attack. ( A reminder to readers that the US president was indeed indicted by Congress, and faced criminal charges for his efforts to overturn the 2020 election.)\par \par \uc2 The BBC\u8217\'20\'19s Chair said today that upon review of the complaint:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe accept that the\par \par \uc2 way the speech was edited did give the impression of a direct call for violent action.\par \par \uc2 The BBC would like to apologise for that error of judgement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 BBC News\uc2  has also issued the same apology in recent hours, adding that there had been no intention to mislead.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 4.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, in response to the resignations of Davie and Turness, Trump had attacked the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s journalism, and praised the Telegraph for highlighting errors.\par \par \uc2 The rightwing Telegraph last week published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/11/03/bbc-report-reveals-bias-donald-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a leaked internal report}}}\uc2  by Prescott, a former BBC standards adviser, who alleged failings in BBC coverage on transgender issues, Gaza and the edit of a Trump speech from January 6 during the Capitol Hill insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Trump wrote on Truth Social:\par \par \uc2 In showing up at New Broadcasting House this morning, the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s outgoing head of news Deborah Turness defended the organisation\u8217\'20\'19s journalists.\par \par \uc2 She also said there was \u8220\'20\'1Cno institutional bias at the BBC\u8221\'20\'1D, a sentiment also repeated by Chair \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 3.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump threatening $1 billion lawsuit against BBC\par \par \uc2 American media is reporting that the US president has threatened to sue the BBC for $1bn (\u163\'00\'A3760m) if the news organisation does not make amends by Friday.\par \par \uc2 NBC and Fox News have details of the letter sent to the BBC, which accuses the organisation of making \u8220\'20\'1Cfalse, defamatory, disparaging, and inflammatory statements\u8221\'20\'1D in the Panorama documentary edit of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 speech.\par \par \uc2 Trump has accused the BBC of defamation in a lawsuit lodged under Florida state law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDue to their salacious nature, the fabricated statements that were aired by the BBC have been widely disseminated throughout various digital mediums, which have reached tens of millions of people worldwide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CConsequently, the BBC has caused President Trump to suffer overwhelming financial and reputational harm,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter reads, according to Fox.\par \par \uc2 It demands amends from the BBC and says that: \u8220\'20\'1CIf the BBC does not comply with the above by November 14, 2025, at 5:00 p.m. EST, President Trump will be left with no alternative but to enforce his legal and equitable rights, all of which are expressly reserved and are not waived, including by filing legal action for no less than $1,000,000,000 (One Billion Dollars) in damages. The BBC is on notice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The BBC is yet to respond to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s demands.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The National Union for Journalists (\uc2 NUJ\uc2 ), which represents more than 30,000 members in media, has reacted to the resignations and set out some of the challenges the new director-general will face when they take up the job from \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a reminder, there is no timescale for when Davie will be replaced and a successor has not been chosen.\par \par \uc2 Laura Davison, \uc2 NUJ\uc2  general secretary, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nuj.org.uk/resource/nuj-reacts-to-bbc-leadership-resignations.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in a statement}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 3.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The chancellor, Rachel Reeves, has been asked about the outgoing BBC director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s resignation in an interview with BBC presenter Matt Chorley ahead of her 26 November budget.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe BBC is rightly held to high standards and they\u8217\'20\'19ve fallen short on this occasion,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLessons do need to be learned, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve got a huge amount of respect for the BBC and I know they\u8217\'20\'19ll come back from this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reeves did not comment on Donald Trump reportedly sending a letter to the BBC threatening legal action. You can read more from her interview in our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/nov/10/labour-rachel-reeves-election-manifesto-tax-budget-news-updates-uk-politics-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK politics live blog}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 3.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Former \u8211\'20\'13 and current \u8211\'20\'13 BBC journalists have reacted to the resignations of outgoing BBC director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and CEO of News Deborah Turness after accusations that the corporation was failing in its duty of impartiality.\par \par \uc2 Lewis Goodall, News Agents presenter and former Newsnight policy editor, said the editing of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech was a \u8220\'20\'1Cmistake\u8221\'20\'1D even though it may not have \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamentally\u8221\'20\'1D misrepresented the US president\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Coverall actions\u8221\'20\'1D in the lead up to January 6 2021 and on the day itself.\par \par \uc2 He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/lewis_goodall/status/1987668429789127074"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 added in a post on X}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m no fan of Davie, indeed I think he\u8217\'20\'19s brought a fair bit of this on himself, but the hysteria over this story is on another level.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Emily Maitlis, who used to present the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s flagship evening news programme Newsnight and is now a News Agents presenter alongside Goodall and Jon Sopel, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/maitlis/status/1987598792343101550"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said }}}\uc2 the story is \u8220\'20\'1Cfar more complicated than the headline resignations suggest- dramatic as those are\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 John Simpson, who joined the BBC in 1966, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/JohnSimpsonNews/status/1987623247903658258"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned}}}\uc2  that the BBC is in \u8220\'20\'1Creal danger now\u8221\'20\'1D, saying the corporation is \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing to need the support of everyone who thinks that public service broadcasting \u8230\'20\'26 is worth defending\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 James Lansdale, \uc2 BBC News\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 diplomatic correspondent, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/BBCJLandale/status/1987789023901831618"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on X}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CAmid all the guff, just this: I am proud to work for \uc2 BBC News\uc2  & even prouder to work alongside so many outstanding colleagues. We are not perfect; we must always strive to be better. But in a darkening world, we remain a shaft of light. Worth mulling. All best from Kyiv.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Key lines from BBC interview with \uc2 Samir Shah\par \par \uc2 There was a lot said by the BBC Chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , in his televised interview with Katie Razzall, the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s culture and media editor.\par \par \uc2 Just minutes after broadcast, the BBC also confirmed it had received a letter threatening legal action from US President Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 In the interview, the BBC Chair:\par \par \uc2 * Apologised for the \u8220\'20\'1Cerror of judgement\u8221\'20\'1D made in the Panorama edit of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech in the lead up to the January 6 Capitol riots, but noted that the edit had been done to \u8220\'20\'1Cconvey the message\u8221\'20\'1D of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech.\par \par \uc2 * Noted that they were prepared for the prospect \u8211\'20\'13 now confirmed \u8211\'20\'13 of Trump litigation.\par \par \uc2 * Denied any \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic bias\u8221\'20\'1D in news coverage, and denied accusations the BBC has an \u8220\'20\'1Canti-Israel bias\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Said the BBC board is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot overly political\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Stressed repeatedly that the BBC has taken action over the past three years in addressing editorial mistakes and that Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s letter did not reveal new issues, but touched on ones already looked at.\par \par \uc2 * Admitted that the range of corrective action taken might not have gone far enough.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'We addressed problems long before Prescott memo'- Shah\par \par \uc2 The Chair also rejected the accusations that the BBC had only admitted to mistakes because of the leak of Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s memo in the Daily Telegraph last week.\par \par \uc2 He says it\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Csimply not true\u8221\'20\'1D that the BBC wasn\u8217\'20\'19t being accountable to mistakes and repeated again that Prescott didn\u8217\'20\'19t unearth the issues but were privy to them precisely because of his position on the ethics and standards committee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe knew all about those issues long before Mr. Prescott said that,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, also referencing the Panorama Trump edit.\par \par \uc2 Shah says they\u8217\'20\'19ve taken action such as issuing disciplinary measures and changing leadership structures. His letter to the the parliamentary committee also specified new watchdog roles that were created to prevent further mistakes happening.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Shah rejects accusation BBC has anti-Israel bias in Middle East coverage\par \par \uc2 When asked to clarify a response on whether the BBC has that bias and failed to address it Shah said:\par \par \uc2 As we reported earlier, in his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://committees.parliament.uk/publications/50118/documents/270340/default/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter to parliament}}}\uc2  Shah identifies changes that had been made at BBC Arabic and in some longform journalism divisions \u8211\'20\'13 where corrections were issued about some Gaza reporting.\par \par \uc2 He says the BBC has put out hundreds and hundreds of hours of coverage of Israel and the Middle East and that while there have been mistakes and \u8220\'20\'1Cwe should own up to mistakes when we have them\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 those errors need to be put in the context of the news organisation\u8217\'20\'19s three years of coverage of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'We have to move forward with new leadership' - Shah\par \par \uc2 Shah is also asked how the BBC, which appears to be at its weakest point now, will forge ahead to deal with the critical negotiations of charter renewal. The current one expires at the end of 2027.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe important thing\u8230\'20\'26 is to start putting in place the new leadership as soon as is proper, and do it properly to in order to do it smoothly and make sure that we address the challenges facing us.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s never easy to be the director general or BBC director of news. So I don\u8217\'20\'19t take that lightly. But we need to do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'I did not want to lose \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 ' - Shah\par \par \uc2 Shah again vigorously defends \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , who led the corporation for five years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI did not want to lose \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, calling him an \u8220\'20\'1Coutstanding director general\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He says no one on the board wanted him to leave and \u8220\'20\'1Cwe were upset by the decision\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But he added, he understood Davie\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8220\'20\'1Cat a very human level\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTim has gone through a lot of attacks. It\u8217\'20\'19s been relentless. It\u8217\'20\'19s also the very, very difficult job to join the BBC, a huge, massive enterprise and also a lot [on] the director in terms of just his physical resilience and also emotional.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have huge admiration for what he\u8217\'20\'19s done, and it\u8217\'20\'19s an admiration shared by the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC board 'not overly political' \u8211\'20\'13 Shah\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll just delve back into that interview now because the BBC Chair made several points during it.\par \par \uc2 Firstly, when asked if he thought the BBC board was overtly political, he said: \u8220\'20\'1CNo, I do not\u8221\'20\'1D and he also dismissed the idea that there were forces working within the BBC to destroy it as \u8220\'20\'1Ca fanciful notion of what happens at the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really does have a diversity of views, as there are in the public, in the country.\u8221\'20\'1DWhen questioned if the board had wanted the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s Head of News, Deborah Turness, to leave, he said this was not up to the board.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s be clear about the role of the board. We are a non-executive with ten of us a non-executive for exactly. The job of the board is to review BBC output and to maintain and to represent the public interest. It is not our job to determine who who should not be the director of news. That is the job of the director general.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the interview broadcast just 20 minutes ago on \uc2 BBC News\uc2 , Shah had suggested that the corporation was perhaps bracing for a Trump legal case.\par \par \uc2 Trump was a \u8220\'20\'1Clitigious fellow\u8221\'20\'1D, so \u8220\'20\'1Cwe should be prepared for all outcomes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump threatening legal action against the BBC\par \par \uc2 The BBC has confirmed it has received a letter from the US president threatening a legal case.\par \par \uc2 Trump has sued several US outlets so far in his second term \u8211\'20\'13 some cases of which have been thrown out in the courts already.\par \par \uc2 Disney{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/dec/14/abc-george-stephanopoulos-trump-15-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid $15m}}}\uc2  to the Trump presidential foundation and another $1m for his lawyer fees to resolve a defamation case involving ABC News anchor George Stephanopoulos last year, while Meta agreed to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jan/29/meta-trump-settlement-25-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $25m settlement}}}\uc2  over Trump\u8217\'20\'19s social media account suspension after the 6 January riot at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Trump settled a legal dispute with CBS News over an interview it broadcast on its 60 Minutes programme with former vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . He is currently engaged in lawsuits with the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Shah rejects Prescott's allegation of 'systemic bias' at \uc2 BBC News\par \par \uc2 He says that the ethics and standards committee to which Prescott was party to, and on which Shah himself sits, has taken significant action in addressing complaints on both specific stories and underlying problems in coverage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll these stories.. and issues that Mr Prescott raises have been considered\u8230\'20\'26 indeed that is how he knows about them because he is a member of the committee\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He says there\u8217\'20\'19s been an impression that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19ve listened to the concerns and done nothing\u8221\'20\'1D and that is not accurate, Shah said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have taken action- it ranges from all sorts of things, from overseeing corrections where we have got it wrong to\u8230\'20\'26 more extreme cases,\u8221\'20\'1D he adds, pointing out changes in team leadership.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s fair to say that this impression that we\u8217\'20\'19re not doing anything is not true.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC runs interview with chair\par \par \uc2 The BBC news channel has run an interview between chair \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  and their media editor Katie Razzall in the immediate aftermath of the release of Shah\u8217\'20\'19s letter to parliament.\par \par \uc2 He has apologised for the Panorama edit of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech, adding that he\u8217\'20\'19s received communications from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s team over it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have received communication from President Trump and his people, and we are now considering how to how to reply to him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked if the US president was suing the BBC, Shah said: I do not know that yet. But he\u8217\'20\'19s a litigious fellow. So we should be prepared for all outcomes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC not corrupt or institutionally biased, Downing Street says\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has said the BBC is not corrupt nor institutionally biased, as the corporation comes under intense pressure for its coverage of a range of issues.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s official spokesperson told reporters:\par \par \uc2 On the future of the BBC licence fee, the spokesperson reportedly refused to confirm whether or not it was on the table as part of a review of the royal charter (see post at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6911d5668f08a98bc75924d2#block-6911d5668f08a98bc75924d2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12.18}}}\uc2  for more information about the charter).\par \par \uc2 Downing Street said it was considering how the BBC can \u8220\'20\'1Cprosper\u8221\'20\'1D with a \u8220\'20\'1Csustainable funding model\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Action taken at BBC Arabic, Shah says\par \par \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  says that the Editorial Guidelines and Standards Committee had, on top of specific story complaints, also identified \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerns about underlying problems \u8211\'20\'13 for example in the Arabic service or long form journalism.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He says \u8220\'20\'1Caction has been taken to tackle this kind of problem\u8221\'20\'1D at BBC Arabic, which is the BBC World Service\u8217\'20\'19s Arabic language channel. This included:\par \par \uc2 * A restructuring of the BBC Arabic team\par \par \uc2 * A new Arabic-speaking Head of Editorial Quality and Standards appointed in the World Service\par \par \uc2 * A social media research unit set up to verify those who appear on BBC Arabic.\par \par \uc2 One of the complaints about BBC Arabic was that it had given a platform to antisemitic voices in their coverage of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Suggestion that the BBC has tried to bury stories is 'simply not true', chairman says\par \par \uc2 In his letter, Shah also notes that the coverage of Michael Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s leaked memo suggested that he had \u8220\'20\'1Cuncovered\u8221\'20\'1D a list of stories and issues that the BBC had tried to \u8220\'20\'1Cbury\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat interpretation is simply not true,\u8221\'20\'1D Shah said.\par \par \uc2 He went on to say that the BBC has tried to be transparent and tackle criticism head on by issuing corrections when journalists have been inaccurate, altering guidance to make the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s stance on issues \u8220\'20\'1Cclearer\u8221\'20\'1D and changing leadership when necessary.\par \par \uc2 Shah said the Editorial Guidelines and Standards Committee (EGSC) accepts that there are \u8220\'20\'1Coccasions when the BBC gets things wrong\u8221\'20\'1D or its reporting \u8220\'20\'1Crequires more context and explanation\u8221\'20\'1D. He cites the casualty figures given when reporting on Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war on Gaza and the international court of justice order as examples.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Shah adds that the Panorama clip of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech was discussed by the ethics and standards committee in January and again in May.\par \par \uc2 The committee also heard a defence from \uc2 BBC News\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe purpose of editing the clip, was to convey the message of the speech made by President Trump so that Panorama\u8217\'20\'19s audience could better understand how it had been received by President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s supporters and what has happening on the ground at that time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The edit was considered and discussed as part of a wider review into the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s US election coverage, Shah said, rather than handed as part of a specific programme complaint.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe points raised in the review were relayed to the Panorama team, including the decision making on this edit. With hindsight, it would have been better to take more formal action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 12.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Edit of Trump speech was an 'error of judgement' , BBC chair admits\par \par \uc2 The BBC chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  has sent a written statement to the Culture, Media and Sport Committee, which scrutinises the work of the \uc2 Department for Culture, Media and Sport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In it, Shah accepted that the editing of Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s speech for Panorama was an \u8220\'20\'1Cerror of judgment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Two sections of the speech that were spliced together were made an hour apart, and the edit did not note that Trump had also said he wanted his supporters to demonstrate peacefully. The BBC was accused of editing it in a way that looked as if Trump had explicitly encouraged the Capitol riots.\par \par \uc2 Shah wrote:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The resignations of outgoing director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and CEO of News Deborah Turness come as the BBC heads into crucial talks with the government over the renewal of its charter, which sets the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s mission, public purposes and funding.\par \par \uc2 Last November, the culture secretary, \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2 , said the government would use a review of the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s royal charter to consider alternative ways of funding the corporation.\par \par \uc2 She has ruled out using general taxation to fund the BBC, saying this would leave it too open to interference from the government of the day, and insisted the public should be involved in decisions on the licence fee.\par \par \uc2 The government has committed to increasing the licence fee in line with inflation each year until 2027.\par \par \uc2 The BBC has been increasingly cash-strapped, following years when the licence was frozen, and has made sweeping cuts to the organisation as it desperately searches for savings.\par \par \uc2 Half a million people cancelled their licence fee in 2023, as younger audiences {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/jul/23/half-million-households-cancelled-bbc-licence-fee-last-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 move towards YouTube and streamers}}}\uc2  and many people get their news from social media or alternative outlets.\par \par \uc2 12.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Reform UK leader \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2  claimed the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Chas been institutionally biased for decades\u8221\'20\'1D as he appeared at a press conference in central London.\par \par \uc2 Farage also said he had spoken with his ally Donald Trump on Friday after concerns were raised about the way a speech by the US president was edited for Panorama.\par \par \uc2 The Reform UK leader said:\par \par \uc2 My colleague Andrew Sparrow has some more of Farage\u8217\'20\'19s comments in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/nov/10/labour-rachel-reeves-election-manifesto-tax-budget-news-updates-uk-politics-live?page=with%3Ablock-6911cfae8f087ec9f3660bbb#block-6911cfae8f087ec9f3660bbb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his UK politics live blog}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Farage said last year half a million people stopped paying the licence fee adding that millions more people could start doing the same if the culture within the coopration remains the same.\par \par \uc2 He say he would like to see the BBC \u8220\'20\'1Cslimmed down\u8221\'20\'1D and to only do \u8220\'20\'1Cstraight news\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that the BBC World Service is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery important\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Farage said:\par \par \uc2 Farage said Davie should be replaced as director-general by \u8220\'20\'1Csomebody from the private sector who has gone into a failing organisation and has not just turned round the profitability, but the culture\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Reform has managed to capitalise on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/sep/09/how-keir-starmers-polling-became-one-of-the-worst-in-the-west-in-charts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record unpopularity for the new Labour government}}}\uc2  and is leading in many opinion polls, meaning Farage could be the UK\u8217\'20\'19s next prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump's assault on the BBC is a serious threat to our national interest, Lib Dem leader says\par \par \uc2 Ed Davey, the Lib Dem leader, has written to the UK\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , Conservative party leader \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  and Reform leader \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2  after what he describes as Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cassault on the BBC\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He wrote in his letter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/EdwardJDavey/status/1987843483349000328/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted to X}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Davey defended the BBC as a public broadcaster in the UK and said that as a trusted source of information helps project Britain\u8217\'20\'19s soft power around the globe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have watched with alarm as Trump and his allies have undermined the free press in the United States, and all the damage that has done to American democracy. We cannot let that happen here,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 His comments come after Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s press secretary, Karoline Leavitt, described the BBC as \u8220\'20\'1C100% fake news\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cpropaganda machine\u8221\'20\'1D in an interview with the Telegraph, in which she said watching BBC bulletins while on trips to the UK \u8220\'20\'1Cruins\u8221\'20\'1D her day and said taxpayers were being \u8220\'20\'1Cforced to foot the bill for a leftist propaganda machine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 11.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Kevin Rawlinson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/10/bbc-resignations-internal-coup-says-former-sun-editor-david-yelland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 notes in this story}}}\uc2 , BBC chair \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  is expected to apologise today to the Commons\u8217\'20\'19 culture, media and sport committee, and to provide further details on the Panorama episode in his response to the Commons culture media and sport committee, which had asked how he would address the concerns.\par \par \uc2 10.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mark Urban, a former presenter on Newsnight, the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s flagship daily current affairs show, has said the corporation has changed in recent years, suggesting that this reflects generational tensions within the newsroom.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe BBC, including Deborah Turness, were not willing to listen to and act sufficiently and energetically upon the recommendations of their own internal system for checking bias,\u8221\'20\'1D Urban told Sky News.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow we saw that leaked memo with a long list of things. We\u8217\'20\'19ve all focused on the Trump interview, but there was stuff about gender issues, there was stuff about race, there was stuff about Gaza and I think when you put all those things collectively together, we understand.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Urban added:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 10.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here are some more comments from the outgoing \uc2 BBC News\uc2  head Deborah Turness, who answered questions on her way into BBC broadcasting house in central London this morning.Journalist: Do you think that the journalists are corrupt like President Trump says?\par \par \uc2 Turness:Of course our journalists aren\u8217\'20\'19t corrupt. Our journalists are hardworking people who strive for impartiality. And I will stand by their journalism.\par \par \uc2 Journalist: Is there institutional bias at the BBC?\par \par \uc2 Turness:There is no institutional bias. Mistakes are made, but there\u8217\'20\'19s no institutional bias.\par \par \uc2 Journalist:But why weren\u8217\'20\'19t the mistakes dealt with on Trump, on antisemitism, on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights?\par \par \uc2 Turness:I\u8217\'20\'19m sure that story will emerge. But for now, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to go in and see our teams.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC director general was slow to act, culture committee chair says\par \par \uc2 We have heard from Culture, Media and Sport committee chair Caroline Dinenage, who has suggested that the outgoing BBC director \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  ignored an internal dossier into bias at the BBC (see post at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6911a7ee8f082a732225a43a#block-6911a7ee8f082a732225a43a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 09.06}}}\uc2  for more detail on the dossier).\par \par \uc2 She said Davie \u8220\'20\'1Cignored\u8221\'20\'1D concerns raised in Michael Prescott\u8217\'20\'19s report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/03/bbc-accused-selectively-editing-trump-clip-capitol-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over the way the speech by Donald Trump was edited for Panorama}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dinenage said she is expecting a letter for BBC Chair \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2  later today, but confirmed that it has not yet arrived.\par \par \uc2 She told BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today:\par \par \uc2 Dinenage added that she thinks it seems \u8220\'20\'1Ca little bit odd\u8221\'20\'1D that her committee has not yet heard from Shah, who is expected to apologise for the way a speech by Donald Trump to crowds at the Capitol on January 6 2021 was edited for current affairs programme Panorama.\par \par \uc2 9.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC News\uc2  is not institutionally biased, Deborah Turness says\par \par \uc2 Deborah Turness, the outgoing CEO of news, said the following as she arrived at the BBC this morning in London:\par \par \uc2 She was quoted as having said that \u8220\'20\'1Cour journalists aren\u8217\'20\'19t corrupt and I will stand by their journalism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Turness made her name as editor of ITV News, before moving to New York in 2013 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/theguardian/2013/may/31/guardian-profile-deborah-turness-itv-nbc-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to run the sprawling NBC News}}}\uc2  operation.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, Turness returned to ITN \u8211\'20\'13 who make news programmes for \uc2 ITV\uc2 , Channel 4, and Channel 5 \u8211\'20\'13 as chief executive.\par \par \uc2 She has been the CEO of \uc2 BBC News\uc2  since 2022, overseeing \uc2 BBC News\uc2  and Current Affairs programming and having responsibility for a team of about 6,000 people.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 What sort of bias has the BBC been accused of?\par \par \uc2 The Telegraph\u8217\'20\'19s leaked BBC memo suggested a range of issues at the BBC, not just the way Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Capitol speech had been selectively edited for the Panorama programme.\par \par \uc2 The leaked memo came from Michael Prescott, a former independent standards adviser to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s editorial guidelines and standards committee.\par \par \uc2 Prescott left his role earlier this year and has not commented on the document, understood to have been leaked by a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 The Telegraph said Prescott alleged there were \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic problems\u8221\'20\'1D, which had not been addressed by senior management, claiming there were \u8220\'20\'1Cstark differences\u8221\'20\'1D between the coverage of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war on Gaza on BBC Arabic and on the main BBC website. One issue Prescott reportedly highlighted is the repeated use of commentators who were antisemitic or pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As my colleague {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/08/bbc-100-fake-news-says-donald-trumps-press-secretary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 notes in this story}}}\uc2 , the 19-page dossier is also reported to have criticised the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s coverage of transgender issues, saying the broadcaster had been \u8220\'20\'1Ccaptured by a small group of [staff] promoting the Stonewall view\u8221\'20\'1D of gender identity issues and that its LGBT desk would \u8220\'20\'1Cdecline to cover any stories raising difficult questions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It is said to have alleged that stories raising complex questions about trans issues were suppressed, with a \u8220\'20\'1Cconstant drip-feed of one-sided stories celebrating the trans experience without adequate balance or objectivity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 8.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 We mentioned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/live/2025/nov/10/bbc-tim-davie-resigns-bias-panorama-donald-trump-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-691184d28f082a732225a397#block-691184d28f082a732225a397"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in an earlier post}}}\uc2  that the Conservative party leader, \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2 , said it was right that the BBC director general \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and CEO of News Deborah Turness had resigned as she accused the corporation of being \u8220\'20\'1Cfull of bias\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Here is her statement, posted on social media, in full:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Davie's resignation 'a failure of governance'\par \par \uc2 The resignations of \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and Deborah Turness was \u8220\'20\'1Ca coup\u8221\'20\'1D, a former newspaper editor has said.\par \par \uc2 David Yelland, who edited The Sun from 1998 to 2003, told BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme that their departure \u8220\'20\'1Cwas a coup, and worse than that, it was an inside job\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat has happened here is there was a failure of governance,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t blame the chairman [\uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 ] as an individual, but the job of the chair of any organisation, a company \u8211\'20\'13 including the BBC \u8211\'20\'13 is to keep their CEO, their top man or woman, in post or fire them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that has not happened, because \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  was not fired. He walked and so there was, that is the definition of a failure of governance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 Government minister Louise Sandher-Jones has rejected suggestions the BBC was institutionally biased.\par \par \uc2 The veterans minister told Sky News:\par \par \uc2 Asked about Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments on the BBC, she said:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump welcomes Davie's resignation\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump welcomed the resignations of \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2  and Deborah Turness. He wrote on his Truth Social platform last night that the way his speech had been edited by Panorama was an attempt to \u8220\'20\'1Cstep on the scales of a presidential election\u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat a terrible thing for Democracy!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The edit suggested Trump told the crowd: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to walk down to the Capitol and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be there with you, and we fight. We fight like hell.\u8221\'20\'1D The words were taken from sections of his speech almost an hour apart.\par \par \uc2 The programme was aired last year, a week before the US election.\par \par \uc2 7.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 Who is \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The media boss, who was responsible for the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s global workforce, has steered the corporation through years of crisis and controversy. Read our profile here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-the-marketing-man-who-became-the-bbcs-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim Davie: the marketing man who became the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s director general}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Reaction to the resignation of \uc2 Tim Davie\par \par \uc2 Lisa Nandy\uc2 , culture secretary, thanked Davie for his work and said the government would help the BBC secure \u8220\'20\'1Cits role at the heart of national life for decades to come\u8221\'20\'1D. She said: \u8220\'20\'1CNow more than ever, the need for trusted news and high quality programming is essential to our democratic and cultural life, and our place in the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2 , Tory leader, claimed the BBC was full of \u8220\'20\'1Cinstitutional bias,\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe new leadership must now deliver genuine reform of the culture of the BBC, top to bottom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lib Dem leader Sir Ed Davey said the corporation needs \u8220\'20\'1Cto turn a new leaf\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Reform leader \uc2 Nigel Farage\uc2  calls it the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clast chance\u8221\'20\'1D, saying the two resignations must be \u8220\'20\'1Cthe start of wholesale change\u8221\'20\'1D at the corporation.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 BBC chair expected to apologise for Panorama's Trump edit\par \par \uc2 Hello and welcome to our coverage of developments at the BBC, where the director general, \uc2 Tim Davie\uc2 , and its CEO of news, Deborah Turness, have resigned after accusations of bias and misleading the public in its coverage of issues including Donald Trump, Gaza and trans rights.\par \par \uc2 In an announcement that caused shock within the corporation, Davie said his departure was \u8220\'20\'1Centirely my decision\u8221\'20\'1D. Davie said his departure would not be immediate and that he was \u8220\'20\'1Cworking through\u8221\'20\'1D timings to ensure an \u8220\'20\'1Corderly transition\u8221\'20\'1D over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The BBC chair, \uc2 Samir Shah\uc2 , is expected to apologise on Monday for the way a speech by Donald Trump to crowds at the Capitol on January 6 2021 was edited for current affairs programme Panorama after several days of pressure on the broadcaster prompted the resignation of Davie and Turness.\par \par \uc2 Turness said controversy around the Panorama edit had \u8220\'20\'1Creached a stage where it is causing damage to the BBC \u8211\'20\'13 an institution that I love\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us for the latest developments throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/nov/09/tim-davie-expected-to-resign-bbc-director-general"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim Davie resigns as BBC director general after accusations of \u8216\'20\'18serious and systemic\u8217\'20\'19 bias in coverage}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251110elba001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
