\par\par \uc2 Life and style\par \b \uc2 A new start after 60: I found my feet in midlife, became a park ranger at 85 \u8211\'20\'13 and retired happily at 100\b0\par\par\uc2 Paula Cocozza \par \uc2 1,415 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2025\par 07:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 At 104, Betty Reid Soskin has had the most extraordinary life, from protest singing to civil rights activism to meeting the Obamas. She reflects on what it takes to stay strong and keep going\par \par \uc2 Betty Reid Soskin was 92 when she first went viral and became, in effect, a rock star of the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 . She was the oldest full-time national park ranger in the US \u8211\'20\'13 this was back in 2013; she\u8217\'20\'19d become a ranger at 85 \u8211\'20\'13 but she had been furloughed along with 800,000 other federal employees during the government shutdown. News channels flocked to interview her. She was aggrieved not to be working, she told them; she had a job to do.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a funny way, I suppose that started lots of things,\u8221\'20\'1D Soskin says. Her memoir, Sign My Name to Freedom, was published in 2018, and a documentary about her work, No Time to Waste, was released in 2020. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.signmynametofreedom.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Another film}}}\uc2  is in the works. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  called her \u8220\'20\'1Cprofoundly inspiring\u8221\'20\'1D. Annie Leibovitz photographed her. Glamour magazine named her woman of the year. Now, Reid Soskin is 104, and \u8220\'20\'1Call of whatever I was supposed to do, I\u8217\'20\'19ve done\u8221\'20\'1D, she says.\par \par \uc2 She retired as a ranger at 100, having helped to establish the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nps.gov/rori/index.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rosie the Riveter World War II Home Front}}}\uc2  national park in Richmond, California, where she shared, day after day, the wartime experiences of people of colour, because \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat gets remembered is determined by who\u8217\'20\'19s in the room doing the remembering\u8221\'20\'1D. At the time, she joked that her job was almost \u8220\'20\'1Clike I\u8217\'20\'19m running a federally funded revolution ... I was very aware that I was in my 90s and I really didn\u8217\'20\'19t have time to waste,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Understandably, Soskin\u8217\'20\'19s sense of time has evolved since entering her second century, softening into something more amorphous. \u8220\'20\'1CNow that I\u8217\'20\'19ve held on a few more years, I really do feel old,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CMemories are getting dimmer and dimmer, and events feel as if they happened yesterday \u8230\'20\'26 and simultaneously many years ago. Time has collapsed in on itself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Political events, too \u8211\'20\'13 she mentions \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s deployment of the national guard to US cities \u8211\'20\'13 are collapsing \u8220\'20\'1Cin on themselves. And I feel as though it\u8217\'20\'19s all of a piece.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Soskin is still not sitting back. \u8220\'20\'1CI follow politics very closely,\u8221\'20\'1D she says on a video call from her home in Richmond, where she lives with her daughter, Di\u8217\'20\'19ara. \u8220\'20\'1CEven going through the 50s and the 60s with civil rights, that was all [progress],\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t feel as if that\u8217\'20\'19s so now ... It\u8217\'20\'19s seemed to me that [Trump] has no idea what he\u8217\'20\'19s doing. I think we\u8217\'20\'19ve lost our sense of direction. And that\u8217\'20\'19s terrifying to me, because I\u8217\'20\'19m going to leave the world in such a shape.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI find myself wondering what [the world] is going to look like and I don\u8217\'20\'19t have any idea. This is a time of chaos \u8230\'20\'26 We grow through life always thinking there\u8217\'20\'19s something better ahead. And for the first time in my life, I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure there is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Soskin was born Betty Charbonnet, and grew up initially in New Orleans; the family moved to Oakland, California, after the floods of 1927. Her father came from a Creole background, her mother a Cajun background, and her great-grandmother, who lived to 102, had been born into slavery in 1846. But after she came to public attention, new episodes of Soskin\u8217\'20\'19s long and varied life kept coming to the fore. There are many different Bettys \u8211\'20\'13 she refers to herself as Betty, as a way to uncouple herself from the men in her life \u8211\'20\'13 and for a long time, she says, she didn\u8217\'20\'19t know \u8220\'20\'1Cwho Betty was\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There was the Betty who had opened Reid\u8217\'20\'19s Records in 1945, one of the first black record shops in California, with her then husband, Mel Reid. There was Betty the singer of protest songs, who surfaced on social media a few years ago in a set of reel-to-reel tapes recorded in her 30s. There was Betty the civil rights and community activist who raised funds for the Black Panthers and later helped to tackle the drug trade in the area around Reid\u8217\'20\'19s Records. There was Betty who worked in local government as a legislative aide. All this before she became famous as the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s oldest, and possibly most outspoken, ranger.\par \par \uc2 It was when \u8220\'20\'1Cthe three men\u8221\'20\'1D in her life died \u8211\'20\'13 Reid; her second husband, the psychologist William Soskin; and her father \u8211\'20\'13 in a three-month period in the late 1980s, that Reid Soskin\u8217\'20\'19s life was transformed. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like I stepped out of one life and went into another,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was actually when my life started. Because I didn\u8217\'20\'19t really know who I was until then. Then I became Betty. Oh, that was wonderful. I really began to see myself as being a part of the world. I began to be in my own shoes. I had things to do \u8211\'20\'13 and that lasted until I was 100. I went on doing things. I was no longer becoming, I was simply being.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2015, she was asked to introduce Obama at the national Christmas tree-lighting ceremony in Washington DC. (She had previously declined an invitation to the White House from \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI remember being led to where [the Obamas] were standing between two flags. And rather than look at the president, I was looking at her [Michelle] and saying out loud, \u8216\'20\'18You are so beautiful.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Soskin had in her pocket that day a photograph of her great-grandmother, Leontine Breaux Allen, whom Betty knew well into her own 20s. I stood there like a page of history,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI was standing beside the president of the United States. I was standing within the shadow of the White House. And it was built by slaves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She holds a commemorative coin up to her camera: Obama slipped one just like it into her hand when he shook it. The original was stolen in a house burglary, so this one is a replacement. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s ever anything that matches the lived moment,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Mementoes are \u8220\'20\'1Clike ashes. They\u8217\'20\'19re simply symbols of what was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Soskin says she no longer thinks of herself as a feminist or an activist, just \u8220\'20\'1Cas a person. I never did like labels.\u8221\'20\'1D She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even regard herself as \u8220\'20\'1Ca singer\u8221\'20\'1D; simply as \u8220\'20\'1Ca Betty who sang\u8221\'20\'1D. She started composing songs on a guitar, a Christmas gift from Reid, when their marriage was disintegrating. \u8220\'20\'1CI was in the middle of a breakdown, and I thought I was remembering things,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, realising only much later that she was actually creating them \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cand seemed to have captured all the things that were important\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She could have been a successful singer. She once performed with Pete Seeger, and after the film-maker Henry Hampton heard her at a Unitarian convention, she says, \u8220\'20\'1Che convinced me that I could sing, and he had me come to Connecticut. I spent two weeks with a musical director.\u8221\'20\'1D But on the eve of her audition tosing at the Village Vanguard in New York she says, she decided to go home instead. She had been to a party in New York: \u8220\'20\'1CI found myself in a room filled with people who were using marijuana. I had never seen it. And I decided then that wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the world for me. I went home.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Soskin only sings now, she says, when she\u8217\'20\'19s asleep: \u8220\'20\'1CI remember every line of everything in my dreams.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In an age when longevity is prized, how does she feel about living so long? \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s given to us. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know that I could have controlled what I\u8217\'20\'19m doing or how I\u8217\'20\'19m living. I just \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t. I think it\u8217\'20\'19s a gift. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know where it will lead or where it\u8217\'20\'19s going to take me. I have no idea. Except it\u8217\'20\'19s off. It\u8217\'20\'19s running free.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2021/may/04/tell-us-has-your-life-taken-a-new-direction-after-the-age-of-65"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tell us: has your life taken a new direction after the age of 60?}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251110elba0018p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
