\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 ShutdownTok: federal workers detail life without pay amid government\u8217\'20\'19s closure\b0\par\par\uc2 Marina Dunbar \par \uc2 1,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2025\par 12:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal employees on social media share practical tips and document lives amid longest US shutdown in history\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown stretches on after becoming the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/04/government-shutdown-longest-history"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longest in history}}}\uc2 , influencers and content creators have stepped into improvised public-service roles by chronicling life without paychecks.\par \par \uc2 Across TikTok, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , federal workers have been publishing \u8220\'20\'1Cshutdown vlogs\u8221\'20\'1D that mix dark humor with practical tips. The federal shutdown has created its own internet sub-genre of resource directories, budget-stretching advice threads and mental health check-ins.\par \par \uc2 Courteney Bush, a longtime federal employee, is chronicling daily life and coping tactics in a TikTok series called the \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@courteney.bee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shutdown Diaries}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. She is a public affairs specialist for the federal government in Washington DC and has worked in communications since 2011.\par \par \uc2 Bush described coping as keeping busy to avoid going awry. She rated her mood during the shutdown as a \u8220\'20\'1CB-minus\u8221\'20\'1D overall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a day-by-day process. I\u8217\'20\'19m just trying to find the positives every day,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all trying to think of this as normally as possible, so that we don\u8217\'20\'19t spiral out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She is one of many individuals who are documenting their journeys and \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@alexis_town18"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shutdown shenanigans}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D as furloughed employees who are aiming to find new routines amid frozen paychecks.\par \par \uc2 She began her TikTok series organically, already regularly sharing day-in-the-life videos. Having already endured three extended shutdowns as a government worker, she saw it as both a creative outlet and a daily purpose.\par \par \uc2 And the trend has reached far beyond just TikTok. Reddit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reddit.com/r/FedEmployees/comments/1oeawa9/furloughed_workers_what_are_you_doing_during_the/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 communities}}}\uc2  for federal workers have become informative guides for financial hardship programs, legal updates and day-to-day coping advice. Similarly, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/aubrey__daly/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Instagram}}}\uc2  accounts have popped up to chronicle users\u8217\'20\'19 shutdown experiences.\par \par \uc2 Essential workers who aren\u8217\'20\'19t furloughed are also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@heavunamari/video/7556966357678492983?_r=1&_t=ZP-90M7Rlmmu9m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posting videos}}}\uc2  about the trials and tribulations of having to go into work while not receiving a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the beauties of social media is that you can build community with people you\u8217\'20\'19ve never met in real life, and connect deeply just through shared experience,\u8221\'20\'1D Bush said. The response to her videos have been overwhelmingly positive, she says, and she\u8217\'20\'19s heard from viewers that her videos helped them feel \u8220\'20\'1Cless alone\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@everydaydrakes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deidre Drakes}}}\uc2 , an army wife, the shutdown is absurd enough to warrant satire. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can either stress about things or laugh,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I figure laughing burns more calories, so I\u8217\'20\'19ll just do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Drakes\u8217\'20\'19s videos mix comedy and brutal honesty. Her first shutdown video joked: \u8220\'20\'1CI may have to do something strange for some change, since the government won\u8217\'20\'19t pay us.\u8221\'20\'1D The line resonated widely. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople were very supportive \u8230\'20\'26 they were sorry my family had to go through this, and they were reposting, sharing and just sending words of encouragement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her following has grown significantly since. \u8220\'20\'1CI leaned into the funny and leaned into the realness behind what people are feeling,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, a lot of people are going through this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Drakes also sees social media as a crucial support network, an outlet for \u8220\'20\'1Cgiving information and finding that community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now, with a pause in Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (Snap) benefits looming, several creators have devoted their accounts to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2025/10/28/snap-advice-social-media/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 giving advice}}}\uc2  for people whose food security is threatened. Some tutorials aim to keep a household fed for as little as $40 a week if benefits lapse.\par \par \uc2 It has inspired a surge of \u8220\'20\'1Ccheap meal\u8221\'20\'1D TikTok content, centering on viral clips where creators demonstrate ultra-budget cooking and share lived experience with food insecurity. Some creators have even assembled and circulated a living list of free resources for furloughed federal workers, food banks, bill-pay assistance and neighborhood-level aid.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@lifewithleauxla"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lola Ajayi}}}\uc2 , a DC-area content creator, has turned her platforms on TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  into a hub for information. Known for covering life in the Washington metropolitan area, she opted to create a now viral {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1p6ku9Jie4fEGPBRQwQIKZmxAS-QFPDHXn64GSkvZxvI/edit?tab=t.0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resource list}}}\uc2  for federal workers in and around DC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the government shutdown first started, I thought people may not necessarily know about the resources,\u8221\'20\'1D Ajayi said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I decided to put out a video with the few resources that I had found at the time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her \uc2 Google\uc2  document of local resources quickly became an ongoing project that she updates every day. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I see something on social media, I\u8217\'20\'19ll verify it and add it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The response was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery overwhelming, very positive\u8221\'20\'1D. Even those outside DC reached out, such as a government employee in Salt Lake City struggling to pay rent. \u8220\'20\'1CThey thanked me for just making my video,\u8221\'20\'1D Ajayi said. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople were sharing it with their neighbors and friends.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@r.oh.bee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Robert Perez}}}\uc2 , an ultramarathoner and government contract worker, the shutdown inspired an endurance challenge. Perez runs a mile each day the government remains closed and films a video explaining a new aspect of the shutdown while he does it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn day one of the shutdown, I just had this idea,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI thought, if it gets up to 30 days, I could do a mile every day. It was a good time to study up on my own civics lessons.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 His videos are intentionally focused on being informative rather than taking a political stance. \u8220\'20\'1CI figured if I do it in a way that\u8217\'20\'19s fair to both sides, people can\u8217\'20\'19t really get upset,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. The series became a hit, with Perez\u8217\'20\'19s account having nearly 100,000 followers on TikTok alone. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been 99% support. People are learning, people are appreciative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And like many creators, he hopes his videos leave a lasting legacy on what the shutdown looked like and how it affected people across the US. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s tough to know that all because of 500-something members of Congress \u8230\'20\'26 the rest of the country is suffering,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI wish people understood that as the shutdown goes on, that suffering is going to continue to grow.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Drakes agrees with this sentiment. \u8220\'20\'1CSocial media is such a big outlet,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1COnce you see something that resonates with you, you\u8217\'20\'19ll say: \u8216\'20\'18Oh man, I thought I was going through this alone.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251108elb8001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
