\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  says UK inflation has peaked after leaving rates at 4%; US job cuts jump as firms turn to AI \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 4,857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2025\par 16:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists predict UK rate cuts in December, after narrow vote to hold Bank Rate at 4% today\par \par \uc2 4.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  has opened the door to a December interest rate cut after signalling inflation has peaked as it kept borrowing costs unchanged before \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s make-or-break budget.\par \par \uc2 With less than three weeks before the chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s tax and spending statement, the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee (MPC) voted by a narrow five-four majority to keep borrowing costs unchanged for a second consecutive meeting.\par \par \uc2 However, City economists said the knife-edge decision and the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s latest predictions for a fall in inflation from the current rate of 3.8% would pave the way for Threadneedle Street to cut rates after the budget.\par \par \uc2 Holding the casting vote, \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2 , the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s governor, said he wanted to \u8220\'20\'1Cwait and see\u8221\'20\'1D whether inflationary pressures would continue to fade and if Reeves\u8217\'20\'19s budget would have an impact.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe held interest rates at 4% today. We still think rates are on a gradual path downwards, but we need to be sure that inflation is on track to return to our 2% target before we cut them again,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors were encouraged that the Bank predicted inflation has peaked, which may pave the way for rate cuts soon \u8211\'20\'13 depending on the economic impact of the budget\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 4.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Another bout of selling is hitting the financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 index of US-listed shares is now down 1%, with the Nasdaq index down 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average is down 0.7%, led by \uc2 Salesforce.com\uc2  (-6.8%) and \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  (-2.5%).\par \par \uc2 Stocks wobbled earlier this week amid worries that a boom in valuations of artificial intelligence (AI) companies could be rapidly cooling.\par \par \uc2 3.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Rathbones\uc2 : More cuts are on the way\par \par \uc2 John Wyn-Evans, head of market analysis at wealth manager \uc2 Rathbones\uc2 , has dubbed today\u8217\'20\'19s decision a \u8216\'20\'18dovish hold\u8217\'20\'19, with the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  playing second fiddle to Budget:\par \par \uc2 3.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 3.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BT sheds broadband customers as housebuilding stumbles\par \par \uc2 In the UK telecoms world, BT has lost a wave of broadband customers to rivals in recent months amid a \u8220\'20\'1Ccompetitive\u8221\'20\'1D market, and revealed another 5,000 jobs have gone under its cost-cutting drive.\par \par \uc2 The telecoms giant said its Openreach broadband customers fell by 242,000 in the second quarter, also because of a weaker broadband market.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive \uc2 Allison Kirkby\uc2  explained that the market had ben flat to slightly down over the past year. She said some of this was \u8220\'20\'1Ccoming off the peaks of Covid, when people put second lines into their home so they could work from home,\u8221\'20\'1D and some of it was related to cost of living pressures. But, she added:\par \par \uc2 BT\u8217\'20\'19s revenues declined by 3% year-on-year to \u163\'00\'A39.8bn in the six months to 30 September. This was driven by declines in its legacy landline service as well as a weaker mobile phone market as people are holding onto their devices for longer. Its pre-tax profit slid by 11% to \u163\'00\'A3862m.\par \par \uc2 Kirkby said \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople are just generally holding onto their devices for longer,\u8221\'20\'1D encouraged by financing schemes launched by BT and others, but also because handsets \u8220\'20\'1Care becoming ever more expensive, and there\u8217\'20\'19s just not the same innovation in them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 BT did have a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery positive reaction\u8221\'20\'1D to the new \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPhone 17, and was pre-selling it ahead of other UK operators, Kirkby said.\par \par \uc2 BT has pushed ahead with a turnaround plan that involves slashing costs and sharpening its focus on the UK and newer business lines. It said it cut its total workforce by 6% in the first half of the year, to 111,000 people. The job cuts form part of a programme to cut costs by \u163\'00\'A33bn a year. So far it has achieved \u163\'00\'A31.2bn over the first 18 months.\par \par \uc2 3.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sterling has gained half a cent against the US dollar today, after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  narrowly failed to cut interest rates today.\par \par \uc2 The pound is trading around $1.31 today, a cent higher than the seven-month low hit earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 3.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK borrowing costs have dipped today.\par \par \uc2 The prices of government bonds (gilts) have risen, which pushes down the yield (interest rate) on the debt, as investors have anticipated interest rate cuts as soon as December.\par \par \uc2 Hussain Mehdi, director of investment strategy at \uc2 HSBC Asset Management\uc2  believes gilts still look attractive at current levels, saying:\par \par \uc2 2.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Janet Mui, head of market analysis at wealth manager \uc2 RBC Brewin Dolphin\uc2 , agrees that governor \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s vote will be \u8216\'20\'18crucial\u8217\'20\'19 in deciding whether the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  cuts interest rates in December:\par \par \uc2 2.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  rate cut: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 Reaction to today\u8217\'20\'19s UK interest rate decision (no change) has been pouring in.\par \par \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2 , chief UK economist at CapitalEconomics, believes today\u8217\'20\'19s decision to hold rates at 4% is a pause in the downward trend in interest rates rather than the end.\par \par \uc2 Dales says:\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB, says UK borrowers could get an early Christmas present from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  in December:\par \par \uc2 James Smith, ING\u8217\'20\'19s developed markets economist for the UK, is more convinced that a rate cut is coming in December, adding:\par \par \uc2 Matt Swannell, chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club, says the MPC was \u8220\'20\'1Cunusually divided\u8221\'20\'1D, leading to the narrow 5-4 vote, adding:\par \par \uc2 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US job cuts in October hit 22-year high as firms embrace AI\par \par \uc2 Away from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interest rate decision, we have new signs that artificial intelligence is hitting the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 U.S.-based employers announced 153,074 job cuts in October, up 175% from the 55,597 cuts announced in October 2024, outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  has reported. That\u8217\'20\'19s the highest for the month since October 2003.\par \par \uc2 Andy Challenger\uc2 , chief revenue officer for \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , says some companies are reducing headcount after adding staff during the Covid-19 pandemic, while others are halting hiring as they adopt AI.\par \par \uc2 Challenger explains:\par \par \uc2 So far this year, US employers have announced 1,099,500 job cuts, an increase of 65% from the 664,839 announced in the first ten months of last year. That\u8217\'20\'19s the highest level since 2020.\par \par \uc2 1.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 John-Paul Ford Rojas of the DailyMail says people are worried that income tax will go up in the budget \u8211\'20\'13 would that give the Bank any impetus to cutting interest rates in December?\par \par \uc2 Andrew\par \par \uc2 Bailey sticks to his guns, repeating that the Bank will assess the budget measures when they are announced, rather than speculating about fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 1.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Szu Ping Chan of the Telegraph asks whether government action in the last year has helped, or hindered, the fight against inflation.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  resists any temptation to criticise the Treasury. He points out that the government has many public policy objectives. For example, that can mean allowing water bills to rise to fund the clean-up of the water industry \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s not for the Bank to oppose that (but it can point out the impact on inflation).\par \par \uc2 1.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 My colleague PhillipInman looks forward, asking why the Bank doesn\u8217\'20\'19t expect to cut rates more quickly in 2026. The Bank\u8217\'20\'19s scenarios show growth slowing and unempoyment rising in 2026, so why does they show restrictive interest rates?\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  replies that the degree of restrictiveness is easing, and the Bank will continue to assess how restrictive the cost of borrowing is.\par \par \uc2 1.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joel Hills of ITVNews asks why the Bank doesn\u8217\'20\'19t support strong pay growth.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  replies that pay growth is an important determinent of inflation, while inflation expectations help to determine wage negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Pay settlements are coming down, he adds, and they will be an \u8220\'20\'1Cimportant part of our considerations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 1.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Phil Aldrick of \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  asks if the Bank would welcome action by \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  to slow increases in regulated prices.\par \par \uc2 Deputy governor ClareLombardelli says that the Bank thinks around 0.4% of the current 3.8% inflation rate is due to those administered prices.\par \par \uc2 1.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bailey: I want more evidence that inflation has peaked before voting to cut rates\par \par \uc2 Helia Ebrahimi of Channel 4 asks how \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  can justify leaving interest rates on hold when the Bank believes inflation has peaked.\par \par \uc2 The governor (who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/06/bank-of-england-interest-rates-andrew-bailey-stock-markets-pound-budget-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-690c8e4d8f08d1d817776655#block-690c8e4d8f08d1d817776655"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effectively the swing voter in today\u8217\'20\'19s 5-4 split}}}\uc2 ) replies that he wants to see more evidence that inflation has passed its peak.\par \par \uc2 Deputy governor \uc2 Dave Ramsden\uc2  (who did vote for a cut) provides some support for Bailey from the governor\u8217\'20\'19s left flank, pointing out that inflation is still 3.8% \u8211\'20\'13 a long way above the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target.\par \par \uc2 1.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bailey: There could be an AI bubble\par \par \uc2 Asked about the risks of an artificial intelligence bubble in the financial markets, \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  says it\u8217\'20\'19s a danger, but also suggests AI could have significant benefits even if valuations are too high.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  governor tells reporters:\par \par \uc2 1.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s no escape from the budget today!\par \par \uc2 Dharshini David of the \uc2 BBC\uc2  asks if the recent increase in national insurance rates, and the minimum wage, made the Bank more hesistant to cut rates.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  says the Bank wanted to see how businesses responded to these higher costs.\par \par \uc2 He says some firms have cut back on hiring, while others have raised salaries by less.\par \par \uc2 1.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sam Fleming of the FinancialTimes reminds \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  that he has suggested he agrees with current interest rate market pricing* \u8211\'20\'13 does that mean 3.5% is the likely terminal rate for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 what is the range of views on this on the committee?\par \par \uc2 (Terminal rate is the point in an easing cycle when a central bank stops cutting interest rates).\par \par \uc2 Governor Bailey replies that the market curve gives a \u8216\'20\'18sensible path\u8217\'20\'19 for interest rates, which isn\u8217\'20\'19t always the case.\par \par \uc2 On the terminal rate issue, he says the committee are split in two camps \u8211\'20\'13 some policymakers do have a prediction, and others (including Bailey) who don\u8217\'20\'19t think there is enough confidence in any estimate of an equilibrium level of rates.\par \par \uc2 * \u8211\'20\'13 in the minutes, Bailey says \u8220\'20\'1CCurrent market pricing is close to the path suggested by a forward-looking Taylor rule, which is a fair description of my position at present.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Mehreen\par \par \uc2 Khan of TheTimes asks Bailey about \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s pre-budget speech on Tuesday, in which the chancellor said she wanted to help the Bank to cut interest rates by fighting inflation. Was the governor not convinced?\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  replies that the Bank is not passing any judgement on what the chancellor said this week, it will wait to hear the budget.\par \par \uc2 12.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Onto questions\u8230\'20\'26.Ed Conway of Sky News asks what impact the impending budget had on today\u8217\'20\'19s interest rate decision.\par \par \uc2 Andrew\par \par \uc2 Bailey says the Bank only knows one thing for certain \u8211\'20\'13 there\u8217\'20\'19ll be a budget on 26 November.\par \par \uc2 He adds that the Bank takes its decisions based on known government policy; it won\u8217\'20\'19t speculate about what might be in the budget.\par \par \uc2 12.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 It appears that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is keen to hear \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget, in just under three weeks, before deciding it is safe to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Governor AndrewBailey says the Bank will receive more data on inflation and cost pressures in the run-up to its next MPC meeting in December.\par \par \uc2 The bank will also be able to analyse how this year\u8217\'20\'19s budget will influence the economy and the outlook for inflation, he adds.\par \par \uc2 12.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Worryingly, the governor of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  then points to signs that the economy is weakening.\par \par \uc2 Andrew\par \par \uc2 Bailey points out that household consumption growth has been weak, with the Bank only expecting a limited recovery in the near term.\par \par \uc2 12.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  then presents the press with several charts to show why the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  believes disinflation will continue, and why it expects wage growth to slow.\par \par \uc2 12.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bailey: Two risks will determine future changes to monetary policy\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  confirms that the Bank believes inflation has reached its peak, at 3.8% in the last few months.\par \par \uc2 He says progress in disinflation has allowed five rate cuts, but cautions that future changes to monetary policy depend on two big factors.\par \par \uc2 First, inflation is still well above the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target \u8211\'20\'13 and there is a risk that it could be sticky, and that inflation expections could have been pushed higher. That could keep inflation higher for longer.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, activity in the economy is below its potential \u8211\'20\'13 as shown by falling vacancy numbers and stalling employment growth. If households and businesses are cautious about spending, inflation could fall below target.\par \par \uc2 12.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  governor AndrewBailey tells reporters in London that the Bank has recently lost a \u8220\'20\'1Cmuch loved and respected colleague\u8221\'20\'1D, senior economist PavandeepDhami.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPav\u8221\'20\'1D was a mainstay supporting the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee for a very long time, Bailey explains, and his \u8220\'20\'1Cinsight and commitment helped shape the documents published today\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 12.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is holding a press conference now, to expain why it has left UK interest rates on hold.\par \par \uc2 12.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In a new move, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2025/november-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published statements }}}\uc2 from its nine MPC members explaining the logic behind their votes, at the end of the minutes of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Governor AndrewBailey argues that upside risks to inflation have become less pressing since August, with signs that slack is building in the economy. He concludes:\par \par \uc2 But dovish policymaker AlanTaylor is rather damning about the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s forecasts, saying that he disagrees with the \u8220\'20\'1Ccentral projection\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Taylor, who thinks the economic outlook is weaker, explains:\par \par \uc2 12.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank predicts growth will pick up in Q4\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  economists predict UK growth will pick up in the final quarter of this year.\par \par \uc2 They warn that underlying GDP growth remains subdued, but expect it to pick up slightly in the near term.\par \par \uc2 The Bank estimates that the economy grew by 0.2% in July-September, less than it had previously forecast, which it attributes to \u8220\'20\'1Cweaker-than-expected growth in exports to the US, as well as disruption linked to the \uc2 Jaguar Land Rover\uc2  cyberattack\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Headline GDP growth is expected to pick up to 0.3% in Q4.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank: CPI inflation is judged to have peaked\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  believes the recent rise in UK inflation is over.\par \par \uc2 Announcing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/06/bank-of-england-interest-rates-andrew-bailey-stock-markets-pound-budget-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-690c859f8f087d41c6d808ed#block-690c859f8f087d41c6d808ed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s decision to leave interest rates on hold}}}\uc2 , the Bank declares \u8220\'20\'1CCPI inflation is judged to have peaked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/oct/22/uk-inflation-unchanged-rachel-reeves-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK inflation has been recorded at 3.8% in July, August and September}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and the Bank is expressing confidence that the process of \u8216\'20\'18disinflation\u8217\'20\'19 isn\u8217\'20\'19t over.\par \par \uc2 Its latest Monetary Policy Report, just released, predicts that inflation is likely to fall to close to 3% early next year before gradually returning towards to the 2% target over the subsequent year.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 The Bank had previously forecast that inflation would peak at 4% in September.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dhingra and Taylor fear interest rates are 'significantly' too high\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policymakers are concerned that interest rates are \u8220\'20\'1Csignificantly\u8221\'20\'1D too high.\par \par \uc2 Four MPC members \u8211\'20\'13 SarahBreeden, SwatiDhingra, DaveRamsden and AlanTaylor \u8211\'20\'13 voted for a quarter-point cut in interest rates today, but were narrowly outvoted when governor \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  joined the other side.\par \par \uc2 The Bank says:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank split 5-4 on rate cut - governor in the majority\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  governor \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2  had the decisive vote at this week\u8217\'20\'19s interest rate decision.\par \par \uc2 The minutes of this week\u8217\'20\'19s meeting show that five of the nine members of the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee voted to hold rates at 4%, while the other four wanted a cut to 3.75%.\par \par \uc2 The bank says:\par \par \uc2 12.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK INTEREST RATE DECISION\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policymakers have left interest rates on hold at 4% as expected, in a narrow vote.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s nine-member monetary policy committee (MPC) has voted to maintain Bank Rate at 4%, resisting any temptation to lower borrowing costs for the sixth time since August 2024.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a blow to borrowers hoping for lower interest rates, but will cheer savers.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers were split 5-4 on the decision, though, showing that the decision was close.More to follow\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 11.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 The pound is a little stronger today, as investors brace for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interest rate decision in just a few minutes. It\u8217\'20\'19s up a third of a cent at $1.309.\par \par \uc2 Shares are lower in London, though, with the FTSE 100 share index down 30 points at 9747 points.\par \par \uc2 11.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a busy morning for fines for financial failings.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.centralbank.ie/news/article/press-release-enforcement-action-against-coinbase-europe-limited-6-November-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Central Bank of Ireland has fined Coinbase Europe}}}\uc2  \u8364\'20\'AC21,464,734 for breaching its anti-money laundering and counter terrorist financing transaction monitoring obligations.\par \par \uc2 It says Coinbase Europe failed to properly monitor more than 30 million transactions over a 12-month period, worth over \u8364\'20\'AC176bn.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  has been hit with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/fd511cbc-d04f-43ea-aa15-e17ef96803af"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a \u8364\'20\'AC45m fine from Germany\u8217\'20\'19s financial watchdog BaFin}}}\uc2  for shortcomings in its anti-money-laundering controls.\par \par \uc2 11.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nick Atkin, CEO of housing assocation Yorkshire Housing, hopes the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  cuts interest rates today.\par \par \uc2 Atkin argues the \uc2 BoE\uc2  could help the economy by accelerating interest rate reductions, saying:\par \par \uc2 11.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 City braces for interest rate decision at noon\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s less than an hour to go until the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  announces its decision on UK interest rates, and excitement is mounting.\par \par \uc2 The fact several banks have recently predicted a cut, from 4% to 3.75%, today, while others expect no change, makes this month\u8217\'20\'19s decision particularly tense.\par \par \uc2 Lawrence Kaplin, chief market strategist at EqualsMoney, says:\par \par \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2  investment director Russ Mould reckons today\u8217\'20\'19s decision is \u8220\'20\'1Chard to call\u8230\'20\'26with several observers expecting a rate cut even if the market is pricing in no change\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mould adds:\par \par \uc2 10.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bad weather may also have hurt UK builders last month.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Pugh, chief economist at RSM UK and Ireland, says:\par \par \uc2 10.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s S&P Global construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  points to a sector firmly in retreat, says Matt Swannell, chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club.\par \par \uc2 9.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 October\u8217\'20\'19s slump in construction activity is a blow to the \u8220\'20\'1CBuild, baby build\u8221\'20\'1D ethic being pushed by UK housing secretary Steve Reed.\par \par \uc2 Property agent Emma Fildes points out that sluggish demand and increased costs hurt the sector:\par \par \uc2 9.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK construction activity falls at fastest pace for over five years\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Activity in the UK construction sector has declined at the fastest pace for over five years.\par \par \uc2 Civil engineering and domestic housebuilding both shrank sharply in October, according to the latest survey of purchasing managers from data provider S&P Global.\par \par \uc2 Construction firms reported a sustained downturn in new work last month, which was blamed on sluggish market conditions, fewer tender opportunities and delays with the release of new projects. There were also reports that elevated political and economic uncertainty had discouraged client spending, the report says.\par \par \uc2 Building firms also cut staff at the fastest rate in just over five years, due to the shortfall in demand which also led to weaker demand for construction products.\par \par \uc2 This pulled the S&P Global UK construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  down to 44.1 in October, from 46.2 in September. That shows the steepest decline in industry activity since May 2020 (any reading below 50 indicates a contraction), when building work was hit by the Covid-19 lockdown.\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at \uc2 S&P Global Market Intelligence\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 9.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Professor Costas Milas, of the Management School at the \uc2 University of Liverpool\uc2 , is surprised by the speculation of a \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rate cut today.\par \par \uc2 He tells us that it\u8217\'20\'19s not a good time to lower borrowing costs:\par \par \uc2 9.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Norway leaves interest rates on hold\par \par \uc2 Over in Oslo, central bankers have voted to leave interest rates on hold.\par \par \uc2 Norway\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  kept its policy interest rate on hold at 4.0% on Thursday, as expected.\par \par \uc2 Norges\par \par \uc2 Bank governor \uc2 Ida Wolden Bache\uc2  said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 Norges\par \par \uc2 Bank also indicated that it expects to cut interest rates over the coming year, if the economy evolves broadly as expected.\par \par \uc2 8.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 China warms on trade deals with \uc2 EU\par \par \uc2 China said on Thursday it is willing to explore the possibility of various trade and investment agreements with the EU.\par \par \uc2 Commerce ministry spokesperson HeYadong told a press conference that the two sides share \u8220\'20\'1Cextensive common interests and huge space for cooperation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The comment followed remarks by Chinese foreign minister WangYi on Tuesday, who told his Estonian counterpart in Beijing that China was ready to negotiate and sign a free trade agreement with the bloc.\par \par \uc2 It comes amid urgent efforts by the \uc2 EU\uc2  to persuade China to ease up on restrictions on the supply of chips and rare earths, vital for car and other industries, something already achieved by Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in Kuwait yesterday, trade commissioner \uc2 Maro\u353\'01\'61 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D\uc2  said the \uc2 EU\uc2  had established a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial channel\u8221\'20\'1D of communication with Chinese authorities to secure the flow of rare earth materials vital for EU industries.\par \par \uc2 He said he had discussed the issue directly with commerce Minister \uc2 Wang Wentao\uc2  several times, stressing that poorly managed export procedures could have a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery negative impact on production and manufacturing in the EU\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 8.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Sainsbury's CEO predicts budget uncertainty will delay spending\par \par \uc2 The boss of Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s has warned that UK consumers are likely to delay purchases until after the government budget on November 26.\par \par \uc2 CEO SimonRoberts told reporters:\par \par \uc2 8.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 The pound is trading near a seven-month low this morning, as the City awaits the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Sterling is up just 0.1% against the US dollar at $1.3064, having threatened to fall below $1.30 earlier this week for the first time since April.\par \par \uc2 Antonio Ruggiero, senior market analyst at \uc2 Convera\uc2 , reckons sterling remains technically oversold, but adds:\par \par \uc2 8.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank shares rise following report Budget tax raid is off the table\par \par \uc2 Shares in UK banks are rising in early trading, following reports that \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  will spare them from a budget tax raid.\par \par \uc2 NatWest\uc2  (+2.4%) and \uc2 Lloyds\uc2  (+2%) are among the top rises on the FTSE 100 this morning.\par \par \uc2 This follows a report in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  that the chancellor wants the sector to remain competitive, rather than hitting it with higher taxes.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/efa985a0-c303-4f0a-8d6a-d1318550490c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The FT say}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 8.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceuticals giant \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  has beaten earnings expectations this morning, helped by strong sales of its cancer, heart and kidney disease drugs.\par \par \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , the most valuable company listed in London, grew its core earnings per share by 12% in the last quarter, with total revenues up 10%.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive PascalSoriot said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 8.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  predicts rate cut, with governor Bailey having casting vote\par \par \uc2 European bank \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  are also predicting a cut to UK interest rates today.\par \par \uc2 They point out that the decision looks to be a very close call, but point to lower-than-expected inflation, a deteriorating labour market and likely significant fiscal tightening in the upcoming Autumn Budget.\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  told clients this morning:\par \par \uc2 8.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Drinks giant \uc2 Diageo\uc2  has cut its forecast for sales and profits in 2026, as it faces up to a slowdown in demand across North America and China.\par \par \uc2 The maker of Pimm\u8217\'20\'19s, Gordon\u8217\'20\'19s gin, Johnnie Walker whisky and Smirnoff vodka now expects its organic net sales growth to be flat to slightly down, while organic operating profit growth is expected to be in the low to mid-single digits.\par \par \uc2 Diageo\uc2  also reported a 2.2% drop in net sales in the last quarter, including \u8220\'20\'1Cweaker results in China\u8221\'20\'1D where demand for white spirits fell, and a softer performance in North America where weak consumer confidence hit US spirits sales.\par \par \uc2 Nik Jhangiani\uc2 , interim chief executive, said:\par \par \uc2 7.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 ITV\uc2  warns UK budget uncertainty is hurting ad revenue\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over this month\u8217\'20\'19s budget is hurting the economy, UK broadcaster and content creator \uc2 ITV\uc2  has warned.\par \par \uc2 ITV\uc2  told the City this morning that advertising demand has weakened this quarter, with businesses showing \u8220\'20\'1Cwidespread caution\u8221\'20\'1D ahead of the budget on 26 November.\par \par \uc2 As a result, it is planning to make \u163\'00\'A335m of \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary savings\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Carolyn McCall\uc2 , \uc2 ITV\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, told shareholders:\par \par \uc2 Those cost-adjustments include \u163\'00\'A320m of savings by moving some programming from this year into 2026.\par \par \uc2 ITV\uc2  will also make \u163\'00\'A315m of non-content savings, through \u8220\'20\'1Creduced discretionary spend\u8221\'20\'1D and by cutting marketing spending.\par \par \uc2 7.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 Speculation about the timing of rate cuts is reaching fever pitch, reports \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB:\par \par \uc2 7.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nomura\uc2 : Rate cut is a close call\par \par \uc2 Japanese bank \uc2 Nomura\uc2  have predicted the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will cut rates today.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday, \uc2 Nomura\uc2  said they expect \u8220\'20\'1Ca hawkish cut\u8221\'20\'1D, telling clients:\par \par \uc2 * We expect the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to cut rates by 25bp at its 6 November meeting and to remove from its guidance any reference to interest rates being \u8220\'20\'1Crestrictive\u8221\'20\'1D and the need for further cuts.\par \par \uc2 * We believe weaker data over the past month support a rate cut, but that there is probably only one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/nov/06/bank-of-england-interest-rates-andrew-bailey-stock-markets-pound-budget-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-690c4c7f8f082152466b6da4#block-690c4c7f8f082152466b6da4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting configuration }}}\uc2 (5-4) that can deliver it. Swing voters Bailey, Breeden and Ramsden will likely be needed to vote for a cut to get it over the line.\par \par \uc2 * We think a rate cut is a close call (we\u8217\'20\'19d put our probability at just 60%), and note that consensus forecasts and market pricing are for no change in rates. The greatest risk to our November cut view is that the MPC opts to wait for substantially more news published ahead of the December meeting.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 How today's rates decision is taken\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s decision on interest rates will be taken by the nine members of the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s Monetary Policy Committee.\par \par \uc2 These experts have a range of views about the correct stance of monetary policy, veering from doves who favour lower rates to protect the economy to hawks who want to prioritise the fight against inflation with higher borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Two members \u8211\'20\'13 AlanTaylor and SwatiDhingra \u8211\'20\'13 are certainly in the dovish camp, while CatherineMann and MeganGreene are on the hawkish end of the table.\par \par \uc2 That leaves governor AndrewBailey, chief economist HuwPill, and deputy governors SarahBreeden, DaveRamsden and ClareLombardelli.\par \par \uc2 The last rate cut, in August, was a 5-4 split \u8211\'20\'13 backed by Bailey, Breeden, Dhingra, Ramsden and Taylor (who had initially wanted an even larger reduction).\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Will Bank of England cut interest rates today?\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 It may not quite be on a knife-edge, but today\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  decision on interest rates is providing plenty of uncertainty for the markets to chew on.\par \par \uc2 At noon, the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee will reveal its latest decision on interest rates, which are currently set at 4%.\par \par \uc2 And while the odds are in favour of a hold, the money markets last night indicated there is a one in three chance that base rate will be cut to 3.75% today. That would be the sixth cut to borrowing costs since August 2024, as the Bank eased back on the restrictive policy imposed to cool inflation.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s certainly a tricky decision for the Bank. Although still too high, UK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/oct/22/uk-inflation-unchanged-rachel-reeves-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation was lower than expected in September at 3.8%}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps a sign that cost of living pressures are starting to cool.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers will also have noted that \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  appeared to prepare the ground for tax rises \u8211\'20\'13 which would be disinflationary, and hurt growth - in a rare pre-budget speech this week.\par \par \uc2 Danni Hewson, head of financial analysis at \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 A recent slowdown in wage growth could also persuade some of the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s nine interest rates policymakers to vote for a cut.\par \par \uc2 Julien Lafargue, chief market strategist at \uc2 Barclays Private Bank\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 8.30am GMT: Eurozone construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for October\par \par \uc2 * 9.30am GMT: UK construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for October\par \par \uc2 * Noon GMT: Bank of England interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 * 12.30pm GMT: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  press conference\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251106elb6001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
