\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Air traffic controllers receive $0 paychecks as government shutdown stretches\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo \par \uc2 436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2025\par 22:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 11,000 air traffic controllers, deemed essential workers, did not receive wages for two weeks of work\par \par \uc2 Air traffic controllers and \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  employees have missed their first paycheck as the federal government shutdown rolls through its fourth week. They remain required to work.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/28/us/politics/shutdown-air-traffic-controllers-duffy.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nearly 11,000 air traffic controllers}}}\uc2 , who are deemed essential workers, received a $0 paycheck on Tuesday, equating to two weeks of unpaid work. \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2 , the transportation secretary, warned at a Tuesday press conference that another missed paycheck could be financially catastrophic for employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of our controllers can make it without this first paycheck; they\u8217\'20\'19ve been in the job 10, 15, 20 years; they\u8217\'20\'19ve planned for days like this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Duffy at the LaGuardia airport in New York City.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is day one,\u8221\'20\'1D added Duffy of the missed payments. \u8220\'20\'1CDay two gets harder, [and] day three is harder after that, as expenses continue to roll [in].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Duffy noted that air travel is still safe, but that flight delays and other travel issues could continue as the shutdown goes on. Flight disruptions have become increasingly common due to staffing issues, according to the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration (FAA)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Staffing issues were reported in Chicago, Dallas, Nashville and other major cities. At the time of publishing, more than 3,000 flights were delayed within, into or out of the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.flightaware.com/live/cancelled/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to \uc2 FlightAware\uc2 . The number of US flights cancelled was 132.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders have also spoken about the financial hardships of essential workers. \u8220\'20\'1CAir traffic controllers have to have 100% of focus 100% of the time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nick Daniels, president of \uc2 National Air Traffic Controllers Association\uc2 , at the same press conference. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I\u8217\'20\'19m watching air traffic controllers going to work \u8230\'20\'26 They\u8217\'20\'19re worried about paying for medicine for their daughter. I got a message from a controller that said, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m running out of money. And if she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get the medicine she needs, she dies.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some airlines have taken to donating meals to unpaid federal workers to alleviate financial hardships. United told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/government-shutdown-air-traffic-controllers-united-airlines-delta-jetblue/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CBS News}}}\uc2  that it was feeding workers at its hubs, including in Chicago, Los Angeles, and Washington DC. Delta and \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  have also said they would be offering food to workers.\par \par \uc2 Air traffic controllers have taken on side gigs such as Uber Eats and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  to make ends meet until the government reopens.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey should never work a side job, that they should never get off a night shift and then go wait tables,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniels at the news conference.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251028elas006mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
