\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Federal workers squeezed as shutdown drags on: \u8216\'20\'18I can\u8217\'20\'19t believe we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to get paid\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,108 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2025\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anxiety and economic stress among federal employees as Republicans and Democrats remain deadlocked on bill\par \par \uc2 More furloughs, more anxiety and more economic stress are bearing down on federal employees as the shutdown of the federal government continues into its fourth week with Republicans and Democrats at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/11/republicans-democrats-us-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 standstill}}}\uc2  on negotiating a budget deal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no sight of this ending and we\u8217\'20\'19re starting to wonder if we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be made whole and if this is going to continue into the next round of pay, which is what we\u8217\'20\'19re headed into now. On Friday, we will be missing our first full paycheck,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnny Jones, council secretary treasurer for the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees (AFGE)\uc2  TSA Council 100, and a TSA employee in Dallas-Fort Worth, Texas, said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow people are really starting to get nervous. They\u8217\'20\'19re starting to make preparations for liquidations or they\u8217\'20\'19re making preparations in their lives of how we\u8217\'20\'19re going to survive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He cited cases of members crying and starting to get desperate, citing the previous shutdown in 2019 where he and other co-workers had to help a co-worker transport her children to Colorado to be with family because she could no longer afford childcare.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just can\u8217\'20\'19t believe we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to get paid,\u8221\'20\'1D Jones added. \u8220\'20\'1C It\u8217\'20\'19s unbelievable. If you worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s and they did this, they could sue you, shut your business down, but you\u8217\'20\'19re working for the government, they cannot pay you, and it\u8217\'20\'19s OK. And this is a problem. People are now going to have to take home debt. People are going to have to take on new things, maybe even work their full shift, pick up their kids and do \uc2 GrubHub\uc2  or something, looking for other means of income on the side.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in Washington, the deadlock continues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have a strategy,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike Johnson, the US House speaker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bsky.app/profile/thebulwark.com/post/3m3pmqe3r342d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  reporters on 21 October, as the House of Representatives\u8217\'20\'19 calendar for October remains {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/MacFarlaneNews/status/1979208878337282066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 empty}}}\uc2 . House members have been on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dccc.org/house-republicans-paid-vacation-hits-one-month-milestone/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid vacation}}}\uc2  since 19 September, when it went on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfQRhVNEjjU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recess}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The House speaker\u8217\'20\'19s office said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CHe has consistently said that the House will return to regular legislative session as soon as \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and the Democrats vote to end the shutdown and reopen the government.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/30/government-shutdown-healthcare-medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held firm}}}\uc2  on a budget that includes extending healthcare subsidies that would prevent health insurance premiums from soaring for millions of Americans, resulting in loss of health insurance for about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbpp.org/research/health/by-the-numbers-harmful-republican-megabill-will-take-health-coverage-away-from"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15 million Americans }}}\uc2 due to the subsidy expirations and cuts to Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Throughout and leading up to the shutdown, federal workers have been subjected to threats by the Trump administration, which have included {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2025/10/07/trump-memo-furloughed-federal-workers-backpay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatening }}}\uc2 to withhold back pay to furloughed workers, conducting reductions in force (though a federal court has temporarily {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/15/federal-worker-firings-blocked-judge-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  the firings), and cutting federally funded infrastructure programs with threats to go after programs deemed priorities for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/government-shutdown-latest-trump-to-meet-with-senate-republicans/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 referred}}}\uc2  to \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2 , the White House office of management and budget director, as \u8220\'20\'1CDarth Vader\u8221\'20\'1D on 21 October.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey call him Darth Vader, I call him a fine man. He\u8217\'20\'19s cutting Democrat priorities, and they\u8217\'20\'19re never going to get them back,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said, in claiming the shutdown allows the administration to enact cuts to federal services and programs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s played hell with our psyche, for sure,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ruark Hotopp, District 8 national vice-president of the \uc2 AFGE\uc2  in the midwest and an employee at the \uc2 US Citizenship and Immigration Service\uc2 , on the consistent attacks on federal workers since January 2025, from \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) cuts, to rhetoric from Vought and other Trump administration officials criticizing federal civilian employees.\par \par \uc2 Hotopp explained he was in Washington DC lobbying various members of Congress around these issues last week, and the threats were laughed off by Republican Senate staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was a Republican senator\u8217\'20\'19s office, and they reassured me that, while we understand what the president\u8217\'20\'19s saying, that we don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with the president\u8217\'20\'19s position, and it is the full intention of the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  to make sure these those folks get paid. So while that\u8217\'20\'19s reassuring to me, to see this sort of public rhetoric to the folks on the frontlines, that\u8217\'20\'19s not reassuring at all,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe president is one of the very first people to say this rhetoric needs to be toned down, while he then fans the flames,\u8221\'20\'1D added Hotopp. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19re going to get back to some sort of normalcy, it has to start with the president himself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nicole Cantello, president of the AFGE Union Local 704 and an attorney at the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  (\uc2 EPA\uc2 ), explained the rollout of furloughs had been \u8220\'20\'1Cchaotic\u8221\'20\'1D for federal employees at the agency, with the \uc2 EPA\uc2  using leftover funds to stave off furloughs in the beginning of the shutdown, only for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eenews.net/articles/epa-hit-with-another-wave-of-furloughs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass furloughs }}}\uc2 to be issued earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m located here in the Great Lakes, and we are the ones that do all the work to try to protect the Great Lakes. Drinking water for over 40 million people come from the Great Lakes. The people that check the health of the lake to those who find cases against polluters, all those inspectors, they were all just furloughed,\u8221\'20\'1D Cantello said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe human health and environment will definitely be impacted. More pollution will go out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She expressed concern for the prolonged shutdown and its impact on attrition. Since January 2025, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  said its workforce had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/newsreleases/epa-announces-reduction-force-reorganization-efforts-save-taxpayers-nearly-three"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slashed}}}\uc2  from 16,155 employees to 12,448 employees through firings, retirements and buyouts. The Trump administration had attempted to cut dozens more workers at the agency through a reduction in force during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGiven everything that\u8217\'20\'19s happened here, who knows who will come back from the furlough?\u8221\'20\'1D Cantello said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have a good handle on that, but I\u8217\'20\'19m worried that we\u8217\'20\'19re losing and more people and the agency will be rendered even more ineffective.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 EPA\uc2  would not comment on or provide numbers on how many workers were furloughed at the agency, but said: \u8220\'20\'1CCongressional Democrats are not only unwilling to vote for a clean funding bill, but their goal is to inflict as much pain on the American people as possible. The false narratives being peddled by union bosses and their Democratic allies are nothing more than deliberate fear-mongering designed to create chaos and deceive hardworking Americans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251025elap001us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
