\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How are unions pushing back against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on labor and layoffs?\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2025\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions are battling in court to halt firings and ensure workers retain their collective bargaining rights\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues his drive to cull the federal workforce and consolidate his power, labor unions have emerged as some of his staunchest opponents.\par \par \uc2 Unions are battling the administration in federal courtrooms nationwide, after filing dozens of lawsuits to try to halt attempts to shed hundreds of thousands of government employees, strip collective bargaining rights from over a million workers, and gut some federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, they made a significant breakthrough: Judge Susan Illston, of the US district court\u8217\'20\'19s northern district of California, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/15/federal-worker-firings-blocked-judge-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 granted}}}\uc2  a temporary restraining order blocking Trump\u8217\'20\'19s latest mass layoffs from the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Such lawsuits are designed to stand up to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe growing autocratic threat we are seeing in the United States each day\u8221\'20\'1D, said Skye Perryman, CEO and president of the legal group Democracy Forward, which has represented unions in a string of cases, including their bid to block Trump\u8217\'20\'19s latest layoffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the United States, working people and organized labor have been responsible for stopping abuses of power and igniting generational change,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CIn light of this history, it is no surprise that working people and organized labor are playing such a role in this time against the harmful actions of this administration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and prominent Democrats have accused Trump of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ctnewsjunkie.com/2025/08/29/labor-leaders-elected-officials-denounce-latest-trump-attacks-on-federal-unions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overseeing}}}\uc2  unprecedented attacks on workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, and of being the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost anti-labor, anti-worker administration\u8221\'20\'1D in the history of the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEach lawsuit demonstrates the same thing in each filing,\u8221\'20\'1D Suzanne Summerlin, a labor attorney in Washington DC, said. \u8220\'20\'1CA government willing to break the law, just to see if anyone will stop it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s governance by impulse, not principle \u8211\'20\'13 like handing the keys to the country over to a group of 12-year-olds,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ll keep going and testing the limits until an adult stops them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s encouraging is that unions working with democracy organizations are stepping up to hold this lawlessness to account,\u8221\'20\'1D Summerlin added. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re defending not just the rights of federal workers, but the idea of a professional, nonpartisan civil service that serves the American people, not a political party. That fight is essential to protecting democracy itself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 According to a tracker by the non-profit law journal Just Security, at least 30 lawsuits have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://filed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2 by unions against the Trump administration. Here are three of the most significant areas they are trying to push back.\par \par \uc2 1. Attacks on labor\par \par \uc2 In March, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/01/trump-federal-unions-labor-bargaining-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  an executive order, invoking national security, which sought to rescind collective bargaining rights for two-thirds of federal workers: over a million employees. In August, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2025/08/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-exempts-agencies-with-national-security-missions-from-federal-collective-bargaining-requirements-6a95/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  another executive order, seeking to rescind collective bargaining agreements at additional federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump supports constructive partnerships with unions who work with him; he will not tolerate mass obstruction,\u8221\'20\'1D the first {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/fact-sheets/2025/03/fact-sheet-president-donald-j-trump-exempts-agencies-with-national-security-missions-from-federal-collective-bargaining-requirements/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 order}}}\uc2  stated. \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , president of \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  (Afge), {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afge.org/article/congress-inches-closer-to-vote-to-overturn-trumps-unionbusting-orders/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  it the \u8220\'20\'1Csingle most aggressive action taken by the federal government against organized labor in US history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Six labor unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afge.org/globalassets/documents/generalreports/2025/american-federation-of-government-employees-afl-cio-et-al-v.-trump-et-al-complaint.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2 a lawsuit challenging the order, which unions argue is unlawful. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that this order is punishment for unions who are leading the fight against the administration\u8217\'20\'19s illegal actions in court \u8211\'20\'13 and a blatant attempt to silence us,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2 , president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of unions in the US, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/press/releases/afl-cio-president-federal-collective-bargaining-executive-order-very-definition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  at the time.\par \par \uc2 The unions initially won a sweeping injunction, only for this to be overturned by a federal appeals court. The administration promptly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/22/trump-federal-workers-union-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stripped hundreds of thousands of federal workers}}}\uc2  of their union contracts in August. Court proceedings in the case are continuing.\par \par \uc2 In June, a federal judge ruled that the \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  must {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://federalnewsnetwork.com/unions/2025/06/judge-orders-tsa-to-revive-collective-bargaining-agreement-for-now/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revive}}}\uc2  its collective bargaining agreement, at least while a legal challenge works its way through the courts, after the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tsa.gov/news/press/releases/2025/03/07/dhs-ends-collective-bargaining-for-tsas-transportation-security"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled}}}\uc2  it in March. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/69733614/american-federation-of-government-employees-afl-cio-v-noem/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trial}}}\uc2  has been set for 2026.\par \par \uc2 2. Workforce cuts\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has tried to drastically reduce the size of the federal government, including by reclassifying thousands of federal jobs as at-will positions, firing probationary workers, and pushing a controversial deferred resignation program, which many workers say they felt threatened and intimidated into taking.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBoth people who took the deferred resignation program offer and those who stayed were forced to choose between two bad options,\u8221\'20\'1D said a former Department of Energy employee who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 The employee accepted the deferred resignation program as their maternity leave ended because they were fearful of potential layoffs at the agency. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel a lot of grief for my job, the great people I worked with who have also left, and my dreams for the projects I was so excited to lead,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 Unions have filed a string of lawsuits to challenge initial firings, reclassifications, and resignation program. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afge.org/article/summary-of-afge-lawsuits-against-trump--how-litigation-works-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Litigation}}}\uc2  in these cases continues.\par \par \uc2 3. Dismantling agencies\par \par \uc2 Beyond efforts to dismiss, or drive out, many employees from the federal government, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s administration has also sought to break apart some of the agencies they work for.\par \par \uc2 Organizations such as the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2  (\uc2 Niosh\uc2 ), the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2 , Voice of America, \uc2 USAID\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/cfpb-union-files-its-petition-for-5164004/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau}}}\uc2  (\uc2 CFPB\uc2 ) have all been in the sights of the White House.\par \par \uc2 A blizzard of lawsuits were filed by unions in a bid to slow, and ultimately stop, these attempts. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s rash move to decimate the \uc2 Niosh\uc2  workforce is not only unlawful but shortsighted,\u8221\'20\'1D Bonnie Robin-Vergeer, Public Citizen Litigation Group attorney, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/press/releases/labor-and-workplace-health-and-safety-groups-sue-restore-programs-national-institute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  in May. \u8220\'20\'1CIn bypassing Congress and effectively shutting down the agency, [the administration] violated federal laws and exceeded its power under the constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many of the targeted departments remain open, for now, although large numbers of their employees are on leave, pending the ultimate outcome of legal battles over their future. Although unions initially won an injunction preventing Trump from gutting the \uc2 CFPB\uc2 , this was overturned by a judge by a DC circuit panel in August. The unions are seeking to appeal.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The White House did not comment on the lawsuits. An automated response said the press office \u8220\'20\'1Cmay experience delays\u8221\'20\'1D during the federal government shutdown, and took the opportunity to blame Democrats for both parties\u8217\'20\'19 failure to reach a deal to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COf the scores of lawsuits that have been filed since January, for the most part, the unions that have filed them, or the individual employees that have filed them, have won in the trial court,\u8221\'20\'1D said Catherine Fisk, Barbara Nachtrieb Armstrong professor of law at the \uc2 University of California Berkeley\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIn many cases, if they\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten to a court of appeals, they have won there, they have prevailed in making the preliminary showing necessary to get the court to order the government to stop what it\u8217\'20\'19s doing and to reinstate the workers while the case gets litigated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fisk pointed to the Civil Service Reform Act of 1978, which regulates every aspect of hirings, promotions, discipline, and firings of federal civilian employees.\par \par \uc2 On the temporary injunction blocking Trump from firing federal employees during the government shutdown, Robin Tholin of Altshuler Berzon, a law firm representing plaintiffs Afge and Afscme in the lawsuit, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe law plainly does not permit this administration\u8217\'20\'19s scheme to order mass firings across the federal government for their own political advantage, including by illegally keeping federal employees working during a government shutdown to carry out the firing of their fellow employees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Once these cases have made their way through the lower courts, the question turns to what happens next \u8211\'20\'13 or, as Fisk put it, \u8220\'20\'1Chow far the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is going to go in allowing the Trump administration to do whatever it wants\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court overruled two injunctions initially won by Afge, which had temporarily halted large-scale reductions of the federal workforce while lawsuits worked their way through lower courts.\par \par \uc2 No one is sure what will happen next. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t hazard a guess on whether the majority of the court is prepared to declare civil service protections for federal civilian employees unconstitutional,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fisk. \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems shocking, because there has been civil service protection for federal civilian employees since the mid-late 19th century.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251019elaj001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
