\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Biggest US labor unions fuel No Kings protests against Trump: \u8216\'20\'18You need a voice to have freedom\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2025\par 00:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With more than 2,700 events planned across the US, one organizer, James Phipps, 75, says: \u8216\'20\'18Protest is all I\u8217\'20\'19ve done all my life\u8217\'20\'19What to know about the anti-Trump protests attracting millionsNo Kings protests: in pictures\par \par \uc2 Recovery from a recent surgery for colon cancer will not stop James Phipps, 75, from attending Saturday\u8217\'20\'19s No Kings demonstration in Chicago, Illinois. \u8220\'20\'1CI have a burning desire to be a part of the protest.\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause that\u8217\'20\'19s all I\u8217\'20\'19ve done all my life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Phipps, born in Marks, Mississippi, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seiu73.org/2022/02/new-documentary-shows-life-of-local-73-member-and-civil-rights-activist/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 involved}}}\uc2  in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/civil-rights-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 civil rights movement}}}\uc2  in the 1960s from the age of 13, when he was part of racially integrating his local high school and organizing with the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. At 15, he became involved in the Mississippi Freedom Labor Union (MFLU), which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://snccdigital.org/events/mississippi-freedom-labor-union-founded/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organized}}}\uc2  sharecroppers for better wages.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the MFLU was organizing cotton pickers. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were paid 30 cents an hour, working in the hot sun, 10 hours a day, which was $3, two and half cents per pound of cotton,\u8221\'20\'1D said Phipps. \u8220\'20\'1CIt broke their necks, backs, pelvis and knees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey had no medical care,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s one of the key things in my mind right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Phipps, who now works in administrative support in Cook county, is a member of SEIU Local 73.\par \par \uc2 He was thankful he had health insurance to cover his recent cancer surgery. The federal government shutdown continues, after Democrats demanded that Republicans address {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/30/government-shutdown-healthcare-medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent Medicaid cuts under Donald Trump}}}\uc2  and extend health insurance subsidies scheduled to expire at the end of the year. The expiration would set the stage for rapidly rising insurance premiums and risk driving an estimated 3.1 million Americans off health insurance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have greedy men thinking about one thing, and that\u8217\'20\'19s about enhancing their pocketbook, their financial wellbeing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Phipps, who has also been alarmed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/video/2025/oct/02/how-chicago-is-resisting-donald-trumps-immigration-crackdown-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aggressive Immigration and Customs Enforcement (Ice) raids}}}\uc2  in Chicago. The Trump administration has defended the raids with false and misleading {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/06/trump-chicago-crime-red-states-violent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claims}}}\uc2  about crime.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no reason why you should walk the streets, taking people out of their home, and they\u8217\'20\'19ve been here for 20 or 30 years,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI had Mexican neighbors live next door to me 41 years. They were some of my best friends in life. We coalesced with each other.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were social with neighbors, with each other, and we loved each other. When one saw somebody died or there was a problem, we were already there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There are parallels, Phipps said, between how immigrants are being treated under Trump to the discriminatory laws he grew up under in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe same struggle that Mexican Americans and people of color are going through, we went through that since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2019/08/14/magazine/1619-america-slavery.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1619}}}\uc2 , especially in the south when we had Jim Crow,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you dared do anything at that time to confront them about the way you were treated, you would end up being found in the river or lynched somewhere, so I identify with what is going on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We didn\u8217\'20\'19t want kings then, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t want kings now\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Some of the largest labor unions in the US are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nokings.org/partners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 involved}}}\uc2  in organizing the No Kings protests, with more than 2,700 demonstrations planned across all 50 states, with the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ) and \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  anchoring events.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnions understand that a voice at work creates power for regular people at work. Unions understand that a voice in democracy creates power for regular folks, for working folks in a society,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThese are two of the main ways that regular folks have any power.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe and labor understand that you need to have a voice to have freedom. Freedom does not come without a voice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While prominent Republicans and Trump administration officials have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/11/republicans-democrats-us-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  the protests amount to \u8220\'20\'1Chate America\u8221\'20\'1D rallies \u8211\'20\'13 in stark contrast to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s description of January 6 rioters as \u8220\'20\'1Cpatriots\u8221\'20\'1D. The Republican congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  went so far as to suggest that Democrats were bowing to the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-terrorist wing of their party\u8221\'20\'1D by standing by demands that Republicans address recent Medicaid cuts and extend health insurance subsidies.\par \par \uc2 Weingarten said the events were actually a response to abuses of power by Trump, and designed to express frustration over his administration\u8217\'20\'19s failure to deal with issues such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/oct/16/inflation-economic-pessimism-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soaring grocery and healthcare prices}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI love America and I resent anyone attempting to take away my patriotism because I want the promise of America to be real for all Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s where labor is. They want the promise of America to be real for our members, and for their families, and for the people we serve.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur founders were a rebellious lot who said, \u8216\'20\'18We don\u8217\'20\'19t want kings.\u8217\'20\'19 And now 249 years later, people are saying, \u8216\'20\'18No, we meant it.\u8217\'20\'19 There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of things that we\u8217\'20\'19ve changed in America, but one of the things that had stayed constant is we didn\u8217\'20\'19t want kings then, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t want kings now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe real threat to this country isn\u8217\'20\'19t peaceful protesters. It\u8217\'20\'19s politicians shutting down our government to protect billionaires and corporate greed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jaime Contreras, executive vice-president for \uc2 SEIU\uc2  32 BJ, which represents 185,000 janitors, security officers, airport workers and other service employees around the east coast of the US. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s ironic to me is you call peaceful protesters \u8216\'20\'18terrorists\u8217\'20\'19, but then the people who destroyed our nation\u8217\'20\'19s Capitol building \u8216\'20\'18patriots\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn 18 October, \uc2 SEIU\uc2  members will be in the streets across the country as part of the No Kings [protests], because America belongs to the people, working people, not to billionaires or a few politicians who think they can rule like kings in a democracy like ours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251018elai00109\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
