\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US Senate\uc2  poised to approve industry lobbyist to lead chemical safety at \uc2 EPA\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Perkins \par \uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2025\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Douglas Troutman\uc2  is confirmed, the top four toxics office at the environmental agency will be held by ex-lobbyists\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is poised to approve Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s nomination of an industry lobbyist to lead the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/epa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Environmental Protection Agency\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  chemical safety office.\par \par \uc2 If the nominee, \uc2 Douglas Troutman\uc2 , is confirmed, the top four toxics office positions at the \uc2 EPA\uc2  will be held by former chemical industry lobbyists, raising new fears about the health and safety of the American public, consumers and workers, campaigners say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe lunatics are running the asylum, and industry is firmly in charge of chemical safety,\u8221\'20\'1D said Scott Faber, vice-president of government affairs for the \uc2 Environmental Working Group\uc2  non-profit, which lobbies on chemical safety issues. \u8220\'20\'1CThey will stop at nothing to reverse the progress that we\u8217\'20\'19ve made in recent years on toxic chemicals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Troutman is currently the chief lobbyist for the \uc2 American Cleaning Institute\uc2 , a trade group representing cleaning product manufacturers including \uc2 BASF\uc2 , Dow and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 . If approved, he would oversee the \uc2 EPA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/aboutepa/about-office-chemical-safety-and-pollution-prevention"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 office of chemical safety and pollution prevention}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among other responsibilities, the office evaluates the safety of new chemicals that industry wants to send to the market, re-evaluates the safety of previously approved chemicals and oversees the pesticides program.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epw.senate.gov/public/_cache/files/1/1/112ed797-a53d-48b6-ae59-60cb964eddb8/93BBA7F61ACA0794BCBC36503F5F280A84D0D25313BDD27640533213161FDE23.10.08.2025-troutman-testimony.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  made before the Senate committee on environment and public works, Troutman used language that suggested he would probably follow the administration\u8217\'20\'19s lead on deregulation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope to bring a reasoned approach to engaging chemical management matters and issues under the belief that economic prosperity and environmental protection are not mutually exclusive,\u8221\'20\'1D Troutman said.\par \par \uc2 He was among lobbyists for industry on the 2016 rewrite of Toxic Substances and Control Act, which was aimed at strengthening protections against toxic chemicals. However, industry successfully worked in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/apr/05/trump-pfas-toxic-forever-chemicals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 loopholes}}}\uc2  that chemical makers and other producers have since exploited.\par \par \uc2 Faber, who has lobbied opposite Troutman on bills, offered an anecdote that he said \u8220\'20\'1Cencapsulates who Doug is\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, public health advocates, industry and California lawmakers worked together to develop and pass {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=201720180SB258"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legislation}}}\uc2  called the Cleaning Product Right to Know Act, which required industry to disclose chemicals that were used in their cleaning products.\par \par \uc2 Before its passage, the contents were shielded behind confidential business information laws. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ewg.org/news-insights/news-release/2023/09/cleaning-products-emit-hundreds-hazardous-chemicals-new-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 EWG research}}}\uc2  has found common cleaning products may contain any of hundreds of volatile organic compounds linked to cancer and other health issues. Another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ewg.org/news-insights/news/study-frequent-use-cleaning-products-may-increase-risk-childhood-asthma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  found frequent use of cleaning products increased the risk of childhood asthma.\par \par \uc2 The California act is among the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most important chemical transparency laws in terms of protecting consumers, Faber said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a huge deal because we cannot properly protect consumers from harm unless we know what\u8217\'20\'19s in the products,\u8221\'20\'1D Faber said.\par \par \uc2 But in 2023, public health advocates and many in industry were caught off guard when Larry Bucshon, then a member of Congress, unexpectedly introduced a piece of legislation that would pre-empt state laws and repeal the Cleaning Product Right to Know Act, Faber said.\par \par \uc2 Public health groups and many industry players initially did not know who was behind the attempted repeal, called the Cleaning Product Communication Act, Faber said. But he added that they soon learned who they say was seemingly its source \u8211\'20\'13 Troutman.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleaninginstitute.org/newsroom/2023/cleaning-product-ingredient-transparency-gets-hearing-capitol-hill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 press release}}}\uc2  at the time, Troutman called the legislation \u8220\'20\'1Ca pro-consumer proposal that provides clarity on how cleaning product manufacturers inform consumers about the ingredients in their products on-package, online or in-app\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Even major cleaning companies like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.ewg.org/upload/pdf/HR5978_Clorox_Ltr.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clorox }}}\uc2 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.ewg.org/upload/pdf/HR_5978_Henkel_Letter.10.20.23.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Henkel}}}\uc2  came out against the bill, and it died without moving, Faber said, but he added that it offers insight into how Troutman may run the chemical safety office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe California law was a really historic breakthrough that united NGOs and industry leaders, and \uc2 Doug Troutman\uc2  sought to repeal it,\u8221\'20\'1D Faber said.\par \par \uc2 Others at the top positions in the chemical safety office include Nancy Beck and Lynn Ann Dekleva. Beck, a deputy administrator in the office, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/27/scientists-chemical-industry-derail-pfas-regulation-drinking-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped lead industry\u8217\'20\'19s charge}}}\uc2  against new drinking water limits for PFAS, or \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D, that are considered among the most toxic human-made substances, and which are estimated to be contaminating drinking water for at least 143 million people.\par \par \uc2 While at the previous Trump \uc2 EPA\uc2  and as an \uc2 American Chemistry Council\uc2  lobbyist after that, Beck {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nrdc.org/bio/daniel-rosenberg/nancy-beck-has-some-explaining-do"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped weaken rules}}}\uc2  around asbestos, methylene chloride, lead and PCBs.\par \par \uc2 Dekleva, a deputy assistant administrator in the chemical safety office, is a former Trump \uc2 EPA\uc2  official and \uc2 DuPont\uc2  executive, who, along with Beck, was accused of helping lead a broader effort in the chemical division to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/mar/23/trump-appointees-epa-toxic-chemical-pfas-pfbs-toxic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interfere in agency science}}}\uc2 , including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/aug/27/epa-whistleblowers-falsifying-risk-assessments-dangerous-chemicals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 altering reports}}}\uc2  for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have long track records at the agency and we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to guess \u8211\'20\'13 we know what they\u8217\'20\'19ve done and what they\u8217\'20\'19re going to do,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Sass, a senior scientist with the \uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2 , which lobbies on toxic chemical issues.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Kyle Kunkler, the new deputy assistant administrator who will oversee the pesticides program, is a former pesticide industry lobbyist. He was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.croplifeamerica.org/news-releases/croplife-america-recognizes-award-winners-at-2020-annual-meeting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 given}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1Crising star\u8221\'20\'1D award in 2020 by CropLife, a big ag trade group.\par \par \uc2 Faber said there was little that can be done to stop the new chemical safety office leadership from shredding regulations, but added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe need state powers more than ever.\u8221\'20\'1D Hundreds of state laws passed in recent years have banned or put in place restrictions on toxic chemicals, and that has forced industry to move away from using them. The Trump \uc2 EPA\uc2 , however, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/apr/05/trump-pfas-toxic-forever-chemicals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed}}}\uc2  a new rule aimed at invalidating most recent state regulations around toxic chemicals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur only hope now is preserving state laws,\u8221\'20\'1D Faber said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251018elai001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgo