\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Using us as political pawns\u8217\'20\'19: federal workers reel over threats of firings and withheld back pay\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,322 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2025\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees express growing anxiety over their pay \u8211\'20\'13 and the future of their jobs \u8211\'20\'13 amid the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown\par \par \uc2 With no end of the federal government shutdown in sight, an estimated 750,000 workers remain furloughed. Hundreds of thousands more are working without pay. They are being \u8220\'20\'1Cheld hostage by a political dispute\u8221\'20\'1D, according to union leaders, as Republicans and Democrats remain deadlocked.\par \par \uc2 In the Oval Office on Tuesday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggested that furloughed employees would not necessarily receive back pay \u8211\'20\'13 despite a legal guarantee \u8211\'20\'13 prompting further unease throughout the federal workforce. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some people that don\u8217\'20\'19t deserve to be taken care of, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll take care of them in a different way,\u8221\'20\'1D the US president said.\par \par \uc2 The administration, meanwhile, continues to threaten mass firings if Democrats stand by their demands. \u8220\'20\'1CIf this keeps going on, it\u8217\'20\'19ll be substantial,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump told reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd a lot of those jobs will never come back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2 , the White House office of management and budget (OMB) director, announced on social media that layoffs had begun. Several federal agencies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/10/federal-workers-pay-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 started announcing layoffs}}}\uc2 , but details remained scant on how many workers would be impacted.\par \par \uc2 After a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/28/us-mass-resignation-federal-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brutal year for the federal workforce}}}\uc2 , employees who spoke to the Guardian expressed growing anxiety over their pay \u8211\'20\'13 and the future of their jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the third time I\u8217\'20\'19ve been furloughed in my federal career,\u8221\'20\'1D said Priscilla Novak, a furloughed federal employee researcher. \u8220\'20\'1CBut this is the first time there were threats of having people be fired en masse. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been checking my email every day to see if I\u8217\'20\'19m fired yet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven before the shutdown, it\u8217\'20\'19s just kind of been one thing after another for us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peter Farruggia, a furloughed employee at the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  (CDC). \u8220\'20\'1CI think a lot of us are expecting the worst, hoping for the best.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot knowing when my next paycheck is going to get here is definitely very daunting,\u8221\'20\'1D Farruggia, also executive committee chair of AFGE Local 2883, which represents CDC workers, added. \u8220\'20\'1CBut at least I paid rent this month, so that was probably the most important thing. If some of my other bills go by the wayside, then it is what it is, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t really have any other options to seek out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I\u8217\'20\'19m hearing is a lot of anxiety, confusion, and chaos,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brent Barron, a \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  employee who serves as president of the National Council of Field Labor Locals, which represents workers at the department outside Washington DC. Some staffers don\u8217\'20\'19t even know whether they\u8217\'20\'19re furloughed or not, he claimed, let alone \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether or not they\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to have a job\u8221\'20\'1D for much longer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of employees out there that can\u8217\'20\'19t even miss one check, let alone have this thing drag on for weeks and weeks and weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barron. About three-quarters of the labor department has been furloughed. \u8220\'20\'1CAll we want to do is do our jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A law signed by Trump during his first term, the Government Employee Fair Treatment Act, guarantees all federal workers receive retroactive back pay once a government shutdown is over.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really baffles me that this administration can just flaunt whatever law and say they don\u8217\'20\'19t have to follow it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barron. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a law that was passed in 2019 by Congress and signed by the president. And we all know who was president in 2019.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump officials are now facing calls to clarify that the federal government will follow the law, and ensure that every furloughed employee receives back pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGiven the clarity of the law, there is no place for the Administration to backpedal on its obligation to pay furloughed workers,\u8221\'20\'1D labor unions and Democracy Defenders Fund, a watchdog group, wrote to the OMB on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Administration\u8217\'20\'19s statements appear to be a naked attempt at inflicting pain on innocent parties to gain advantage in the shutdown.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 OMB is led by Vought, an architect of the rightwing Project 2025 blueprint. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oBH9TmeJN_M"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 private speech in 2023}}}\uc2 , Vought spoke of wanting to put officials \u8220\'20\'1Cthrough trauma\u8221\'20\'1D to reduce the capacity of the federal government. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen they wake up in the morning, we want them to not want to go to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the administration continues to threaten mass layoffs, raising the prospect of further cuts beyond the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/22/us/politics/trump-federal-workers.html#:~:text=Probationary%20Federal%20Employees-,Year%20Will%20End%20With%20300%2C000%20Fewer%20Federal%20Workers%2C%20Trump%20Official,the%20end%20of%20the%20year."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  300,000 federal employees}}}\uc2  set to be removed from the government by the end of this year through firings and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/28/us-mass-resignation-federal-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attrition programs}}}\uc2 , officials have also been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/eenews/2025/10/08/judge-compels-trump-admin-to-reveal-shutdown-layoff-plans-00597619"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered}}}\uc2  by a federal judge to provide specifics on the status of any layoff plans, the agencies affected, and whether any federal employees have been recalled to work to carry out reductions in force.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people and the workers who keep this country running are being held hostage by a political dispute, by a petty political dispute that they have nothing to do with,\u8221\'20\'1D Greg Regan, president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s transportation trades department, said during a press conference this week. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is entirely vindictive and the only victims are going to be this country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve all seen the reports every single time we go through this stupid process of a shutdown, how much the American taxpayers lost. It\u8217\'20\'19s a drain on our economy. It\u8217\'20\'19s a drain on our safety. It\u8217\'20\'19s a drain on the people that live here. So we need to put this to an end.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18People cannot focus on their jobs\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Almost all \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  (TSA) employees are required to work without pay during shutdowns, in a bid to minimize the threat of disruptions at key travel hubs like airports.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty has been particularly unnerving for newer, lower-paid employees, according to Cameron Cochems, a lead TSA officer and vice-president for \uc2 AFGE\uc2  Local 1127, which represents the administration\u8217\'20\'19s employees in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 Workers are worried about when they start missing paychecks, he said, adding that several have asked where to get low interest loans to float them through missed paychecks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels kind of like there\u8217\'20\'19s just a train coming and you can hear the whistle blowing, but every day it gets a little closer and closer to us,\u8221\'20\'1D Cochems told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd right now we can barely hear the whistle because we\u8217\'20\'19re still focused on our jobs, we\u8217\'20\'19re still focused on the mission, which is protect the nation\u8217\'20\'19s transportation system to ensure freedom of movement for people in commerce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut once that paycheck doesn\u8217\'20\'19t come, I think that that train whistle is going to get louder in everyone\u8217\'20\'19s heads, and it could get so loud that people cannot focus on their job because they\u8217\'20\'19re focusing on things like \u8216\'20\'18The bank is calling me for the fifth time today\u8217\'20\'19, or \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t know how to pay for my daycare,\u8217\'20\'19 things like that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Threats made about federal workers not being entitled to back pay by Trump and his top officials have heightened anxieties and fears and \u8220\'20\'1Cthrown a lot more people for a loop, especially the people that are disadvantaged, single parents or living paycheck to paycheck\u8221\'20\'1D, added Cochems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt just feels like they\u8217\'20\'19re intentionally using us as political pawns, and they intentionally want to make our jobs and lives unstable,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven worse than morale is the future implication for how our government runs,\u8221\'20\'1D added Novak. \u8220\'20\'1CI think having a strong civil service that is not politically motivated is the most effective to render modern services for our citizens. Furloughed workers want to go back to work. We need Congress to pass a budget.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The White House and office of management and budget did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251012elac0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
