\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 White House announces federal worker layoffs as shutdown nears third week\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Washington and Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par 16:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  fights issue in court as union leader warns of \u8216\'20\'18devastating effects\u8217\'20\'19 for Americans\par \par \uc2 The White House announced layoffs of federal workers on Friday, making good on a threat it had made in response to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-federal-government-shutdown-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US government shutdown}}}\uc2 , which now appears set to stretch into a third week.\par \par \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2 , the director of the White House office of management and budget, wrote on social media that \u8220\'20\'1CRIFs have begun\u8221\'20\'1D, referring to the government\u8217\'20\'19s reduction-in-force procedure to let employees go.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cand.457131/gov.uscourts.cand.457131.39.0_1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 document filed with a federal court }}}\uc2 revealed that hundreds of layoffs took place across the executive branch, including notices sent to employees at the Department of Commerce, the Department of Energy, the Department of Education and several others.\par \par \uc2 The document shows that about 315 employees at the Department of Commerce, 466 employees at the Department of Education, 187 employees at the Department of Energy, from 1,100 to 1,200 employees at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , 442 employees at the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2 , 176 employees at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and 1,446 employees at the Department of Treasury received RIF notices.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders warned the layoffs would have \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastating effects\u8221\'20\'1D on services relied upon by millions of Americans, and pledged to challenge the moves in court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is disgraceful that the Trump administration has used the government shutdown as an excuse to illegally fire thousands of workers who provide critical services to communities across the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , national president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees (AFGE)\uc2 , which represents 800,000 federal and DC government workers.\par \par \uc2 Vought had warned that federal agencies could slash jobs if the government shuts down, but the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  largely held off after funding lapsed last week. Asked at a press conference before Vought\u8217\'20\'19s announcement why no layoffs had occurred, the top Senate Republican, \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , signaled they would happen soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe White House has now for 10 days laid off doing anything in hopes that enough Senate Democrats would come to their senses and do the right thing and fund the government,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy expectation is, yes, they\u8217\'20\'19re going to start making some decisions about how to move money around, which agencies and departments are going to be impacted, which programs are going to be impacted, which employees are going to be impacted. That\u8217\'20\'19s what a shutdown does.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of labor unions in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/AFLCIO/status/1976692348513140930"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 responded}}}\uc2  to Vought\u8217\'20\'19s post on Friday, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica\u8217\'20\'19s unions will see you in court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 AFGE\uc2  and the \uc2 American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees\uc2  (\uc2 AFSCME\uc2 ) filed for a temporary restraining order to block the Trump administration from carrying out any reductions in force (RIFs) during the shutdown. The unions filed an emergency motion for a temporary restraining order on Friday, following Vought\u8217\'20\'19s post.\par \par \uc2 Lee Saunders\uc2 , president of the \uc2 AFSCME\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CThese mass firings are illegal and will have devastating effects on the services millions of Americans rely on every day. Whether it\u8217\'20\'19s food inspectors, public safety workers, or the countless other public service workers who keep America running, federal employees should not be bargaining chips in this administration\u8217\'20\'19s political games.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy illegally firing these workers, the administration isn\u8217\'20\'19t just targeting federal employees, it\u8217\'20\'19s hurting their families and the communities they serve every day. We will pursue every available legal avenue to stop this administration\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful attacks on public service workers\u8217\'20\'19 freedoms and jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Congressional {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  have refused to vote for a Republican-backed bill to restore funding unless it includes an array of healthcare-centered concessions. After holding seven unsuccessful votes on the parties\u8217\'20\'19 spending bills, the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  leaders have put the chamber in recess until next Tuesday, meaning the standoff is unlikely to be resolved before then.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs came on the same day government employees received only a partial paycheck covering the final days of September but not the beginning of October, since appropriations lapsed at the start of the month.\par \par \uc2 At a Friday-morning press conference, the Republican House speaker, Mike Johnson, blasted Senate Democrats for not supporting the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s bill, which passed his chamber on a near party-line vote.If the government is not reopened by next Wednesday, US military personnel are set to miss a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the last paycheck that 700,000 federal workers will see until Washington Democrats decide to do their job and reopen the government,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStarting next week, American service members, many of whom live paycheck to paycheck, are going to miss a full paycheck. If Democrats don\u8217\'20\'19t end this shutdown by Monday, then that October 15 date will pass us by.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johnson has kept the House out of session throughout the shutdown in an effort to pressure Senate Democrats into supporting the Republican funding proposal. Earlier this week, a group of House Democrats sent the speaker a letter asking him to allow a vote on legislation that would ensure US troops get paid during a shutdown, but Johnson has refused to bring lawmakers back to Washington.\par \par \uc2 The Senate has become a chokepoint in the funding battle because any legislation needs at least 60 votes to advance in the chamber. In exchange for their support, Democratic senators are demanding that premium tax credits for Affordable Care Act health plans be extended beyond their end-of-the-year expiration date.\par \par \uc2 They are also seeking safeguards against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/16/explainer-rescissions-trump-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rescissions}}}\uc2  of congressionally approved funding, a restoration of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/jul/22/npr-editor-in-chief-to-step-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 money for public media}}}\uc2  outlets and an undoing of cuts to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/30/government-shutdown-healthcare-medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Medicaid}}}\uc2  healthcare program for poor and disabled Americans.\par \par \uc2 Max Stier\uc2 , the president and CEO of the non-profit \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2 , condemned the gridlock\u8217\'20\'19s impact on government workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is wrong to make federal employees suffer because our leaders in Congress and the White House have failed to keep our government open and operational,\u8221\'20\'1D Stier said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur air traffic controllers, VA nurses, smoke jumpers and food inspectors are not responsible for this government shutdown, and they shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t bear the financial burden created by the failures of our elected officials. The irony is that members of Congress and senior White House leaders are continuing to be paid.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, on 7 October, a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/eenews/2025/10/08/judge-compels-trump-admin-to-reveal-shutdown-layoff-plans-00597619"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered }}}\uc2 the Trump administration to provide specifics on the status of any layoff plans, the affected agencies and whether any federal employees had been recalled to work to carry out layoffs, by Friday, 10 October.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanprogress.org/article/the-trump-administration-plans-to-retaliate-for-a-government-shutdown-by-firing-federal-employees-but-can-it/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  on 30 September argued that a government shutdown limits the ability of the Trump administration to carry out firings, citing guidance from the office of management and budget that admitted any permanent layoffs need to have been initiated before the shutdown began.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CConstraints on permanently firing federal employees during a shutdown largely exist because of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/legal/appropriations-law/resources"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Antideficiency Act}}}\uc2  and the distinction between \u8216\'20\'18shutdown furloughs\u8217\'20\'19 that happen during a lapse in congressional appropriations and \u8216\'20\'18administrative furloughs\u8217\'20\'19, which are department and agency procedures on how to permanently let staff go, including \u8211\'20\'13 for example \u8211\'20\'13 through a RIF,\u8221\'20\'1D the report, authored by Greta Bedekovics, associate director of democracy policy at the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 , states. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s threats to layoff federal employees should be understood as a goal of the administration that will be pursued with or without a government shutdown and should not drive lawmakers\u8217\'20\'19 decisions on whether to support government funding bills.\u8221\'20\'1DShrai Popat contributed additional reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251010elaa00567\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
