\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump is \u8216\'20\'18obsessed\u8217\'20\'19 with seeming pro-worker \u8211\'20\'13 but his actions suggest otherwise\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Tait in Washington \par \uc2 1,256 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par 13:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Agriculture and other sectors are suffering under the administration\u8217\'20\'19s policies, adding up to a grim outlook\par \par \uc2 Unpaid forced leave and mass firings are hardly the first things to spring to mind as hallmarks of a golden age of the American worker.\par \par \uc2 Yet these were the possibilities floated by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  this week as he addressed a government shutdown that began on 1 October and is showing no imminent sign of ending as Democrats and Republicans attempt to stare each other down in a dispute over funding priorities.\par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2025/10/07/trump-memo-furloughed-federal-workers-backpay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports emerged of a White House memorandum}}}\uc2  suggesting that furloughed federal workers might not receive back pay, Trump \u8211\'20\'13 who ostentatiously posed as the champion of American workers during last year\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election campaign \u8211\'20\'13 was quick to twist the knife.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would say it depends on who we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about,\u8221\'20\'1D he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2025/10/07/white-house-furloughed-workers-00596231"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some people that really don\u8217\'20\'19t deserve to be taken care of, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll take care of them in a different way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Friday, office of management and budget director Russell Vought \u8211\'20\'13 who infamously said he wanted to put federal workers \u8220\'20\'1Cin trauma\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/russvought/status/1976686105199268177"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on X}}}\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe RIFs [\u8221\'20\'1Dreductions in force\u8221\'20\'1D, administration terminology for federal job cuts] have begun\u8221\'20\'1D, and within hours, agencies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/10/federal-workers-pay-government-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began confirming}}}\uc2  that notices had gone out.\par \par \uc2 That promises to heap more misery on a federal workforce already decimated and demoralized following job losses imposed by the unofficial \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D, also known as Doge, in the early months of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say this tells the true story of the US worker\u8217\'20\'19s plight under Trump 2.0.\par \par \uc2 While voicing the rhetoric of blue-collar solidarity in his election campaigns and public appearances, Trump has enacted policies that have worsened the economic realities of the working person in myriad ways, they argue.\par \par \uc2 The tax-and-spending provisions in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s flagship \u8220\'20\'1Cbig beautiful bill\u8221\'20\'1D (passed by Congress in the summer), tariffs and the administration\u8217\'20\'19s agenda of mass deportation of undocumented people are all taking a toll on workers\u8217\'20\'19 living conditions, by raising costs and driving down wages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump is obsessed with a lot of the aesthetics of being pro-worker,\u8221\'20\'1D said Samantha Sanders, government affairs director at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a thinktank.\par \par \uc2 She cited an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/trump-administration-pauses-new-miner-safety-measures-amid/story?id=120685399"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appearance at the White House in April in the company of coal miners}}}\uc2  and a giant banner of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s face hung from the Department of Labor building in Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut when it comes down to actual actions, we know, from his personal life to his policy life, he just does not deliver on those things,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s protestations of being on the ordinary worker\u8217\'20\'19s side conceal a multitude of policy sins that make their lives worse while acting as a friend and ally to the wealthy, the powerful and big business, say critics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s applied tariffs, in an un-nuanced way, and deported people that were supposedly taking American jobs and filling their schools,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sanders, who said Trump had been \u8220\'20\'1Chonest and dishonest\u8221\'20\'1D about his intentions to a segment of voters who sought clear solutions to their economic problems and had, in many cases, become disaffected with the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut the other promises, like addressing the cost of living, bringing back jobs, saying \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m going to increase wages\u8217\'20\'19, etc, there\u8217\'20\'19s clearly no strategy or policy plan to make any of that happen in a sustained way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A White House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/articles/2025/09/president-trump-is-delivering-for-american-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 press release}}}\uc2  issued last month to mark Labor Day painted a rosy picture of \u8220\'20\'1Csurging native-born employment and rising blue-collar wages \u8230\'20\'26 [and] innovative workforce initiatives like expanded apprenticeships and trade school funding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Gbenga Ajilore\uc2 , chief economist at the \uc2 Center on Budget and Policy Priorities\uc2 , said the administration\u8217\'20\'19s assault on federal workers had knock-on effects.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe federal workforce serves American workers, low- and moderate-income families,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou think about the Veterans Administration, \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 . I used to work for Department of Agriculture, and we had a missionary project focused on rural communities and rural development. That workforce is getting decimated, which is going to impact those things.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s across-the-board tariffs \u8211\'20\'13 a trademark policy that he has hailed as a revenue-boosting device that could also revive long-lost US manufacturing \u8211\'20\'13 disproportionately affects workers while big corporations are afforded protections, according to Ajilore.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLook at the range of tariffed goods: fertilizer, steel, aluminum, pharmaceuticals, heavy trucks, lumber, furniture,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s almost like, you name it, there\u8217\'20\'19s a tariff on it. That raises cost for consumers. At the same time, tariffs on these goods are inputs towards businesses, which have to raise their costs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s talking about bringing back manufacturing, but tens of thousands of jobs have gone from manufacturing in the past couple of months and a lot of it is because of increased costs from tariffs. When businesses make adjustments to maintain their margin, labor is always the first to go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yet while manufacturing workers took a hit, \uc2 Apple\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 many of whose computer products are made in China and other Asian countries \u8211\'20\'13 was granted an exemption, Ajilore pointed out.\par \par \uc2 Deportations of undocumented people had created a rising climate of fear among many in the agricultural sector \u8211\'20\'13 which has traditionally attracted many immigrant workers \u8211\'20\'13 said Antonio De Loera, spokesperson for the \uc2 United Farm Workers (UFW)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Compounding that effect, the Trump administration last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/10/02/2025-19365/adverse-effect-wage-rate-methodology-for-the-temporary-employment-of-h-2a-nonimmigrants-in-non-range"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 changed the rules}}}\uc2  governing wage levels for documented workers on temporary, non-immigrant H-2A visas, in effect allowing them to be paid less than laborers already living in the US.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ufw.org/farm-workers-condemn-2-46-billion-in-trump-wage-cuts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UFW estimates}}}\uc2  that the change will cost workers across the entire US farm sector $2.46bn in wages a year, as farmers react by hiring cheaper guest workers in place of higher-paid existing laborers, US citizens as well as undocumented workers.\par \par \uc2 De Loera, who warned that the impact would be to drive wages down across the sector, said farm workers were being made to pay the costs of a bailout the administration was preparing for farmers \u8211\'20\'13 who have complained about the impact of tariffs and a labor shortage caused by deportations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that there\u8217\'20\'19s discussions of a big farm bailout coming, but in the meantime, this is one of the things that they\u8217\'20\'19ve done to keep the farmers happy,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re taking more money out of the pockets of some of the hardest-working and poorest-paid workers in America to give it to wealthy landowners who are politically well-connected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ajilore said others sectors were suffering from a wider economic problem caused by the administration\u8217\'20\'19s policies, adding up to a grim outlook for workers generally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe American worker is struggling,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor market is frozen. There hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been a lot of layoffs, but there\u8217\'20\'19s very little hiring and so there\u8217\'20\'19s very little churn, which is one reason why the US labor market is normally so robust \u8211\'20\'13 people lose a job but are able to get new jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe long-term unemployment, people who have been out of work for more than 27 weeks, has gone up from 20% to 25% of the jobless. And a larger share of them are college graduates, who would normally be able to get jobs. So workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t able to experience mobility or progress \u8211\'20\'13 and at the same time, costs are going up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020251011elab00231\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
